STORIES['hard-or-soft'] = {
  about: 'a rock, a pillow, a sponge and a towel, and the girl who sorted them by feel',
  more: [{ serial: 'S441', after: 0, alt: 'Four things on a mat, the girl looking at them' }, { serial: 'S442', after: 2, alt: 'A hand pressing a hard rock and a soft pillow' }, { serial: 'S1740', after: 3, alt: 'A wet sponge dripping next to a dry towel' }, { serial: 'S1741', after: 4, alt: 'The four things sorted into pairs' }],
  title: 'By feel', art: 'S440', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl feeling a rock, a pillow, a wet sponge and a dry towel laid out on a mat',
  words: [
    'Mia had four things and one question: what are they like? A rock, a pillow, a sponge and a towel. She looked at them, and looking did not tell her much.',
    'So she touched them, one at a time, slowly.',
    'The rock was hard; it did not squish at all. The pillow was soft, and her hand sank right in. The sponge was wet, cold and dripping, and the towel was dry, warm from the sun.',
    'Hard or soft, wet or dry. Her hands knew what her eyes did not.',
    'She sorted them, hard and soft in one pair and wet and dry in another. Every thing had properties you could feel.',
    'Then she went around the house. The spoon was hard, the bear was soft, the bath mat was wet and the book was dry. She felt it all.',
    'Things have properties: hard or soft, wet or dry.',
  ],
};
// Grade 3, in the fewest words that still make a story.
STORIES['equivalent-fractions'] = {
  about: 'two pizzas, an argument over who got more, and slices that matched crust to crust',
  more: [{ serial: 'S444', after: 1, alt: 'A dad with his arms folded, smiling, while a boy and a girl argue over a pizza box' }, { serial: 'S445', after: 2, alt: 'One big half slice and two quarter slices lined up crust to crust, matching exactly' }, { serial: 'S1749', after: 4, alt: 'A pizza cut into eight thin slices, the girl holding up two skinny ones' }, { serial: 'S1750', after: 5, alt: 'Four thin slices lined up beside one big half slice, covering the same space' }, { serial: 'S1751', after: 6, alt: 'Three same-size pizzas cut in two, four and eight, the same amount of each shaded' }],
  title: 'Two pizzas', art: 'S443', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two same-size pizzas on a kitchen counter, one cut in two and one cut in four, a boy and a girl leaning over them',
  words: [
    'Leo and his sister Ava ordered two pizzas the same size. One came cut into two big pieces, the other into four smaller ones. Leo took one big piece. Ava took two small ones and said she had more, because two is more than one.',
    'Leo was sure she was wrong, but he could not say why. Two pieces did look like more. The box smelled of oregano, and the cheese was going cold. Prove it, their dad said. Do not shout it.',
    'So they laid Ava\'s two pieces beside Leo\'s one and lined up the crusts. The edges matched exactly. Two quarters covered the very same pizza as one half.',
    'The pizza had not changed. Only the cuts had. Cutting each half in two doubled the bottom number, from two to four. Taking twice as many pieces doubled the top, from one to two. Double both and nothing changes.',
    'The next Friday, a pizza came cut into eight thin slices. Ava grabbed two. Same as last week, she said. Leo laughed, because two eighths was a skinny strip. She had changed the bottom number and not the top.',
    'To match one half, she needed four of the eight slices, because one times four is four and two times four is eight. She took two more, and the crusts lined up again.',
    'That is an equivalent fraction. Multiply the top and the bottom by the same number, and the amount stays put. Change only one, and the amount changes.',
  ],
};
STORIES['comparing-fractions'] = {
  about: 'a birthday choice between one third and one eighth, and the big number that fooled a girl',
  more: [{ serial: 'S447', after: 0, alt: 'The girl choosing the eighth, her hand shooting up while her uncle holds the knife over the chocolate cake' }, { serial: 'S448', after: 1, alt: 'A thick third slice next to a thin eighth slice on two plates' }, { serial: 'S1752', after: 2, alt: 'Two same-size circles on a napkin, one cut into three big wedges and one into eight thin ones' }, { serial: 'S1753', after: 4, alt: 'Five thin cake slices on one plate and three on another, every slice the same size' }, { serial: 'S1754', after: 3, alt: 'A cake cut into many thin slices beside the same cake cut into a few thick ones' }],
  title: 'Which slice is bigger?', art: 'S446', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at her birthday party eyeing a big chocolate cake while her uncle holds the cake knife',
  words: [
    'At Maya\'s birthday party, her uncle held a knife over the chocolate cake and offered her a choice. One third of the cake, or one eighth. Maya loved big numbers, and eight is much bigger than three. She chose the eighth.',
    'Then her cousin Ben got one third. His slice was a thick wedge with frosting piled on the edge. Hers was a thin sliver she could almost see through. She had picked the bigger number and gotten the smaller cake. How?',
    'Her uncle drew two circles on a napkin, both the same size. He cut one into three pieces and the other into eight. Cut a cake into three and each piece is big. Cut the same cake into eight and each piece is small.',
    'The bottom number counts how many pieces the whole was cut into. More pieces means thinner slices. So when the top numbers match, one and one, the smaller bottom wins. One third beats one eighth.',
    'Later he cut a second cake into eight equal slices for the guests to take home. Ben took three. Maya took five. This time there was no trick. Every slice was an eighth, the same size, so more slices meant more cake. Five eighths beat three eighths.',
    'That is how to compare fractions. Same bottom, the bigger top wins. Same top, the smaller bottom wins. When neither matches, rename them so the bottoms match, then look again. And never trust a big number until you know what it counts.',
  ],
};
STORIES['fractions-on-a-line'] = {
  about: 'a model grasshopper, a ruler with unmarked steps and the walk from zero to three quarters',
  more: [{ serial: 'S450', after: 0, alt: 'A close look at one inch of a wooden ruler with three small unmarked lines, a boy squinting at it' }, { serial: 'S451', after: 1, alt: 'A pile of straw bits cut too short and too long, a glue bottle drying in its cap' }, { serial: 'S1755', after: 2, alt: 'A green bead sliding along the ruler in four equal steps' }, { serial: 'S1756', after: 4, alt: 'Six straw legs cut to the same length, laid in a row beside the ruler' }, { serial: 'S1757', after: 5, alt: 'A winding road with signposts at equal steps, a straw grasshopper hopping from one to the next' }],
  title: 'Marks on the ruler', art: 'S449', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a desk building a model grasshopper out of drinking straws, a wooden ruler beside him',
  words: [
    'Sam was building a model grasshopper for the science fair. Its legs were bits of drinking straw, and each one had to be three quarters of an inch long. His ruler showed 0 and 1. Between them sat three little marks with no numbers at all. Where was three quarters?',
    'He guessed and cut. Too short. He guessed again. Too long. The pile of straw bits grew, and the glue was drying in its cap. Six legs to make, and not one was right. Those little marks had to mean something. But what?',
    'His teacher put a green bead at 0 and slid it along. The three marks cut the space from 0 to 1 into four equal steps. Four steps made one whole inch, so each step was one quarter.',
    'Step: one quarter. Step: two quarters. Step: three quarters. The bead stopped on the third mark. The bottom number, four, told Sam how many steps make a whole. The top number, three, told him how many steps to walk.',
    'Sam cut all six legs at the third mark, and every one matched. Then he noticed the middle mark. Two steps out of four, exactly halfway. Two quarters and one half were the same spot on the road.',
    'A number line is a road with signposts. Cut 0 to 1 into equal steps, and every fraction is a place on that road. The bottom number sets the size of each step. The top number says how far to walk.',
  ],
};
STORIES['building-fractions'] = {
  about: 'five wooden blocks, three fifths built by hand and two granola bars shared by three',
  more: [{ serial: 'S453', after: 1, alt: 'The girl pushing wooden blocks around on the rug, a pencil and paper set aside' }, { serial: 'S454', after: 2, alt: 'Three blocks pulled forward from the row of five, each block the same size' }, { serial: 'S1758', after: 3, alt: 'The three blocks split into a pile of two and a pile of one' }, { serial: 'S1759', after: 4, alt: 'Two granola bars each cut into three equal pieces on a plate, three children reaching in' }, { serial: 'S1760', after: 5, alt: 'Unit pieces, fifths and thirds, stacking up into whole bars' }],
  title: 'Blocks of a fifth', art: 'S452', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl and her little brother kneeling on a rug beside five wooden blocks joined into one long bar',
  words: [
    'Nadia had five wooden blocks that fit together into one long bar. The bar was one whole. Her little brother Omar asked her to show him three fifths. She stared at the blocks. A fraction was a thing you wrote, not a thing you built. Or was it?',
    'She wrote it on paper. Three over five. Omar shrugged. He wanted to see it, not read it. The blocks smelled of pine and clicked as she pushed them around, and then she saw it.',
    'Each block was one fifth of the bar. She pulled three forward. One fifth, one fifth, one fifth. Three fifths, sitting on the table where anyone could count them.',
    'Omar pushed two of the three blocks together and left one apart. Was that still three fifths? It was: two fifths and one fifth, the same three blocks in two piles. Push all five together and it was five fifths, the whole bar again.',
    'At snack time their mom brought two granola bars for three kids: Nadia, Omar and a friend. Nadia cut each bar into three equal pieces, thirds, just like her blocks. Each kid took one piece from each bar. Two bars shared by three people is two thirds apiece.',
    'A fraction is built from unit pieces, one fifth or one third at a time. It can be taken apart in more than one way. It is also a sharing that has not been finished yet. Once you can build it, you can see it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['prefixes-and-suffixes'] = {
  about: 'a boy with a long spelling list and the short words hiding inside it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S456', after: 1, alt: 'A big sister covering the front of a word card with one finger while her little brother leans in, surprised' }, { serial: 'S457', after: 2, alt: 'A plain little word creature trying on a hat for its front and a cape for its back in a dress-up closet' }, { serial: 'S1764', after: 3, alt: 'The little word creature taking off its hat and cape, standing plain and proud' }, { serial: 'S1765', after: 4, alt: 'The boy crossing long words off his list, a small word peeking out of each one' }, { serial: 'S1766', after: 5, alt: 'The next morning, a teacher handing the boy his spelling paper with every word right and a gold star' }],
  title: 'Happy, unhappy, happily', art: 'S455', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy slumped at a kitchen table over a long spelling list, his big sister leaning in to help',
  words: [
    'Diego had a spelling list with happy, unhappy and happily on it. Three long words to learn, he thought, and there were fifteen more below them. He groaned and put his head on the table. The paper smelled like fresh printer ink.',
    'His big sister slid a finger over the first two letters of unhappy. What was left? Happy. Then she covered the last two letters of happily. Happy again. Diego sat up. Were these even three words?',
    'They were one word wearing costumes. Un goes on the front and means not, so unhappy means not happy. A piece on the front is a prefix. Ly goes on the end and turns happy into a way of doing something. A piece on the end is a suffix.',
    'Take the costume off, his sister said, and the small word is still standing there.',
    'Diego went down the list hunting for costumes. Hopeful: hope, plus ful, which means full of. Full of hope. Hopeless: hope, plus less, which means without. Without hope. Reread: re, which means again, plus read. Read it again. The long list was shrinking into short words he already knew.',
    'He finished in ten minutes and spelled every word right the next morning.',
    'Half the long words you will ever meet are short words in costume. Find the front piece, find the end piece, and read the middle. Once you can see the seams, long words stop being scary.',
  ],
};
STORIES['fact-or-opinion'] = {
  about: 'a dog show, a scale and the sentence nobody could check',
  more: [{ serial: 'S459', after: 0, alt: 'A man proudly lifting a fluffy brown dog while the girl writes in her notebook' }, { serial: 'S460', after: 2, alt: 'A boy with a beagle and a woman with a poodle arguing by the dog pens' }, { serial: 'S1767', after: 3, alt: 'The fluffy brown dog sitting on a big scale, the needle steady' }, { serial: 'S1768', after: 4, alt: 'A teacher and the girl looking at a notebook together at a classroom desk' }, { serial: 'S1769', after: 5, alt: 'The girl handing her finished story to her teacher, both smiling' }],
  title: 'Check it or argue it', art: 'S458', cast: [],
  alt: 'A busy county dog show, a girl reporter with a notebook among dogs and their owners',
  words: [
    'At the county dog show, Priya was writing for the school paper. Her neighbor lifted his fluffy dog, Biscuit, and told her two things. Biscuit weighs thirty pounds. Biscuit is the best dog here. Priya wrote both down.',
    'The next morning her teacher circled the second sentence in red. Priya had heard it with her own ears. Wasn\'t that enough to make it true?',
    'She went back to prove it and could not. A boy by the next pen was sure his beagle was the best. A woman with a poodle was sure hers was. Nobody changed anybody\'s mind.',
    'The first sentence was different. Priya put Biscuit on the scale, and the needle stopped at thirty. Anyone could check it and get the same answer. That is a fact. The second sentence could only be argued, because it was what her neighbor felt. That is an opinion.',
    'Her teacher showed her the clue words: best, worst, beautiful, boring. They tell you how somebody feels, not what is true. Opinions are fine to print, she said, as long as you say whose they are.',
    'Priya fixed her story. Biscuit weighs thirty pounds. His owner thinks he is the best dog at the show. Both sentences were true now.',
    'Every sentence you read is one or the other. Ask, could I check this with a scale, a map or a clock? If yes, it is a fact. If all you can do is argue, it is an opinion, and it belongs to the person who said it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['sequence-and-cause'] = {
  about: 'a muddy kitchen floor and the little words that turned a tangle into a story',
  more: [{ serial: 'S462', after: 1, alt: 'A guilty wet dog sitting by the back door beside a dripping mop' }, { serial: 'S463', after: 2, alt: 'A dog rolling happily in a muddy yard in the rain' }, { serial: 'S1770', after: 3, alt: 'The boy pointing from the muddy floor back toward the rainy window, explaining' }, { serial: 'S1771', after: 4, alt: 'The wet dog shaking water off just inside the door, drops flying' }, { serial: 'S1772', after: 5, alt: 'The boy mopping the kitchen floor while the dog watches and his mom smiles' }],
  title: 'Because and so', art: 'S461', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy and his mom in the kitchen doorway staring at a trail of muddy paw prints across the floor',
  words: [
    'Sam came home to a trail of muddy paw prints across the kitchen floor. His mom stood in the doorway, keys still in her hand. What happened here? she asked. Sam knew. He had seen all of it. But when he tried to say it, the words came out in a tangle.',
    'The dog. The rain. The door. The mud. His mom shook her head. That told her what, but not in what order, and not why. The dog looked guilty, and the mop was dripping.',
    'So Sam tried the order words. First, it started to rain. Next, the dog rolled in the yard. Then I opened the door. Finally, the dog ran across the floor. Now his mom could see it happen, one step at a time.',
    'But she still wanted to know why. Sam tried a small word. The floor is muddy because the dog came in from the rain. Because pointed backward, at the reason.',
    'Or the other way around. The dog was soaked, so the floor got muddy. So pointed forward, at what happened next. The rain was the cause. The muddy floor was the effect.',
    'His mom handed him the mop. And the effect of that, she said, is that you are cleaning it.',
    'A cause is why. An effect is what happened. Because looks back at the cause, so looks ahead to the effect, and first and then tell the order. Pick the right little word, and a tangle turns into a story.',
  ],
};
STORIES['states-of-matter'] = {
  about: 'an ice cube, a glass and a kettle, and the morning they turned out to be the same water',
  more: [{ serial: 'S465', after: 1, alt: 'A puddle spreading across a plate where the ice cube used to be, a kettle whistling behind' }, { serial: 'S466', after: 2, alt: 'Water being poured into a tall glass, taking its shape' }, { serial: 'S1779', after: 3, alt: 'Steam rising from a kettle spout and spreading through the kitchen air' }, { serial: 'S1780', after: 4, alt: 'A boy touching a foggy kitchen window, tiny water drops on the glass, an ice tray going into the freezer behind him' }, { serial: 'S1781', after: 5, alt: 'An ice cube, a glass of water and a cloud of steam side by side, the same water in three states' }],
  title: 'Three states in the kitchen', art: 'S464', cast: [],
  alt: 'A kitchen counter at breakfast with an ice cube on a plate, a glass of water and a steaming kettle',
  words: [
    'On Saturday morning, Theo dropped an ice cube on a plate and left it on the counter. He poured water into a glass. His mother put the kettle on the stove. Three different things, he thought. Ice, water and steam.',
    'When he came back, the ice cube was gone. In its place was a puddle, spreading across the plate. The kettle was whistling, and the kitchen window had gone foggy. Where did the ice go? And where did that fog come from?',
    'His mother tapped the plate. The ice cube kept its square shape until it warmed. That is a solid. Then it melted and spread out flat, taking the shape of the plate. That is a liquid. Pour it into a glass and it becomes glass-shaped.',
    'The kettle was the next step. Heat the water enough and it boils. It turns into steam, a gas that spreads to fill the whole kitchen. You cannot hold a gas or pour it. It goes wherever there is room.',
    'Theo touched the foggy window. It was cold and wet. The steam had cooled on the glass and turned back into tiny drops of water. He filled the ice tray and put it in the freezer. By lunch the water was solid again.',
    'Everything around you is a solid, a liquid or a gas. A solid keeps its shape. A liquid takes the shape of its container. A gas fills whatever holds it. Heat moves matter up that list, and cooling brings it back down. Same water, the whole way through.',
  ],
};
STORIES['forces-and-motion'] = {
  about: 'a wagon of pumpkins, a grassy hill and the three forces that moved it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S468', after: 1, alt: 'The wagon slowing to a stop in thick grass while the boy pulls hard on the handle' }, { serial: 'S469', after: 2, alt: 'A dad kneeling by the wagon wheels, pointing at the grass' }, { serial: 'S1782', after: 3, alt: 'The boy pulling and the girl pushing the pumpkin wagon up the grassy hill together' }, { serial: 'S1783', after: 4, alt: 'The empty wagon rolling back down the hill on its own, the children watching' }, { serial: 'S1784', after: 5, alt: 'Three arrows around a rolling ball: a push, the rub of friction and the pull of gravity' }],
  title: 'Pushes and pulls', art: 'S467', cast: [],
  alt: 'A red wagon loaded with three pumpkins at the bottom of a grassy hill, a boy standing beside it',
  words: [
    'The red wagon sat at the bottom of the hill, loaded with three pumpkins for the fall sale at the top. Jamal stood beside it and waited. Nothing happened. Wagons, it turns out, do not move by themselves.',
    'He grabbed the handle and pulled, and the wagon rolled, squeaking, up the sidewalk. Then his sister steered it onto the grass to take a shortcut. The wagon slowed and stopped, even though Jamal was still pulling hard. The grass was fighting him. Why?',
    'His dad knelt by the wheels. A force is a push or a pull, he said, and nothing starts or stops without one. Jamal\'s pull was one force. The grass was another. It rubbed against the wheels and slowed them. That rubbing is called friction.',
    'So Jamal pulled and his sister pushed from behind. Two forces in the same direction. The wagon crawled up the grass and reached the top, and the pumpkins sold in an hour.',
    'At the top, Jamal let go of the empty wagon for one second. It rolled straight back down the hill without anyone touching it. Gravity had pulled it, the same pull that makes a dropped ball fall. At the bottom, the grass rubbed it slower and slower until it stopped.',
    'Nothing moves without a force. A push or a pull gets things going, friction slows moving things down, and gravity pulls everything toward the ground. Watch anything roll, slide or fall, and ask which force did that.',
  ],
};
STORIES['life-cycles'] = {
  about: 'a jar of pond water and the eggs that turned into something new every few weeks',
  more: [{ serial: 'S471', after: 1, alt: 'Tadpoles with long tails swimming in quick circles in a jar' }, { serial: 'S472', after: 2, alt: 'A tadpole with tiny back legs and a shrinking tail, seen up close' }, { serial: 'S1785', after: 3, alt: 'An egg, a tadpole, a tadpole with legs and a frog arranged in a circle' }, { serial: 'S1786', after: 4, alt: 'A bean seed, a sprout and a flowering bean plant in a row of pots on a sunny sill' }, { serial: 'S1787', after: 5, alt: 'A girl letting little frogs go at the muddy edge of a pond' }],
  title: 'The jar of eggs', art: 'S470', cast: [],
  alt: 'A glass jar of pond water on a windowsill with a clump of frog eggs inside, a girl looking closely',
  words: [
    'Rosa scooped a clump of clear jelly from the pond into a jar on the classroom windowsill. Inside the jelly were hundreds of little black dots. Her brother said they were dirt. She said they were eggs. Nobody could tell.',
    'About a week later the dots had wriggled free. They had tails and swam in quick, jerky circles. They were tadpoles. Rosa fed them boiled lettuce and drew them every Friday.',
    'After about six weeks, two tiny back legs appeared. A few weeks after that came front legs, and the tails began to shrink. Every time Rosa looked, the thing in the jar was different. Was it even the same animal?',
    'It was. Her teacher drew a circle on the board: egg, tadpole, tadpole with legs, frog. Each stage leads to the next. And a grown frog lays eggs, which starts the circle again. That circle is called a life cycle.',
    'A bean does it too, her teacher said. Seed, sprout, plant, flower, and the flower makes new seeds. A butterfly goes egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly. The stages look nothing alike, but each one makes the next.',
    'In June, Rosa carried the little frogs back to the pond and let them go at the muddy edge. The next spring she went back to look. Floating by the reeds was a fresh clump of clear jelly, full of black dots.',
    'Every living thing goes around its own circle of stages. A frog, a bean, a butterfly and you. The shapes change, but the circle keeps turning.',
  ],
};
STORIES['paragraph-shape'] = {
  about: 'a paragraph about a dog that a girl built one sentence at a time',
  more: [{ serial: 'S474', after: 0, alt: 'A long messy heap of scribbled sentences on a notebook page beside a sleeping dog' }, { serial: 'S475', after: 2, alt: 'The scruffy dog trotting in with a pair of lost shoes in its mouth' }, { serial: 'S1800', after: 3, alt: 'The girl reading her sentences one at a time, pointing at each with her pencil' }, { serial: 'S1801', after: 4, alt: 'The dog sneaking a slice of birthday cake off the kitchen counter' }, { serial: 'S1802', after: 5, alt: 'A wooden table with a wide top resting on four sturdy legs' }],
  title: 'The shape of it', art: 'S473', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a desk writing, a scruffy dog beside her holding a newspaper in its mouth',
  words: [
    'Lily had to write a paragraph about her dog, Pepper, and she had a hundred things to say. He brings in the newspaper. He finds her lost shoes. He once ate a whole birthday cake. She wrote it all in one heap, and it sounded like a shopping list.',
    'Her teacher asked one question. What is this paragraph about? My dog, said Lily. Yes, said the teacher, but what about him? Every sentence in the heap was true. Not one of them said the big thing.',
    'So she wrote the big thing first. Pepper is the best helper in our house. That was the idea, and it went in the first sentence. Then the heap became proof. He brings in the newspaper. He finds my lost shoes.',
    'Then she read each sentence and asked the test question: is this about the same idea? The newspaper, yes. The shoes, yes. The birthday cake? That was not helping. That was stealing.',
    'The cake did not get thrown away. It got a paragraph of its own, with its own first sentence. Pepper is also a thief. Now Lily had two paragraphs, two ideas, and nothing in the wrong place.',
    'Her teacher drew it as a table. The first sentence is the tabletop, the idea everything rests on. The details are the legs, and a leg from a different table makes it wobble.',
    'That is the shape of a paragraph. One idea in the first sentence, details after it holding it up, and a new paragraph for every new idea.',
  ],
};
STORIES['explain-how'] = {
  about: 'a paper boat, a card of steps and the two folds that went missing',
  more: [{ serial: 'S477', after: 1, alt: 'A boy holding a torn paper hat, stuck and puzzled' }, { serial: 'S478', after: 2, alt: 'Hands opening a folded paper hat at the bottom and flattening it into a diamond' }, { serial: 'S1803', after: 3, alt: 'The boy folding paper slowly and writing a line on a card after each fold' }, { serial: 'S1804', after: 4, alt: 'A little paper boat floating in a sink full of water' }, { serial: 'S1805', after: 5, alt: 'A stranger\'s hands following a card of steps, a paper boat taking shape' }],
  title: 'The paper boat', art: 'S476', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two boys at a kitchen table with sheets of paper, one writing folding steps on a card for the other',
  words: [
    'Marcus could fold a paper boat with his eyes closed. His friend Eli could not, so Marcus wrote the steps on a card for him. Fold the paper in half. Fold the top corners down. Fold the bottom strips up. Pull it open. Easy.',
    'Eli tried it at the kitchen table. He got a paper hat, and then he got stuck. Pull what open? Every way he pulled, the hat tore. He brought Marcus the ripped hat and the card.',
    'Marcus read his card and frowned. Every step on it was right, but his hands did more than it said. After the hat, you open it at the bottom and flatten it into a diamond. Then you fold the bottom corners up and flatten it again. He had skipped what he no longer noticed doing.',
    'So he folded one slowly and wrote down every move as his hands made it. What you need: one sheet of paper. Then seven steps, in the order they really happen. Seven, not four.',
    'Eli followed the new card without asking a single question. Hat. Diamond. Smaller diamond. Pull the top corners apart. The boat popped open, and it floated in the sink.',
    'Explaining how to do something has two parts: say what is needed, then give every step in order. The test is to picture a stranger following your words. A missing step leaves them stuck, and steps out of order leave them with a mess.',
  ],
};
STORIES['give-a-reason'] = {
  about: 'a class that wanted a pet, and the girl who said why properly',
  more: [{ serial: 'S480', after: 0, alt: 'A classroom of children shouting with their hands in the air while the teacher waits calmly' }, { serial: 'S481', after: 2, alt: 'A boy standing at his desk with his mouth open, stuck on his second reason' }, { serial: 'S1806', after: 4, alt: 'A teacher writing four lines on the board while the girl watches' }, { serial: 'S1807', after: 5, alt: 'A little orange fish swimming in its new tank on the classroom windowsill' }, { serial: 'S1808', after: 6, alt: 'The class cheering around the new fish tank' }],
  title: 'I think, because', art: 'S479', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl standing in front of her class beside an empty fish tank, classmates listening',
  words: [
    'Mia\'s class wanted a pet. When the teacher asked why, twelve kids shouted at once. Because pets are cool! Because! Just because! The teacher waited for the noise to stop. That is not a reason, she said. That is a wish.',
    'Mia wanted the pet more than anyone. But she could hear that the shouting was not working. Wanting something was not the same as making a case for it. What did a case even look like?',
    'Then Leo stood up for a hamster. I think we should get a hamster, because hamsters are fun. The class clapped. The teacher nodded and asked him for a second reason. Leo opened his mouth, closed it and sat down.',
    'Mia put up her hand and said it in order. I think our class should get a fish. Because a fish would teach us to take care of something every day. Because a fish is quiet and will not bother the class next door.',
    'Then she said it again, in new words. A fish would be the perfect classmate. The teacher wrote it on the board in four lines: what I think, a reason, a reason, and what I think again.',
    'They voted, and the fish won. It got a name, Captain Bubbles, and a job chart.',
    'That is how to give a reason. Say what you think. Say because, with two real reasons. Then say it again at the end in fresh words. A wish only gets clapped for. A reason gets a fish.',
  ],
};
STORIES['equal-groups'] = {
  about: 'three bags of apples and the boy who was too slow counting them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S483', after: 1, alt: 'The aunt answering with a smile while her nephew is still counting, the line of customers growing' }, { serial: 'S484', after: 2, alt: 'Apples tipped out onto the table in three neat rows of five' }, { serial: 'S1809', after: 3, alt: 'A tray of apples in rows and columns, a finger counting down one column' }, { serial: 'S1810', after: 4, alt: 'The boy turning a tray of twelve apples sideways, four rows becoming three' }, { serial: 'S1811', after: 5, alt: 'Equal groups all over the market: egg cartons, bundles of carrots and baskets of peaches' }],
  title: 'Three bags', art: 'S482', cast: [],
  alt: 'Three paper bags of apples on a market stall, a boy counting with his finger, a line of customers waiting',
  words: [
    'Ben was helping at his aunt\'s apple stall at the farmers market. A customer pointed at three paper bags, five apples in each, and asked how many apples that was. Ben counted every apple with his finger. One, two, three. The customer sighed. Ben lost his place and started over.',
    'His aunt could say the answer before he opened the second bag. Fifteen. Every time. Was she guessing? The apples smelled sweet, and the line was getting longer.',
    'She tipped the apples onto the table in three rows of five. Five, ten, fifteen, she said, tapping one row at a time. Three groups of five is three fives added together: five plus five plus five. That is all multiplying is. Three times five is fifteen.',
    'Rows and columns like that have a name, she said. An array. Count the rows, count how many are in each row, and multiply.',
    'The next customer wanted four bags with three apples in each. Ben made four rows of three. Twelve. Then he turned the tray sideways, and the rows became three rows of four. Still twelve. Four groups of three and three groups of four were the same apples, seen from a new side.',
    'The first number says how many groups. The second says how many are in each one. Whenever things come in equal groups, you do not have to count them one by one. Groups times how many in each group, and the counting is already done.',
  ],
};
STORIES['times-tables'] = {
  about: 'the fact that would not stick, and the rectangle that made it stay',
  more: [{ serial: 'S486', after: 1, alt: 'A grandfather and his granddaughter at a kitchen table, a paper napkin and a pen between them' }, { serial: 'S487', after: 2, alt: 'A napkin covered in dots in a rectangle, turned sideways on the table' }, { serial: 'S1812', after: 3, alt: 'The girl counting by fives on her fingers while her grandfather smiles' }, { serial: 'S1813', after: 4, alt: 'The rectangle of dots split down the middle, five columns on one side and two on the other' }, { serial: 'S1814', after: 5, alt: 'The girl marching to school in the morning, saying numbers in time with her steps' }],
  title: 'Seven times eight', art: 'S485', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a table with a grid of colored dots on paper, seven across and eight down, tracing the rows',
  words: [
    'Every fact in the times table stuck for Lena except one. Seven times eight. She learned it every night and lost it every morning, like a sock that kept vanishing in the wash.',
    'Her grandfather asked what seven times eight even meant. Fifty-six, she said. No, he said, what does it mean? Lena did not know. She had been memorizing a sound, not a thing.',
    'He drew a rectangle of dots on a napkin, seven across and eight down. Then he turned the napkin sideways. Eight across and seven down. Seven eights and eight sevens were one rectangle, looked at two ways. Every fact works both ways, so learning half the table gets you the whole thing.',
    'Then he showed her how to build the hard fact from easy ones. She already knew her fives: every fact ends in five or zero. Five eights are forty. She knew her twos, because twos just double. Two eights are sixteen. Forty and sixteen make fifty-six.',
    'The rectangle split right down the middle to prove it: five columns on one side, two on the other, seven columns in all.',
    'He had one more trick: say five, six, seven, eight, and there it is, because fifty-six is seven times eight. The numbers march in order, and Lena said it all the way to school.',
    'The facts up to ten times ten are the tools every bigger problem uses. Practice a few at a time, a little each day. A fact you can picture, and build from facts you know, is a fact you keep.',
  ],
};
STORIES['sharing-equally'] = {
  about: 'twelve cookies, four friends and a fight that ended with a deal',
  more: [{ serial: 'S489', after: 0, alt: 'Four children grabbing cookies from one plate and arguing' }, { serial: 'S490', after: 2, alt: 'Four plates with three cookies on each, all matching' }, { serial: 'S1815', after: 3, alt: 'The girl holding up three fingers and then four, checking her work' }, { serial: 'S1816', after: 4, alt: 'Two more friends coming through the door, everyone turning to the girl with the plate' }, { serial: 'S1817', after: 5, alt: 'Six children at the table, two cookies on every plate' }],
  title: 'Deal them out', art: 'S488', cast: [],
  alt: 'Four children around a table with four plates, a girl dealing cookies out one at a time from a plate of twelve',
  words: [
    'Twelve cookies, four friends, and nobody wanted to be the one who got fewer. Everyone grabbed. Somebody got five, somebody got just two, and the arguing started before anyone took a bite.',
    'Grace took the plate back. There had to be a fair way. Twelve was too many to split by just looking. She could feel the warm cookies through the plate, and everyone was staring at her.',
    'She dealt them out like cards. One for each friend, around the table. Then another round, and another. No favorites, no grabbing, no arguing. The plate was empty, and every plate had three. Twelve divided by four is three.',
    'She could check it backward, too. Three cookies on each of four plates is three times four, and three times four is twelve. Dividing undoes multiplying. The same three numbers make both facts.',
    'Then the doorbell rang. Two more friends, and now there were six. Nobody grabbed this time. Everyone looked at Grace. She asked herself the backward question: what times six makes twelve? Two. So each of the first four gave one cookie back, and all six had two.',
    'Dividing is sharing out equally, one round at a time, like dealing cards. When a division looks hard, flip it around and ask the multiplying question: what times the divisor makes the total? The answer to that is the answer to this. Twelve divided by six is two, because six times two is twelve.',
  ],
};
STORIES['add-subtract-1000'] = {
  about: 'two jars of marbles and the column that overflowed',
  more: [{ serial: 'S492', after: 0, alt: 'A boy with his eyes squeezed shut, trying to add two jars of marbles in his head' }, { serial: 'S493', after: 1, alt: 'A crowded sum on paper with one column squeezed too full, the boy scratching his head' }, { serial: 'S1818', after: 2, alt: 'A teacher pointing at the ones column while the boy carries a small one to the next column' }, { serial: 'S1819', after: 3, alt: 'Marbles sorted into bags of a hundred, cups of ten and loose ones' }, { serial: 'S1820', after: 4, alt: 'The boy counting marbles into a bag for his cousin' }],
  title: 'Column by column', art: 'S491', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two big jars of marbles on a desk, a boy writing a sum in neat columns on paper',
  words: [
    'Hugo had 256 marbles in one jar and 378 in another, and he wanted one number for the whole collection. He added them in his head and got a different answer every time.',
    'The trouble was the ones. Six and eight make fourteen, and fourteen will not fit in one column. He wrote the whole 14 there anyway, and the answer came out enormous and wrong. Where was the extra ten supposed to go?',
    'His teacher showed him to go column by column, ones first. Six and eight is fourteen. Write the 4, and carry the ten to the tens column. Tens: five and seven and the carried one make thirteen. Write the 3, and carry the hundred. Hundreds: two and three and one make six. 634 marbles.',
    'Every number up to a thousand is built the same way, from hundreds, tens and ones. 634 is six hundreds, three tens and four ones. Each column holds one kind, and a ten that spills over belongs next door.',
    'Then his cousin asked for 125 marbles. Two steps: put the jars together, then give some away. Ones first again. Four ones are not enough to take away five, so Hugo borrowed a ten, and fourteen take away five is nine. Two tens take away two is zero. Six hundreds take away one is five. 509 left.',
    'Adding and subtracting big numbers is small columns, in order. Carry when a column makes ten or more. Borrow when a column runs short. And read a two-step problem all the way through before you start.',
  ],
};
STORIES['elements-of-art'] = {
  about: 'one cup drawn seven ways, once for each element',
  more: [{ serial: 'S495', after: 0, alt: 'A girl drawing a cup again and again, a pile of cup drawings growing beside her' }, { serial: 'S496', after: 2, alt: 'A cup drawn three ways: as an outline, as a flat cutout shape and shaded round' }, { serial: 'S1827', after: 3, alt: 'The cup drawn three more ways: bright red, rough with a dry brush, and tiny on a wide empty table' }, { serial: 'S1828', after: 4, alt: 'The same cup drawn with a thick bold line beside one drawn with a thin wobbly line' }, { serial: 'S1829', after: 5, alt: 'Seven cup drawings pinned in a row on a classroom wall' }],
  title: 'A cup, seven ways', art: 'S494', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at an art table with seven small drawings of the same cup in a row, each done differently',
  words: [
    'Ines drew a cup, and her art teacher said, again. Ines drew it again. Again, said the teacher, but this time only lines. Then only a flat shape. By the fourth cup, Ines was sure she was being punished.',
    'She was not. Each cup was built from just one of the seven elements of art, the pieces every picture is made of. The teacher lined them up across the table so Ines could see them side by side.',
    'Line was just the edges, a mark traveling around the cup. Shape was the outline filled in flat, like a paper cutout. Form was shaded on one side, so the cup looked round enough to pick up.',
    'Color turned it red. Texture made it rough, dragged on with a dry brush. Space put the cup small and far away, with a whole empty table around it. Value used no color at all, only light, dark and every gray between.',
    'Then the teacher took the line cup and drew it twice more. Once with a thick, heavy line, and the cup looked loud and sure of itself. Once with a thin, wobbly line, and the same cup looked nervous. Ines laughed. One element had changed the mood.',
    'Seven cups, and none looked like the others. Ines had thought art was talent. It turned out to be ingredients.',
    'The elements of art are line, shape, form, color, texture, space and value. Look for them in any picture, from a scribble to a masterpiece, and you will see how it was built.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-color-wheel'] = {
  about: 'three jars of paint and every color that came out of them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S498', after: 1, alt: 'Red and yellow paint puddles meeting and swirling into orange' }, { serial: 'S499', after: 2, alt: 'A brush mixing blue and green paint into a blue-green swirl' }, { serial: 'S1830', after: 3, alt: 'A painted color wheel with the warm half glowing and the cool half in shade' }, { serial: 'S1831', after: 4, alt: 'A painting of a warm sunset over a calm blue sea with a small orange boat' }, { serial: 'S1832', after: 5, alt: 'A pink sky fading into deep blue night at the edges of the painting' }],
  title: 'Three jars', art: 'S497', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at an art table with three jars of red, yellow and blue paint, mixed puddles between them',
  words: [
    'Ravi had three jars of paint, red, yellow and blue, and a picture in his head that needed orange, green and purple. He asked for more jars. His art teacher said the three jars already held everything.',
    'He did not believe her until he mixed. Red and yellow made orange. Yellow and blue made green. Blue and red made purple. Three primary colors had made three secondary colors, and he had not opened a new jar.',
    'Then he mixed blue with a little green and got blue-green, a color between the two. That kind is called tertiary: a primary mixed with the secondary beside it.',
    'The teacher had him set the colors in a circle. One half glowed: red, orange and yellow, the warm colors, like fire and sun. The other half sat back: green, blue and purple, the cool colors, like water and shade.',
    'Ravi painted a sunset with the warm half and a calm sea with the cool half. Then he put a small orange boat on the blue water, and it jumped off the page. Orange and blue sit across the wheel from each other. Complementary colors make each other look brighter.',
    'For the sky he added white to red and got pink, a tint. For the night coming in at the edges he added black to blue, a shade.',
    'Red, yellow and blue are the primaries, and every other color on the wheel can be mixed from them. Three jars hold the whole wheel.',
  ],
};
STORIES['looking-at-a-picture'] = {
  about: 'a painting looked at four times, in the order most people get backward',
  more: [{ serial: 'S501', after: 0, alt: 'The girl frowning and turning away from the painting after a quick glance' }, { serial: 'S502', after: 1, alt: 'A painting of a woman at a window holding a letter, gray light and one red chair' }, { serial: 'S1833', after: 2, alt: 'Light pouring in from the left of the painting and landing brightest on the letter' }, { serial: 'S1834', after: 4, alt: 'The girl pointing at the red chair in the painting while explaining to her teacher' }, { serial: 'S1835', after: 5, alt: 'The girl and her teacher standing back from the painting, the girl nodding thoughtfully' }],
  title: 'Four looks', art: 'S500', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl standing in front of a large painting in a museum, her teacher beside her, four footprints on the floor leading up to it',
  words: [
    'Zoe looked at the painting for three seconds and said she did not like it. Her teacher said that was step four, and asked her to go back to step one.',
    'Step one: describe. Say only what is there. A woman at a window. A letter in her hands. Gray light. A red chair. Zoe was not allowed to say whether any of it was good. Just what was there.',
    'Step two: analyze. How is it made? The light comes in from the left and lands brightest on the letter. The chair is the only warm color in a room full of gray and blue.',
    'Where did your eye go first, the teacher asked. To the letter. That is the focus, she said, and the painter put it there on purpose, with the brightest light in the whole picture.',
    'Step three: interpret. What does it mean, and why do you think so? The letter matters to her. The red chair says the room is not as cold as it looks. Zoe had to point at the painting to prove each idea.',
    'Step four: judge. Now the judging had something under it. Zoe still did not love the painting. But she could say what it was doing and how, and that the red chair was a small, smart choice.',
    'Describe, analyze, interpret, then judge. Most people start at judging, the least interesting step, and stop there. Three looks before the verdict, and the verdict gets better.',
  ],
};
STORIES['three-levels-of-government'] = {
  about: 'a pothole, a highway and a stamp, and the three governments a boy met in one morning',
  more: [{ serial: 'S504', after: 0, alt: 'A crew in orange vests working on a highway overpass as a boy rides by' }, { serial: 'S505', after: 2, alt: 'A city hall with a library, a fire truck and a street crew in front of it' }, { serial: 'S1845', after: 3, alt: 'The pink granite capitol in Austin and, far behind it, the white Capitol dome in Washington' }, { serial: 'S1846', after: 4, alt: 'A boy standing at a microphone at a city council meeting, council members listening' }, { serial: 'S1847', after: 5, alt: 'A three-story building with local, state and national governments on its floors, a small boy at the front door' }],
  title: 'Three levels', art: 'S503', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a bike beside a pothole on a city street, a highway overpass in the distance, a letter with a stamp in his basket',
  words: [
    'On Saturday, Tomas hit a pothole on his bike, rode past a crew fixing the highway overpass and mailed a birthday card to his grandmother. Three ordinary things. Then his uncle asked him who fixes the pothole, who fixes the highway and who delivers the card.',
    'The government, Tomas said. His uncle asked, which one? Tomas did not know there was more than one. It felt like a trick question, and the pothole was still there.',
    'There are three, his uncle said, stacked like floors in a building. The ground floor is local: your city, led by a mayor and a city council. It fixes streets, runs the library and sends the fire truck. The pothole belongs to them.',
    'The middle floor is the state: Texas, led by a governor and a legislature in Austin. It builds the highways, runs the state parks and gives out driver\'s licenses. The top floor is national: the United States, led by a president and Congress in Washington, D.C. It prints the money and delivers the mail.',
    'Tomas wanted the pothole gone, so his uncle took him to a city council meeting. Tomas stood up and described the hole, how deep it was and where. A council member wrote it down. Two weeks later, a city truck filled it.',
    'Local, state and national. Three levels, three leaders, three sets of jobs. The closer the level, the easier it is to walk in and be heard, even when you are nine.',
  ],
};
STORIES['how-we-decide'] = {
  about: 'a school rule, a walk signal and a class vote, and who each one covers',
  more: [{ serial: 'S507', after: 0, alt: 'A dad holding his son back at a crosswalk, the pedestrian signal showing a red hand' }, { serial: 'S508', after: 2, alt: 'A family, a class and a team, each gathered in its own small circle' }, { serial: 'S1848', after: 3, alt: 'Lawmakers in a grand chamber at the Texas Capitol in Austin raising their hands to vote' }, { serial: 'S1849', after: 4, alt: 'A class of children raising their hands in a field trip vote, the zoo side a little bigger' }, { serial: 'S1850', after: 5, alt: 'The whole class at the zoo, the museum fans grinning at a giraffe' }],
  title: 'Rule or law?', art: 'S506', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy slowing to a walk in a school hallway, a crosswalk signal glowing red through the window',
  words: [
    'Noah got told off twice in one day. Once at school for running in the hall, and once on the way home for stepping into the street before the walk signal came on. His dad said one of those was a rule and one was a law. Noah said they felt exactly the same.',
    'They both told him what to do. They both had someone watching. But would a police officer ever stop a kid for running down a school hallway? Noah laughed. Of course not. So what made them different?',
    'Who they cover, his dad said. A rule is for one group: a family, a class, a team. The school made the hall rule, and it stops at the school door. A law is for everyone in a city, a state or the whole country, and breaking it has a set punishment.',
    'The walk signal law covers everyone in Texas. The state legislature in Austin made it. Laws for the whole country come from Congress. The President carries them out, and the courts decide what they mean when people disagree.',
    'The next day, Noah\'s class voted on a field trip. Fourteen kids chose the zoo and ten chose the museum. More than half picked the zoo, so the zoo won by four. That is majority rule.',
    'The museum kids groaned, but they still got to go, and they still get a vote next time. That is the deal that makes voting work.',
    'Rule or law, ask who it covers.',
  ],
};
STORIES['earning-and-choosing'] = {
  about: 'eleven dollars, a book and a ball, and the thing that got left on the shelf',
  more: [{ serial: 'S510', after: 0, alt: 'Six dollars from chores and five from a birthday card laid out on a bed and counted' }, { serial: 'S511', after: 1, alt: 'A kind cashier shaking her head at the counter while the girl holds up her money' }, { serial: 'S1851', after: 2, alt: 'The girl walking out of the store with the book, glancing back at the ball on the shelf' }, { serial: 'S1852', after: 3, alt: 'A lemonade stand at the end of a driveway on a sunny Saturday' }, { serial: 'S1853', after: 4, alt: 'The girl dropping a dollar into a glass savings jar, a new ball under her arm' }],
  title: 'The eleven dollars', art: 'S509', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a store counter with coins and bills in her hand, a book and a ball on the shelf in front of her',
  words: [
    'Hana had eleven dollars. Six came from chores and five from a birthday card. At the store she found the book she wanted for eight dollars and the ball she wanted for seven. She counted her money twice. It did not get any bigger.',
    'She tried every trick. Maybe the ball was on sale. It was not. Maybe she could pay the rest next week. The cashier smiled and shook her head. Eight and seven make fifteen, and eleven is not fifteen.',
    'That is scarcity. There is never enough for everything, so every choice gives something up. Hana chose the book. The ball stayed on the shelf, and the ball was the real cost of the book. The thing you give up has a name: the opportunity cost.',
    'She still had three dollars, and she still wanted that ball. So on Saturday she set up a lemonade stand at the end of the driveway. Anyone in the country is free to start a business like that. It is called free enterprise.',
    'She sold twelve cups at fifty cents each, six dollars in all, and paid her mom back one dollar for the sugar and cups. Now she had eight. She bought the ball for seven and dropped the last dollar into her savings jar.',
    'Every time you spend money, time or attention, you are not spending it on something else. The trick is not to avoid that. It is to know what you are giving up, and to choose on purpose.',
  ],
};
// Grades 4 and 5, in the fewest words that still make a story.
STORIES['summarizing'] = {
  about: 'a two-hour movie, a one-minute bus stop, and the boy who had to leave almost everything out',
  more: [{ serial: 'S513', after: 1, alt: 'A movie scene with a dog, a pair of silly boots and a busy market' }, { serial: 'S514', after: 2, alt: 'A school bus squealing to a stop, the boy counting on three fingers' }, { serial: 'S1854', after: 3, alt: 'A girl crossing a moonlit desert, following the stars toward a hidden city' }, { serial: 'S1855', after: 4, alt: 'The boy walking home, a lost map drifting away on the wind' }, { serial: 'S1856', after: 6, alt: 'The friend on the bus smiling out the window, the whole story in his head' }],
  title: 'One minute', art: 'S512', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two boys at a bus stop, one talking fast with his hands, a bus coming down the street',
  words: [
    'Noah had seen the movie. His friend Eli had not, and Eli\'s bus was one minute away. Tell me the story, Eli said. Noah started at the very beginning, with the hero\'s breakfast, and the bus was already turning the corner.',
    'He could not do it. There were two hours of movie, and he loved all of it: the dog, the joke about the boots, the song in the market. If he left any of it out, was it even the same story?',
    'The bus squealed to a stop, and he had thirty seconds. He grabbed the three questions his teacher always asked. Who was it about? What happened? How did it end?',
    'A girl lost her map, crossed a desert without it, and found the hidden city by following the stars. No breakfast, no dog, no song. Eli said, oh, that sounds good, and got on the bus knowing exactly what the movie was about.',
    'Noah tested the rest. Could he cut the joke about the boots and still have the story? Yes, so it was a detail. Could he cut the lost map? No. Without it, there was no desert and no story.',
    'He had almost added, and it was the best movie ever. He was glad he had not. That was his opinion, not the story.',
    'A summary keeps the big events and drops the small details. It stays in order and leaves your opinion out. The test is the bus stop: if a friend hears it in a minute and knows the story, you summarized it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['making-inferences'] = {
  about: 'muddy boots, a sigh, and the thing nobody said out loud',
  more: [{ serial: 'S516', after: 1, alt: 'A boy jumping into a big puddle on the way home from school, mud flying' }, { serial: 'S517', after: 3, alt: 'A little girl pointing at a car in the driveway while her big sister shakes her head' }, { serial: 'S1857', after: 4, alt: 'A girl reading a storybook in bed, the page showing a girl with an umbrella sighing at a rainy window' }, { serial: 'S1858', after: 5, alt: 'An umbrella and a sigh drawn side by side, both pointing to a rain cloud' }, { serial: 'S1859', after: 6, alt: 'A magnifying glass held over a pair of muddy boots' }],
  title: 'The muddy boots', art: 'S515', cast: [],
  alt: 'A pair of muddy rain boots dropped by a front door, a mother looking at them, a girl peeking from the stairs',
  words: [
    'When Tessa came downstairs, her brother\'s boots were by the door, caked in mud to the ankles. Her mom looked at them and sighed. Nobody said a word about where he had been.',
    'And yet Tessa knew. Puddle jumping, on the way home from school, the way he always did after rain. Nobody had told her. How could she be so sure of something she had not been told?',
    'Because she had two things. A clue in front of her, the mud on the boots. And something she already knew, that her brother could not pass a puddle without jumping in it. A clue plus what you know makes an inference.',
    'Her little sister had a different idea. Mom sighed because the car is broken, she said. Tessa looked around. No clue anywhere said anything about a car. That was not an inference. It was just a guess.',
    'That night, Tessa read a story that began: Maya grabbed her umbrella and sighed at the window. It never said rain. It never said Maya was unhappy. Tessa knew both before she reached the next line.',
    'The umbrella was the clue for rain. The sigh was the clue for unhappy. Tessa knew what umbrellas and sighs mean, and the writer had trusted her to know.',
    'Writers do not say everything. They show muddy boots and a sigh, and they trust you to work out the rest. An inference is a clue in the text plus what you already know. No clue, no inference.',
  ],
};
STORIES['similes-and-metaphors'] = {
  about: 'a brave friend, and the two ways a girl found to say how brave',
  more: [{ serial: 'S519', after: 0, alt: 'The biggest kid in school looming over a small boy while a girl steps in front of him without blinking' }, { serial: 'S520', after: 1, alt: 'A girl frowning at a growing pile of dull, crossed-out words on her notebook page' }, { serial: 'S1860', after: 3, alt: 'A girl standing tall, a lion\'s shadow stretching behind her' }, { serial: 'S1861', after: 4, alt: 'The girl drawn as a lion outright, mane and tail and all, still in her school clothes' }, { serial: 'S1862', after: 5, alt: 'A noisy school cafeteria like a zoo, and beyond it a hallway as quiet as a library' }],
  title: 'Brave as a lion', art: 'S518', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl standing up for a small boy on a playground, a faint lion drawn in the air beside her',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s friend Maya had stood up to the biggest kid in school without blinking. Rosa wanted to write it down, and the word brave did not seem big enough. Brave was what you called someone at the dentist.',
    'She tried adding more brave. Very brave. Really very brave. It got longer and weaker every time. The bravest thing she had ever seen was turning into a pile of dull words.',
    'Then she stopped describing Maya and compared her to something else. Maya is as brave as a lion. The little word as made it a simile, a comparison that uses like or as.',
    'Then she went further. Maya is a lion. No like, no as. Maya simply was one, for the length of the sentence. That is a metaphor: saying one thing is another thing it plainly is not.',
    'Neither sentence is meant to be taken literally. Maya has no mane and no tail. But both put a lion in the reader\'s head, fast, and a lion says more than a page of very brave ever could.',
    'Once she knew the trick, Rosa heard it everywhere. At lunch her brother said the cafeteria was a zoo. A metaphor. Her teacher said the hallway was as quiet as a library. A simile. Neither was meant literally, and both were perfectly clear.',
    'Like or as makes a simile. Saying one thing is another makes a metaphor. Both compare, and both let a reader feel what plain words only describe.',
  ],
};
STORIES['text-structure'] = {
  about: 'a chapter that made no sense until a boy read its road signs',
  more: [{ serial: 'S522', after: 0, alt: 'A boy lost on a road with no signs, a thick book under his arm' }, { serial: 'S523', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher pointing at the small words between sentences, lit up like stepping stones' }, { serial: 'S1863', after: 2, alt: 'A volcano erupting in steps: rumbling, then smoking, then lava spilling over' }, { serial: 'S1864', after: 4, alt: 'Two kinds of volcanoes side by side, a wide gentle one and a tall steep one' }, { serial: 'S1865', after: 6, alt: 'A winding road with blank signs at every turn, a boy walking it with confidence' }],
  title: 'Road signs', art: 'S521', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy reading a thick science book while a winding road with blank road signs rises out of its pages',
  words: [
    'Jamal read the science chapter twice and could not have told you what it was about. The words were fine one at a time. Together they felt like a road with no signs, and he kept missing the turns.',
    'His teacher told him to stop reading the sentences and read the little words between them instead. First. Then. Because. As a result. However. Jamal thought she was joking. Those were the words you skipped.',
    'They were the signs. First, then and finally meant a sequence, one step after another, like the steps of a volcano erupting. Because and as a result meant cause and effect: this happened, so that happened.',
    'Both, unlike and however meant comparing, two things set side by side. And the words problem and solved meant a problem and its solution. Four shapes, and the little words told him which one he was on.',
    'Jamal went back to the chapter. The first section said first, then, finally: a sequence. The next said because three times in one paragraph: causes and effects. The last said unlike and however: a comparison of two kinds of volcanoes. He knew what was coming before it came.',
    'When he saw because, he knew an effect was on its way. When he saw however, he knew a difference was coming. The chapter made sense on the first try.',
    'Every text is built one of a few ways, and signal words tell you which. Read the signs, and you will know the shape of the road before you walk it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['forms-of-energy'] = {
  about: 'one kitchen before breakfast, and the five kinds of energy a boy counted in it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S525', after: 1, alt: 'The boy standing in the kitchen with a pencil and an empty page, looking around' }, { serial: 'S526', after: 2, alt: 'A toaster glowing orange inside with bread in it' }, { serial: 'S1875', after: 3, alt: 'Every machine in the kitchen with little arrows going in and out of it' }, { serial: 'S1876', after: 4, alt: 'A glowing lamp with warm light and a little heat shimmering above the bulb' }, { serial: 'S1877', after: 5, alt: 'The boy rubbing his cold hands together, then tapping a spoon on his bowl' }],
  title: 'Five kinds before breakfast', art: 'S524', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a bright kitchen at breakfast with a lamp, a toaster, a radio, a wall outlet and a spinning fan',
  words: [
    'Amir had to find five kinds of energy for homework, and he was sure it would take all day. Energy sounded like something in a power plant, or a rocket. Not something you would find at home before breakfast.',
    'He stood in the kitchen with a pencil and nothing on the page. The lamp was on. The toaster was ticking. His dad was listening to the radio, and the fan was turning. He had been in this room a thousand times and never seen energy in it.',
    'Then he looked again. The lamp gave off light. The toaster glowed with heat, and he could smell the bread starting to brown. The radio made sound. The outlet fed the fan electrical energy, and the fan turned it into spinning blades: mechanical energy, the energy of moving things.',
    'Five kinds, one kitchen, before he had even eaten. But the best part was what came next. Every machine in the room took in one form and gave out another.',
    'The lamp took in electrical energy and gave out light, plus a little heat, which is why the bulb was warm. The toaster turned electrical energy into heat. The radio turned it into sound. The fan turned it into motion.',
    'Amir rubbed his cold hands together and felt them warm up. Motion into heat. He tapped a spoon on his bowl. Motion into sound. He was a machine too.',
    'Energy comes in forms you can name, and machines change one form into another. Ask of anything: what form goes in, and what comes out?',
  ],
};
STORIES['circuits'] = {
  about: 'a bulb that would not light until the last wire closed the loop',
  more: [{ serial: 'S528', after: 0, alt: 'A dark little bulb on a table, one wire hooked from the battery to it' }, { serial: 'S529', after: 2, alt: 'The bulb lit, the wire touching the battery, the girl grinning' }, { serial: 'S1878', after: 3, alt: 'A boy holding a plastic straw and then a metal paper clip between the wire and the battery' }, { serial: 'S1879', after: 4, alt: 'A cut wire showing shiny copper inside and plastic coating outside' }, { serial: 'S1880', after: 5, alt: 'A paper clip switch taped to a board, swung open and then closed' }],
  title: 'The loop', art: 'S527', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a table with a battery, a bulb and two wires, the second wire stopping just short of the battery',
  words: [
    'Jade had a battery, a bulb and two wires. The instructions said the bulb would light. She hooked one wire from the battery to the bulb and waited. Nothing. She wiggled it. Still nothing. The bulb sat there like a glass egg.',
    'She checked the battery. It was new. She checked the bulb. It was fine. Everything worked, and nothing worked. Her brother said the electricity was stuck in the battery, which did not help.',
    'Then she noticed the second wire. It ran from the bulb back toward the battery and stopped an inch short. She touched it to the battery. Light! She lifted it. Dark. Electricity did not want a road. It wanted a loop, from the battery, through the bulb and back home.',
    'Her brother wanted a turn, so she let him close the gap. He tried a plastic straw between the wire and the battery first. Dark. Then a metal paper clip. Light. The metal let electricity through. The plastic stopped it.',
    'Metals like copper, iron and aluminum are conductors: they carry electricity. Rubber, plastic, wood and glass are insulators: they block it. That is why a wire is metal inside and plastic outside. The metal carries the flow, and the plastic keeps it away from your hand.',
    'Jade taped a paper clip to the board so she could swing it on and off the wire. Now she had a switch. Open, dark. Closed, light.',
    'Every circuit is a loop, and electricity flows only when the loop is complete. Break it anywhere, and the light goes out.',
  ],
};
STORIES['changing-land'] = {
  about: 'a sandcastle, a cliff and the week of weather that broke, moved and rebuilt the beach',
  more: [{ serial: 'S531', after: 1, alt: 'Waves crashing against a rocky cliff at the end of a beach, sand piled in its cracks' }, { serial: 'S532', after: 2, alt: 'A grandpa crouching beside a cracked rock with a plant root growing through it' }, { serial: 'S1881', after: 3, alt: 'Waves washing the last of a sandcastle out to sea' }, { serial: 'S1882', after: 4, alt: 'A new bank of sand piled up farther down the beach, the girl pointing at it' }, { serial: 'S1883', after: 5, alt: 'The girl pressing a shell into the new sand bank, smiling' }],
  title: 'The sandcastle', art: 'S530', cast: [],
  alt: 'A tall sandcastle on a beach at sunset, a girl beside it, rain clouds gathering out at sea',
  words: [
    'Sofia built the best sandcastle of her life on the first day of vacation. Three towers, a moat, and a wall you could stand a shell on. She came back every morning to check on it, and every morning it was a little less castle.',
    'At the end of the beach stood a rocky cliff. Waves slammed into it, and in its cracks lay sand and broken shell. Sofia asked her grandpa a question she had never thought to ask. Where does sand even come from?',
    'From rock, he said. Water, ice, wind and plant roots crack rock into smaller and smaller pieces over many years. That breaking is called weathering. Most beach sand is bits of rock and shell broken down that way.',
    'By the fourth day, the waves had carried half of her castle out to sea. By the last day there was nothing left at her spot at all. Carrying pieces away is called erosion, and water does most of it.',
    'And yet, farther down the beach, a fresh bank of sand had piled up where none had been. Where the water slowed down, it dropped what it was carrying. Dropping the pieces somewhere new is called deposition.',
    'Sofia pressed a shell into the new sand bank. Her castle had not vanished. It had moved.',
    'Break, carry, drop. Weathering, erosion and deposition shape every hill, canyon and beach on Earth. A sandcastle shows in one week what a mountain shows in a million years.',
  ],
};
STORIES['adaptations'] = {
  about: 'a duck, a cactus and a desert fox, and why each one looked so strange',
  more: [{ serial: 'S534', after: 1, alt: 'A zookeeper talking with a boy beside the duck pond' }, { serial: 'S535', after: 2, alt: 'A close look at a webbed foot pushing water like an oar, beside a thick cactus stem' }, { serial: 'S1884', after: 3, alt: 'A small desert fox with enormous ears curled up asleep in the sand' }, { serial: 'S1885', after: 4, alt: 'The desert fox hunting under a starry night sky' }, { serial: 'S1886', after: 5, alt: 'A polar bear standing out among green forest trees, looking puzzled' }],
  title: 'The right tools', art: 'S533', cast: [],
  alt: 'A duck paddling on a pond in the foreground and a tall cactus in a desert in the distance, a boy looking between them',
  words: [
    'At the zoo, Ravi watched a duck paddle across the pond and then walked into the desert house, where a cactus stood covered in spines. He thought both looked ridiculous. Feet like paddles. A plant like a pincushion. Why would anything grow that way?',
    'He asked the keeper whether the duck minded its silly feet. She asked him a question back. What would happen to a duck with feet like a chicken? He pictured it paddling hard and going nowhere.',
    'Every strange part, she said, is a tool for a problem. Webbed feet push against the water like oars. The cactus has a thick stem that stores water for the dry months, and its spines keep thirsty animals from biting in.',
    'In the next room, a small desert fox with enormous ears was curled up asleep. Ravi guessed the ears were for hearing. They do help, the keeper said, but they also let heat escape, and that keeps the fox cool in the desert sun.',
    'An adaptation is a part or a habit that helps a living thing survive where it lives. The fox has a habit too. It sleeps through the hot day and hunts in the cooler night.',
    'Then she asked one more question. What if you moved a polar bear to a forest? Its white fur hides it on snow. Among green trees, it would stand out.',
    'When something looks odd, ask two questions: what problem does this place give it, and how does this part solve it? The answers turn the strangest creatures into the smartest.',
  ],
};
STORIES['topic-sentences'] = {
  about: 'a paragraph about dogs that a boy wrote backward, and then the right way round',
  more: [{ serial: 'S537', after: 0, alt: 'A cat asleep on a windowsill while a dog trots in with a newspaper' }, { serial: 'S538', after: 2, alt: 'The boy writing one big sentence at the top of his page, the dog watching' }, { serial: 'S1896', after: 3, alt: 'A dog fetching a stick, a dog guarding a gate and a guide dog in a harness walking beside its owner' }, { serial: 'S1897', after: 4, alt: 'The boy lifting a sleeping cat out of a picture full of busy dogs' }, { serial: 'S1898', after: 5, alt: 'An open door with a blank sign above it and a tidy room inside where everything belongs' }],
  title: 'The big thing first', art: 'S536', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy writing a paragraph about dogs at a kitchen table, a golden retriever resting its head on his knee',
  words: [
    'Sam wrote a paragraph about dogs. They fetch. They guard the house. They guide people who cannot see. My cat sleeps all day. It was all true, and his teacher wrote one question in the margin: what is this paragraph about?',
    'Sam was annoyed. It was about dogs, obviously. But when he read it again, he saw that every sentence was a fact standing on its own. Nobody had told the reader what the facts added up to.',
    'So he wrote the big thing first. Dogs make good helpers. That is a topic sentence, and it makes a promise: the rest of the paragraph will show how.',
    'Then the facts followed, and suddenly they were not loose facts. They were three ways of proving one idea. Fetching helps. Guarding helps. Guiding helps most of all.',
    'Then Sam asked the test question of every sentence: does this back up the first one? My cat sleeps all day. No. It was true, and funny, but it was about a cat, not dogs helping. It moved to a paragraph of its own.',
    'His teacher called the topic sentence the sign on the door. It tells you what is inside the room. Every sentence after it has to belong in that room.',
    'Write the big thing first. Then make every sentence after it back the big thing up. A reader will know where they are going from the very first line, and so will you.',
  ],
};
STORIES['opinion-paragraph'] = {
  about: 'a longer recess, and the girl who turned wanting it into a case for it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S540', after: 0, alt: 'A class shouting at a smiling principal, who holds up one hand for quiet' }, { serial: 'S541', after: 1, alt: 'The girl at her desk at home, face red, crumpling her first draft' }, { serial: 'S1899', after: 2, alt: 'Children running at recess, then sitting alert at their desks, in two panels' }, { serial: 'S1900', after: 4, alt: 'A dad reading a page at the kitchen table, eyebrows raised, impressed' }, { serial: 'S1901', after: 5, alt: 'Children pouring out onto a sunny playground for a longer recess' }],
  title: 'Longer recess', art: 'S539', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at the front of a classroom reading from a page, classmates listening, a playground through the window',
  words: [
    'Lena wanted a longer recess, and so did everyone. When the principal visited, half the class shouted it at her. She smiled and said, tell me why, and the room went quiet. Wanting something, it turned out, was not a reason.',
    'Lena went home and tried to write why. The first draft said recess should be longer because it should. She read it back and felt her face go hot. It was the same shout, written down.',
    'The second draft had a shape. Her opinion came first: I think recess should be longer. Then reason one, with a helper word: first, we think better after we have run around.',
    'Then reason two: also, friends who play together get along better in class. The little words first and also held the reader by the hand, one step at a time.',
    'Then a closing sentence that said the opinion again in new words: give us twenty more minutes outside, and you will get better students back. Her dad read it and said it sounded like a lawyer wrote it.',
    'The principal read it twice, then read it to the teachers. Recess grew by ten minutes the very next week, and nobody had to shout.',
    'An opinion paragraph is an opinion, two reasons and a closing sentence. Say what you think at the start, prove it in the middle, and say it again at the end. That is how a wish becomes an argument.',
  ],
};
STORIES['narrative-paragraph'] = {
  about: 'the day a bird got into the house, and the girl who wrote it so a reader could see it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S543', after: 0, alt: 'A sparrow shooting in through an open front door on a hot afternoon' }, { serial: 'S544', after: 1, alt: 'A girl frowning at her one short sentence, trying to picture the bird' }, { serial: 'S1902', after: 3, alt: 'The bird perched on a curtain rod, everyone in the room frozen with their hands up, a cat under the couch' }, { serial: 'S1903', after: 4, alt: 'Dad sliding the window open and the bird flying out into the light' }, { serial: 'S1904', after: 5, alt: 'A teacher circling one line on a page and smiling' }],
  title: 'The bird in the house', art: 'S542', cast: [],
  alt: 'A sparrow flying in circles around a hanging lamp in a living room, a family frozen below it',
  words: [
    'A sparrow flew in through the open door, and the whole afternoon turned upside down. That night Ava had to write about something that happened. She wrote: a bird got in and then it got out. Her teacher wrote back: I was not there. Let me see it.',
    'Ava did not know what that meant. It was true. A bird got in and got out. But reading it back, she could not see the bird either, and she had been standing right under it.',
    'So she wrote it again in three parts. The beginning said who and where: our living room, a hot afternoon, the door propped open. Then a sparrow shot in like a thrown ball.',
    'The middle said what happened. It circled the lamp twice, landed on the curtain rod, and everyone in the room froze with their hands up. The cat crouched under the couch, tail twitching.',
    'The end said how it turned out. Dad slid the window open, and after one more circle the bird found the light and was gone. Nobody breathed until it was over the fence.',
    'Her teacher circled one line: everyone froze with their hands up. Ava had not written that they were scared. She had shown it, and the reader felt it anyway.',
    'A small story has a beginning, a middle and an end. Show what happened with words a reader can see: not a bird got in, but a sparrow like a thrown ball. Your reader was not there. Your words take them.',
  ],
};
STORIES['first-texans'] = {
  about: 'three peoples of early Texas, and the three kitchens the land gave them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S546', after: 0, alt: 'A boy waving his hand over a big map of Texas pinned to a classroom wall' }, { serial: 'S547', after: 2, alt: 'A Caddo village of tall round grass houses beside rows of corn in the wet east' }, { serial: 'S1905', after: 3, alt: 'A Comanche rider following a buffalo herd across a dry plain, a folded tipi on a pole drag behind' }, { serial: 'S1906', after: 4, alt: 'Karankawa fishers paddling a dugout canoe across a calm coastal bay' }, { serial: 'S1907', after: 5, alt: 'People of several nations meeting to trade under a big tree' }],
  title: 'Three kitchens', art: 'S545', cast: [],
  alt: 'Three scenes side by side: a Caddo cornfield in green woods, Comanche riders on open plains, Karankawa fishers on a gray coast',
  words: [
    'When Diego\'s class was asked what the first Texans ate, he said buffalo, because that was the picture in every book. His teacher put a map of Texas on the wall and asked where. Diego waved his hand over the whole state.',
    'Then she pointed at three places. The wet forests of the east. The dry, open plains of the north and west. The gray, marshy coast. Would a buffalo hunter do well in a marsh? Would a fisher do well on a dry plain? Diego was not so sure anymore.',
    'In the wet east, rain fell often and the soil was rich. The Caddo farmed corn, beans and squash, and they lived in villages of tall, round houses made of grass. People who farm can stay put.',
    'On the dry plains, the Comanche followed the buffalo on horseback. They rode into Texas in the 1700s, on horses whose ancestors had come over with the Spanish. The herds kept moving, so the people carried tipis they could pack.',
    'Along the coast, the Karankawa fished the bays from dugout canoes and gathered shellfish. They moved with the seasons, camping wherever the food was best that season.',
    'Many nations lived in Texas, and they traded, fought and made peace with one another for centuries. When Spain, Mexico and the United States arrived, each found peoples already at home.',
    'Land decides a way of life. Look at where a people lived, and you can guess what they ate, what they built and how far they moved. The map was the first cookbook.',
  ],
};
STORIES['spanish-and-mexican-texas'] = {
  about: 'a mission bell that rang under two flags, and the door that let the settlers in',
  more: [{ serial: 'S549', after: 1, alt: 'Spanish soldiers and priests building the stone walls of a mission beside a river' }, { serial: 'S550', after: 2, alt: 'The same mission with the flag of Mexico now flying over it' }, { serial: 'S1908', after: 3, alt: 'A line of covered wagons of American settlers arriving on a Texas road' }, { serial: 'S1909', after: 4, alt: 'A crowded town square with Tejano families and newly arrived settlers arguing' }, { serial: 'S1910', after: 6, alt: 'An old stone mission church standing today, its bell tower against a blue sky' }],
  title: 'The mission bell', art: 'S548', cast: [],
  alt: 'A stone mission with a bell tower at sunrise, the Spanish flag on the pole, a few families at the gate',
  words: [
    'The bell in the old mission near San Antonio had rung every morning for about a hundred years, since Spain built the mission, its walls and the little farm around it. Lucia\'s great-great-grandmother had heard it as a girl. The bell never changed.',
    'Spain had claimed Texas in the 1500s. From the 1690s on, it built missions and forts to spread its faith and hold the land, and San Antonio grew up around them. Lucia had never known any flag on the pole but Spain\'s.',
    'Then, in 1821, Mexico won its independence from Spain. One morning the bell rang under a new flag on the same pole. Texas was part of Mexico now. Lucia wondered how a place could belong to one country at breakfast and another by lunch.',
    'Mexico had a problem. Texas was huge and had few settlers, and an empty land is hard to hold. So Mexico opened the door to American settlers. Stephen F. Austin brought the first three hundred families in the 1820s.',
    'They came, and kept coming. Within about ten years, Americans outnumbered the Tejanos, the Texans of Mexican descent like Lucia\'s family. The newcomers disagreed with Mexico City about laws, language and slavery.',
    'The bell rang over more and more faces that had never seen Spain or Mexico. One of the old missions had another name by then: the Alamo.',
    'Two flags in a row, Spain and then Mexico, and the roots of a third growing under the same bell. The missions are still standing, and some still ring their bells.',
  ],
};
STORIES['texas-joins-the-union'] = {
  about: 'a flag with twenty-eight stars, a reading in Galveston, and the day freedom arrived late',
  more: [{ serial: 'S552', after: 0, alt: 'A grandfather pointing at one star on an old flag' }, { serial: 'S553', after: 1, alt: 'The wide Rio Grande winding between two countries under a big sky' }, { serial: 'S1913', after: 2, alt: 'The flag of the United States in the 1860s flying over a quiet town, every star still on it' }, { serial: 'S1914', after: 4, alt: 'A crowd gathered in Galveston hearing the news of freedom read aloud, faces lifting with joy' }, { serial: 'S1915', after: 5, alt: 'A wooden derrick at Spindletop spraying oil high into the air' }],
  title: 'The 28th star', art: 'S551', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a dusty attic unfolding an old flag with her grandfather',
  words: [
    'Mia found an old flag in her grandfather\'s attic with twenty-eight stars on it, not fifty. He told her the twenty-eighth star was Texas. For nine years Texas had been a country of its own. Then, in 1845, it joined the United States as the twenty-eighth state.',
    'Mexico had never accepted that Texas was free, and a fight over the border helped start a war between the United States and Mexico in 1846. When it ended, the border was the Rio Grande.',
    'But the story was not a straight line. In 1861 Texas left the Union to join the Confederacy in the war over slavery. Mia asked whether the star came off the flag. It did not. The United States never removed the stars of the states that left.',
    'When that war ended, freedom did not reach Texas the day it was declared. Mia asked how freedom could be late. The news traveled slowly, and some people did not want it heard.',
    'On June 19, 1865, Union soldiers arrived in Galveston with the order that every enslaved person in Texas was free. That day became Juneteenth, a holiday that began in Texas and now belongs to the whole country.',
    'Then, in 1901, at Spindletop near Beaumont, oil roared out of the ground, and Texas changed again. Six flags had flown over Texas by then: Spain, France, Mexico, the Republic of Texas, the Confederacy and the United States.',
    'Statehood in 1845, the Confederacy in 1861, Juneteenth in 1865 and oil in 1901. History is a line with bends in it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['multi-digit-multiplication'] = {
  about: 'twenty-three boxes of crayons and the boy who counted them in two pieces',
  more: [{ serial: 'S555', after: 1, alt: 'The boy counting crayons one at a time, a small pile growing while the clock ticks' }, { serial: 'S556', after: 2, alt: 'Crayon boxes sorted into one big pile and a small pile of three' }, { serial: 'S1916', after: 3, alt: 'A teacher handing the boy a bin full of marker bags, both grinning' }, { serial: 'S1917', after: 4, alt: 'The boy multiplying on a notepad, arrows linking each piece to every other piece' }, { serial: 'S1918', after: 5, alt: 'A rectangle split into four smaller rectangles, one for each piece of the multiplication' }],
  title: 'Twenty-three boxes', art: 'S554', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in an art supply room with a tall stack of small crayon boxes, counting them into two piles',
  words: [
    'The art teacher asked Leo to count the crayons in the supply room. There were 23 boxes with 4 crayons in each. Leo started multiplying in his head, lost the number and started again. Twenty-three fours is a lot to hold at once.',
    'He tried counting the crayons one at a time and got to thirty before the teacher came back. This was going to take all afternoon. The boxes smelled like wax, and he was tired of them already.',
    'Then he broke the 23 into pieces he could handle: twenty boxes and three boxes. Twenty boxes of four is 80 crayons. Three boxes of four is 12. And 80 plus 12 is 92. Two easy multiplications and one addition, instead of one hard one.',
    'The teacher was impressed, so she gave him a harder one. The markers came in 14 bags of 23. Now both numbers were big. Leo broke both of them. Twenty and three. Ten and four.',
    'He multiplied every piece by every piece. Twenty times ten is 200. Twenty times four is 80. Three times ten is 30. Three times four is 12. Then he added them: 200, 80, 30 and 12 make 322 markers.',
    'He checked with a picture: a rectangle 23 wide and 14 tall, cut into four smaller rectangles, one for each piece. The four areas added up to the whole.',
    'Every big multiplication is a set of small ones in disguise. Break the numbers into tens and ones, multiply each piece, then add the pieces together.',
  ],
};
STORIES['long-division'] = {
  about: 'ninety-six stickers, four friends and the sharing that went round in rounds',
  more: [{ serial: 'S558', after: 0, alt: 'Four friends waiting at a table, chins in their hands, bored' }, { serial: 'S559', after: 1, alt: 'Four equal piles of stickers, a smaller bunch left in the girl\'s hand' }, { serial: 'S1919', after: 3, alt: 'A teacher writing a neat long division tower on a small whiteboard' }, { serial: 'S1920', after: 4, alt: 'A little brother dropping one more sticker onto the sheet, one sticker left over at the end' }, { serial: 'S1921', after: 5, alt: 'The girl multiplying back on paper and nodding, the check exact' }],
  title: 'Sharing in rounds', art: 'S557', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a table with a big sheet of stickers and four small piles in front of four friends',
  words: [
    'Grace had a sheet of 96 stickers and four friends waiting. She did not want to hand them out one at a time. That was 96 moves, and her friends were already bored.',
    'So she tried a bigger step. Could everyone get twenty? Four twenties is 80. Yes. She peeled off twenty for each friend and had 16 left. Sixteen was a much smaller problem than 96.',
    'Second round. Four more each used up all 16. Twenty and four: every friend had 24, and the sheet was empty. Share the big part first, see what is left, then share that.',
    'Her teacher showed her the written way, one digit at a time. How many fours fit in 9, the first digit? Two, with 1 left over. Bring down the 6 to make 16. How many fours fit in 16? Four, exactly. The digits made 24. The same answer, in a tidier tower.',
    'The next day her little brother added one more sticker to the sheet, 97 in all. Everyone got 24 again, and one sticker was left in her hand. That leftover is the remainder: 97 divided by 4 is 24, remainder 1.',
    'To check it, Grace multiplied back. Four times 24 is 96. Add the remainder, 1, and you get 97. The check came out exactly.',
    'Long division shares a big number in steps. Share what you can, bring down what is left, and always check by multiplying back and adding the remainder.',
  ],
};
STORIES['factors-and-multiples'] = {
  about: 'twelve chairs and every way a boy could arrange them in equal rows',
  more: [{ serial: 'S561', after: 0, alt: 'Chairs in one long row stretching across a classroom like a bus' }, { serial: 'S562', after: 1, alt: 'The boy dragging chairs into new rows again and again, tired' }, { serial: 'S1922', after: 2, alt: 'Chairs arranged in two neat rows, then three, then four' }, { serial: 'S1923', after: 4, alt: 'A class counting by fours as they set chairs in rows of four' }, { serial: 'S1924', after: 5, alt: 'A thirteenth chair arriving, and one long line the only way to fit them all' }],
  title: 'Twelve chairs', art: 'S560', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in an empty classroom arranging chairs into rows, arrangements chalked on the floor',
  words: [
    'Dev had to set out twelve chairs for the school play, in equal rows. He put them in one long row of twelve and stepped back. It looked like a bus. He tried five rows and had two chairs left over with nowhere to go.',
    'Some numbers of rows worked, and some did not, and he could not tell which until he had dragged every chair. There had to be a way to know first.',
    'He wrote down the ones that worked. One row of twelve. Two rows of six. Three rows of four. Four rows of three. Six rows of two. Twelve rows of one. The numbers that worked were 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12.',
    'Those are the factors of twelve, his teacher said: the numbers that go into it exactly, with nothing left over. Five was not a factor. That was why two chairs had nowhere to go.',
    'For the next show, the class wanted chairs in rows of four. Four, eight, twelve, sixteen, twenty: any of those numbers of chairs would fill the rows exactly. Those are the multiples of four, what four makes when you count by it.',
    'Then a latecomer brought a thirteenth chair. Dev tried every row he could think of. Only one long line worked. Thirteen has exactly two factors, itself and one, so it is prime.',
    'A factor goes into a number exactly. A multiple is what a number makes. A prime has exactly two factors. And a prime number of chairs only ever makes one long line.',
  ],
};
STORIES['equivalent-and-decimals'] = {
  about: 'a coin called a quarter, and the girl who found three names for it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S564', after: 1, alt: 'Four quarters arranged in a circle to make one dollar' }, { serial: 'S565', after: 2, alt: 'A square of pennies on a table, one corner of it covered by a single quarter' }, { serial: 'S1925', after: 3, alt: 'A decimal point drawn as a tiny doorway between whole dollars and cents' }, { serial: 'S1926', after: 4, alt: 'Three dimes beside thirty pennies on a table, the same amount' }, { serial: 'S1927', after: 5, alt: 'Seven dimes in one row and six dimes and a nickel in another, a girl comparing them' }],
  title: 'The quarter', art: 'S563', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding a quarter up to the light, a dollar bill and four quarters laid out on a desk',
  words: [
    'June found a quarter on the sidewalk and asked her dad why it was called that. Because it is a quarter of a dollar, he said. But he also called it twenty-five cents, and the price tag at the store said 0.25. Three names, one small coin.',
    'She lined up four quarters, and they made a dollar. So one quarter was one fourth of the whole. But twenty-five? And that dot in 0.25? None of it looked like a fourth.',
    'Her dad drew a dollar as a hundred pennies in a square, ten rows of ten. A quarter covered twenty-five of them. Twenty-five out of a hundred can be written as a fraction, 25/100, or as a decimal, 0.25. One fourth, twenty-five hundredths and 0.25 are the same money.',
    'A decimal is a fraction with 10 or 100 on the bottom, he said, written with a point instead of a line. The first place after the point is tenths. The second is hundredths.',
    'So a dime is one tenth of a dollar, 0.1. Three dimes are 0.3, and so are thirty pennies, 0.30. The zero at the end changes nothing, because three tenths and thirty hundredths are the same amount.',
    'Then he gave her a puzzle. Which is more, 0.7 or 0.65? June said 0.65, because 65 looks bigger than 7. Her dad lined up the points. Seven tenths against six tenths. Seven dimes beat six dimes and a nickel. The tenths decide first.',
    'Fractions and decimals are two names for one amount. Read decimals place by place, tenths first.',
  ],
};
STORIES['add-fractions'] = {
  about: 'half a pizza, a quarter of a pizza and a boy who could not add them until he cut',
  more: [{ serial: 'S567', after: 1, alt: 'The boy frowning at his paper beside a cold pizza, the answer looking wrong' }, { serial: 'S568', after: 2, alt: 'The half pizza cut into two quarters with a pizza wheel' }, { serial: 'S1928', after: 3, alt: 'Seven quarter slices laid out: four making one whole pizza and three more beside it' }, { serial: 'S1929', after: 4, alt: 'A sister walking off with three pizza slices, one whole pizza left in the box' }, { serial: 'S1930', after: 5, alt: 'A pizza cut into eighths with half of its slices left' }],
  title: 'Half and a quarter', art: 'S566', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a counter with half a pizza in one box and a quarter of a pizza in another',
  words: [
    'Two boxes came back from the party. Half a pizza in one, a quarter of a pizza in the other. Mateo wanted to know how much pizza that was altogether. He wrote one half plus one fourth, then stopped. Halves and fourths were different sizes.',
    'He tried adding the tops and the bottoms and got two sixths, which was less pizza than he had started with. That could not be right. The cheese was cold, and the math was getting worse.',
    'So he cut. One slice of the pizza wheel through the half, and it became two quarters. Now every piece was a quarter. Two quarters plus one quarter is three quarters. Same bottoms, so he added the tops and kept the bottom.',
    'The next weekend there were leftovers from two parties: seven quarter slices in all. Seven fourths. Mateo laid them out. Four quarters made one whole pizza, and three quarters were left over. Seven fourths is one and three fourths.',
    'His sister ate three of the slices. Seven fourths take away three fourths is four fourths. Same bottoms, so he subtracted the tops. Four fourths was exactly one whole pizza.',
    'At the end, he looked at a pizza cut into eighths with four slices left. Four eighths. Both numbers could be divided by four, so four eighths was the same as one half. Simplify at the end, and the answer gets easier to read.',
    'Fractions add and subtract when their pieces are the same size. Same bottoms: work with the tops, keep the bottom.',
  ],
};
STORIES['balance-and-pattern'] = {
  about: 'a face, a flower and a fence, and the three kinds of balance in them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S570', after: 0, alt: 'A drawing of a face folded down the middle, both halves matching' }, { serial: 'S571', after: 1, alt: 'A flower seen from above, its petals spreading evenly from the center' }, { serial: 'S1937', after: 2, alt: 'A big quiet tree on one side of a page balanced by a small bright red bird on the other' }, { serial: 'S1938', after: 3, alt: 'A garden fence with pickets repeating, every fourth one painted red' }, { serial: 'S1939', after: 4, alt: 'The same fence with one bright yellow picket in the middle catching the eye' }],
  title: 'A face, a flower, a fence', art: 'S569', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy sketching in a garden with a face, a flower and a fence drawn on one page',
  words: [
    'Felix drew a face and folded the paper down the middle. The two halves matched. Then he drew a flower from above, and no fold worked. It matched all the way around its center instead. His teacher said he had found two of the three kinds of balance by accident.',
    'A face is symmetrical: the same on both sides of a line. It feels calm, like the front of a cathedral. A flower seen from above is radial: everything spreads from a center, like a wheel.',
    'The third kind took a tree. Felix drew a big, quiet tree on the left side of a page and a small, bright red bird on the right. Different things, but the page felt even. That is asymmetrical balance, and it feels alive.',
    'Then he drew the garden fence. Picket, picket, picket: a shape repeated with a rule, which is pattern. He painted every fourth picket red, and the fence had a beat. Pattern with a beat is rhythm, the same word musicians use.',
    'Last, he painted one picket bright yellow in the middle of the fence. Everyone who looked at the drawing looked there first. Change one thing in a pattern and the eye goes straight to it. That is emphasis, made out of almost nothing.',
    'Balance is how a picture stands still. Rhythm is how it moves.',
    'Balance can be symmetrical, radial or asymmetrical. Pattern is repetition with a rule, and rhythm is pattern with a beat. A face, a flower and a fence hold it all.',
  ],
};
STORIES['making-space-on-paper'] = {
  about: 'a road that ran into the distance on a flat sheet of paper',
  more: [{ serial: 'S573', after: 0, alt: 'A flat road drawn like a ribbon straight across a page' }, { serial: 'S574', after: 1, alt: 'Two trees drawn so the near one covers part of the far one' }, { serial: 'S1940', after: 2, alt: 'A big detailed tree low on the page and a small pale tree high up behind it' }, { serial: 'S1941', after: 3, alt: 'A horizon line with a road, fence posts and rooftops all running to one vanishing point' }, { serial: 'S1942', after: 5, alt: 'Trees with the gaps between them shaded, showing the shapes of the empty space' }],
  title: 'The road into the distance', art: 'S572', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl drawing a road that narrows to a point on the horizon, trees shrinking along it',
  words: [
    'Clara drew a road, and it lay flat on the page like a ribbon. She wanted it to go somewhere. Her teacher said a page has no depth, so depth has to be faked, and there were four fakes and one ruler.',
    'Overlap: the near tree covers part of the far one, so the far one must be behind. It is the simplest trick, and the strongest. Clara tried it with two trees.',
    'Size: the near tree is drawn bigger, and the far tree smaller. Placement: the far tree sits higher on the page. Detail: the near tree has every leaf, and the far one is a pale green smudge.',
    'Then the ruler. Clara drew a horizon line where the sky met the ground. She picked one point on it, the vanishing point. The road\'s two edges ran toward that point until they met, and every fence post and rooftop leaned toward it too.',
    'The road left the page. It went somewhere now, over a hill and out of sight, and Clara wanted to walk it.',
    'Her teacher pointed at the gaps between the trees. The trees were positive space, the shapes of the things. The gaps were negative space, the shapes around them. Artists plan the empty parts as carefully as the full ones.',
    'Overlap, size, placement and detail make a flat page look deep. Perspective lines everything up to a vanishing point on the horizon. Depth is a trick, and the trick has rules.',
  ],
};
STORIES['art-tells-a-story'] = {
  about: 'a hand outlined on a cave wall, and the thousands of years of reasons since',
  more: [{ serial: 'S576', after: 0, alt: 'Someone long ago blowing red paint around a hand pressed to a cave wall' }, { serial: 'S577', after: 1, alt: 'A cave guide with a flashlight talking to a small tour group' }, { serial: 'S1943', after: 2, alt: 'Painted animals running along a cave ceiling' }, { serial: 'S1944', after: 3, alt: 'A carved stone queen beside a tall church window of colored glass stories' }, { serial: 'S1945', after: 5, alt: 'Three masks side by side: a festival mask, an ancestor mask and a portrait mask on a wall' }],
  title: 'The handprint', art: 'S575', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a cave tour looking at a red hand outline on the rock wall, his own hand raised beside it',
  words: [
    'On the cave tour, Theo saw a red hand on the wall, outlined in paint that someone had blown around their fingers more than twenty-five thousand years ago. Before writing. Before farms. Theo asked why anyone would do that.',
    'Nobody knows for certain, the guide said, and that was the beginning of a long answer. People made art before they wrote a single word, and every culture since has made it, never for only one reason.',
    'Some art records. Painted animals running across a cave ceiling may show the animals people knew and hunted.',
    'Some art honors: a carving of a queen, a statue of a leader, a painting for someone who died. Some art teaches: a church window full of stories for people who could not read.',
    'Some art decorates: a pot with a pattern, because plain was not enough. And some art questions: a painting that made a whole city argue about what was fair.',
    'Theo asked about masks. The guide said one mask might hide a face at a festival, another might hold an ancestor\'s spirit, and another might hang on a wall as a portrait. Same object, three meanings. So always ask who made a thing, when and for whom.',
    'Art records, honors, teaches, decorates and questions. The hand on the wall might have been any of those. It might have simply meant, I was here. It is one of the oldest messages we have, and it still reads.',
  ],
};
STORIES['adding-decimals'] = {
  about: 'two prices at a bake sale, and the boy who added them the wrong way first',
  more: [{ serial: 'S579', after: 1, alt: 'A long line of kids waiting behind the boy at the bake sale table' }, { serial: 'S580', after: 2, alt: 'Two prices written one above the other with the decimal points lined up' }, { serial: 'S1967', after: 3, alt: 'A five-dollar bill beside 5.00 written on a napkin, with 3.85 lined up under it' }, { serial: 'S1968', after: 4, alt: 'The boy estimating on his fingers: about two and a half plus about one and a half makes about four' }, { serial: 'S1969', after: 5, alt: 'Coins and dollar bills lined up in columns under their decimal points' }],
  title: 'Two prices', art: 'S578', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a bake sale table with a cupcake priced 2.50 and a cookie priced 1.35, coins in his hand',
  words: [
    'Marcus wanted a cupcake for two dollars fifty and a cookie for one dollar thirty-five. He added the numbers the way they looked on the sign, digits pushed together, and got a total that made the girl at the table laugh. It was more than a pizza.',
    'He tried again in his head and got a different number. The line behind him was growing and his coins were sweating in his hand. Numbers with a dot in them seemed to have their own rules.',
    'The girl wrote the two prices one above the other and lined up the dots. Cents under cents, dollars under dollars. Then she added column by column, just like whole numbers, and the dot stayed where the dots were. Three dollars eighty-five.',
    'Marcus paid with a five-dollar bill, and now the change was his problem. He wrote 5 as 5.00, because a whole number has a hidden decimal point at its end, and zeros can fill the empty places without changing anything. Five dollars minus three eighty-five, cents under cents: one dollar fifteen.',
    'He checked it the fast way before he walked off. Two fifty is about two and a half, one thirty-five is about one and a half, and together they make about four. Three eighty-five is close to four, so the answer made sense. An estimate cannot give the exact answer, but it catches a silly one.',
    'Decimals are money, and money lines up. Put the decimal points under each other, add or subtract each column, and carry the point straight down. Every other rule you know for whole numbers still works.',
  ],
};
STORIES['multiplying-fractions'] = {
  about: 'half a pizza, a hungry girl, and the slice that turned out to be a quarter',
  more: [{ serial: 'S582', after: 0, alt: 'A girl looking at one small slice on her plate beside an open pizza box' }, { serial: 'S583', after: 1, alt: 'The half pizza cut into two quarters, one lifted out' }, { serial: 'S1970', after: 2, alt: 'A notebook with one half times one half written and a quarter drawn beside it' }, { serial: 'S1971', after: 3, alt: 'A measuring cup filled to one half beside a recipe card that asks for three quarters of a cup of flour' }, { serial: 'S1972', after: 4, alt: 'Three plates, each holding one quarter of a pizza' }],
  title: 'Half of a half', art: 'S581', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a kitchen counter with half a pizza in a box, cutting it down the middle',
  words: [
    'There was half a pizza left in the box, and Priya was allowed half of it. She ate her share and felt cheated. It was one slice. Half of something should be big, she thought, and this was small.',
    'She tried to write what she had eaten. Half of a half. A half times a half? That gave her one over four, which was a quarter, and a quarter was smaller than either half she had started with. How could multiplying make something smaller?',
    'Because of meant multiply, and she was multiplying by a fraction. Half of a whole pizza is big. Half of half a pizza is a quarter, and the box agreed: two cuts, four pieces, one in her hand. Tops times tops, bottoms times bottoms, one over four.',
    'Her mother was baking and wanted two thirds of a recipe that called for three quarters of a cup of flour. Priya tried the rule. Two times three is six, three times four is twelve: six twelfths. Simplified, that is one half. Half a cup, less than the recipe asked, which made sense, because she was making less.',
    'Then she tried a whole number. Three friends each ate a quarter of a pizza. A whole number is itself over one, so three over one times one over four is three over four. Three quarters of a pizza, gone.',
    'When you multiply by a fraction, you take a part of something, and a part is smaller. Tops times tops, bottoms times bottoms, then simplify if you can. And when a problem says of, it is telling you to multiply.',
  ],
};
STORIES['dividing-by-two-digits'] = {
  about: '288 eggs, cartons of 24, and the guess that got a boy most of the way',
  more: [{ serial: 'S585', after: 0, alt: 'A boy filling one egg carton by hand, trays of eggs all around him' }, { serial: 'S586', after: 2, alt: 'Ten full cartons stacked, a smaller pile of eggs left over' }, { serial: 'S1973', after: 3, alt: 'A pile of twelve eggs left over beside twelve full cartons' }, { serial: 'S1974', after: 4, alt: 'Thirteen cartons in a stack, the last one half full' }, { serial: 'S1975', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook page showing 288 divided by 24, an estimate above and the check 12 × 24 = 288 below' }],
  title: 'Cartons of 24', art: 'S584', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a barn beside a big tray of eggs, a stack of empty egg cartons, counting on his fingers',
  words: [
    'Owen had 288 eggs and cartons that held 24. His aunt wanted to know how many cartons to order. Owen started filling cartons by hand to count them and gave up around the fourth one. There had to be a faster way.',
    'Dividing by a number like 4 was easy. Dividing by 24 felt impossible. Twenty-four was too big to see how many times it fit into anything, and there were far too many eggs to count one by one.',
    'So he guessed. Ten cartons would hold 240 eggs. That left 48. Two more cartons would hold exactly 48. Ten and two: twelve cartons. He checked it backward, twelve times twenty-four is 288, and the answer stood.',
    'The next week there were 300 eggs. Owen guessed with rounder numbers: 24 is close to 25, and four 25s make 100, so 300 would need about twelve cartons. Twelve cartons held 288, and 12 eggs were left over. A remainder.',
    'The remainder changed the answer. Twelve eggs would not fit in twelve full cartons, so his aunt needed thirteen, and the last one was half full. Division gives a number, and the question decides what to do with what is left. His aunt said that was the most useful thing he had learned all week.',
    'Dividing by a two-digit number is guessing well. Estimate how many times the divisor fits, take that much away, bring down what is left and estimate again. Then multiply your answer back and see if it lands.',
  ],
};
STORIES['volume'] = {
  about: 'a box of sugar cubes and the girl who counted them without counting them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S588', after: 0, alt: 'A girl losing count of sticky sugar cubes spilled across a table' }, { serial: 'S589', after: 1, alt: 'One layer of cubes, four across and three deep' }, { serial: 'S1976', after: 2, alt: 'The box seen from the side, two layers high' }, { serial: 'S1977', after: 3, alt: 'A bigger box of sugar cubes, five across, four deep and three layers high' }, { serial: 'S1978', after: 4, alt: 'A single tiny cube one centimeter on each side beside a ruler' }],
  title: 'Sugar cubes', art: 'S587', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl looking into an open box of sugar cubes packed in neat rows and layers',
  words: [
    'Lena\'s job was to count the sugar cubes in the box before the party. She lifted them out one at a time and lost count at nineteen. Then she put them back and lost count again. The cubes were sticky and the party was in an hour.',
    'Her grandmother asked how many were in one layer. Lena looked at the top: four across, three deep. Twelve. And how many layers? She looked at the side. Two. She still did not see how that helped.',
    'Twelve cubes in a layer, two layers. Twelve twice is twenty-four. She had counted the whole box in two looks. The length times the width gave one layer, and the height told her how many layers to stack.',
    'At the party her grandmother brought out a bigger box. Five across, four deep, three layers. Lena did not lift a single cube. Five times four is twenty in a layer, and three layers of twenty is sixty.',
    'Her grandmother said scientists count the same way, with tiny cubes one centimeter on each side. A box that holds sixty of them has a volume of sixty cubic centimeters. The cube is the unit, and the answer counts how many would fit.',
    'The volume of a box is length times width times height, and the reason is the sugar cubes. Picture one layer, then count the layers. Every box, tank and room is filled the same way, one layer at a time.',
  ],
};
STORIES['order-of-operations'] = {
  about: 'a math sentence that came out two different ways, and the package that settled it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S591', after: 0, alt: 'Two friends arguing over a card that says 3 + 4 × 2' }, { serial: 'S592', after: 1, alt: 'A boy reading the card left to right with his finger under each number' }, { serial: 'S1982', after: 2, alt: 'The 4 × 2 wrapped like a parcel with an 8 on its label' }, { serial: 'S1983', after: 3, alt: 'A card showing (3 + 4) × 2 = 14 with the parentheses drawn in red' }, { serial: 'S1984', after: 4, alt: '12 ÷ 3 × 2 on a whiteboard with arrows running left to right' }],
  title: 'The package', art: 'S590', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a desk with 3 + 4 × 2 on a card, a small wrapped package drawn around the 4 × 2',
  words: [
    'Two friends solved 3 + 4 × 2 and got two answers. Sam added first and got fourteen. Eli multiplied first and got eleven. They argued until the teacher came over, and each was sure the other had broken something.',
    'Sam said you read left to right, like a sentence. That seemed fair. Eli said multiplying came first. That seemed like a rule someone had made up. Who decided?',
    'The teacher drew a box around the 4 × 2. Think of it as a package, she said. It arrives already wrapped. Multiplication and division are packages, so their answer, eight, is ready before any adding starts. Then add the three. Eleven. If you want to add first, you need parentheses to say so.',
    'Sam got his fourteen back, with parentheses. (3 + 4) × 2 means add first, because the parentheses say so: seven, then fourteen. Both boys had been right about something. Sam had been right about the answer to a different question.',
    'The teacher wrote one more: 12 ÷ 3 × 2. Division and multiplication are equal partners, she said, so go left to right. Twelve divided by three is four, and four times two is eight. Doing the multiplying first would give two, and two is wrong.',
    'Parentheses first, then multiply and divide from left to right, then add and subtract from left to right. It is a rule everyone agreed to so that a math sentence means one thing everywhere, and the package is how to remember it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['theme'] = {
  about: 'two friends, a fight, and the question of what the story was really about',
  more: [{ serial: 'S594', after: 1, alt: 'A reading test with two friends written as the answer and half right marked beside it' }, { serial: 'S595', after: 2, alt: 'The same two friends laughing together at the end of the day' }, { serial: 'S2018', after: 3, alt: 'A girl on a winners stand holding a medal and looking unhappy' }, { serial: 'S2019', after: 4, alt: 'A book about a soldier and a book about a dog side by side, one sentence written between them' }, { serial: 'S2020', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook with the word topic on one line and theme on the line below it' }],
  title: 'What it was really about', art: 'S593', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two friends, both girls, sitting apart on a school bench after an argument, a book open between them',
  words: [
    'The reading test asked what the story was about, and Kai wrote two friends. He was proud of that answer. The story was about two friends who fought and made up. What else could it be about?',
    'His teacher marked it half right. Half. He had named exactly who was in the story. He went back and read it a third time, looking for the half he had missed, and found only the same two friends.',
    'Then she asked a different question. Not who is in it, but what does it say? The friends fought, said awful things, and were still friends by the last page. The story was saying that a real friendship can survive a fight. That was the theme, the thing the story said about life. The friends were only the topic.',
    'Kai tried again with the next story, about a girl who lied to win a race and then could not enjoy the medal. He wrote honesty. His teacher said honesty was still only a topic, one word. A theme is a whole sentence: winning by cheating takes the joy out of winning.',
    'Later Kai noticed that two very different books, one about a soldier and one about a dog, said the same thing: loyalty costs something. Different topics, one theme. The theme was the part that could travel from book to book.',
    'Every story has both. The topic is what it is about: two friends, a dog, a war. The theme is what it says about being alive, and you find it by looking at what changed by the end. Name the topic, then ask what the story wanted you to know.',
  ],
};
STORIES['point-of-view'] = {
  about: 'the same afternoon told twice, once through the eyes and once from the corner',
  more: [{ serial: 'S597', after: 0, alt: 'Two drafts on a desk, one starting with I saw and one with she saw' }, { serial: 'S598', after: 1, alt: 'A girl reading two drafts aloud with a puzzled face' }, { serial: 'S2021', after: 2, alt: 'The view from a camera on the girl\'s head: the storm through her own window, as first person sees it' }, { serial: 'S2022', after: 3, alt: 'A page of directions written with you, a small map beside it' }, { serial: 'S2023', after: 4, alt: 'The same room from the corner, the girl small at the window and her little brother hiding under a blanket' }],
  title: 'Two cameras', art: 'S596', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl writing at a window as storm clouds gather, a small drawn camera on her head and another on a tripod in the corner',
  words: [
    'Ana had to write about the afternoon the storm came, and she wrote it two ways by accident. In the first draft she wrote, I saw the storm coming. In the second she wrote, she saw the storm coming. Her teacher said both were fine and they were completely different.',
    'How could the same afternoon be different depending on one word? The storm was the storm. The window was the window. Ana read both drafts aloud and could feel the difference but could not name it.',
    'Her teacher explained with cameras. When you write I, the camera is on your head. The reader sees only what you see and feels what you feel. That is first person. When you write she, the camera is on a tripod in the corner. The reader can see the whole room, including you. That is third person.',
    'Then her teacher showed a third camera, a rare one. You see the storm coming. That is second person, and it puts the reader inside the story as the one doing things. Directions and some games use it, but most stories do not.',
    'Third person has two kinds of tripod too. One can only see inside one character\'s head. The other can see inside everyone, knowing what each person thinks. Ana rewrote the storm from that all-knowing corner and found out her little brother had been scared the whole time.',
    'I and we mean first person. He, she and they mean third person. The point of view decides what the narrator can know and what the reader gets to see, so choose the camera before you choose the words.',
  ],
};
STORIES['idioms'] = {
  about: 'a friend who said break a leg, and the boy who took her at her word',
  more: [{ serial: 'S600', after: 0, alt: 'A girl backstage whispering to a boy in costume before the curtain rises' }, { serial: 'S601', after: 1, alt: 'The boy checking his own legs in alarm behind the curtain' }, { serial: 'S2024', after: 2, alt: 'The boy bowing on stage to applause, both legs fine' }, { serial: 'S2025', after: 3, alt: 'A boy with a cloud drawn around his head, forgetting his lunch at the door' }, { serial: 'S2026', after: 4, alt: 'A notebook titled idioms with phrases written in English and Spanish' }],
  title: 'Break a leg', art: 'S599', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a school play costume backstage, wide-eyed, a girl beside him giving a thumbs up',
  words: [
    'Right before Theo went on stage for the school play, his friend Maya grabbed his arm and said, break a leg. He froze. He had known Maya since kindergarten and she had never wished him harm before.',
    'He spent the first scene worrying about his legs. Had she cursed him? Was there a loose board? By the second scene he had said all his lines without falling, and Maya was grinning at him from the wings.',
    'Afterward she explained. Break a leg means good luck in the theater. Nobody breaks anything. It is an idiom, a phrase whose meaning is not in its words at all. You cannot work it out. You learn it by meeting it, the way you learn a nickname.',
    'Theo started collecting them. His grandmother said he had his head in the clouds when he forgot his lunch. His coach said the test would be a piece of cake. His dad told him to hold his horses when he rushed. None of it was about clouds, cake or horses.',
    'Maya said other languages have their own. In Spanish, estar en las nubes, to be in the clouds, also means daydreaming. But many idioms do not travel. Translate break a leg word for word into another language and the actor might really worry.',
    'Raining cats and dogs. Piece of cake. Hit the hay. Every language is full of idioms, and each one means something its words do not say. When a phrase makes no sense taken literally, ask what it means to the people who use it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['text-evidence'] = {
  about: 'a claim about a scared boy, and the line on page twelve that proved it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S603', after: 0, alt: 'Two classmates arguing across a book club table' }, { serial: 'S604', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher waiting while two students search their books' }, { serial: 'S2027', after: 2, alt: 'The page with a single line of text glowing, his hands shook' }, { serial: 'S2028', after: 3, alt: 'A boy pointing to a line on page fifteen about yawning at dinner' }, { serial: 'S2029', after: 4, alt: 'A notebook with a claim written and a page number beside it' }],
  title: 'Point to the line', art: 'S602', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl with a paperback open to page twelve, her finger on one line, a classmate leaning in to see',
  words: [
    'In the book club, Priya said the boy in the story was scared. Marcus said he was not, he was just tired. They argued for five minutes and got nowhere, because each was sure and neither had anything but being sure.',
    'The teacher asked one question. Where? Priya said, in the middle somewhere. Marcus said, you can just tell. The teacher waited. Being sure, it turned out, did not count.',
    'Priya opened the book and hunted. Page twelve: his hands shook as he reached for the door. There it was. Not a feeling about the boy but a line the author had written, and it showed fear more directly than any other line in the chapter. Marcus read it and gave in.',
    'Then Marcus found his own line on page fifteen: he yawned twice at dinner. Tired had evidence too. The teacher smiled. A story can hold more than one truth. The boy was scared at the door and tired by dinner, and both claims now stood on something.',
    'The last step was to explain. Priya wrote her claim and the line in quotation marks with its page number. Then she added one sentence saying how the line proved it: shaking hands are what fear does to a body. Claim, evidence, explanation.',
    'A claim about a text needs a line from the text behind it. Point to the page. Pick the line that shows the claim most directly, not the one that sort of hints at it. Evidence is what turns being sure into being right.',
  ],
};
STORIES['mixtures-and-solutions'] = {
  about: 'sand in one glass, salt in the other, and the one that vanished',
  more: [{ serial: 'S606', after: 0, alt: 'A boy stirring sand into one glass and salt into another at a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S607', after: 1, alt: 'The boy tasting the clear water and making a salty face' }, { serial: 'S2000', after: 2, alt: 'The sandy water poured off, sand left in the bottom of the glass' }, { serial: 'S2001', after: 3, alt: 'A shallow dish of white salt crystals on a sunny windowsill' }, { serial: 'S2002', after: 4, alt: 'A glass of water with a small pile of salt resting on the bottom, no longer dissolving' }],
  title: 'Two glasses', art: 'S605', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two glasses of water on a kitchen table, one cloudy with sand settling, one clear, a boy with a spoon',
  words: [
    'Theo stirred a spoon of sand into one glass of water and a spoon of salt into the other. The sand swirled, then sank. The salt swirled, then was gone. Same spoon, same water, and one of them had disappeared.',
    'Where did the salt go? He looked from the side and from the top. Nothing. He was ready to say it had stopped existing until he took a sip and pulled a face. It was in there, all right, in every drop.',
    'The sand had made a mixture. Each part kept its own properties, and he could pour the water off and leave the sand behind. The salt had made a solution, a special mixture where one part dissolves so completely into the other that you cannot pick it out, though you can still taste it.',
    'His sister showed him how to get the salt back. She poured some of the salty water into a shallow dish and set it on a sunny windowsill. Three days later the water was gone and a crust of white crystals was left. Evaporation had pulled the solution apart.',
    'Theo kept adding salt to a fresh glass to see how much would vanish. Spoon after spoon disappeared, until one spoonful sat on the bottom and stayed. The water could hold no more. Warm water, his sister said, would have held a little more before it stopped.',
    'A mixture keeps the properties of its parts and can be separated. A solution is a mixture where one part dissolves and spreads evenly through the other. Sand and salt look alike in the spoon and behave nothing alike in the glass.',
  ],
};
STORIES['earth-sun-moon'] = {
  about: 'a spinning top, a lamp and a marble, and the girl who built the sky on a kitchen table',
  more: [{ serial: 'S609', after: 0, alt: 'A dark kitchen table with one lamp glowing, a top and a marble beside it' }, { serial: 'S610', after: 1, alt: 'The top circling the lamp, half of it lit, half in shadow' }, { serial: 'S2003', after: 2, alt: 'The marble circling the top in a small ring' }, { serial: 'S2004', after: 3, alt: 'Four views of the marble around the top: full, half, a thin sliver and dark' }, { serial: 'S2005', after: 4, alt: 'A marble with a painted dot always facing the spinning top as it circles' }],
  title: 'Top, lamp, marble', art: 'S608', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a kitchen table spinning a top near a glowing lamp, a marble beside the top',
  words: [
    'Ava could not keep the three things straight. A day, a month, a year. They were all just time, and the sky did not come with labels. Her dad cleared the kitchen table and turned off every light but one.',
    'He set a spinning top near the lamp. Which side of the top is lit? The side facing the lamp. Now watch the top spin. Every side gets its turn in the light and its turn in the dark. Ava said, day and night. He nodded. One spin, one day.',
    'Then he carried the spinning top in a slow circle around the lamp. One lap, one year. Then he rolled a marble in a small ring around the top. One ring, about one month, and the marble was the moon. Three motions, three clocks.',
    'Then her dad crouched to the height of the top and asked her to watch the marble. As it went around, Ava saw its lit side full, then half, then a thin sliver, then dark. The marble never changed shape. She was seeing different amounts of its sunny half. Those are the phases of the moon.',
    'There was one more trick. As the marble circled, her dad turned it slowly so the same painted dot always faced the top. The real moon does that too, spinning once for every trip around Earth, which is why we always see the same face.',
    'The Earth spins for day and night, circles the sun for the year, and the moon circles the Earth for the month. Once you have seen it on a table, you never lose it in the sky.',
  ],
};
STORIES['inherited-and-learned'] = {
  about: 'brown eyes, a bicycle, and the question that sorted them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S612', after: 0, alt: 'A boy and his father side by side at a mirror, both with brown eyes' }, { serial: 'S613', after: 1, alt: 'The boy falling off a bike on a lawn, his father laughing kindly' }, { serial: 'S2009', after: 2, alt: 'Two columns on a page: from my parents, and from practice' }, { serial: 'S2010', after: 3, alt: 'A spider spinning a web beside a dog sitting for a treat' }, { serial: 'S2011', after: 4, alt: 'A long-legged boy running on a school track while a coach holds a stopwatch' }],
  title: 'Eyes and wheels', art: 'S611', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy comparing his brown eyes with his father\'s in a mirror, a bicycle leaning by the door',
  words: [
    'Diego\'s homework asked which of his traits were inherited and which were learned. He had brown eyes, like his dad. He could ride a bike, like his dad. Both came from his dad, he figured, so both were inherited.',
    'His dad laughed and asked how the bike riding had gone at first. Diego remembered. Twenty falls, two scraped knees, one bent pedal. His dad had not handed him the skill. He had handed him the bike.',
    'That was the difference. Brown eyes came from his parents before he was born, and no amount of practice could change them. That is inherited. Riding came from falling twenty times and getting up twenty-one. That is learned. The question that sorts them is simple: could this have been practiced?',
    'Diego tried the question on animals. A spider spins its first web without ever seeing one, so that behavior is inherited; scientists call it instinct. His neighbor\'s dog sits when told, but only because someone taught it with a hundred treats. That is learned.',
    'Some things mix both. Height is mostly inherited, but good food helps a body reach it. Diego inherited long legs from his mom, but how fast he runs also depends on how much he practices. The legs came first. The speed came from the track.',
    'Inherited traits come from parents. Learned traits come from practice and experience. Every living thing has both, and the way to tell them apart is to ask whether it could have been learned at all.',
  ],
};
STORIES['informational-piece'] = {
  about: 'a school tour that a boy wrote down, one room per paragraph',
  more: [{ serial: 'S615', after: 0, alt: 'A boy writing one very long paragraph in a notebook' }, { serial: 'S616', after: 1, alt: 'A boy searching a crowded page for the word library' }, { serial: 'S2057', after: 2, alt: 'Three doors in a hallway: the gym, the library and the garden' }, { serial: 'S2058', after: 3, alt: 'A school guide with a heading over each paragraph, a small map and a captioned photo' }, { serial: 'S2059', after: 5, alt: 'A finished guide with an introduction, three headed paragraphs and a conclusion' }],
  title: 'The tour', art: 'S614', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy leading a small group through a school entrance hall, a notebook in his hand',
  words: [
    'New families were coming to see the school, and Diego was asked to write a guide. He wrote everything he knew in one long paragraph: the gym, the library, the lunch line, the garden, the smell of the art room. His teacher read it and said she felt lost.',
    'Lost? It was all there. He had not left anything out. But when he read it back, he could not find the library either. It was buried between the lunch line and the garden with nothing to mark the door.',
    'So he wrote it like a tour. An introduction that said what the school was: the entrance. One paragraph for the gym, one for the library, one for the garden: the rooms, each with its own idea and its own details. A conclusion that invited them to visit: the exit. Now a reader could walk through it.',
    'Then he added signs. A heading over each paragraph, like the names over real doors: The Gym, The Library, The Garden. A small map with the rooms labeled. A caption under his photo of the garden. Readers who skimmed could still find their way.',
    'His teacher crossed out one sentence: the library is the best room. Informational writing gives facts, she said, and best is an opinion. He changed it to something a reader could check: the library has four thousand books and a reading corner by the window.',
    'An informational piece is a building. Introduction with the topic, a paragraph for each part, a conclusion at the end. Each paragraph holds one idea and the details that belong to it. Write it like a tour and nobody gets lost.',
  ],
};
STORIES['opinion-essay-5'] = {
  about: 'a class pet, five paragraphs, and the girl who learned where each one goes',
  more: [{ serial: 'S618', after: 0, alt: 'A girl rereading two paragraphs that say the same thing, looking puzzled' }, { serial: 'S619', after: 2, alt: 'Three cards in the middle of the row, each with a different reason drawn on it' }, { serial: 'S2060', after: 3, alt: 'A fish tank in a calm classroom next door' }, { serial: 'S2061', after: 4, alt: 'A sign-up sheet for taking the class pet home on weekends' }, { serial: 'S2062', after: 5, alt: 'The conclusion card, the opinion card\'s picture repeated with a bigger smile' }],
  title: 'Five paragraphs', art: 'S617', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a desk with five index cards laid out in a row, a hamster cage drawn on the first',
  words: [
    'Rosa had one paragraph about why the class should have a pet, and it was a good one. Her teacher asked for five. Rosa thought that meant saying the same thing five times, and her second paragraph was the first one in different words.',
    'By the third she had run out of ways to say it. The essay was getting longer and emptier at the same time, like a balloon. There had to be a reason for five.',
    'There was. Paragraph one: the opinion. Our class should have a pet. Paragraphs two, three and four: one reason each, with room to explain it. It teaches us to care. It calms the room. It is fun. Paragraph five: the opinion again, carried by the three reasons behind it. Five jobs, five paragraphs.',
    'Her teacher said each reason needed something to stand on. It calms the room became: when the class next door got a fish tank, their teacher said the loudest part of the day got quieter. A reason with an example is harder to wave away.',
    'Rosa also thought about who would read it. The principal would decide, and the principal would worry about weekends. So Rosa added a sentence to her conclusion: families could sign up to take the pet home, one weekend each.',
    'An opinion essay is an introduction with the opinion, one paragraph per reason, and a conclusion that says it again with the reasons behind it. Linking words like first, also and finally carry the reader from one to the next. Five is not more of the same. It is five different jobs.',
  ],
};
STORIES['personal-narrative-5'] = {
  about: 'the Saturday the training wheels came off, told with the slow part slowed down',
  more: [{ serial: 'S621', after: 0, alt: 'A short three-sentence story on a page beside a clock showing eight seconds' }, { serial: 'S622', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher holding up two fingers beside a drawing of a bike' }, { serial: 'S2063', after: 2, alt: 'The training wheels lying in the grass beside a wrench' }, { serial: 'S2064', after: 3, alt: 'A boy on a bike with wind in his hair, laughing' }, { serial: 'S2065', after: 4, alt: 'The boy riding away by himself down the sidewalk, his dad watching' }],
  title: 'The moment', art: 'S620', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy wobbling on a bike in a driveway, his father\'s hand just leaving the seat',
  words: [
    'Sam wrote about the day he learned to ride a bike in three sentences. My dad took the training wheels off. I rode. It was fun. It was all true and it took eight seconds to read, which felt wrong for the biggest day of that summer.',
    'His teacher asked which part had mattered most. The moment his dad let go, Sam said. She asked how long that moment had taken. About two seconds. And how many words had he given it? Two.',
    'He wrote it again and slowed down at the right place. First the scene: a Saturday, the driveway hot, the wheels in the grass. Then the build: the wobble, his dad running beside him, the hand on the seat. Then the moment, stretched out: the hand gone, the front wheel shaking, and the second where it stopped shaking. Then what changed: he was a rider now.',
    'His teacher pointed at one word: fun. Fun tells, she said. Show it instead. Sam crossed it out and wrote what his body did: the wind in his ears, his mouth open, a laugh that came out before he could stop it. The reader could feel the fun without being told.',
    'Then he added the one thing his dad said, in quotation marks: You are doing it by yourself. Real words from a real person made the moment sound true. Sam kept the whole story on one page, and it took a full minute to read.',
    'A personal narrative sets the scene, builds to the moment, and says what changed. The secret is pace. Move quickly through the ordinary parts and slow right down at the moment that mattered, so the reader lives there as long as you did.',
  ],
};
STORIES['thirteen-colonies'] = {
  about: 'thirteen colonies, three rows on a map, and the girl who saw the pattern in the weather',
  more: [{ serial: 'S624', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning at thirteen flashcards spread across her bed' }, { serial: 'S625', after: 1, alt: 'A grandfather pointing at a coastal map while a girl looks at where the colonies sit' }, { serial: 'S2036', after: 2, alt: 'The northern band: rocky shore, fishing boats and small farms under a cold sky' }, { serial: 'S2037', after: 3, alt: 'Colonists at a New England town meeting raising hands to vote' }, { serial: 'S2038', after: 4, alt: 'The southern band: big farms of wide green fields along a warm river' }],
  title: 'Three rows', art: 'S623', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl tracing an old map of the Atlantic coast with thirteen colonies marked in three bands of color',
  words: [
    'Maya had to learn thirteen colonies for a test, and thirteen felt like a lot of names to hold. She tried a song. She tried flashcards. By Thursday she could remember nine, and never the same nine.',
    'Her grandfather looked at her map and asked what the weather was like in each place. Maya said she was studying colonies, not weather. He asked again, and she looked at where they sat along the coast.',
    'They fell into three rows. New England up top, cold and rocky, where people fished and built ships and lived in tight towns. The Middle colonies in between, with rich soil for wheat and busy ports. The South, warm and wide, with big farms along slow rivers. Settled between 1607 and 1732 by people wanting land, faith or a fresh start, and the land told each row how to live.',
    'Her grandfather added one more pattern. The colonists were far from the king, so they learned to govern themselves. Virginia had an elected assembly, the House of Burgesses, from 1619. New England towns held meetings where settlers argued and voted on local matters. That habit of deciding for themselves would matter later.',
    'And not everyone came by choice. In the South especially, the big farms depended on enslaved Africans, brought by force and denied every choice the colonists prized. The three rows were shaped by weather and soil, and also by who was made to do the work.',
    'Thirteen colonies in three regions, each with its own way of living, shaped by its own weather and soil. Learn the three rows first, and the thirteen names have somewhere to hang.',
  ],
};
STORIES['road-to-revolution'] = {
  about: 'four steps that turned tax-paying colonists into a country, told through one family\'s teapot',
  more: [{ serial: 'S627', after: 0, alt: 'A very old teapot on a high shelf in a family kitchen' }, { serial: 'S628', after: 1, alt: 'Colonists reading a tax notice nailed to a wall, frowning' }, { serial: 'S2039', after: 2, alt: 'Crates of tea splashing into a dark harbor at night, seen from the shore' }, { serial: 'S2040', after: 3, alt: 'Delegates arriving at a brick hall in Philadelphia in 1774' }, { serial: 'S2041', after: 5, alt: 'A crowd in a town square listening as a declaration is read aloud' }],
  title: 'Four steps to a war', art: 'S626', cast: [],
  alt: 'A colonial family at a kitchen table, a teapot pushed aside, a printed notice on the wall',
  words: [
    'Eli\'s family had a very old teapot that nobody used, and a story to go with it. In 1765 his ancestors had to pay a new tax on paper, stamps and later tea, and they had never been asked. No vote, no say, just a bill.',
    'They grumbled and paid and grumbled more. Eli wondered why grumbling turned into a war. Plenty of people pay taxes they hate. What made these people different?',
    'It happened in steps. In 1765, taxes with no vote. In 1773, colonists dumped tea into Boston Harbor rather than pay, and the teapot went on a shelf for good. In 1775, shots at Lexington. And on July 4, 1776, a declaration that governments exist to serve the people, and a people may change one that does not.',
    'Eli found more steps in between. In 1770, British soldiers fired into a crowd in Boston and five colonists died, and the colonists called it a massacre. In 1774, Britain closed Boston Harbor to punish the tea party, and delegates from twelve colonies met in Philadelphia as the First Continental Congress.',
    'The colonists had a slogan for all of it: no taxation without representation. They were not against every tax. They were against taxes voted by a Parliament across the ocean, where they had no members at all.',
    'Taxes without a vote, then protests, then shots, then a declaration. Each step made the next one possible, and none of them was the whole story alone. A war is rarely one event. It is a staircase, and the teapot sat on the first step.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-constitution'] = {
  about: 'a first plan too weak to pay its own bills, and the second plan that has lasted',
  more: [{ serial: 'S630', after: 0, alt: 'A worn set of papers marked as failed, an empty money chest beside them' }, { serial: 'S631', after: 1, alt: 'One state saying no while delegates from every other state wait around a table' }, { serial: 'S2042', after: 2, alt: 'Three drawn figures side by side, Congress, the President and the Courts, linked by arrows' }, { serial: 'S2043', after: 3, alt: 'Two houses of Congress drawn side by side: a big House by population and a Senate with two seats for every state' }, { serial: 'S2044', after: 4, alt: 'A newspaper announcing that the ninth state has approved the Constitution' }],
  title: 'The second plan', art: 'S629', cast: [],
  alt: 'Delegates around a long table in a candlelit hall, the old Articles of Confederation beside an empty money chest, one man standing to speak',
  words: [
    'The country\'s first plan of government, the Articles of Confederation, could not even pay its own bills. Nadia could not believe it. A whole country with no way to collect money, no way to settle fights between states, no one in charge.',
    'She asked why they did not just fix it. Her teacher said they tried, and every fix needed every state to agree, and one state always said no. The plan was so weak that even fixing it was impossible.',
    'So in 1787, fifty-five delegates met in a hot hall and wrote a second plan. It gave the country three branches: a Congress to make laws, a President to carry them out, and courts to judge them. Each branch could stop the others, so no one branch could take over. Then a Bill of Rights was added to protect what the government could never touch.',
    'The big states and the small states nearly broke the meeting apart. Big states wanted votes by population. Small states wanted one vote each. The answer was a compromise: two houses of Congress, the House of Representatives by population and the Senate with two senators from every state.',
    'The plan still had to be approved. Nine of the thirteen states had to say yes, and in 1788 the ninth one did. Some states agreed only after a promise that a list of rights would be added. In 1791 the first ten amendments, the Bill of Rights, kept that promise.',
    'The Constitution was written after the Articles failed: three branches that check each other, and a Bill of Rights added soon after. The point was never that the government would be strong. The point was that no part of it could be too strong.',
  ],
};
STORIES['growing-west'] = {
  about: 'a purchase that doubled the map, and the trails, gold and rails that filled it in',
  more: [{ serial: 'S633', after: 0, alt: 'A girl unfolding a map where a huge middle piece has just been added' }, { serial: 'S634', after: 1, alt: 'The peoples who had always lived on the plains, a village beside a river on land that was new only to the map' }, { serial: 'S2045', after: 2, alt: 'A wagon train on a long trail across the plains toward mountains' }, { serial: 'S2046', after: 3, alt: 'Explorers in a canoe on a wide river, a guide pointing west' }, { serial: 'S2047', after: 4, alt: 'A steam train crossing the same plains on new rails, gold pans by the track' }],
  title: 'Doubling the map', art: 'S632', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl unfolding a map of the young United States that grows as it opens, a wagon trail drawn across it',
  words: [
    'In 1803 the United States bought a piece of land from France for fifteen million dollars, and the country doubled in size overnight. Priya thought that sounded like a great deal. Her teacher asked what the country planned to do with all that empty space.',
    'Nothing, at first. Nobody lived there but the peoples who had always lived there, and few Americans knew what was in it. A map twice as big is not twice as useful if the new half is blank.',
    'Then the blank half filled. Trails carried wagons west through the plains. In 1848 gold turned up in California, and tens of thousands rushed to it. A war with Mexico added the southwest, and railroads would later follow the trails. By 1850 the country reached the Pacific Ocean, and the map that had doubled was full to the far edge.',
    'First came the explorers. In 1804 Lewis and Clark set out up the Missouri River with a small crew, helped along the way by guides like Sacagawea, and reached the Pacific in 1805. Their maps and notes filled in some of the blank half.',
    'The filling had a cost. The land was never empty to the nations who lived there. As settlers moved west, the government forced many Native peoples off their homelands, including the Cherokee on the Trail of Tears in 1838. The railroad that crossed the continent in 1869 carried more settlers still.',
    'The Louisiana Purchase doubled the country in 1803; trails, gold and war carried it to the Pacific by 1850. A country grows twice: once on paper, and once when people go and live in the lines.',
  ],
};
STORIES['civil-war'] = {
  about: 'a country that split over slavery, and the four years it took to mend',
  more: [{ serial: 'S636', after: 0, alt: 'A boy on an attic floor reading an old letter pulled from a shoebox' }, { serial: 'S637', after: 1, alt: 'A map of the United States torn down the middle, two colors, 1861' }, { serial: 'S2048', after: 3, alt: 'A field at Gettysburg with a small crowd listening to a speaker on a wooden platform' }, { serial: 'S2049', after: 4, alt: 'A quiet theater balcony draped in black cloth in April 1865' }, { serial: 'S2050', after: 5, alt: 'A stitched map, whole again, with a new amendment pinned beside it' }],
  title: 'The split', art: 'S635', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding an old letter on a farmhouse porch, a map on the wall behind him divided into two colors',
  words: [
    'In a shoebox in the attic Marcus found a letter from 1862, written by a soldier to his sister. The soldier wrote that the country had split in two and he did not know if it would ever be one again. Marcus read it three times. A country can split?',
    'It can. Between December 1860 and June 1861, eleven southern states left the Union over slavery, which they wanted to keep and expand. The rest of the country said no. Neither side would give in, and the argument became a war.',
    'The war lasted four years. In 1863 President Lincoln declared the enslaved people in the rebelling states free. In 1865 the South surrendered, more than six hundred thousand soldiers had died, and the 13th Amendment ended slavery everywhere in the country. The soldier\'s sister got her letter. The country got itself back, changed.',
    'Marcus looked up the soldier\'s years. The war had begun at Fort Sumter in April 1861. In July 1863 the Union won at Gettysburg, in Pennsylvania, and that November Lincoln gave a two-minute speech there about a nation that should not perish.',
    'Five days after the main Southern army surrendered in April 1865, Lincoln was shot at a theater in Washington, and he died the next morning. He did not live to see the 13th Amendment ratified that December, or the long work of rebuilding the South that came after.',
    'A split over slavery, war from 1861 to 1865, emancipation in 1863, and an amendment that ended slavery for good. The letter in the shoebox is how history reaches a boy in an attic: one family at a time.',
  ],
};
// Middle school, in the fewest words that still make a story.
STORIES['dividing-fractions'] = {
  about: 'three pizzas, a party of hungry kids, and the division that came out bigger than it went in',
  more: [{ serial: 'S639', after: 0, alt: 'A boy staring at three whole pizzas while a crowd of hungry kids waits' }, { serial: 'S640', after: 1, alt: 'A notebook with a crossed-out answer of one and a half kids' }, { serial: 'S2069', after: 2, alt: 'The three pizzas cut into six halves, six children reaching for them' }, { serial: 'S2070', after: 3, alt: 'Three quarters of a pizza cut into six equal slices' }, { serial: 'S2071', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook page with 3 ÷ 1/2 = 6 and a flipped fraction beside it' }],
  title: 'How many halves', art: 'S638', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a party table with three whole pizzas in a row, each about to be cut in half',
  words: [
    'Jamal had three pizzas and a rule that everyone at the party got half a pizza. How many kids could he feed? He wrote three divided by one half and stopped, because everything he knew said dividing made numbers smaller, and the answer had to be at least three.',
    'He tried to work it the long way and got one and a half, which meant one and a half kids, which was ridiculous. The pizzas were cooling. The kids were counting.',
    'So he stopped calculating and looked at the pizzas. Each one held two halves. Three pizzas held six halves. Six kids. Dividing by a half was not making the pizzas smaller; it was counting how many halves fit inside them. And the shortcut said the same thing: flip the half into two over one and multiply. Three times two, six.',
    'At the end of the party three quarters of a pizza was left, and his mother wanted to know how many eighth-size slices it made. Three quarters divided by one eighth. Flip the eighth and multiply: three quarters times eight is six. Six slices. He checked it backward: six eighths is three quarters.',
    'Jamal liked the checking best. Division asks how many of one thing fit inside another, and multiplication puts them back together. If the answer multiplies back to where you started, it is right.',
    'To divide by a fraction, flip it and multiply. Dividing by something smaller than one gives a bigger answer, because you are counting small pieces inside a big thing. Three pizzas, six halves, six happy kids.',
  ],
};
STORIES['area-of-triangles'] = {
  about: 'a triangular garden bed and the rectangle that happened to be drawn around it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S642', after: 0, alt: 'A girl kneeling by a triangular garden bed with a soil catalog, Tuesday circled on a calendar' }, { serial: 'S643', after: 1, alt: 'The rectangle of string with the triangle inside, the leftover corners shaded' }, { serial: 'S2075', after: 3, alt: 'A boy stretching a tape along the slanted side of the triangle while a plumb line hangs straight down beside it' }, { serial: 'S2076', after: 4, alt: 'A paper parallelogram with a triangle cut from one end and slid to the other to make a rectangle' }, { serial: 'S2077', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook with a rectangle, a diagonal, and one half written under it' }],
  title: 'Half a rectangle', art: 'S641', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl kneeling beside a triangular garden bed, a rectangle of string pegged around it',
  words: [
    'The new garden bed was a triangle, and Priya needed its area to order soil. She knew rectangles: base times height. But a triangle had a slanted side and no rule she could remember, and the soil truck was coming Tuesday.',
    'She pegged a rectangle of string around the bed just to see it better. Base six feet, height four. The rectangle was twenty-four square feet. That was too much, but by how much?',
    'She looked at the two leftover corners of the rectangle, the parts outside the triangle. Together they made up exactly the same shape as the triangle itself. The triangle was half of the rectangle around it. Twenty-four divided by two: twelve square feet. Six bags of soil, not twelve.',
    'Her brother wanted to measure the slanted side instead. It was five feet. Six times five, halved, is fifteen, three square feet too many. The height has to be the straight-up distance from the base to the top corner, the way a plumb line would hang.',
    'The next bed was a slanted four-sided shape, a parallelogram. Priya cut a paper copy, sliced the triangle off one end and slid it to the other. It became a rectangle with the same base and height, and the same area. Base times height, no halving, because nothing had been cut away.',
    'A parallelogram is base times height. A triangle is half of that, because every triangle is half of a rectangle drawn around it. Use only the straight-up height, never the slanted side, and the halving does the rest.',
  ],
};
STORIES['one-step-equations'] = {
  about: 'a mystery box on a balance scale, and the trick of taking the same thing off both sides',
  more: [{ serial: 'S645', after: 0, alt: 'A boy staring at a sealed box on a balance scale, weights on both pans' }, { serial: 'S646', after: 1, alt: 'A boy scribbling guesses of ten and six beside a scale that tips one way and then the other' }, { serial: 'S2078', after: 2, alt: 'Five weights lifted off each pan at the same time, the box left alone against seven weights' }, { serial: 'S2079', after: 3, alt: 'Three identical boxes on one pan balancing twelve weights split into three groups of four' }, { serial: 'S2080', after: 4, alt: 'A notebook with 7 + 5 = 12 and 3 × 4 = 12, each checked' }],
  title: 'The mystery box', art: 'S644', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a balance scale, a sealed box and five small weights on one pan, twelve weights on the other, level',
  words: [
    'On the science table sat a balance with a sealed box and five weights on one pan and twelve weights on the other, perfectly level. Owen\'s job was to find out how heavy the box was without opening it. He stared at it as if the answer might be written on the lid.',
    'He tried guessing. Ten? Then the box plus five would be fifteen, and the scale would tip. Six? Then eleven, and it would tip the other way. Guessing could take all afternoon.',
    'Then he did something simpler. He took five weights off the left pan and five off the right at the same time. The beam did not move. Now the box sat alone on one side and seven weights on the other. The box weighed seven. He had undone the adding by subtracting, and the balance had stayed true because he did it to both sides.',
    'The next setup had three identical boxes on one side and twelve weights on the other. Owen split each side into three equal groups: one box against four weights. He had undone multiplying by dividing both sides by three. Three x equals twelve, so x equals four.',
    'Then he checked both answers by putting them back. Seven plus five is twelve. Three times four is twelve. Both scales would stay level, so both answers were right. Checking takes ten seconds and catches most mistakes.',
    'An equation is a balance. Do the same thing to both sides and it stays level. Undo adding by subtracting, undo multiplying by dividing, and check by putting the answer back in. The box is x, and x was seven all along.',
  ],
};
STORIES['claims-and-reasons'] = {
  about: 'a boy who argued for a later school start, and the reason that gave way under him',
  more: [{ serial: 'S648', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a podium with sweaty hands and three index cards' }, { serial: 'S649', after: 1, alt: 'A girl in the back row raising her hand while the class laughs' }, { serial: 'S2108', after: 2, alt: 'Three index cards on a podium, the middle one crossed out' }, { serial: 'S2109', after: 3, alt: 'An index card with a note citing a doctors group and the time 8:30' }, { serial: 'S2110', after: 4, alt: 'The boy at the podium holding up the two cards that held' }],
  title: 'The reason that fell', art: 'S647', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a classroom podium with three index cards, one of them crossed out',
  words: [
    'Marcus had three minutes at the podium and one thing he wanted: school should start an hour later. That was his claim. He had three index cards, one reason on each, and his hands were sweating onto the top one.',
    'Card one: teenagers\' bodies are wired to fall asleep late, so early starts cut into their sleep. Card two: his cousin in Ohio starts later and likes it. Card three: students who sleep more get better grades. He read all three and sat down feeling good. Then a girl in the back raised her hand and asked what his cousin liking it had to do with the rest of them. The room laughed. Marcus did not have an answer.',
    'On the walk home he worked out what had gone wrong. A claim is the thing you are trying to prove. A reason is only worth keeping if it actually holds the claim up, the way a leg holds up a table. Card one held: sleep science supports a later start for everyone. Card three held: better sleep, better grades, for everyone. Card two was about one person\'s taste. Pull on it and the claim did not move at all. He crossed it out.',
    'The next week he came back with evidence under each card that was left. For card one he cited the American Academy of Pediatrics, a national group of doctors for children, which in 2014 recommended that middle and high schools start no earlier than 8:30. The girl in the back nodded. A reason with evidence under it is a leg with a foot.',
    'Every argument you will ever hear works the same way: find the claim first, then push on each reason and see whether the claim moves. The strong ones hold. The weak ones, like a cousin in Ohio, fall away, and the argument is better without them.',
  ],
};
STORIES['tone-and-mood'] = {
  about: 'the same rain described by two writers, and the girl who could feel the difference before she could name it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S651', after: 0, alt: 'A cozy page, rain drumming cheerfully on a warm roof while a family plays cards' }, { serial: 'S652', after: 1, alt: 'A gray page, endless rain on an empty street and a man at a window' }, { serial: 'S2111', after: 2, alt: 'A girl pointing to two phrases on a page: drummed happily and would not stop' }, { serial: 'S2112', after: 3, alt: 'Two coin purses side by side, one labeled thrifty and one labeled stingy' }, { serial: 'S2113', after: 4, alt: 'An empty playground on a sunny afternoon, one swing moving with no wind' }],
  title: 'Two rains', art: 'S650', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a window seat with two open books, rain streaking the glass behind her',
  words: [
    'Ava read two stories on the same rainy afternoon. In the first, the rain drummed happily on the roof and the family played cards. In the second, the rain would not stop and a man stared out at an empty street. Same rain. Ava felt cozy after one and trapped after the other.',
    'Her teacher asked what the difference was. Ava said the rain. But it was the same rain, falling on the same kind of day. The difference was somewhere in the words, and she could not put her finger on it.',
    'She pointed to them. Drummed happily: the first writer liked the rain, and that attitude was tone. Would not stop, empty street: those words made her feel shut in, and that feeling was mood. The writer chose the words. The words made the tone and the mood. And Ava could prove it by pointing.',
    'Her teacher tested it with one word. A thrifty man and a stingy man may spend exactly the same money, but thrifty sounds wise and stingy sounds mean. Words carry feelings along with their meanings, and choosing between them is how a writer sets the tone.',
    'Then Ava wrote a sunny afternoon that felt frightening. The playground was empty. The swings moved though there was no wind. Nothing in it was dark, and the mood was dark anyway. The details had done it.',
    'Tone is the writer\'s attitude. Mood is the feeling you get. Both come out of the words on the page, so when someone asks how you know, point to the words. Two rains, two feelings, and the weather had nothing to do with it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['word-roots'] = {
  about: 'one root, five long words, and the boy who stopped being afraid of vocabulary lists',
  more: [{ serial: 'S654', after: 0, alt: 'A boy groaning over five word cards on a library table' }, { serial: 'S655', after: 1, alt: 'A boy holding the cards import and export with a question mark between them' }, { serial: 'S2114', after: 2, alt: 'A porter carrying bags, with the word port drawn on his cap' }, { serial: 'S2115', after: 3, alt: 'Arrows on a map showing across, in and out, labeled trans, im and ex' }, { serial: 'S2116', after: 4, alt: 'A pair of spectacles beside an old telephone and a word card that says spect' }],
  title: 'The root port', art: 'S653', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a library table with five long words on cards, the letters p-o-r-t glowing in every one',
  words: [
    'Marcus had five words to learn by Friday: transport, import, export, portable and porter. Five unrelated words, he thought, five separate things to memorize. He groaned and started with the first.',
    'By Wednesday he could spell them and not one of them meant anything to him. He kept mixing up import and export. They looked almost the same, and that was the problem, or so he thought.',
    'Then his tutor circled four letters in every word. Port. It meant carry. Transport: carry across. Import: carry in. Export: carry out. Portable: able to be carried. Porter: someone who carries. Five words, one meaning, and the front piece told him which way the carrying went. The list learned itself.',
    'The pieces around the root had meanings too. Trans means across, im means in, ex means out, and able means able to be. His tutor called them prefixes and suffixes: the front and back of a word, with the root in the middle carrying the meaning.',
    'By Friday Marcus was hunting roots on his own. Spect means look: inspect, spectator, spectacles. Tele means far and phone means sound, so a telephone carries sound from far away. Many of these pieces came into English from Latin and Greek, and they are still doing their old jobs.',
    'A root you know unlocks every word built on it. When a long word looks new, look for the part you already know. Port, aqua, bio, geo, tele: a few dozen roots open thousands of doors.',
  ],
};
STORIES['central-idea'] = {
  about: 'three paragraphs about bees that were secretly doing the same job',
  more: [{ serial: 'S657', after: 0, alt: 'A girl writing bees pollinate flowers on a worksheet in a garden' }, { serial: 'S658', after: 1, alt: 'A worksheet with two answers marked wrong in red' }, { serial: 'S2117', after: 2, alt: 'Three workers pulling one rope, each paragraph drawn as a worker' }, { serial: 'S2118', after: 3, alt: 'The article with one sentence written above it in bold: we need bees, and we can help them' }, { serial: 'S2119', after: 4, alt: 'A four-sentence summary written on an index card' }],
  title: 'The same job', art: 'S656', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl reading an article in a garden, a bee on a flower beside her, three paragraphs glowing on the page',
  words: [
    'The article had three paragraphs. One about how bees pollinate. One about bees dying. One about what farmers could do. Lena had to say what the whole thing was about, and she wrote down the first paragraph\'s fact. Bees pollinate flowers.',
    'Marked wrong. She tried the second fact. Bees are dying. Also wrong. She had read the article, she could repeat every detail in it, and she could not say what it was about. That felt unfair.',
    'Her teacher asked what all three paragraphs were doing together. Not what each said, but what job they shared. Lena read them again as a team. Bees do essential work, bees are in trouble, and here is what to do about it. All three were pulling the same rope: we need bees, and we can help them. That was the central idea.',
    'Her teacher added one more difference. The topic was bees, one word. The central idea was a sentence about bees: we need them, and we can help them. A topic names the subject. A central idea says something about it.',
    'Then Lena wrote a summary: the central idea first, then one key detail from each paragraph to hold it up, in her own words and without her opinion. Four sentences told the whole article.',
    'The central idea is what every paragraph is helping to say. A detail from one paragraph is never the central idea, however true it is. Ask what job the paragraphs share, and the answer is the idea holding the whole thing up.',
  ],
};
STORIES['elements-and-compounds'] = {
  about: 'letters, words, and the girl who saw the alphabet in a glass of water',
  more: [{ serial: 'S660', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning at a homework page that says water is hydrogen and oxygen' }, { serial: 'S661', after: 1, alt: 'A row of single letters on tiles, each one glowing on its own' }, { serial: 'S2087', after: 2, alt: 'The tiles joined into a word, water, with a drop beside it' }, { serial: 'S2088', after: 3, alt: 'A salt shaker beside a tiny piece of shiny metal and a jar of pale green gas' }, { serial: 'S2089', after: 4, alt: 'A wall chart of the periodic table with a girl pointing at H and O' }],
  title: 'Letters and words', art: 'S659', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding a glass of water up to the window, letters H and O floating in it like alphabet pasta',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s science homework said water was made of hydrogen and oxygen, and she found that hard to believe. Hydrogen was a gas that burned. Oxygen was a gas she breathed. Water was a drink. How could two gases add up to a drink?',
    'She read the formula, H2O, and it looked like a code. Two Hs and an O. She knew the letters but not the word, and she could not see how letters became something you could swim in.',
    'Her brother said to think of it as spelling. H is a letter, one kind of atom, an element. O is another letter, another element. H2O is a word spelled from them: two hydrogen atoms joined to one oxygen atom, and the word is a compound with properties of its own. Letters burn and breathe. The word puts out fires.',
    'He showed her another word. Sodium is a soft metal that fizzes, and can burst into flame, in water. Chlorine is a poisonous yellow-green gas. Joined, they spell NaCl: table salt, which she sprinkled on her dinner. The compound had none of the danger of its letters.',
    'The alphabet has 26 letters. Chemistry has 118 elements, laid out in the periodic table, and every substance on Earth is spelled from them. Some words are short, like O2, the oxygen she breathed. Some run to thousands of letters, like the proteins in her own body.',
    'An element is one kind of atom. A compound is two or more kinds joined, and it can be nothing like its parts. Read a formula the way you read a word: the letters say which elements, the small numbers say how many of each.',
  ],
};
STORIES['heat-transfer'] = {
  about: 'a pan handle, a pot of soup and a campfire, and the three roads heat took to reach one boy',
  more: [{ serial: 'S663', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a campfire shaking a burned hand, a pan handle nearby' }, { serial: 'S664', after: 1, alt: 'A close look at a metal pan handle glowing with heat along its length' }, { serial: 'S2090', after: 2, alt: 'Soup swirling in a pot, hot broth rising in the middle and sinking at the sides' }, { serial: 'S2091', after: 3, alt: 'An oven mitt, a wooden spoon and a pan with a plastic handle on a camp table' }, { serial: 'S2092', after: 4, alt: 'A metal cup and a wooden bowl side by side on a table at night, a hand touching each' }],
  title: 'Three ways', art: 'S662', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a campfire with a pan on the flames, a pot of soup bubbling beside it, his face lit by the fire',
  words: [
    'At the campsite, Diego burned his hand on a pan handle that had never touched the flame. Then he watched the soup stir itself, and then he felt the fire warm his face from six feet away with nothing in between. Heat seemed to go wherever it wanted.',
    'He asked his aunt how the handle got hot when only the pan was in the fire. She asked him to think about the three things he had just noticed, and whether they were really the same trick.',
    'They were three tricks. The handle was conduction: heat passing by touch through the metal, atom to atom, up to his hand. The soup was convection: hot broth rose, cooled at the top, sank at the sides, and carried heat around in a loop. His face was radiation: heat crossing the empty air as rays, the way sunlight crosses space.',
    'His aunt handed him an oven mitt and a wooden spoon. Some materials let heat pass quickly, like metal. Others slow it down, like wood, cloth and plastic. Those are insulators, which is why good pans have wooden or plastic handles.',
    'That night the metal cup on the table felt colder than the wooden bowl beside it, though both had sat in the same cool air for hours. They were the same temperature. The metal just pulled heat out of his fingers faster. Cold, his aunt said, is only heat leaving.',
    'Heat always moves from warmer to cooler, and it has three roads. Conduction by touch, convection by a moving liquid or gas, radiation by rays that need nothing to travel through. One campfire shows all three in a minute.',
  ],
};
STORIES['plate-tectonics'] = {
  about: 'two rugs on a living room floor, and the whole surface of the Earth',
  more: [{ serial: 'S666', after: 1, alt: 'The two rugs pulled apart, bare floor showing in the gap' }, { serial: 'S667', after: 2, alt: 'The Earth\'s shell drawn as plates on a globe, arrows pushing them together and apart' }, { serial: 'S2093', after: 3, alt: 'South America and Africa drawn as two puzzle pieces with the same small reptile fossil on each' }, { serial: 'S2094', after: 4, alt: 'Snowy Himalayan peaks beside the crack of the San Andreas Fault running through dry California hills' }, { serial: 'S2095', after: 5, alt: 'The rugs sliding past each other, one catching and jerking' }],
  title: 'Two rugs', art: 'S665', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a living room floor pushing two thick rugs together so they buckle into a ridge',
  words: [
    'Kai\'s teacher said mountains were made by the ground moving, and Kai laughed. The ground did not move. He had stood on it his whole life and it had never gone anywhere.',
    'That night he pushed two rugs together on the living room floor and they buckled into a ridge. He pulled them apart and the bare floor showed in the gap. He slid them past each other and they caught, then jerked. He sat back on his heels and stopped laughing.',
    'The Earth\'s outer shell is not one piece. It is plates, like rugs, riding on hot rock that flows very slowly, and moving a few centimeters a year. Push two together and they buckle: mountains. Pull them apart and new floor rises into the gap: a rift, or the middle of an ocean. Slide them past each other and they catch and jerk: an earthquake.',
    'His teacher showed him the proof people first noticed. The coast of South America fits into the coast of Africa like two puzzle pieces. And fossils of the same small ancient reptile, Mesosaurus, are found on both, though it could never have swum the ocean between them.',
    'The pushing has not stopped. India is still pressing into Asia, and the Himalayas, the tallest mountains on Earth, are still rising a few millimeters a year. In California two plates slide past each other along the San Andreas Fault, and every earthquake there is one of those jerks.',
    'The Earth\'s shell is plates that move. Pushing together makes mountains, pulling apart makes new floor, sliding past makes earthquakes. The ground does move. It just moves slower than a boy on a rug can feel.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ecosystems'] = {
  about: 'grass, a grasshopper, a bird and a hawk, and the sunlight that traveled through all of them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S669', after: 1, alt: 'Sunlight falling on grass, the grass glowing faintly green' }, { serial: 'S670', after: 2, alt: 'Mushrooms and fungi on a fallen log at the edge of the meadow' }, { serial: 'S2099', after: 3, alt: 'A pyramid drawn in a notebook: many grass blades at the bottom, one hawk at the top' }, { serial: 'S2100', after: 4, alt: 'A food web drawn on a page: grass, grasshopper, lizard, mouse, bird and hawk joined by arrows' }, { serial: 'S2101', after: 5, alt: 'A hawk perched on a fence post at sunset above a glowing meadow' }],
  title: 'Follow the energy', art: 'S668', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a meadow watching a grasshopper on a blade of grass, a bird on a fence and a hawk circling above',
  words: [
    'Maya watched a hawk circle a meadow and wondered what a hawk was made of. Birds, she supposed. And birds were made of grasshoppers. And grasshoppers were made of grass. So was a hawk made of grass? That felt wrong and right at the same time.',
    'She followed it back one more step. What was grass made of? Dirt? Water? She dug up a blade and it did not look like dirt at all. Something else had built it.',
    'Sunlight had. Grass catches light and makes food from it, which makes grass a producer. The grasshopper eats the grass, the bird eats the grasshopper, the hawk eats the bird, and every one of them is a consumer, passing the sun\'s energy up the line. When the bird dies, fungi and bacteria break it down and return it to the soil: the decomposers. Then the grass grows again.',
    'Her teacher added a catch. Energy passes up the chain, but only about a tenth of it at each link; the rest is used for living or lost as heat. That is why a meadow has millions of grass blades, thousands of grasshoppers, dozens of birds and one hawk.',
    'And the chain is really a web. The grasshopper is also eaten by a lizard, the hawk also hunts mice, and the mice eat seeds from the same grass. Take one strand away, and the others feel it.',
    'Producers make food, consumers eat, and decomposers break things down and return what they held to the soil. Energy flows from the sun through everything alive, one link at a time, and most of it is spent along the way. A hawk really is made of sunlight, with several stops along the way.',
  ],
};
STORIES['density'] = {
  about: 'two boxes the same size, one of feathers and one of rocks, and the question of which one sinks',
  more: [{ serial: 'S672', after: 1, alt: 'A boy on a dock holding a heavy box in one arm and a light one in the other, his arm aching' }, { serial: 'S673', after: 2, alt: 'The rock box sinking below the surface while the feather box floats' }, { serial: 'S2102', after: 3, alt: 'A kitchen scale with a wooden block and a stone of the same size beside it' }, { serial: 'S2103', after: 4, alt: 'A tall glass with honey, water and oil settled in three layers' }, { serial: 'S2104', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook with a fraction drawn: mass on top, volume on the bottom' }],
  title: 'Feathers and rocks', art: 'S671', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a dock holding two identical boxes over the water, one labeled by a drawn feather, one by a drawn rock',
  words: [
    'Theo had two boxes exactly the same size. One was packed with feathers and one with rocks. His cousin bet him that size was what made things sink, and that two boxes the same size would do the same thing in the water.',
    'Theo was not sure. The boxes were the same size, that was true. But one of them made his arms ache and the other felt empty. Could the water tell the difference?',
    'He dropped them in. The feather box floated. The rock box sank straight down. Same space, different mass. The rock box was denser: more stuff packed into the same volume. Water has its own density, and anything denser than water goes under, whatever its size.',
    'At home he weighed a wooden block and a stone of the same size, fifty cubic centimeters each. The wood was 30 grams: 0.6 grams in every cubic centimeter. The stone was 130 grams: 2.6 grams in every one. Water is 1. The wood floated and the stone sank, just as the numbers said.',
    'Then he poured honey, water and oil into one glass. They settled into layers, the densest at the bottom: honey, then water, then oil on top. Liquids have densities too, and they sort themselves.',
    'Density is mass divided by volume. Denser than water sinks; less dense floats, and size has nothing to do with it. A steel ship floats because its whole hull, air and all, is less dense than the water it pushes aside.',
  ],
};
STORIES['argument-with-evidence'] = {
  about: 'a school that needed more water fountains, and the numbers that finally made anyone listen',
  more: [{ serial: 'S675', after: 0, alt: 'Two water fountains in a long school hallway, a line of students waiting' }, { serial: 'S676', after: 1, alt: 'A boy frowning at a short reply letter that says everyone gets thirsty' }, { serial: 'S2147', after: 2, alt: 'A nurse\'s log book open on a desk, a column of tally marks' }, { serial: 'S2148', after: 3, alt: 'The boy presenting a chart to a principal who is nodding' }, { serial: 'S2149', after: 4, alt: 'A board with trust the source written beside a nurse log and a question mark' }],
  title: 'Show the numbers', art: 'S674', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a school water fountain with a clipboard, a line of thirsty students behind him',
  words: [
    'Diego\'s school had two water fountains for six hundred students, and he wanted more. He wrote to the principal: we need more fountains because students get thirsty. The principal wrote back: everyone gets thirsty. Nothing changed.',
    'Diego was angry. His reason was true. Students did get thirsty. But true was not the same as convincing, and he did not know what was missing. He read the letter three times looking for it.',
    'His science teacher asked one question. How do you know? Diego went to the nurse. She had logged forty headaches last month, most in the afternoon, most from students who had not had water since lunch. He wrote it again: claim, reason, and now evidence, the nurse\'s forty.',
    'He also answered the principal\'s worry before she could raise it. Fountains cost money, he wrote, but forty afternoon trips to the nurse cost class time every month. Naming the other side and answering it is called a counterclaim and a rebuttal. The principal ordered two fountains.',
    'His teacher added one more rule. Evidence counts most when a reader can trust where it came from. The nurse\'s log was better than a guess, and far better than a friend saying so. A number is only as good as the person who counted it.',
    'An argument is a claim, reasons, and evidence for each reason. A reason without evidence is only a feeling, however true it happens to be. Show the numbers, and a feeling becomes a case.',
  ],
};
STORIES['compare-and-contrast'] = {
  about: 'two river towns side by side, and what one bridge revealed',
  more: [{ serial: 'S678', after: 0, alt: 'A notebook page where both have a market is written twice and nothing else' }, { serial: 'S679', after: 1, alt: 'A ferry crossing the river toward the second town' }, { serial: 'S2150', after: 2, alt: 'A market square in the first town, busy with stalls' }, { serial: 'S2151', after: 3, alt: 'Two ways to lay out an essay drawn side by side: two big blocks, and a row of matched pairs' }, { serial: 'S2152', after: 4, alt: 'A Venn diagram of two towns, a shared market in the middle' }],
  title: 'Two towns', art: 'S677', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl standing on a riverbank between two small towns, a bridge on one side and a ferry on the other',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s assignment was to compare two towns on the same river. She wrote that both had a market and both sat on the water. Then she ran out of things to say. They were the same, and the same is boring to write about.',
    'Her father asked how she got from one to the other. The first town had a bridge. The second had a ferry that ran twice a day. Rosa wrote that down as a difference and still could not see why anyone would care.',
    'Then she thought about what the difference did. The bridge town had a market every day, because farmers could cross whenever they liked. The ferry town had a market twice a week, when the ferry ran. The alike part set up the question. The different part answered it. And what it showed was that a river crossing shapes a whole town\'s week.',
    'Her teacher showed her two ways to lay it out. Block by block: everything about the first town, then everything about the second. Or point by point: markets in both towns, then crossings in both towns. Point by point kept the differences side by side, where a reader could see them.',
    'Rosa drew a Venn diagram first: two overlapping circles, the shared things in the middle, each town\'s own things on its side. Then she wrote with signal words, both and similarly for the middle, while and however for the sides.',
    'Compare by saying how things are alike, how they differ, and what the difference shows. The first two are lists. The third is the point, and it is the only part a reader remembers.',
  ],
};
STORIES['narrative-with-dialogue'] = {
  about: 'two lines of talk that told a reader more than a page of description',
  more: [{ serial: 'S681', after: 0, alt: 'A boy frowning at a full page of description with a note that says not yet believable' }, { serial: 'S682', after: 2, alt: 'A page of description crossed out, two short lines of dialogue written beneath it' }, { serial: 'S2153', after: 3, alt: 'A page of dialogue where each new speaker starts a new paragraph, the indents marked in pencil' }, { serial: 'S2154', after: 4, alt: 'A girl checking her watch at a bus stop while a boy arrives out of breath' }, { serial: 'S2155', after: 5, alt: 'The two characters seen close up, one annoyed, one sheepish' }],
  title: 'Let them talk', art: 'S680', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy writing a page about two characters, a girl and a boy at a bus stop drawn above it with speech bubbles',
  words: [
    'Kai wrote a whole page describing his two characters. She was strict and always on time. He was easygoing and always late. His teacher read it and said she did not believe either of them yet.',
    'Not believe them? He had explained exactly what they were like. But explaining, it turned out, was the problem. The reader was being told, and readers do not trust being told.',
    'He cut the page and let them talk. "You\'re late," she said. "The bus was early," he said. Two lines, and a reader knew them both: her sharpness, his shrug. The dialogue also moved the story, because now they had somewhere to be and a problem getting there. Quotation marks around the words, a comma before the tag, and the characters did the rest.',
    'His teacher showed him one more rule of the page: every time a new person speaks, start a new paragraph. That way a reader always knows who is talking, even when the tags drop away.',
    'Then Kai tried an action instead of a tag. She checked her watch. "You\'re late." The watch did the work of said, and it showed how impatient she was. He kept a few saids too; readers barely notice them, which is exactly why they work.',
    'Dialogue moves the story and shows character faster than description ever can. Put quotation marks around the spoken words, a comma before the tag, and let people reveal themselves by what they say.',
  ],
};
STORIES['maps-and-hemispheres'] = {
  about: 'an orange with lines drawn on it, and the girl who finally understood where she was',
  more: [{ serial: 'S684', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning at a homework page asking for latitude and longitude' }, { serial: 'S685', after: 1, alt: 'The orange with rings drawn around it from the middle, like belts' }, { serial: 'S2126', after: 2, alt: 'The orange with lines from top to bottom, like segments of a peel' }, { serial: 'S2127', after: 3, alt: 'The orange cut in half at the belt and then in half from top to bottom, four hemispheres in a row' }, { serial: 'S2128', after: 4, alt: 'A pin in a map of Texas at about 30 degrees north and 98 degrees west' }],
  title: 'The orange globe', art: 'S683', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl drawing rings and segments on an orange with a marker at a kitchen table',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s homework asked for the latitude and longitude of her town, and both numbers meant nothing to her. Lines on a globe. Why did a round world need lines at all, and why two kinds?',
    'Her grandmother handed her an orange and a marker. Draw a belt around the middle, she said. Maya did. That was the equator. Now draw more belts above it and below it. Those were latitude, and they told you how far north or south you stood.',
    'Then lines from the top to the bottom, like the segments of a peel. Those were longitude, counted east or west from one line everyone had agreed on. Where a belt crossed a segment, you had one spot on the orange, and one spot on the Earth. Seven continents and five oceans sat somewhere on that grid, and so did Maya\'s town.',
    'Her grandmother cut the orange along the belt: two halves, north and south, the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. Then she cut along the agreed line and the one opposite it: east and west. Every place on Earth sits in one northern or southern half and one eastern or western half.',
    'Maya found her own town at last. Latitude runs from 0 at the equator to 90 at each pole, and longitude from 0 to 180 east or west. Austin sits at about 30 degrees north and 98 degrees west, in the Northern and Western Hemispheres.',
    'Latitude measures north or south of the equator; longitude measures east or west of the prime meridian. Two numbers name any place on the planet. The world has seven continents and five oceans, and every one of them has an address.',
  ],
};
STORIES['what-culture-is'] = {
  about: 'everything a newcomer would have to be taught, and how long the list turned out to be',
  more: [{ serial: 'S687', after: 0, alt: 'A short list on a whiteboard: rules and where the bathroom is' }, { serial: 'S688', after: 1, alt: 'Students at lunch learning what to eat and how to eat it' }, { serial: 'S2129', after: 2, alt: 'A whiteboard crowded from edge to edge with words, customs and holidays' }, { serial: 'S2130', after: 3, alt: 'A pizza on a table with a small map showing its path from Italy across the world' }, { serial: 'S2131', after: 5, alt: 'Food, music and words traveling with people who move, drawn as a road between two towns' }],
  title: 'What we would have to teach', art: 'S686', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a whiteboard filling a long list, a new student watching with a friendly, puzzled face',
  words: [
    'A new student was coming from another country, and Theo\'s class was asked to list what she would need to know. Theo thought it would be a short list. The classroom rules. Where the bathroom was. Done.',
    'Then the list started growing. The words for things. What to eat and how to eat it. Which holidays the school kept. What was polite and what was rude. How close to stand, when to shake hands, what everyone already knew without being told. By the end it filled the board and spilled onto the next one.',
    'That whole board was culture: the way of life a group shares. Language, religion, customs, food, arts and government, and the thousand small habits underneath them. Theo had never seen his own culture because he had never had to explain it. The list was the first time it had stood still long enough to look at.',
    'Cultures also borrow from each other. Pizza came from Italy and is now eaten nearly everywhere. Words travel too: kindergarten came into English from German. When a custom, a food or an idea spreads from one group to another, geographers call it cultural diffusion.',
    'The new student taught the class some things as well: a greeting from her country, a game from her old school, a dish she brought to lunch. By spring some of those had become part of the classroom too. Culture is never finished; it keeps picking things up.',
    'Culture is the way of life a group shares, and it travels: with people who move, with food, with music, with words. Everyone has one, and the quickest way to see yours is to imagine teaching it to someone who was not born into it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['kinds-of-government'] = {
  about: 'a family fight over the remote, and the five ways a country decides who decides',
  more: [{ serial: 'S690', after: 0, alt: 'A brother holding the remote all night while the family watches' }, { serial: 'S691', after: 1, alt: 'One person alone holding the remote, everyone else watching' }, { serial: 'S2132', after: 2, alt: 'Five small drawings of the remote: a crown, a fist, a circle of hands, a religious leader and a raised vote' }, { serial: 'S2133', after: 3, alt: 'A king waving from a balcony while lawmakers debate in a hall below' }, { serial: 'S2134', after: 4, alt: 'The whole family raising hands to vote on a show' }],
  title: 'The remote', art: 'S689', cast: [],
  alt: 'A family on a couch, one child holding the television remote high while everyone else reaches for it',
  words: [
    'In Nadia\'s house the remote was power. Whoever held it decided what everyone watched. On Monday her older brother kept it all night. On Tuesday her parents took it back. On Wednesday there was a vote. Nadia thought it was just a family. Her civics teacher said it was a whole unit.',
    'How could a remote be a government? Nadia laughed. Then the teacher asked one question about every government they had studied, and it was the same question. Who decides?',
    'One person holds the remote for life because of who their parents were: a monarchy. One person grabs it and will not let go: a dictatorship. A small circle passes it among themselves: an oligarchy. Religious leaders decide what is watched: a theocracy. Everyone votes on the channel: a democracy. Five governments, one remote.',
    'Some countries mix them. The United Kingdom has a king, but elected lawmakers make the laws; the king has little real power. That is a constitutional monarchy, a crown held inside the rules.',
    'Democracies differ too. When everyone votes on every question, as the family did on Wednesday, it is a direct democracy. When people elect representatives to vote for them, as Americans do in Congress, it is a representative democracy.',
    'Governments differ by who decides: the people, a monarch, one dictator, a small group or religious leaders. The rest of the differences follow from that one answer, and the question works on any country and any couch.',
  ],
};
STORIES['kinds-of-economies'] = {
  about: 'who decides what gets made, from a grandmother\'s loom to a shopping mall',
  more: [{ serial: 'S693', after: 0, alt: 'A grandmother weaving at a loom in a village, the same cloth her mother wove' }, { serial: 'S694', after: 1, alt: 'A girl looking between three buildings, unable to choose which is right' }, { serial: 'S2135', after: 2, alt: 'A busy market where buyers and sellers bargain over prices' }, { serial: 'S2136', after: 3, alt: 'A girl with one coin choosing between a book and a movie ticket' }, { serial: 'S2137', after: 4, alt: 'A notebook with 50 billion divided by 10 million equals 5,000 written out' }],
  title: 'Who decides', art: 'S692', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a busy market street with three shops in a row, a traditional loom, a government office and a bustling stall',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s grandmother wove the same cloth her mother had woven, because that was what her village had always made. Rosa\'s uncle lived in a country where the government told factories what to build each year. Rosa lived where shops sold whatever people would buy. Three families, three worlds. Which one was right?',
    'Rosa could not decide, and her teacher said that was the point. None of them was simply right. Each one was an answer to one question, and it was the same question every time.',
    'Who decides what gets made? In a traditional economy, custom and the elders decide. In a command economy, the government decides. In a market economy, buyers and sellers decide by what they buy and sell. Most real countries mix them, and the size of an economy is measured by GDP per person: everything made in a year, shared out across everyone living there.',
    'The question exists because of scarcity: there is never enough of everything for everyone. Rosa had money for a book or a movie, not both. Whatever she gave up to get the other was its opportunity cost. Every economy is a way of making choices like that for a whole country.',
    'Her teacher did one with numbers. A country whose economy makes 50 billion dollars in a year and has 10 million people has a GDP per person of 5,000 dollars. It is an average, so it cannot show whether the money is shared evenly or held by a few.',
    'Economies differ by who decides: custom, the government, the market, or a mix. GDP per person is GDP divided by the population, and it tells you how much economy each person has to live on. The loom, the office and the mall are three answers to one question.',
  ],
};
STORIES['people-on-the-move'] = {
  about: 'a family that left one place for another, and the two forces that did the moving',
  more: [{ serial: 'S696', after: 0, alt: 'A boy of seven looking back at a small town from a train window' }, { serial: 'S697', after: 1, alt: 'A closed factory gate in a quiet town, a sign hanging crooked' }, { serial: 'S2138', after: 2, alt: 'A crowded city street with lit shop windows and people everywhere' }, { serial: 'S2139', after: 3, alt: 'A square of city blocks marked with 1,000 people on 4 square miles' }, { serial: 'S2140', after: 4, alt: 'Families carrying bags across a border bridge, leaving a war behind them' }],
  title: 'Push and pull', art: 'S695', cast: [],
  alt: 'A family with suitcases on a train platform, a small dry town behind them and a tall city ahead',
  words: [
    'When Jamal was seven his family moved from a small town to a huge city, and for years he blamed his parents for it. He had liked the town. Nobody had asked him. Then in sixth grade he had to write about why people move, and he asked his mother.',
    'There was no work in the town, she said. The factory had closed. And there was a job in the city. Jamal had assumed it was one decision, but it was two forces, and both of them had been stronger than his liking the town.',
    'Geographers call them push and pull. Push factors drive people out of a place: no work, war, drought, floods. Pull factors draw them somewhere else: jobs, safety, family, land. And where people end up gets crowded: population density is people divided by area, and water, food and work decide it. The city was dense because it pulled.',
    'Jamal worked out an example. A town of 1,000 people on 4 square miles has 250 people per square mile. A city block can hold more people than that on a single street. Density is not about how big a place is. It is about how many share it.',
    'Some moves are chosen and some are not. Families who move for a job choose it. Refugees are pushed out by war or danger with little choice at all. Moving within a country is internal migration; moving to another country is international migration.',
    'People move away from what pushes and toward what pulls. Population density is how many people share each square mile, and it follows water, food and work. Every family that ever moved was pushed by something and pulled by something else.',
  ],
};
STORIES['world-regions-today'] = {
  about: 'a neighborhood on a bigger map, and what makes strangers into neighbors',
  more: [{ serial: 'S699', after: 1, alt: 'A neighborhood seen from above: one street, one school and one corner store' }, { serial: 'S700', after: 2, alt: 'A coastline with several countries along it, all colored the same blue' }, { serial: 'S2141', after: 3, alt: 'A dry grassland belt south of a great desert crossing several countries, shaded as one band' }, { serial: 'S2142', after: 4, alt: 'Highways leading into a city center from many small towns around it' }, { serial: 'S2143', after: 5, alt: 'A mountain range, a landform, crossing several borders and shaded as one region' }],
  title: 'The neighborhood on the map', art: 'S698', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy tracing a region on a large world map with his finger, countries inside it sharing one color',
  words: [
    'The test asked Marcus to name the regions of Africa, and he could not see any. A region was not a country. It had no flag, no border he could find on the map. How could he name something that was not there?',
    'His teacher asked him to think about his own neighborhood. It had no border either. Nobody had drawn a line around it. But everyone in it knew where it started and stopped. What made it a neighborhood?',
    'The neighbors shared something. The same street, the same school, the same corner store. A region is a neighborhood on a bigger map, and its neighbors share something too: a landform, a climate, a language or a history. The countries along one coast are a region. The countries across one desert are another. The shared thing draws the line.',
    'His teacher gave him examples from Africa. The Sahel is a dry belt of grassland just south of the Sahara, crossing many countries that share its rains and its droughts. North Africa shares the Arabic language and much of its history. What the places have in common draws the line of a region.',
    'Some regions are drawn around a center instead. A city and all the towns whose people drive into it for work make one region, even with no shared landform at all. And some regions live mostly in people\'s minds, like the places folks simply call the South.',
    'A region is a place that shares a landform, a climate, a language or a history, and every continent is made of them. Find what the places share, and the region draws itself.',
  ],
};
STORIES['proportions'] = {
  about: 'three pens for sixty cents, and the boy who could not price five until he priced one',
  more: [{ serial: 'S702', after: 0, alt: 'A boy in a school store line counting on his fingers while the cashier waits' }, { serial: 'S703', after: 2, alt: 'One pen alone on the counter with two dimes beside it' }, { serial: 'S2156', after: 3, alt: 'A straight line on a graph from zero through 1 pen at 20 cents and 5 pens at a dollar' }, { serial: 'S2157', after: 4, alt: 'A road sign reading 150 miles in 3 hours beside a measuring cup of rice' }, { serial: 'S2158', after: 5, alt: 'A row of five pens with a dollar under them and a note: find one unit, then multiply' }],
  title: 'The price of one', art: 'S701', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a school store counter holding three pens, a sign showing sixty cents, five pens waiting on the shelf',
  words: [
    'The school store sold three pens for sixty cents, and Marcus wanted five. He tried to work it out in line and got a different answer every time. Sixty for three, so five would be, what, a dollar twenty? Ninety? The cashier waited.',
    'He had the price of three and needed the price of five, and five was not three times anything easy. That was the snag. He was trying to jump straight from three to five.',
    'So he stopped at one. Sixty cents for three pens means twenty cents for one pen. Now five pens was easy: five times twenty, a dollar. He handed it over. And to check, he cross-multiplied: three over sixty equals five over a hundred, since three times a hundred is sixty times five.',
    'His math teacher called twenty cents the unit rate, the price of one, and said it was the constant that ties the two numbers together. Price equals twenty cents times the number of pens. Double the pens, double the price; zero pens, zero cents. That straight-line pattern is what makes a relationship proportional.',
    'Marcus found unit rates everywhere after that. A car that drives 150 miles in 3 hours goes 50 miles in one. A recipe for 4 people that uses 2 cups of rice needs half a cup per person, so 6 people need 3 cups.',
    'In any proportion, find what one unit is worth, then multiply by however many you need. Cross-multiply to check. The price of one is the key that opens the price of anything.',
  ],
};
STORIES['integers'] = {
  about: 'five dollars in a jar, three owed to a sister, and the debt that was forgiven',
  more: [{ serial: 'S705', after: 0, alt: 'A girl holding a jar of five dollars and a note that says three owed' }, { serial: 'S706', after: 1, alt: 'A girl staring at a 5 and a 3 written side by side on paper, puzzled' }, { serial: 'S2162', after: 2, alt: 'A number line with the jar at five and a marker moving from two to five as the note is torn' }, { serial: 'S2163', after: 3, alt: 'A boy with four small notes pinned to a board, each saying three owed' }, { serial: 'S2164', after: 4, alt: 'A family at dinner with a napkin that says negative times negative is positive' }],
  title: 'The debt', art: 'S704', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding a jar with five dollars in it, a note reading three owed pinned beside it, her sister in the doorway',
  words: [
    'Nadia had five dollars in her jar and owed her sister three. So how much did she really have? Five, said her hands. Two, said her conscience. Owing money was like having less than nothing in that spot, and she did not know how to write that.',
    'She tried writing five and three side by side and staring at them. The owing part would not sit still. It was a minus, somehow, and minuses were supposed to make things smaller.',
    'Then her sister forgave the debt. Nadia gained no cash, but she was three dollars better off, because a minus three had been taken away. Before, she was at two: five in the jar plus a minus three. Two minus negative three is five. On a number line, taking away a negative moves you right, and adding a negative moves you left. When a plus and a minus meet, the bigger one wins and keeps its sign.',
    'Her brother had a harder case. He owed three dollars to each of four friends. Four times negative three is negative twelve: twelve dollars in the hole. A positive times a negative is negative. When the friends forgave every debt, he was twelve dollars better off.',
    'Their mother added the last rule at dinner. A negative times a negative is positive, the way taking away four debts of three dollars is the same as gaining twelve. The signs had rules, and the rules matched the money.',
    'Adding a negative moves left; subtracting a negative moves right. When a negative and a positive meet, the difference takes the sign of the larger. Money owed is the number line with feelings, and forgiveness is subtracting a negative.',
  ],
};
STORIES['two-step-equations'] = {
  about: 'socks, shoes and the order you take them off, and the boy who used it on an equation',
  more: [{ serial: 'S708', after: 0, alt: 'A boy frowning at an equation tangled with fractions after dividing first' }, { serial: 'S709', after: 2, alt: 'The equation 2x + 3 = 11 with the plus 3 circled as the last thing done' }, { serial: 'S2165', after: 3, alt: 'A bike rental sign reading three dollars plus two dollars an hour' }, { serial: 'S2166', after: 4, alt: 'A number line with a closed dot at four and shading down to zero' }, { serial: 'S2167', after: 5, alt: 'The steps peeled back: 2x = 8, then x = 4' }],
  title: 'Shoes then socks', art: 'S707', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a hallway pulling off shoes before socks, an equation drawn faintly on the wall behind him',
  words: [
    'Two x plus three equals eleven. Diego wanted to divide by two first, because the two came first, and got a mess with fractions in it. He tried subtracting three from just one side and the equation stopped being true. He put his pencil down.',
    'The problem had two steps hiding in it, and he did not know which to undo first. Undoing in the wrong order was like taking off socks before shoes.',
    'That was the trick. Whatever was done to x last is undone first. Someone had multiplied x by two, then added three. The plus three came last, so it went first: subtract three from both sides, and two x equals eight. Then undo the multiplying: divide both sides by two, and x is four. Two fours plus three is eleven. Shoes off, then socks.',
    'His teacher gave him a real one. A bike rental costs three dollars plus two dollars an hour, and Diego had eleven dollars. How many hours? Two h plus three equals eleven, the same equation in a new coat. Four hours.',
    'Then she changed a few words: at most eleven dollars. Two h plus three is less than or equal to eleven, an inequality, and it undoes the same way. Subtract three, divide by two: h is at most four. Any number of hours from zero to four would do.',
    'Undo the steps in reverse order: the last thing done to x is the first thing you undo, and you do it to both sides. Then put the answer back in and check. Any equation with two steps is just shoes and socks.',
  ],
};
STORIES['circles'] = {
  about: 'a string wrapped around a can, and the number that was hiding in every circle',
  more: [{ serial: 'S711', after: 0, alt: 'A girl wrapping a string around a soup can and marking where it meets' }, { serial: 'S712', after: 1, alt: 'The string laid straight next to three cans and a little extra' }, { serial: 'S2168', after: 2, alt: 'A circle with its diameter drawn across and its radius drawn from the center' }, { serial: 'S2169', after: 3, alt: 'A paper label 23.6 centimeters long with a small tab for glue' }, { serial: 'S2170', after: 4, alt: 'A 12-inch pizza beside a 16-inch pizza, the bigger one nearly twice the area' }],
  title: 'The string and the can', art: 'S710', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl wrapping a string around a soup can, then laying the string flat beside three cans in a row',
  words: [
    'Lena needed the distance around a soup can for a label, and a ruler would not bend. She wrapped a string around it, marked where it met, and laid the string flat. Then she wondered how many cans across the string was.',
    'She lined up cans. Three cans across, and a little bit more. She tried a mug. Three mugs across, and a little bit more. A plate. Three plates, and the same little bit. The little bit would not go away.',
    'It never does. The distance around any circle is about three and a bit times the distance across it, and the bit is always the same: 3.14, called pi. So circumference is 3.14 times the diameter. And the space inside, the area, is 3.14 times the radius times the radius. The radius is half the diameter. Lena cut her label to length.',
    'Her soup can was 7.5 centimeters across, so the label needed 3.14 times 7.5, about 23.6 centimeters, plus a little overlap for the glue. The string had said the same thing, a bit less exactly.',
    'Her brother asked about pizza. A 12-inch pizza has a radius of 6, and 3.14 times 36 is about 113 square inches. A 16-inch pizza has a radius of 8: 3.14 times 64, about 201 square inches. Four more inches across gave nearly twice as much pizza. Pi itself never ends, 3.14159 and on forever, but 3.14 is close enough for labels and pizza.',
    'Circumference is 3.14 times the diameter. Area is 3.14 times the radius squared. Every circle carries the same hidden number, and a string around a can is where most people meet it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['authors-purpose'] = {
  about: 'three pages from the same newspaper, and the shape that told a girl what each one wanted',
  more: [{ serial: 'S714', after: 0, alt: 'A girl reading three pages of a Sunday paper at breakfast' }, { serial: 'S715', after: 1, alt: 'A weather report page, all numbers and dates, next to an advertisement with bold words' }, { serial: 'S2207', after: 2, alt: 'A comic strip panel with a small plot, the girl laughing' }, { serial: 'S2208', after: 3, alt: 'A cereal box with its vitamins in small print under a big slogan' }, { serial: 'S2209', after: 4, alt: 'Two pages about the same mattress, an ad from the company and a review from a magazine that tests beds' }],
  title: 'Three pages', art: 'S713', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a breakfast table with a newspaper spread open to a weather report, an advertisement and a comic strip',
  words: [
    'Nadia read three pages of the Sunday paper in a row. A weather report. An advertisement for a mattress. A comic strip. Her teacher asked what each writer wanted from her, and Nadia said, to be read. That was true and useless.',
    'She looked again for what else they might want. The pages felt different in her hands, but she could not say how, and the words on each were just words.',
    'Then she noticed the shape. The weather report was facts and dates, laid out to be checked: it wanted to inform. The advertisement was full of should, must and best: it wanted to persuade. The comic had a setup and a punch line: it wanted to entertain. Three pages, three purposes, and each shape told her which.',
    'Her teacher added a warning. Many pages mix purposes. A cereal box lists its vitamins, which informs, in order to make you buy it, which persuades. The main purpose is the one the rest of the page serves, and on the cereal box that is selling.',
    'She also learned to ask who wrote it. The mattress ad came from the company selling the mattress. A review of the same mattress by a magazine that tests beds might still persuade, but it had less reason to tilt the facts.',
    'Inform, persuade or entertain. The shape of the writing tells you which, and the purpose tells you how to read it: check the facts, doubt the pitch, enjoy the story. Ask what the writer wants before you decide what to believe.',
  ],
};
STORIES['evidence-quality'] = {
  about: 'one friend who swore by a vitamin, and the ten thousand people who did not',
  more: [{ serial: 'S717', after: 0, alt: 'The friend, hand on heart, telling his one story' }, { serial: 'S718', after: 1, alt: 'A boy frowning between a friend\'s story and a thick printed study that disagree' }, { serial: 'S2210', after: 2, alt: 'A page of a study with a bar chart of ten thousand people' }, { serial: 'S2211', after: 3, alt: 'Two groups of people holding identical pill bottles, one bottle secretly holding a look-alike pill' }, { serial: 'S2212', after: 4, alt: 'A sticky note in a wallet that says happening together is not the same as causing' }],
  title: 'One story against ten thousand', art: 'S716', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a pharmacy aisle holding a bottle of vitamins, a friend talking beside him, a thick study printed on a shelf tag',
  words: [
    'Marcus\'s friend swore a vitamin had cured his colds. Every winter, not one cold. Marcus was ready to spend his allowance on a bottle, because his friend was honest and the story was true.',
    'Then he found a study of ten thousand people that found the vitamin did not prevent colds. Now he had two true things that disagreed. His friend was not lying. Neither was the study. How could both be true?',
    'They could, because they were different kinds of evidence. His friend was one story: one person, one winter, no way to check what else changed. The study was many cases from a checkable source, counted and compared. One honest story is a start. Ten thousand counted cases are evidence. He kept his allowance.',
    'His teacher pointed out what the study had that the story did not: a comparison group. Half the people took the vitamin and half took a look-alike pill with nothing in it, and neither group knew which. Only then could the study tell whether the vitamin, and not something else, made the difference.',
    'His friend\'s winter might have gone well for other reasons: fewer sick classmates, more sleep, luck. When two things happen together, one did not necessarily cause the other. Marcus wrote that on a sticky note for his wallet.',
    'Many cases from a checkable source beat one story or a feeling, however sincere. Weak evidence does not make a claim false, but it does not make it true either. Ask how many, and who counted.',
  ],
};
STORIES['connotation'] = {
  about: 'three words that weighed the same on a scale and felt nothing alike',
  more: [{ serial: 'S720', after: 0, alt: 'Two friends walking home in silence after a careless word' }, { serial: 'S721', after: 1, alt: 'Three words on a kitchen scale, all the same weight' }, { serial: 'S2213', after: 2, alt: 'Three words, slim, thin and skinny, each carrying a different small feeling' }, { serial: 'S2214', after: 3, alt: 'The same idea said two ways, curious and nosy, with two different faces' }, { serial: 'S2215', after: 4, alt: 'An apartment ad that says cozy and a coach calling a player determined' }],
  title: 'Slim, thin, skinny', art: 'S719', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a clothing store with three price tags reading three different words, a friend beside her frowning at one',
  words: [
    'Rosa called her friend skinny and her friend went quiet for the rest of the walk home. Rosa had meant it kindly. Skinny meant thin, and thin meant slim, and slim was a compliment. Where had it gone wrong?',
    'She lined the words up. Slim. Thin. Skinny. On a scale they meant the same thing. She would have sworn they were interchangeable.',
    'They were not, because words carry more than their meaning. Slim comes with approval. Thin is plain. Skinny carries a little worry, or a little scorn. That extra weight is connotation, the feeling a word carries beyond what it says. Swap a word for a plainer one, and notice what disappears. That is the connotation.',
    'Her teacher gave the plain meaning a name too, denotation: what a dictionary says. Curious and nosy share a denotation, wanting to know things, but curious sounds like a compliment and nosy like a complaint. House and home name the same building, and only one of them has a family in it.',
    'Rosa started hearing connotation everywhere. An ad called a small apartment cozy. A coach called a stubborn player determined. Choosing the warm word or the cold one was how writers, and friends, took a side without saying so.',
    'Connotation is the feeling a word carries beyond its meaning. Writers choose it on purpose, and readers feel it whether they notice or not. Rosa apologized the next day, with a better word.',
  ],
};
STORIES['character-motive'] = {
  about: 'a girl in a novel who lied to her brother, and the reader who worked out why',
  more: [{ serial: 'S723', after: 0, alt: 'A boy about to close a novel in a library, frowning' }, { serial: 'S724', after: 1, alt: 'The girl hiding the letter behind her back, under pressure, as her brother walks in' }, { serial: 'S2216', after: 2, alt: 'A notebook page with two headings: wants and fears' }, { serial: 'S2217', after: 3, alt: 'A page of a novel with actions underlined and a margin note: shown, not told' }, { serial: 'S2218', after: 4, alt: 'A brother and sister reading a bank letter together at a kitchen table' }],
  title: 'What she wanted and what she feared', art: 'S722', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy reading a novel in a library, a drawn figure of a girl beside him with two thought bubbles, one bright and one dark',
  words: [
    'In the novel, the girl lied to her brother about the letter from the bank. Kai stopped reading. She had been honest for two hundred pages. Why now? The author never said, and Kai wanted to close the book.',
    'His teacher asked him to look not at what the girl said but at what she did under pressure. She hid the letter. She took a second job. She stopped sleeping.',
    'Kai wrote two columns. What she wanted: her brother\'s respect. What she feared: losing the house. The lie sat right where those two met. She could not tell him without losing the first, and she could not let him find out without the second coming true. Nobody had told Kai her motive. Her actions had.',
    'His teacher gave him a name for what the author had done: indirect characterization. The author never wrote she is afraid. She showed it through the girl\'s actions, her thoughts, what she said and how others reacted to her. Direct characterization just tells you; indirect makes you work it out.',
    'Near the end the girl told her brother the truth, and he helped her keep the house. She had changed, and a character who changes over a story is called dynamic. The motive had not vanished; she had found a way to satisfy both columns.',
    'Motive is what a character wants or fears, and it is shown by what they do, especially under pressure. Predict a character by their motive, not by their last line of dialogue, and the book stops surprising you for the wrong reasons.',
  ],
};
STORIES['body-systems'] = {
  about: 'a bite of an apple, and the whole city inside that had to work together to use it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S726', after: 0, alt: 'A boy biting an apple while his teacher asks what happens next' }, { serial: 'S727', after: 1, alt: 'A boy tracing the path of a bite on a body diagram, stopping at a question mark after the stomach' }, { serial: 'S2186', after: 2, alt: 'The city map: roads for blood vessels, a kitchen for the stomach, a power plant for the lungs' }, { serial: 'S2187', after: 3, alt: 'Trucks on the roads carrying supplies from the kitchen to every building' }, { serial: 'S2188', after: 4, alt: 'A boy sweating at recess, a heart drawn beating faster beside him' }],
  title: 'The city inside', art: 'S725', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy biting an apple, a faint drawing of a city map with roads and buildings glowing over his chest',
  words: [
    'Jamal bit into an apple and thought nothing of it. His science teacher asked him to think of everything. What had to happen for one bite to become part of his body?',
    'He said, you swallow it. She waited. He said, then it goes to your stomach. She was still waiting, and Jamal realized he did not actually know what happened next.',
    'Think of a city. The stomach and intestines are the kitchen, breaking the bite into pieces the body can use. The heart and blood vessels are the roads and trucks, carrying those pieces to every building. The lungs are the air supply. The brain and nerves are the phone lines running it all. Every system is a team of organs with one main job, and one bite needs all of them.',
    'The kitchen does more than chop. The stomach churns the bite with acid, and the small intestine, a tube longer than Jamal is tall, pulls the sugars and other nutrients into the blood. Then the nutrients and the oxygen carried by red blood cells from the lungs ride the roads together to every muscle.',
    'The city also keeps itself steady. When Jamal ran at recess and got hot, his skin sweated to cool him and his heart sped up to deliver more oxygen. The body is always adjusting to stay balanced, and scientists call that homeostasis.',
    'Each body system is a team of organs with one job, and the systems depend on each other. One bite of an apple is a delivery to a whole city, and the city works only because no system works alone.',
  ],
};
STORIES['weather-systems'] = {
  about: 'air that piles up and slides like water on a slope, and the girl who saw the wind before it arrived',
  more: [{ serial: 'S729', after: 0, alt: 'A wind sock on a hilltop pointing toward approaching storm clouds' }, { serial: 'S730', after: 2, alt: 'Cool heavy air drawn as a hill of blue, warm air as a valley of red' }, { serial: 'S2192', after: 3, alt: 'A lake beach on a summer afternoon, a cool breeze drawn blowing off the water toward warm land' }, { serial: 'S2193', after: 4, alt: 'Two air masses meeting along a line of tall clouds' }, { serial: 'S2194', after: 5, alt: 'A weather map with a blue cold front line and a red warm front line' }],
  title: 'The slope of the air', art: 'S728', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a hilltop watching a wall of clouds approach, her hair blowing toward the storm',
  words: [
    'From the hilltop Priya watched a line of clouds come across the valley, and felt the wind pick up before they arrived. Her uncle said the wind was blowing toward the storm. That seemed backward. Should the storm not push the wind ahead of it?',
    'She could not see air, so she could not see what was pushing what. All she had was the feeling on her face and a line of clouds getting closer.',
    'Her uncle told her to think of air as water on a slope. Cool air is heavy and piles up: high pressure. Warm air is light and rises: low pressure. The heavy air slides toward the light, and that sliding is wind. The storm was a place where warm air was rising, so the wind ran toward it. And the line of clouds was a front, where two air masses met and one shoved the other up.',
    'The sun sets it all going. It heats the ground unevenly, land faster than water, dark fields faster than pale sand, so some air warms and rises while other air stays cool. On a summer afternoon near a lake, the breeze blows in off the cool water toward the warm land.',
    'Fronts come in kinds. A cold front, cold air pushing under warm air like a shovel, brings tall clouds and short, hard storms, with cooler air behind them. A warm front, warm air sliding up over cold air, brings long, gentle rain. Priya\'s line of tall clouds was a cold front.',
    'Warm air rises and cool air sinks; wind blows from high pressure to low. A front is where two air masses meet, and that is where the weather happens. Feel the wind on a hill and you can read the sky.',
  ],
};
STORIES['natural-selection'] = {
  about: 'pale moths on a tree that turned sooty, and the color change that no moth ever made',
  more: [{ serial: 'S732', after: 0, alt: 'A boy reading a book about moths in an old factory town' }, { serial: 'S733', after: 1, alt: 'A bird plucking a pale moth from a dark trunk' }, { serial: 'S2195', after: 2, alt: 'A row of dark moths on the trunk a few years later, one pale one left' }, { serial: 'S2196', after: 3, alt: 'Pale trunks with lichen again, pale moths hidden on them and a dark moth easy to spot' }, { serial: 'S2197', after: 4, alt: 'A dish in a lab where a few germs survive a medicine and multiply' }],
  title: 'The moths', art: 'S731', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy looking at moths on a soot-darkened tree trunk, one pale moth easy to spot, dark ones almost invisible',
  words: [
    'Theo read that the moths near an old factory town had turned dark over a hundred years, and he pictured moths going darker the way skin tans. His teacher said no moth had ever changed color in its life. Theo said then how did they turn dark?',
    'She showed him a photo of a tree trunk gone black with soot, and a pale moth sitting on it like a light on a hill. Birds eat moths. Which one would a bird see first?',
    'The pale one. On sooty trees, pale moths were easy to see and got eaten. Dark moths hid and lived, and they had dark children, because color is inherited. Year after year, more dark moths, fewer pale. Nobody changed. The group changed, because the ones who fit the place had the most children.',
    'Then the air was cleaned up. After laws cut the soot in the second half of the twentieth century, the trunks grew pale with lichen again, the pale moths came back, and the dark ones became rare. The same rule ran in reverse.',
    'The same thing happens in hospitals. When a medicine kills most of a germ, the few germs that happen to survive it multiply, and soon the medicine works less well. Nobody taught the germs anything. The medicine chose which ones would have children.',
    'Natural selection needs three things: variation, survival and inheritance. Helpful differences get passed on more, and over generations the group shifts to fit its place. No moth turned dark. The forest chose which moths would have children.',
  ],
};
STORIES['energy-in-ecosystems'] = {
  about: 'a thousand blades of grass and one hawk, and the tenth that passes up at every step',
  more: [{ serial: 'S735', after: 0, alt: 'A girl counting grass, grasshoppers, birds and one hawk in a meadow' }, { serial: 'S736', after: 1, alt: 'Energy drawn as water in buckets passed from grass to grasshoppers, most of it spilling on the way' }, { serial: 'S2201', after: 3, alt: 'A grasshopper, a bird and a hawk giving off wavy lines of heat' }, { serial: 'S2202', after: 4, alt: 'A fallen log with mushrooms and new grass growing from the soil beside it' }, { serial: 'S2203', after: 5, alt: 'A single hawk circling over a field full of grass' }],
  title: 'The pyramid', art: 'S734', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a meadow drawing a pyramid in a notebook: a wide base of grass, then grasshoppers, then birds, then one hawk',
  words: [
    'Maya wondered why the field had thousands of blades of grass, hundreds of grasshoppers, a few dozen birds and one hawk. If hawks were the strongest, why were there not more of them?',
    'She counted energy instead of animals. Say the grass held a thousand units of energy from the sun. How much reached the grasshoppers that ate it? She assumed most of it. That turned out to be the mistake.',
    'Only about a tenth. A grasshopper spends most of what it eats on moving, breathing and staying warm, and only a tenth becomes grasshopper. A thousand units of grass make a hundred units of grasshopper, ten of bird, one of hawk. The energy leaks away at every step, so each level has to be much bigger than the one above it.',
    'Maya asked where the lost energy went. Most of it left as heat, from every animal breathing, moving and keeping warm. Heat does not come back to the grass. That is why the field needs new sunlight every day.',
    'The matter is different. When the hawk dies, decomposers break its body down, and the carbon and nitrogen in it return to the soil to build new grass. Matter goes around in a circle. Energy flows through in a line, from the sun to heat.',
    'Only about a tenth of the energy passes to the next level of a food chain. That is why there is much more grass than there are hawks, and why the pyramid of life is always wide at the bottom.',
  ],
};
STORIES['summary-and-response'] = {
  about: 'a summary that gave nothing away, and the response that finally got to say something',
  more: [{ serial: 'S738', after: 0, alt: 'A one-sentence summary with its last four words crossed out in red' }, { serial: 'S739', after: 1, alt: 'A boy puzzling over a note that says not yet' }, { serial: 'S2225', after: 2, alt: 'A page split by a line: a calm summary above, an opinionated response below' }, { serial: 'S2226', after: 3, alt: 'A reader nodding at a short summary in the writer\'s own words, unable to tell what the writer thinks' }, { serial: 'S2227', after: 4, alt: 'A treeless parking lot by a school shimmering in afternoon heat' }],
  title: 'The summary that gave nothing away', art: 'S737', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy writing at a desk with an article beside him, one paragraph neat and plain, a second one full of underlines',
  words: [
    'Diego\'s summary of the article was one sentence long and it was mostly his opinion. The article says cities should plant more trees, which is obviously right. His teacher crossed out the last four words and wrote: not yet.',
    'Not yet? Diego thought summarizing was about what he thought of the thing. Otherwise what was the point of reading it?',
    'The point came in two parts. First, the summary: the article says cities should plant more trees, for shade and cleaner air, and it gives three cities as examples. Nothing about what Diego thought. A reader could not guess his opinion, and that was the test. Then the response, where he finally got to say obviously right, and why.',
    'His teacher gave him the rules for the first job. Name the author and the title. Give the main idea and the key details in your own words. Keep it much shorter than the original, and leave yourself out.',
    'The response had choices. Diego could agree and add a reason of his own, or disagree and say why. He could also connect the article to something he had seen, like the treeless parking lot by his school that baked every afternoon. He did all three in one paragraph.',
    'Summarize fairly first, then respond. Keep your opinion out of the summary so a reader can trust it, and then spend the response on what you think and what you would push back on. Two jobs, two paragraphs.',
  ],
};
STORIES['argument-with-a-counterclaim'] = {
  about: 'the objection a girl was afraid to say out loud, and what happened when she said it first',
  more: [{ serial: 'S741', after: 0, alt: 'A girl rehearsing at a microphone in an empty school gym' }, { serial: 'S742', after: 1, alt: 'The coach\'s objection written on a card: it would cut practice time' }, { serial: 'S2228', after: 2, alt: 'The girl\'s answer on the next card: practice can shift, and rested players play better' }, { serial: 'S2229', after: 3, alt: 'A scarecrow made of straw beside a card with an easy objection crossed out' }, { serial: 'S2230', after: 4, alt: 'A page of sentence starters: while it is true that, some say, in fact' }],
  title: 'The objection said out loud', art: 'S740', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a microphone in a school gym, a coach in the front row with folded arms, a sentence beginning Some say on her page',
  words: [
    'Priya was arguing for a later school start, and she knew exactly what the coach would say: it would cut practice time. She planned to leave that out and hope nobody thought of it. Her teacher read the draft and asked where the coach was.',
    'Priya said she had left him out on purpose. If she brought up the objection, would she not be arguing against herself?',
    'The opposite. She wrote it in: some say a later start would cut practice time. True, up front. Then she answered it: practice could shift later, and rested players play better than tired ones. When the coach stood up at the meeting to object, half the room said, she already covered that. Her argument looked stronger for having met him first.',
    'Her teacher warned her about one trap. It is easy to answer a weak objection that nobody really holds. That is called a straw man, and audiences can smell it. Priya had chosen the objection the coach would actually make, which is why answering it worked.',
    'She also learned the sentences that carry it. While it is true that practice would move, rested players play better. Some say the buses cannot change; in fact, other districts have changed theirs. Each sentence gives the other side its due, then turns.',
    'Name the best objection and answer it. A fair counterclaim makes your argument stronger, not weaker, because it shows you have looked at the other side and still stand where you stand.',
  ],
};
STORIES['explanatory-essay'] = {
  about: 'a board game explained to someone who had never seen a board, and the essay that started there',
  more: [{ serial: 'S744', after: 0, alt: 'A grandmother reading a draft and asking what a piece is' }, { serial: 'S745', after: 2, alt: 'A single die and a single piece on the board, the first rule' }, { serial: 'S2231', after: 3, alt: 'A page with first, then, after that and finally circled like signposts' }, { serial: 'S2232', after: 4, alt: 'A small drawing of the board with an arrow showing which way to move' }, { serial: 'S2233', after: 5, alt: 'A finished page with each new word underlined and an example beside it' }],
  title: 'Starting where the reader is', art: 'S743', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy explaining a board game to his grandmother at a table, pieces and dice between them, one rule at a time',
  words: [
    'Sam had to explain how a board game worked to someone who had never played, and his first draft began with the scoring. His grandmother read it and asked what a piece was. Sam had been playing since he was six and had forgotten anyone could not know.',
    'He started again, from the top, and got lost in a different way: every sentence had three new words in it. His grandmother read it kindly and asked what a turn was.',
    'The third draft started where she was. A board, a die, a piece. Then one rule, roll the die and move that many spaces, and then the next rule. Each new word got its meaning the moment it appeared, and each rule got an example she could picture. By the end she was beating him.',
    'His teacher showed him what held the third draft together: transitions. First, then, after that, finally. Each one told his grandmother where she was in the explanation, the way signs tell you where you are in a building.',
    'He added one small drawing, the board with an arrow showing which way to move, and a heading over the part about winning. A picture and a heading did in one glance what a paragraph had struggled to do.',
    'An explanatory essay starts where the reader is and adds one idea at a time. Define a new word when it appears, and give an example for every rule. If your grandmother can follow it, anyone can.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-first-texans'] = {
  about: 'four regions of Texas, four ways of life, and the boy who found them on one drive',
  more: [{ serial: 'S747', after: 0, alt: 'A car on a long Texas highway, the land changing outside the window' }, { serial: 'S748', after: 1, alt: 'Wet flat coastal land with fishing camps, then rolling wooded hills' }, { serial: 'S2234', after: 2, alt: 'A Caddo village of tall round grass houses beside fields of corn in the piney woods' }, { serial: 'S2235', after: 3, alt: 'A dugout canoe in a shallow bay, a Comanche tipi on the plains and an adobe village by a western river' }, { serial: 'S2236', after: 4, alt: 'High dry plains with buffalo, then mountains and basins in the far west' }],
  title: 'Four regions', art: 'S746', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy looking out a car window as the land changes from flat green coast to rolling hills to dry high plains to mountains',
  words: [
    'Kai\'s family drove across Texas in one long day, and Kai noticed the land keep changing outside the window. Flat and wet and green. Then rolling hills. Then high and dry and endless. Then mountains. He asked if they had left the state. They had not.',
    'His mother said the peoples who lived here first would have felt those changes more than he did, because the land decided what they ate. Kai did not see how land could decide anything.',
    'It decided everything. Along the wet Gulf Coast, the Karankawa fished and gathered in the bays. In the piney woods of East Texas, still part of the Gulf Coastal Plains, the Caddo farmed corn, beans and squash in villages. On the rolling North Central Plains, the Wichita farmed along the rivers and hunted buffalo. On the high, dry Great Plains, the Comanche followed the buffalo on horseback. In the Mountains and Basins of the far west, the Jumano farmed beside the rare rivers.',
    'The land even decided their houses. The Caddo built tall, round houses of grass over wooden frames. The Comanche carried tipis they could take down and move with the herds. The Karankawa paddled dugout canoes through the shallow bays, and the Jumano along the western rivers lived in adobe villages.',
    'Kai started reading the land from the car. Where it rained, people farmed. Where it was dry and open, people followed the animals. Where the bays were shallow and full of fish, people fished.',
    'Texas has four natural regions: the Gulf Coastal Plains, the North Central Plains, the Great Plains, and the Mountains and Basins. Each shaped how its first peoples lived, and you can still see the borders from a car window.',
  ],
};
STORIES['spain-and-mexico-in-texas'] = {
  about: 'three centuries of a Spanish claim, a few missions, and the door Mexico opened',
  more: [{ serial: 'S750', after: 0, alt: 'A girl looking at a Texas map from 1800 with only a few dots on it' }, { serial: 'S751', after: 1, alt: 'San Antonio in 1718, a small mission and a few houses on a big empty map' }, { serial: 'S2237', after: 2, alt: 'Wagons of American settlers rolling through an open gate in 1821' }, { serial: 'S2238', after: 3, alt: 'A small French fort on a marshy coast, and Spanish priests building a mission inland' }, { serial: 'S2239', after: 4, alt: 'Stephen F. Austin with a map of his colony as families arrive with wagons' }],
  title: 'Three centuries and a door', art: 'S749', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at an old stone mission at sunset, a Spanish flag faded on a pole, a wide empty plain behind it',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s history book said Spain owned Texas for three hundred years, and she pictured three hundred years of Spanish towns. Then she looked at the map from 1800. San Antonio. A few missions. And a great deal of nothing.',
    'Three centuries and almost no one? How could a country claim a place that size and hardly live in it? Maya thought the map must be missing something.',
    'It was not. Spain claimed Texas from the 1500s and settled little of it beyond San Antonio, founded in 1718, and a string of missions from the 1690s. The land was far from Mexico City and hard to hold. In 1821 Mexico won its independence, inherited the empty claim, and opened the door to American settlers to fill it. They came faster than anyone in Mexico City expected.',
    'Her teacher explained why the missions were built at all. In 1685 a French explorer, La Salle, landed on the Texas coast and built a fort. Spain, alarmed, sent priests and soldiers to build missions in the east to hold its claim. Fear of France did more to settle early Texas than any plan.',
    'Mexico\'s door had a name: the empresario system. Stephen F. Austin brought the first three hundred families, and many more followed. By the early 1830s settlers from the United States far outnumbered the Mexican Texans, called Tejanos, and quarrels over laws, slavery and self-rule began to grow.',
    'Spain claimed Texas for three centuries and settled little of it. Mexico won independence in 1821 and invited settlers, who soon outnumbered everyone else. A claim on a map is not the same as a people on the land, and that gap is the next chapter.',
  ],
};
STORIES['revolution-and-republic'] = {
  about: 'from a small cannon in Gonzales to eighteen minutes at San Jacinto',
  more: [{ serial: 'S753', after: 1, alt: 'A small cannon under a homemade flag that says come and take it' }, { serial: 'S754', after: 2, alt: 'The walls of the Alamo at dawn, quiet, no fighting shown' }, { serial: 'S2240', after: 3, alt: 'Delegates signing a declaration by lamplight in a small wooden building' }, { serial: 'S2241', after: 4, alt: 'A new capital town rising on the Colorado River, a log capitol among the trees' }, { serial: 'S2242', after: 5, alt: 'A watch showing eighteen minutes past the hour on the field at San Jacinto' }],
  title: 'From a cannon to eighteen minutes', art: 'S752', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a flag with a cannon and the words come and take it, a river and a low sun behind him',
  words: [
    'Theo could not understand how a war could be won in eighteen minutes. His grandfather said the Texas Revolution had been, at San Jacinto, and Theo said that sounded like a story someone made up later.',
    'His grandfather said, then start earlier. In October 1835, at Gonzales, the Mexican army came to take back a small cannon, and the settlers flew a flag that said come and take it. That was the first shot. Eighteen minutes was the last one.',
    'In between were six months. In March 1836 the Alamo fell after a siege, and at Goliad hundreds of prisoners were executed. Sam Houston retreated east, gathering men and anger. On April 21 at San Jacinto his army surprised the Mexican camp during its afternoon rest, and the fighting was over in eighteen minutes. Santa Anna was captured, and Texas was a republic.',
    'His grandfather added a date Theo had never heard. On March 2, 1836, while the Alamo was still under siege, delegates at Washington-on-the-Brazos signed the Texas Declaration of Independence. The war was already on; that paper said what it was for.',
    'After San Jacinto, the Republic of Texas lasted almost ten years. Sam Houston was elected its first president, and in 1839 the capital moved to a new town on the Colorado River named for Stephen F. Austin.',
    'The revolution ran from Gonzales in October 1835 to San Jacinto in April 1836, with the Alamo and Goliad between. Eighteen minutes ended it only because six months had built it. That is how most quick endings work.',
  ],
};
STORIES['statehood-and-civil-war'] = {
  about: 'a republic that became a state, a war that followed within a year, and freedom that arrived late',
  more: [{ serial: 'S756', after: 0, alt: 'A girl on courthouse steps asking her aunt how a country can move' }, { serial: 'S757', after: 1, alt: 'A map showing Texas joining the United States in 1845, a new star added' }, { serial: 'S2243', after: 2, alt: 'A map with the Nueces River and the Rio Grande marked, the land between them shaded' }, { serial: 'S2244', after: 3, alt: 'A crowd in Galveston on June 19, 1865, hearing an order read aloud' }, { serial: 'S2245', after: 4, alt: 'Families celebrating Juneteenth in a park today' }],
  title: 'A state, a war, and June nineteenth', art: 'S755', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding an old photo of her great-great-great-grandmother in front of the same house, a Republic of Texas flag and a United States flag beside it',
  words: [
    'Ava\'s great-great-great-grandmother was born in the Republic of Texas and died in the United States without ever moving house. Ava asked how that was possible. Her aunt said the country moved, not the grandmother.',
    'In 1845 Texas joined the Union as the twenty-eighth state. Within a year the United States was at war with Mexico over where Texas ended. Ava thought that was the whole story. Her aunt said it was the first third.',
    'Mexico said the border was the Nueces River; the United States said the Rio Grande, farther south. The war ended in 1848 with the Rio Grande as the line, and Mexico gave up a huge area that became much of the American Southwest.',
    'In 1861 Texas left the Union to join the Confederacy in a war over slavery. The war ended in 1865, but freedom reached Texas late: on June 19, 1865, soldiers in Galveston read the order that every enslaved person in the state was free. Juneteenth started there. Ava\'s grandmother had lived through all of it in one town.',
    'The order was read by a Union general, Gordon Granger, about two and a half years after the Emancipation Proclamation. For a long time Juneteenth was celebrated mostly in Texas. In 2021 it became a national holiday.',
    'Texas joined the Union in 1845, war with Mexico followed in 1846, Texas left for the Confederacy in 1861, and freedom arrived in Galveston on June 19, 1865. One lifetime can hold a whole map of changes.',
  ],
};
STORIES['cattle-cotton-and-oil'] = {
  about: 'three fortunes made from the same ground, one after another',
  more: [{ serial: 'S759', after: 0, alt: 'A town welcome sign with a cattle brand, an old cotton gin museum behind it' }, { serial: 'S760', after: 1, alt: 'A boy on a fence counting three things on his fingers' }, { serial: 'S2246', after: 2, alt: 'A cattle drive heading north toward a railroad, dust rising' }, { serial: 'S2247', after: 3, alt: 'A rail line reaching a Texas town and barbed wire fencing an open range' }, { serial: 'S2248', after: 4, alt: 'The Spindletop gusher of 1901 spraying oil high over a wooden derrick' }],
  title: 'Three fortunes', art: 'S758', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a fence looking across a landscape divided in three: cattle on the range, cotton in the fields, an oil derrick on the horizon',
  words: [
    'Jamal\'s town had a cattle brand on the welcome sign, a cotton gin turned into a museum, and an oil pump nodding beside the highway. He asked which one Texas was really about. His uncle said, all three, in order.',
    'How could one state be about three different things? Jamal thought a place picked one thing and stuck to it, the way his town had picked its football team and never let go.',
    'Texas did not. From 1867, for about twenty years, cattle walked from Texas to the railroads in Kansas, and the drives made the first fortunes. Cotton came next, filled the fields and became the state\'s biggest crop. And in 1901 at Spindletop, oil blew more than a hundred feet into the air and changed everything again. Each fortune came from the same ground, and each one paid for the next.',
    'The drives ended almost as fast as they began. Railroads reached Texas itself, so cattle no longer had to walk to Kansas, and barbed wire fenced the open range into ranches. The cowboys who had ridden the trails became ranch hands, and some of the ranches grew bigger than whole counties.',
    'Oil changed more than the owners. Towns grew up around the wells almost overnight, refineries rose along the Gulf Coast, and money from oil on state land helped pay for Texas schools and universities.',
    'Cattle, cotton and oil, one after another, from the same land. When you ask what a place is about, the honest answer is usually a sequence, not a single thing.',
  ],
};
STORIES['modern-texas'] = {
  about: 'six flags over one state, and the seventh flag that only the twentieth century could raise',
  more: [{ serial: 'S762', after: 0, alt: 'A girl counting six flags on a plaza' }, { serial: 'S763', after: 1, alt: 'An empty seventh flagpole beside the six flags' }, { serial: 'S2249', after: 2, alt: 'A rocket lifting off with Mission Control drawn in the foreground' }, { serial: 'S2250', after: 3, alt: 'A farm road leading to the skylines of Texas cities, Houston, Dallas and San Antonio drawn in a row' }, { serial: 'S2251', after: 4, alt: 'A computer chip, a hospital tower and a cargo ship around a map of Texas' }],
  title: 'From farms to skylines', art: 'S761', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl standing before a row of six historic flags with a modern city skyline and a rocket rising behind them',
  words: [
    'Rosa counted the six flags on the plaza: Spain, France, Mexico, the Republic of Texas, the Confederacy, the United States. Six countries, one state. She thought the story was finished. Her teacher said the twentieth century added a seventh flag that was not a flag at all.',
    'Rosa did not know what that meant. All the flags were up there already. What could the twentieth century have added?',
    'A skyline. In one century Texas went from farms and ranches to the second-largest state by land and by people. NASA came to Houston and put Mission Control in Texas, and the first words spoken from the surface of the moon were addressed to Houston. Highways, airports and glass towers rose where cotton had been. The seventh flag was the modern state itself.',
    'Most Texans now live in cities. Houston, San Antonio, Dallas, Austin and Fort Worth are all among the largest cities in the country, and more than thirty million people call the state home.',
    'The work changed too. Oil still matters, but so do computer chips in Austin, the world\'s largest medical center in Houston, and ports and farms that send goods around the world. A state that once lived on one fortune at a time now lives on many at once.',
    'In the twentieth century Texas became the second-largest state by land and people, and the space program made Houston famous around the world. The six flags are the past. The skyline is the seventh.',
  ],
};
STORIES['slope'] = {
  about: 'a gentle hill, a steep hill, and the boy who measured them with a ruler instead of his legs',
  more: [{ serial: 'S765', after: 0, alt: 'A boy pushing his bike up a steep hill, legs tired, a gentle hill behind him' }, { serial: 'S766', after: 1, alt: 'A steep line rising two squares for every one across on graph paper' }, { serial: 'S2252', after: 2, alt: 'A gentle line rising two squares for every four across' }, { serial: 'S2253', after: 3, alt: 'Two lines on graph paper falling to the right, labeled minus two and minus one half, and a flat line labeled zero' }, { serial: 'S2254', after: 4, alt: 'A road sign showing a truck on a slope and 6 percent' }],
  title: 'Two hills', art: 'S764', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a bike at the foot of two hills, one gentle and one steep, a ruler sketch floating beside him',
  words: [
    'Owen rode his bike up two hills on the way to school. The first was easy. The second made his legs burn. He knew the second was steeper, but when his teacher asked how much steeper, he had nothing but a feeling in his legs.',
    'He drew the two hills on graph paper and stared. Both went up. Both went across. Steep was somewhere in the relationship between the two, and he could not name it.',
    'Then he counted. The steep hill rose two squares for every one square across. Two over one, slope two. The gentle hill rose two squares for every four across. Two over four, slope one half. Rise over run, and the bigger the number, the harder the climb.',
    'On the way home both hills ran the other way. Going down, each line fell as it went across, so its slope was negative: minus two and minus one half. The flat stretch by the park rose nothing at all, a slope of zero.',
    'Road signs use slope too. A sign warning of a 6 percent grade means the road rises or falls 6 feet for every 100 feet across. The steep line in Owen\'s drawing, at slope two, would be a 200 percent grade, so he admitted he had drawn his hill a little steeper than it was.',
    'Slope is rise over run. In y = mx + b, m is the slope and b is where the line crosses the y-axis. Owen\'s legs had known the answer all along; now the ruler could say it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['exponents'] = {
  about: 'a sheet of paper folded five times, and the pile that grew faster than anyone guessed',
  more: [{ serial: 'S768', after: 0, alt: 'A girl and her brother shaking hands on a bet over a single sheet of paper' }, { serial: 'S769', after: 1, alt: 'The paper after two folds, four layers fanned out' }, { serial: 'S2255', after: 2, alt: 'The thick pile after five folds, thirty-two layers, with two times two times two times two times two written beside it' }, { serial: 'S2256', after: 3, alt: 'A notebook where her brother wrote two to the third times two to the second is two to the fifth, and eight times four is thirty-two' }, { serial: 'S2257', after: 4, alt: 'A single flat sheet labeled 2⁰ = 1 beside a folded one' }],
  title: 'Five folds', art: 'S767', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl folding a sheet of paper for the fifth time, the pile suddenly thick, a notebook beside her',
  words: [
    'Priya bet her brother that folding a sheet of paper five times would make ten layers. Five folds, two layers each, ten. It was obvious. Her brother said thirty-two and would not say why.',
    'She folded once. Two layers. Twice. Four. Three times. Eight. That was not going up by two each time. It was doubling, and doubling ran away from her fast.',
    'Five folds: two times two times two times two times two. Thirty-two layers. The exponent, the little five, was just counting how many times she had multiplied by two. Two to the fifth. Her brother had counted the folds, not the layers. Each fold doubled everything that came before it.',
    'Her brother showed her the shortcut. Two to the third times two to the second is two to the fifth, because three twos and two more twos make five twos. Eight times four is thirty-two either way.',
    'Then he asked about zero folds: one sheet, one layer. Two to the zero is one, and so is any other number to the zero, except zero itself. Divide two to the fifth by two to the fifth and you get two to the zero, and anything divided by itself is one.',
    'An exponent counts the multiplying. With the same base, add exponents to multiply and subtract them to divide. Five folds is thirty-two layers, and ten folds would be 1,024, if anyone could fold a sheet that many times.',
  ],
};
STORIES['square-roots'] = {
  about: 'forty-nine floor tiles and the girl who had to lay them in a perfect square',
  more: [{ serial: 'S771', after: 0, alt: 'A girl on a hot patio trying six tiles across, a pile of spare tiles beside her' }, { serial: 'S772', after: 1, alt: 'A girl holding one tile and wondering what number times itself makes forty-nine' }, { serial: 'S2258', after: 2, alt: 'A tidy square of tiles, seven across and seven down' }, { serial: 'S2259', after: 3, alt: 'A number line with 49 and 64 marked and the square root of 50 just past seven' }, { serial: 'S2260', after: 4, alt: 'A calculator showing the square root of two, 1.41421, going on forever' }],
  title: 'Forty-nine tiles', art: 'S770', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl kneeling on a patio with forty-nine square tiles, laying them out in rows',
  words: [
    'Rosa had forty-nine tiles and one job: lay them in a square, no leftovers. She tried six across. One row short and some spare. She tried eight. Not enough tiles. The patio was hot and the tiles were heavy.',
    'A square meant the same number across as down. So she needed a number that, times itself, made forty-nine. She had never thought of a number that way before.',
    'Seven. Seven rows of seven is forty-nine. That is the square root: the number that, multiplied by itself, gives you the total. The perfect squares, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, are the tile counts that make tidy squares. Any other count leaves a leftover, and its root sits between two whole numbers.',
    'Her aunt asked about fifty tiles. No whole number times itself makes fifty, so the square root of fifty lies between seven and eight, just past seven: about 7.07. Rosa would have one tile left over, and a patio that could never be a perfect square. Seven times seven is 49; seven and a bit times itself is 50.',
    'Some roots never come out even at all. The square root of two goes on forever without repeating, 1.41421 and on. Numbers like that are called irrational, and the ancient Greeks are said to have been upset to discover them.',
    'A square root asks which number times itself gives this. Know the perfect squares by heart, and a root you cannot find exactly still lives between two that you can.',
  ],
};
STORIES['scientific-notation'] = {
  about: 'a number with too many zeros, and the decimal point that slid to save it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S774', after: 0, alt: 'A boy counting zeros on his fingers, one too many written in his notebook' }, { serial: 'S775', after: 2, alt: 'The number 150,000,000 with an arrow sliding the decimal point eight places' }, { serial: 'S2264', after: 3, alt: 'A red blood cell drawn beside 0.000007 meters and 7 × 10⁻⁶' }, { serial: 'S2265', after: 4, alt: 'A star chart with Proxima Centauri circled and 4.0 × 10¹³ kilometers beside it' }, { serial: 'S2266', after: 5, alt: 'Scientific notation on the board: a number from 1 to 10 times a power of ten, 1.5 × 10⁸' }],
  title: 'Sliding the point', art: 'S773', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a whiteboard writing a very long number, the zeros trailing off the edge',
  words: [
    'Marcus had to copy the distance to the Sun into his notebook: 150,000,000 kilometers. By the fourth zero he had lost count. By the sixth he had written one too many, and the Sun was now ten times farther away.',
    'Zeros were the problem. They looked alike, they were easy to drop, and nobody could tell at a glance whether a number had eight or nine.',
    'His teacher slid the decimal point. Move it eight places to the left and 150,000,000 becomes 1.5. Then write how far it moved: times ten to the eighth. 1.5 × 10⁸. Every digit that mattered was there, and the size lived in the exponent instead of a trail of zeros.',
    'Small numbers work the same way in reverse. A red blood cell is about 0.000007 meters across. Slide the point six places to the right and write a negative exponent: 7 × 10⁻⁶ meters.',
    'His teacher gave him the nearest star after the Sun, Proxima Centauri: about 40,000,000,000,000 kilometers away. Marcus wrote 4.0 × 10¹³ and did not have to count a single zero twice. Light from that star takes more than four years to reach us.',
    'Scientific notation is a number from 1 to 10, times a power of ten. The exponent counts how many places the decimal point moved: left for a big number, right for a tiny one. It is how scientists write the very large and the very small without losing a zero.',
  ],
};
STORIES['flawed-reasoning'] = {
  about: 'a washed car, a rainstorm, and the four ways an argument can look right and be wrong',
  more: [{ serial: 'S777', after: 0, alt: 'A family laughing at a joke while a boy stands beside a wet, freshly washed car' }, { serial: 'S778', after: 1, alt: 'A boy on a school bus frowning out the window at the clouds' }, { serial: 'S2291', after: 2, alt: 'Four cards laid out, each showing a broken chain link with a different name' }, { serial: 'S2292', after: 3, alt: 'The boy pointing at a list in his own handwriting in the back of a notebook, grinning' }, { serial: 'S2293', after: 4, alt: 'A weather forecast on a phone showing rain for Sunday, posted before Saturday' }],
  title: 'The car and the rain', art: 'S776', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with a bucket beside a freshly washed car as rain begins to fall, a frustrated look on his face',
  words: [
    'Marcus washed the car on Saturday and it rained on Sunday. His uncle said, that is what you get for washing it, and everyone laughed. Marcus laughed too. Then he thought about it on the bus and stopped laughing. Did washing the car really cause the rain?',
    'It felt true. It happened right after. But when he tried to explain how a wet car pulls clouds across the sky, he could not. The argument had a shape that looked like reasoning and a middle made of nothing.',
    'His teacher had names for that. False cause: one thing came after another, so it must have been caused by it. Overgeneralizing: every dog I met bit me, so every dog bites. Attacking the person: you are only saying that because you are a kid. False choice: either we ban phones or nobody learns. Name the flaw, and the argument loses its grip.',
    'She added two more shapes. Everyone is doing it, so it must be right: the bandwagon. It is true because it is true, said a louder way: circular reasoning. Marcus started a list in the back of his notebook and caught three in one week of commercials.',
    'The fix for false cause is one question: what else could explain it? It had rained on Sunday because a storm had been forecast all week, and the forecast came out before the car was washed.',
    'Flawed reasoning looks like reasoning until you name it. Overgeneralizing, false cause, attacking the person, false choice: shapes that fool people every day. Once you can see the shape, you cannot unsee it, and the washed car stops causing rain.',
  ],
};
STORIES['irony'] = {
  about: 'a fire station that burned down, and the difference between bad luck and irony',
  more: [{ serial: 'S780', after: 0, alt: 'A town gathered across the street from a burned fire station' }, { serial: 'S781', after: 1, alt: 'A girl with her arm in a cast the day before a swim meet' }, { serial: 'S2294', after: 2, alt: 'A woman in a storm saying great weather, umbrella inside out' }, { serial: 'S2295', after: 3, alt: 'A board listing situational, verbal and dramatic irony' }, { serial: 'S2296', after: 4, alt: 'A stage with the audience seeing a shadow the hero cannot see' }],
  title: 'The fire station', art: 'S779', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl looking at a burned fire station across the street, a fire truck parked outside it untouched',
  words: [
    'The fire station in Priya\'s town burned down, and the whole town called it ironic. A month later Priya broke her arm the day before a swim meet, and her friend said that was ironic too. Priya was not sure both could be right.',
    'Bad luck was everywhere. If every bad thing was irony, the word did not mean much. There had to be something more in the fire station than in her arm.',
    'There was a gap. A fire station is the one building that should never burn, so what happened stood against what everyone expected. That gap is irony. Her arm was just bad luck; nobody expects a broken arm to be impossible. The gap can sit between expected and actual. It can sit between what is said and what is meant, as in great weather in a storm. It can sit between what the audience knows and what the hero does not.',
    'Her teacher gave the three gaps their names. The fire station is situational irony. Great weather in a storm is verbal irony. When the audience knows the villain is behind the door and the hero does not, that is dramatic irony.',
    'Priya found dramatic irony in the next play they read. The audience knew the letter had been switched; the two friends on stage did not, and every kind word between them hurt to watch.',
    'Irony is a gap: expected against actual, said against meant, known against unknown. Bad luck alone is not irony. The fire station qualifies. The arm, however sore, does not.',
  ],
};
STORIES['allusions'] = {
  about: 'one word at the dinner table that carried a whole book with it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S783', after: 1, alt: 'A faint drawing of an old miser in a nightcap hovering over the boy\'s shoulder' }, { serial: 'S784', after: 2, alt: 'Three small drawings: a miser, a heel with an arrow, a wooden horse at a city gate' }, { serial: 'S2297', after: 3, alt: 'A slingshot and a giant\'s shadow beside a small team celebrating' }, { serial: 'S2298', after: 4, alt: 'A friend staring blankly across a lunch table as a boy explains Hamlet' }, { serial: 'S2299', after: 5, alt: 'A bookshelf with a few classic spines glowing' }],
  title: 'One word, one story', art: 'S782', cast: [],
  alt: 'A family at a dinner table, a boy holding tight to a plate of dessert while his sister rolls her eyes',
  words: [
    'Kai would not share his dessert, and his sister said, do not be such a Scrooge. Kai had never read the book. He still knew exactly what she meant, and he gave her half.',
    'How did one word do that? She had not told a story. She had not even said what a Scrooge was. Kai looked it up later and found a short novel by Charles Dickens about a miser who counted coins while others went cold.',
    'That is an allusion. A writer borrows a whole story in a few words and trusts the reader to bring the rest. Scrooge means stingy, an Achilles\' heel means the one weak spot, a Trojan horse means a gift that hides a trap. Know where the word points, and you know what the writer meant.',
    'Allusions come from everywhere people share stories. A small team beating a giant one is a David and Goliath game, from the Bible. Starting a trouble that lets loose many more is opening Pandora\'s box, from a Greek myth.',
    'An allusion only works if the reader knows the story. Kai called his friend a Hamlet for taking ten minutes to choose a sandwich, and his friend just stared. Kai laughed and explained, and later he read the play himself.',
    'An allusion borrows a whole story in a few words. When a word seems to carry more weight than it should, ask where it points, and read that story too. Writers pack their sentences with other people\'s books.',
  ],
};
STORIES['objective-summary'] = {
  about: 'a summary that came out as a review, and the mirror that fixed it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S786', after: 0, alt: 'A girl reading an article about a park at dusk at a library table' }, { serial: 'S787', after: 1, alt: 'A summary with the word unfortunately circled in red' }, { serial: 'S2300', after: 2, alt: 'The same summary rewritten in plain words, no circles' }, { serial: 'S2301', after: 3, alt: 'A list of leaning verbs crossed out, rant, admit and whine, beside argue, state and report' }, { serial: 'S2302', after: 4, alt: 'Two people on opposite sides of a park gate both nodding at the same summary' }],
  title: 'The mirror', art: 'S785', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a library table holding a page up to a small mirror, an article open beside her',
  words: [
    'Rosa summarized an article that argued the park should close at dusk. Her summary said the article unfortunately argued the park should close at dusk. Her teacher circled one word.',
    'Unfortunately. Rosa had not meant to put herself in the summary. But there she was, in one adverb, disagreeing with an article that had not asked her opinion.',
    'A summary is a mirror, her teacher said, not a review. Say what the text says, in neutral words. The article argues that the park should close at dusk, for safety. That is all. No liked, no hated, no unfortunately. Watch for the single words that let an opinion back in: sadly, of course, so-called, only. And keep the article\'s reasons: fewer accidents after dark, and fewer complaints from neighbors.',
    'Verbs can lean too. The article argues, states or reports. It does not rant, admit or whine. Rosa had once written that an author admitted something, and her teacher asked whether the author had confessed or simply said it.',
    'Her teacher gave her a test for fairness. Would the person who wrote the article agree that her summary said what they meant? And would someone who disagreed with it agree too? If both would sign it, the mirror is clean. Rosa tried it on her next summary, and both her parents signed.',
    'An objective summary says what the text says in neutral words and leaves your opinion out. The dangerous words are the small ones. A mirror does not sigh.',
  ],
};
STORIES['chemical-reactions'] = {
  about: 'a folded sheet of paper, a burned one, and the change a boy could not undo',
  more: [{ serial: 'S789', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a campfire holding a paper plane in one hand and a burning scrap in the other' }, { serial: 'S790', after: 1, alt: 'The paper folded into a plane, then unfolded flat again' }, { serial: 'S2279', after: 2, alt: 'A scrap of paper turning to ash and smoke over the flame' }, { serial: 'S2280', after: 3, alt: 'A list of signs on a board: new color, bubbles, heat or light, a new solid' }, { serial: 'S2281', after: 4, alt: 'A sealed bag of baking soda and vinegar puffed up on a scale that has not changed' }],
  title: 'Fold it, burn it', art: 'S788', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a folded paper airplane, a small pile of ash beside a campfire',
  words: [
    'Theo folded a sheet of paper into a plane, then flattened it out again. Same paper, a few creases. Then, at the campfire, he let a scrap catch the flame. It curled, went black, and became ash and smoke. He could not fold it back.',
    'Both were changes. Why could one be undone and the other could not? The paper had gone somewhere, and it was not coming back.',
    'Folding was a physical change. The paper was still paper, just a different shape. Burning was a chemical change. The paper\'s atoms had rearranged into new substances, ash and gases, and no atom was created or lost. The paper was not gone. It was everywhere, in a form no fold could reach.',
    'His teacher listed the signs that give a chemical change away: a new color, bubbles of a new gas, heat or light, or a solid appearing out of a liquid. The burning scrap had shown three of them at once: a black color, smoke, and heat with light.',
    'In class they mixed baking soda and vinegar inside a sealed plastic bag on a scale. The bag puffed up with carbon dioxide, but the scale did not move. Every atom was still in the bag, just arranged into new substances.',
    'A chemical change makes a new substance; a physical change does not. In every reaction, atoms are rearranged, never created or destroyed. Fold it and it is still paper. Burn it and it is something else.',
  ],
};
STORIES['scale-of-the-universe'] = {
  about: 'nesting boxes, and the girl who found the Earth inside the smallest one',
  more: [{ serial: 'S792', after: 1, alt: 'The boxes lined up from smallest to largest, each labeled by a drawn planet, sun, galaxy' }, { serial: 'S793', after: 2, alt: 'The moon circling the Earth, the Earth circling the Sun, and the Sun inside a spiral galaxy' }, { serial: 'S2282', after: 3, alt: 'A beam of sunlight drawn crossing to the Earth, a clock beside it showing eight minutes' }, { serial: 'S2283', after: 4, alt: 'A night sky with a faint band of stars, the girl pointing up' }, { serial: 'S2284', after: 5, alt: 'The Andromeda galaxy drawn as a pale spiral, labeled 2.5 million light-years' }],
  title: 'Boxes inside boxes', art: 'S791', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl opening a set of nesting boxes in an attic, a tiny blue marble in the smallest one',
  words: [
    'Nadia found a set of nesting boxes in the attic. Inside the biggest was a smaller one, and inside that a smaller one, down to a tiny box with a blue marble in it. Her uncle said, that is where we live.',
    'The marble was the Earth, he said. Nadia laughed. Then she asked what the next box was, and the next, and stopped laughing somewhere around the fourth.',
    'The moon circles the Earth. The Earth and its neighbors circle the sun: the solar system. The sun is one star among hundreds of billions in a galaxy. The galaxy is one among billions in the universe. Each layer sits inside the next, and the distances are so vast that astronomers measure them by how far light travels in a year.',
    'Her uncle gave her the numbers. Light from the Sun reaches the Earth in about eight minutes. Light from the nearest star after the Sun takes more than four years. A light-year, the distance light travels in a year, is about 9.5 trillion kilometers.',
    'Our galaxy, the Milky Way, is about 100,000 light-years across, and its stars are the faint band you can see across a dark sky. The nearest big galaxy, Andromeda, is about 2.5 million light-years away, so the light we see from it left long before the first modern humans walked the Earth.',
    'Moon, planet, solar system, galaxy, universe: each is inside the next. Distances are measured in light-years, because kilometers run out of zeros. The marble is small, and it is home.',
  ],
};
STORIES['speed-and-graphs'] = {
  about: 'a walk to school drawn as one line, and the boy who could read where he had run',
  more: [{ serial: 'S795', after: 0, alt: 'A boy with a stopwatch marking distances on a map of his walk to school' }, { serial: 'S796', after: 1, alt: 'The flat part of the line, a shoe being tied' }, { serial: 'S2285', after: 2, alt: 'A distance-time graph with a flat part, a gentle slope and a steep slope labeled still, slow and fast' }, { serial: 'S2286', after: 3, alt: 'The steep part of the line, the boy running for the bell' }, { serial: 'S2287', after: 4, alt: 'A line on a graph bending upward in a curve, labeled speeding up' }],
  title: 'The walk on a graph', art: 'S794', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a desk looking at a graph of his walk to school, a flat part, a gentle slope and a steep one',
  words: [
    'Kai timed his walk to school and marked how far he had gone every minute. Then he plotted it: time across, distance up. He expected a straight line. Instead the line had a flat bit, a gentle stretch and a steep jump at the end.',
    'The flat part puzzled him. Time kept passing, but the distance did not move. Had he stopped? He thought back. He had stopped to tie his shoe.',
    'That was it. On a distance-time graph, flat means standing still. A gentle slope means moving slowly, the stroll past the park. A steep slope means moving fast, the sprint for the bell. The slope is the speed. And the whole trip\'s average speed was the total distance over the total time, one gentler line from start to finish.',
    'He worked out the numbers. The whole trip was 1,200 meters in 15 minutes, an average of 80 meters a minute. The last sprint covered 200 meters in a single minute, which is why that part of the line stood up so steeply.',
    'His teacher showed him one more shape. When he sped up little by little, the line did not jump from gentle to steep; it curved, bending upward as it went. A curve on a distance-time graph means the speed is changing, and that is acceleration.',
    'On a distance-time graph, flat is still, sloped is moving, steeper is faster, and a curve is speeding up or slowing down. The slope is the speed, and average speed is the whole distance over the whole time. A walk becomes a line, and the line remembers the shoe.',
  ],
};
STORIES['weathering-to-fossils'] = {
  about: 'a laundry pile, a cliff, and why the bottom is always the oldest',
  more: [{ serial: 'S798', after: 1, alt: 'The shirt at the bottom of the pile, worn on Monday' }, { serial: 'S799', after: 2, alt: 'A cliff of stripes with a shell fossil in a lower layer' }, { serial: 'S2288', after: 3, alt: 'Rain, ice and tree roots cracking a boulder into sand while a river carries it away' }, { serial: 'S2289', after: 4, alt: 'A limestone hill near Austin with a fossil shell in the rock' }, { serial: 'S2290', after: 5, alt: 'A folded cliff where the layers bend and turn over' }],
  title: 'The laundry pile', art: 'S797', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy digging through a tall pile of laundry, a cliff of rock layers drawn on the wall behind him',
  words: [
    'Diego was looking for the shirt he had worn on Monday. It was Friday. The pile of laundry on his floor was tall, and Monday was nowhere near the top.',
    'He started digging. Thursday, Wednesday, Tuesday. Each shirt was under the one that came after it. Monday was at the bottom, because it had gone in first, and everything since had piled on top.',
    'Rock layers work the same way. Mud and sand settle one layer on top of another, year after year, so the deepest layer is the oldest. A fossil buried deep is older than one near the surface, and the layer around it, sand or mud or shells, says what the place was like when that creature lived.',
    'Where did the mud and sand come from? Weathering, his teacher said: rain, ice and roots crack rock into smaller and smaller pieces. Rivers and wind carry the pieces away, which is erosion, and drop them somewhere else in a new layer, which is deposition.',
    'A creature buried quickly in that mud can become a fossil, as minerals from the water slowly take the place of its shell or bone. The limestone hills around Austin were once the floor of a shallow sea, and people still find fossil shells in them.',
    'Layers pile up over time, so deeper is older, unless the Earth has folded or overturned them. Fossils and the layers around them tell what a place was like and when. A cliff is a laundry pile that took a million years.',
  ],
};
STORIES['thesis-and-outline'] = {
  about: 'a topic nobody would argue with, and the fight worth picking instead',
  more: [{ serial: 'S801', after: 0, alt: 'The sentence dogs are pets crossed out, nobody arguing' }, { serial: 'S802', after: 1, alt: 'A plain sentence wearing a costume mask labeled thesis' }, { serial: 'S2321', after: 2, alt: 'An outline: one thesis at the top, three reasons beneath' }, { serial: 'S2322', after: 3, alt: 'A thesis sentence with the word because circled and three arrows from it' }, { serial: 'S2323', after: 4, alt: 'An outline page with evidence under each reason and a line for the other side: cats are cheaper' }],
  title: 'A fight worth picking', art: 'S800', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a desk with his first thesis, dogs are pets, crossed out and a new one written underneath',
  words: [
    'Sam\'s first thesis was dogs are pets. His teacher asked who would disagree. Sam thought about it. Nobody. Not one person on Earth would argue with him, and that, she said, was the problem.',
    'A thesis that everyone agrees with is a topic wearing a thesis costume. There was nothing to prove and nowhere for the essay to go.',
    'He tried again. Dogs make better pets than cats for busy families. Now someone would argue. His cousin would argue right now. And once he had a claim someone could fight, the reasons lined up under it on their own: dogs get you outside, dogs fit a schedule, dogs guard the house. The outline wrote itself.',
    'His teacher pushed once more: a strong thesis also says why. Dogs make better pets than cats for busy families because they build a routine. Now the because pointed at the reasons before he had even listed them.',
    'Under each reason in the outline went a piece of evidence and a note about the other side: cats are cheaper, some would say. He gave that a line too, and planned his answer. The outline now held the whole essay on one page. When he sat down to write, every paragraph already knew its job.',
    'Write the thesis first, then list the reasons under it. A good thesis is one someone could disagree with, because an essay is a fight worth picking, and a fight needs another side.',
  ],
};
STORIES['evidence-paragraph'] = {
  about: 'twelve near-misses at a crosswalk, and the sentence most writers leave out',
  more: [{ serial: 'S804', after: 0, alt: 'A crossing guard counting near-misses on a clipboard at a busy corner' }, { serial: 'S805', after: 2, alt: 'A paragraph with claim and evidence, a blank line between them' }, { serial: 'S2324', after: 3, alt: 'A crosswalk with flashing yellow lights and a car stopping' }, { serial: 'S2325', after: 4, alt: 'A photo of the corner at rush hour clipped to a letter' }, { serial: 'S2326', after: 5, alt: 'The blank line filled in, the paragraph whole' }],
  title: 'The missing sentence', art: 'S803', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a street corner with a clipboard, a car braking hard at the crossing behind her',
  words: [
    'Ana wrote to the city. Claim: the town needs a safer crossing at Oak and Third. Evidence: twelve near-misses this year, counted by the crossing guard. She was proud of the number. The city wrote back and asked what the number had to do with a crossing.',
    'Ana was furious. The connection was obvious. Twelve near-misses meant danger, and a crossing fixed danger. But she had not written that sentence. She had assumed it.',
    'That sentence has a name: the warrant. It says why the evidence counts for the claim. Twelve near-misses mean that twelve times someone was nearly hurt, and a crossing that makes drivers stop would cut that number. Claim, evidence, warrant.',
    'The warrant also told her what to ask for. On a busy road, a traffic engineer told her, plain painted stripes alone do little; flashing lights that drivers can see make them far more likely to stop. Ana changed her claim to match her warrant.',
    'Her teacher added one more habit: pick evidence a stranger can check. The crossing guard\'s count was good. A photo of the corner at rush hour was better still. She sent it all again, and the city sent a crew to put up a crossing with flashing lights.',
    'A paragraph that proves something has three parts: the claim, the evidence, and the warrant that says why the evidence counts. The warrant is the sentence most often missing, because the writer already believes it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['full-essay'] = {
  about: 'five paragraphs, one skeleton, and the writer who built the body before the head',
  more: [{ serial: 'S807', after: 0, alt: 'A boy staring at a blank page, a clock showing twenty minutes gone' }, { serial: 'S808', after: 2, alt: 'A skeleton drawn beside the cards: one head, three ribs, one base' }, { serial: 'S2327', after: 3, alt: 'The first card being written last, a thesis in bold' }, { serial: 'S2328', after: 4, alt: 'A row of index cards with first, also and most of all written between them' }, { serial: 'S2329', after: 5, alt: 'A boy reading his essay aloud at a desk, pencil marks in the margins' }],
  title: 'Body first', art: 'S806', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy writing at a desk with five index cards in a row, the middle three filled in and the end cards blank',
  words: [
    'Diego stared at a blank page for twenty minutes trying to write his introduction. He could not introduce an essay he had not written. Every opening sentence promised something he did not have yet, and he deleted each one.',
    'His teacher told him to skip it. Write the body first. Diego thought that was cheating. Essays started at the top.',
    'They are read from the top. They are not built from it. He wrote three body paragraphs, one reason each, with evidence and a warrant. Then the introduction was easy: it promised exactly what the body delivered, with the thesis at the end of it. The conclusion looked back at the three reasons and said the thesis again in fresh words. Five paragraphs, one skeleton.',
    'The introduction also needed a hook, a first line to make a reader lean in. Diego used a question his cousin had asked him, and the thesis came at the end of the paragraph, where a reader expected it.',
    'Between the body paragraphs he added transitions, first, also, most of all, so a reader could feel the reasons stacking up. Then he read the whole essay aloud and fixed every sentence he tripped over. His ears caught mistakes his eyes had skipped.',
    'A five-paragraph essay is an introduction with the thesis, three body paragraphs and a conclusion that looks back. Write the body first, then the introduction that fits it. Nobody builds a house starting with the roof.',
  ],
};
STORIES['founding-documents'] = {
  about: 'three documents on one wall, and the girl who found that each answered a different question',
  more: [{ serial: 'S810', after: 0, alt: 'A girl in a museum squinting at three old documents that look alike' }, { serial: 'S811', after: 2, alt: 'The Declaration with the word why drawn above it' }, { serial: 'S2309', after: 3, alt: 'A faded paper labeled Articles of Confederation beside an empty treasury chest' }, { serial: 'S2310', after: 4, alt: 'Three linked circles for Congress, the president and the courts, with arrows between them' }, { serial: 'S2311', after: 5, alt: 'The Bill of Rights with a drawn locked gate beside it' }],
  title: 'Why, how, and what may not', art: 'S809', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a museum hall looking at three framed documents side by side',
  words: [
    'On the class trip, Maya stood in front of three framed documents and could not tell them apart. Old paper, fancy writing, the same few names at the bottom. Why did a country need three?',
    'Her guide asked her three questions instead. Why did the colonies leave? How does the government work? What may the government never do? Maya could not answer any of them, but she noticed they were different questions.',
    'Each document answered one. The Declaration of 1776 said why: governments exist for the people, and this one had failed them. The Constitution of 1787 said how: three branches, and a Congress where big and small states share power under the Great Compromise. The Bill of Rights of 1791 said what may never be done: speech, worship, a fair trial, untouched.',
    'Her guide added a fourth paper that was not on the wall. Before the Constitution came the Articles of Confederation, in force from 1781. They made a government so weak it could not even collect taxes to pay its soldiers. The Constitution was the second try.',
    'The three branches keep watch on one another. Congress writes the laws, the president can veto them, Congress can override the veto with two thirds of each house, and the courts can strike down a law that breaks the Constitution. Checks and balances, the guide called it.',
    'The Declaration says why, the Constitution says how, and the Bill of Rights says what may never be done. Three questions, three answers, and a country runs on all of them at once.',
  ],
};
STORIES['early-republic'] = {
  about: 'a new country learning to drive, and the four lessons it took',
  more: [{ serial: 'S813', after: 0, alt: 'A teenager holding a new learner\'s permit beside a textbook open to 1789' }, { serial: 'S814', after: 2, alt: 'A drawn map doubling in size as a hand unfolds it' }, { serial: 'S2312', after: 3, alt: 'Two explorers and a Shoshone woman in a canoe on a western river' }, { serial: 'S2313', after: 4, alt: 'A crowd of men at a polling place, and outside the rope, women and a Black man who could not vote' }, { serial: 'S2314', after: 5, alt: 'A map showing the land Jefferson doubled, with small portraits of Washington and Jackson in the corner' }],
  title: 'Learning to drive', art: 'S812', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a driving lesson on a country road, old maps and a flag on the seat beside him',
  words: [
    'Jamal got his learner\'s permit the week his class started the early republic, and his teacher said the two had a lot in common. A new country in 1789 was a driver with a brand-new license and no idea where the road went.',
    'Jamal did not see it. A country was not a car. But then he thought about his own first weeks: learning the habits, going farther than felt safe, a scare, and then finally being allowed to carry passengers.',
    'That was the order. Washington set the habits: two terms, then go home. Jefferson went farther, buying Louisiana in 1803 and doubling the map. The War of 1812 was the scare that made a loose collection of states feel like one nation. And in Jackson\'s time, states dropped their property rules and many more ordinary white men gained the vote.',
    'Jefferson sent Lewis and Clark west in 1804 to see what he had bought, and Sacagawea, a Shoshone woman, helped guide the way. In 1803 the Supreme Court also claimed a power the Constitution never spelled out: to strike down laws that break it.',
    'Jamal\'s teacher made sure the lesson had its other side. The vote widened only for white men; women, enslaved people and most free Black men were still shut out. And under Jackson, the Indian Removal Act of 1830 forced the Cherokee and other nations from their homelands, on the road later called the Trail of Tears.',
    'Washington set the habits, Jefferson doubled the land, the War of 1812 made a nation, and Jackson widened the vote for some while driving others from their land. A republic learns to drive the way a teenager does, and it can hurt people on the road.',
  ],
};
STORIES['sectional-crisis'] = {
  about: 'every new state a coin toss, and the compromises that bought less time each throw',
  more: [{ serial: 'S816', after: 0, alt: 'A girl reading a chapter about each new state while the whole nation holds its breath on the map' }, { serial: 'S817', after: 1, alt: 'A ledger with two columns, slave and free, kept even by hand' }, { serial: 'S2315', after: 2, alt: 'The deals drawn in a row: the Missouri Compromise of 1820, the Compromise of 1850, Kansas in 1854, a courtroom for Dred Scott and a ballot for Lincoln' }, { serial: 'S2316', after: 3, alt: 'A printing press running copies of a novel in 1852' }, { serial: 'S2317', after: 4, alt: 'South Carolina delegates signing a paper to leave the Union in December 1860' }],
  title: 'The coin toss', art: 'S815', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a map of the United States flipping a coin over each new territory',
  words: [
    'Rosa noticed something strange in the chapter. Every time the country added a state, the whole nation held its breath. Why did one more state matter so much?',
    'Because each new state was a coin toss: slave or free. Every state sent senators, and whichever side had more senators could make the laws. The map was a scoreboard, and both sides counted every square.',
    'So they made deals. The Missouri Compromise of 1820 kept the score even. The Compromise of 1850 bought a few more years. The Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854 let settlers vote and turned Kansas into a battlefield. Then the Dred Scott decision said the score could not be kept at all, and in 1860 Lincoln won without a single southern state. The tosses were over.',
    'The Compromise of 1850 also carried the Fugitive Slave Act, which forced people in free states to help return those who had escaped. Many Northerners who had looked away from slavery now had to face it, and a novel, Uncle Tom\'s Cabin, made millions more feel it in 1852.',
    'The score on the map was about people. By 1860 nearly four million people were enslaved in the United States. When Lincoln was elected, South Carolina left the Union in December 1860, and other states soon followed.',
    'Each new state asked slave or free, and the compromises of 1820, 1850 and 1854 each bought less time than the last. Dred Scott and Lincoln\'s election ended the bargaining. A country can only put off a question about human freedom for so long.',
  ],
};
STORIES['reconstruction'] = {
  about: 'three promises written into the Constitution, and the century it took to keep them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S819', after: 0, alt: 'The 13th, 14th and 15th written on three doors, one after another' }, { serial: 'S820', after: 1, alt: 'A school for freed people in a small wooden building, students of every age with slates' }, { serial: 'S2318', after: 2, alt: 'Soldiers marching away from a Southern town in 1877' }, { serial: 'S2319', after: 3, alt: 'A crowd at a march in the 1960s holding the same three numbers' }, { serial: 'S2320', after: 4, alt: 'A president signing a law with civil rights leaders standing behind him' }],
  title: 'Three promises', art: 'S818', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on courthouse steps holding three folded papers, a long shadow stretching down the steps',
  words: [
    'Theo read the three amendments in a row and thought the story was over. The 13th ended slavery. The 14th made everyone born here a citizen, with equal protection of the laws. The 15th said no one could be denied the vote because of race; women would wait until 1920. Three promises, all in writing, by 1870. What was left to fight about?',
    'For a few years the promises held. Freed people opened schools and churches, and Black men voted and served in office. In 1870 Hiram Revels of Mississippi became the first Black United States senator.',
    'Then Theo turned the page to 1877 and the soldiers went home from the South. Within a few years, the same states that had signed the promises found ways around them: poll taxes, literacy tests, laws that split every town in two. The promises stayed on the page and left the streets.',
    'It took nearly a century to bring them back. The marches, the courts and the laws of the 1950s and 1960s did not write new promises. They forced the country to keep the three it already had. Theo\'s grandmother had been at one of those marches, carrying a sign with three numbers on it.',
    'The laws finally closed the loopholes. The 24th Amendment of 1964 banned poll taxes in federal elections, and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 ended the literacy tests.',
    'The 13th, 14th and 15th Amendments promised freedom, citizenship and the vote; after 1877 the promise was broken for decades, and the civil rights movement made the country keep it. A promise on paper is the beginning of a story, not the end.',
  ],
};
// Grades 9 and 10, told for readers.
STORIES['multi-step-equations'] = {
  about: 'socks on both sides of the bed, and the equation that cleaned up the same way',
  more: [{ serial: 'S822', after: 0, alt: 'A teenage boy frowning at 5x + 3 = 2x + 15 in a notebook beside a messy bed' }, { serial: 'S823', after: 2, alt: 'The socks gathered into one basket on one side' }, { serial: 'S2330', after: 3, alt: 'Pockets emptied onto a bed beside 3(x + 2) written out' }, { serial: 'S2331', after: 4, alt: 'Two equations side by side, one ending in 1 = 2 marked no solution, one ending in 2 = 2 marked every number' }, { serial: 'S2332', after: 5, alt: 'An equation cleaned up line by line: every x gathered on one side, every number on the other, then x = 4' }],
  title: 'Two sides of the bed', art: 'S821', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage boy standing between two piles of socks on either side of a bed, an equation written on the wall',
  words: [
    'Owen\'s room had socks on both sides of the bed, and his math homework had x on both sides of the equals sign. 5x + 3 = 2x + 15. He had solved plenty of equations with x on one side. This one felt like it was fighting back.',
    'He tried subtracting 3, then got stuck staring at 5x and 2x on opposite sides, like two piles of laundry with a bed in between. Nothing he knew told him which pile to touch first.',
    'His sister told him to clean the room. Gather every sock on one side. Take 2x from both sides: 3x + 3 = 15. Now gather the numbers on the other side. Take 3 from both: 3x = 12. Divide by 3: x = 4. He put 4 back into the original, and both sides said 23.',
    'The next one had parentheses: 3(x + 2) = 2x + 10. His sister said to empty the parentheses first, the way you empty pockets before laundry: 3x + 6 = 2x + 10. Take 2x from both sides and 6 from both sides, and x = 4 again.',
    'Then she showed him two strange ones. x + 1 = x + 2 cleans up to 1 = 2, which is never true, so it has no solution at all. 2x + 2 = 2(x + 1) cleans up to 2 = 2, which is always true, so every number works.',
    'Whatever the shape of the equation, the moves are the same. Clear any parentheses, gather every x on one side and every number on the other, then undo what is left and check. A room and an equation both clean up the same way.',
  ],
};
STORIES['functions'] = {
  about: 'a vending machine that never lied, and the rule inside it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S825', after: 0, alt: 'The button 3 pressed and one pretzel dropping' }, { serial: 'S826', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher showing a machine with a rule inside: press 1, get 3; press 2, get 5; press 3, get 7' }, { serial: 'S2333', after: 2, alt: 'f(3) = 2 × 3 + 1 = 7 written across the machine window' }, { serial: 'S2334', after: 3, alt: 'Two buttons, 3 and 4, both dropping pretzels' }, { serial: 'S2335', after: 4, alt: 'A straight line on a graph with a vertical line crossing it once' }],
  title: 'The honest machine', art: 'S824', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl pressing a button on a vending machine, a small drawn rule floating in its window',
  words: [
    'The vending machine outside the gym was the most honest thing at Lena\'s school. Press 3, get a pretzel. Every single time. Press 3 again, another pretzel. Never a candy bar, never nothing, never two.',
    'Her teacher called it a function, and Lena thought that was an odd name for a snack machine. Then he showed her one with a rule inside: press 1, get 3. Press 2, get 5. Press 3, get 7. Lena could not see the machine\'s insides, but she could see its habit.',
    'The rule was f(x) = 2x + 1. Press 3 and the machine doubles it and adds one: f(3) = 7. One input, one output, always the same. If pressing 3 ever gave two different answers, it would not be a function. It would be a broken machine.',
    'Lena asked about the machine by the library, where buttons 3 and 4 both gave pretzels. That was still a function, her teacher said. Two inputs may share an output. What a function may never do is give two outputs for one input.',
    'He drew the rule as a graph, a straight line, and showed her the test: run a vertical line anywhere across it. If the line ever crosses the graph twice, one input has two outputs, and it is not a function.',
    'A function gives one output for each input, every time. f(3) means the rule applied to 3. When you can trust a machine the way Lena trusts the pretzel button, you are looking at a function.',
  ],
};
STORIES['systems-of-equations'] = {
  about: 'two clues at a birthday party, and the one pair of numbers that fit both',
  more: [{ serial: 'S828', after: 0, alt: 'The first clue: two numbers that add to 5' }, { serial: 'S829', after: 1, alt: 'The second clue card: their difference is 1, with pairs listed below it' }, { serial: 'S2336', after: 2, alt: 'The lock opening on 3 and 2' }, { serial: 'S2337', after: 3, alt: 'Two equations stacked with the y terms crossed out as they cancel' }, { serial: 'S2338', after: 4, alt: 'Two lines on graph paper crossing at the point 3, 2' }],
  title: 'Two clues', art: 'S827', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a party holding two folded clue cards, a wrapped gift with a number lock on it',
  words: [
    'The gift had a number lock, and Marcus\'s uncle gave him two clues on two cards. Two numbers add to 5. Their difference is 1. Marcus tried the first clue alone and found too many answers: 0 and 5, 1 and 4, 2 and 3.',
    'The second clue alone was no better: 4 and 3, 7 and 6, a hundred and ninety-nine. One clue narrowed the world. It did not pin it down.',
    'He used them together. From the first card, the second number was 5 minus the first. He put that into the second clue: the first number minus, in parentheses, 5 minus the first, equals 1. Opening the parentheses flips the minus inside to a plus, so two of the first number, minus 5, make 1. Two of the first are 6, and the first number is 3. The second was 2, and the lock opened.',
    'His uncle showed him a quicker way. Stack the clues: x plus y is 5, x minus y is 1. Add them, and the ys cancel: 2x is 6, so x is 3. Adding equations to cancel one unknown is called elimination.',
    'Then he drew both clues as lines on graph paper. Each line held every pair that fit one card. They crossed at one point, 3 and 2, the only pair on both lines. The answer to a system is where the lines meet.',
    'A system of equations is two clues about the same unknowns. Substitute one into the other, or add them to cancel an unknown, solve, then check the pair in both. One clue narrows; two clues decide.',
  ],
};
STORIES['factoring'] = {
  about: 'a rectangle that had been multiplied out, and the girl who un-multiplied it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S831', after: 0, alt: 'A girl at a desk staring at x² + 5x + 6 beside a cake that will not un-bake' }, { serial: 'S832', after: 1, alt: 'The rectangle cut into four parts: x squared, 3x, 2x, 6' }, { serial: 'S2339', after: 2, alt: 'Two numbers, 2 and 3, circled, with a product of 6 and a sum of 5' }, { serial: 'S2340', after: 3, alt: 'Minus 3 and 2 circled, with a product of minus 6 and a sum of minus 1' }, { serial: 'S2341', after: 4, alt: 'A square with a smaller square cut from its corner, labeled x² minus 9' }],
  title: 'Un-multiplying', art: 'S830', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a desk with a rectangle drawn on graph paper, its sides labeled x + 2 and x + 3',
  words: [
    'Priya could multiply (x + 2) by (x + 3) and get x² + 5x + 6 without thinking. Then the homework handed her x² + 5x + 6 and asked where it came from. Going backward felt like un-baking a cake.',
    'She drew the rectangle. Its area was x² + 5x + 6, and its sides had to be x plus something and x plus something else. The somethings had to multiply to 6, the corner piece. And they had to add to 5, the strip along the middle.',
    'Two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to 5: 2 and 3. So x² + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3). And once it was two factors, a new door opened: if the whole thing equals zero, one of the factors must be zero, so x is either minus 2 or minus 3.',
    'Her teacher gave her one with a minus: x² − x − 6. Two numbers that multiply to minus 6 and add to minus 1: minus 3 and 2. So it is (x − 3)(x + 2), and she checked by multiplying it back out.',
    'Then a shortcut with no middle at all: x² − 9. There is no x term, so the two numbers must cancel each other: 3 and minus 3. x² − 9 = (x − 3)(x + 3), a pattern called the difference of squares.',
    'To factor, find two numbers that multiply to c and add to b. If a product is zero, one of the factors is zero, which is how a factored equation gives up its answers. Un-multiplying is baking in reverse.',
  ],
};
STORIES['exponential-growth'] = {
  about: 'a rumor that doubled at every telling, and the class that counted it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S834', after: 0, alt: 'A boy whispering in a hallway at first period, two friends leaning in' }, { serial: 'S835', after: 1, alt: 'A tree diagram: one, two, four, eight' }, { serial: 'S2342', after: 2, alt: 'Two lines on a graph, one climbing gently, one shooting up' }, { serial: 'S2343', after: 3, alt: 'A notebook showing 2 to the tenth power is 1,024' }, { serial: 'S2344', after: 4, alt: 'A bar chart of 100 dollars growing to about 200 over ten years' }],
  title: 'The rumor', art: 'S833', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy telling a rumor to two friends at first period, and by lunch the whole school whispering',
  words: [
    'Diego told two friends a rumor at first period. By lunch the whole school knew. He had told exactly two people. How could two become six hundred by noon?',
    'His math teacher asked him to count. He told two. Each of them told two: four. Then eight, then sixteen. Diego assumed the rumor grew by a couple of people every round, and by ten rounds that would be twenty or so. Not six hundred.',
    'But it was not adding two each round. It was doubling. Two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two. After ten rounds, over a thousand. Adding the same amount each step is linear and climbs like stairs. Multiplying by the same amount each step is exponential and climbs like a rocket.',
    'His teacher wrote it as a power. In round n, the number of new listeners is 2 to the nth. Round ten is 2 to the tenth: 1,024 people hearing it in that round alone, more than the whole school.',
    'Things can shrink the same way. A medicine that halves in the blood every six hours goes 100 milligrams, then 50, then 25: exponential decay. And money grows like the rumor: 100 dollars earning 7 percent a year doubles in about ten years, then doubles again.',
    'Adding the same amount each step is linear growth. Multiplying by the same amount is exponential growth, and it runs away from every straight line eventually. A rumor is exponential. So is a savings account, given time.',
  ],
};
STORIES['rhetorical-appeals'] = {
  about: 'three ways to sell one bicycle, and which one fit the claim',
  more: [{ serial: 'S837', after: 0, alt: 'The girl pointing at her own scuffed knees, years of riding' }, { serial: 'S838', after: 1, alt: 'A brother sketching a badge, a heart and a chart on a napkin' }, { serial: 'S2345', after: 2, alt: 'Three signs at the bike: ethos, trust me; pathos, feel this; logos, the reasoning and the numbers' }, { serial: 'S2346', after: 3, alt: 'A bike shop owner holding a receipt beside a new bike and a smiling child' }, { serial: 'S2347', after: 4, alt: 'A sad-faced ad with nothing written under it, crossed out' }],
  title: 'Three ways to sell a bicycle', art: 'S836', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage girl at a garage sale beside a bicycle, three thought bubbles above her with a badge, a heart and a chart',
  words: [
    'Priya was selling her old bicycle at the garage sale and had three pitches ready. Trust me, I have ridden it for years. Imagine the wind on your face on the first warm day of spring. It costs a third of a new one and the frame is aluminum, so it will last for years. The first buyer walked away from all three.',
    'Her brother said she had used every appeal in the book and had no idea what any of them was for. Priya said they were just sentences. He said each one was asking the buyer to trust a different thing.',
    'The first was ethos: trust me, because I know. The second was pathos: feel this, because feeling moves people. The third was logos: here is the reasoning and the numbers. None was better than the others. The question was whether the appeal fit the claim, and a buyer asking whether the brakes worked did not need the wind on his face.',
    'Her brother said the best pitches mix them. A bike shop owner who says the brakes are new, here is the receipt, and your kid will be safe on the hill is using all three at once.',
    'He also warned her about the misuse. An appeal to feeling with nothing underneath it is how bad arguments win: a sad story instead of a reason. Priya decided to keep the wind on your face, but only after the brakes.',
    'Ethos is credibility, pathos is feeling, logos is reasoning. Every argument leans on one or more, and the test is whether the appeal fits the claim. The second buyer asked about the brakes; Priya answered with logos, and the bicycle rolled away.',
  ],
};
STORIES['theme-development'] = {
  about: 'a character who trusted no one on page one and handed over a key on the last',
  more: [{ serial: 'S840', after: 0, alt: 'A boy writing the single word trust on a worksheet' }, { serial: 'S841', after: 1, alt: 'The first page: a girl with her arms crossed, a locked door behind her' }, { serial: 'S2348', after: 2, alt: 'The gap between the first page and the last: a girl who trusted no one, then a key held out, labeled theme' }, { serial: 'S2349', after: 3, alt: 'A neighbor fixing a lock while a girl watches from the doorway' }, { serial: 'S2350', after: 4, alt: 'A key drawn in the margin of a notebook beside the word symbol' }],
  title: 'The key', art: 'S839', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy reading a novel in bed, a small drawn key glowing on the final page',
  words: [
    'Marcus finished the novel and wrote its theme: trust. One word. His teacher said trust was a topic, not a theme, and asked him what the book said about it. Marcus said it was about trust, and he was starting to feel like they were speaking different languages.',
    'She told him to go back to page one and then to the last page, and to ignore the three hundred pages in between for a minute. Page one: the girl trusted no one, and she had good reasons. Last page: she handed him the key to her house.',
    'That gap was the theme. Not trust, but something like this: trust is a risk that becomes possible only after someone earns it slowly. The three hundred pages were the earning, step by step. The theme was not stated anywhere. It grew, and you could track it by what changed.',
    'His teacher asked where the change turned. Marcus found it on page one hundred and ninety: the girl let a neighbor fix her lock, and watched him the whole time. It was the first thing she let anyone touch.',
    'The key itself was doing work too. A key is a symbol, an object that stands for something bigger. On page one the door was locked. On the last page the key was held out. The author never wrote the word trust; the key said it.',
    'A theme grows through a text. Find what a character believes at the start, what they do at the end, and the sentence that explains the change. That sentence is the theme, and the pages in between are how it developed.',
  ],
};
STORIES['word-choice-effect'] = {
  about: 'a girl who sprinted to the door, and what was lost when she only ran',
  more: [{ serial: 'S843', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning at a sentence on a worksheet, the word sprinted underlined' }, { serial: 'S844', after: 1, alt: 'The word sprinted on a page, then swapped for ran' }, { serial: 'S2351', after: 2, alt: 'A list of what the swap lost: urgency, fear, a reason' }, { serial: 'S2352', after: 3, alt: 'Pairs of words on cards: whispered and said, strolled and walked' }, { serial: 'S2353', after: 4, alt: 'Two short sentences in large letters: She sprinted to the door. It was locked.' }],
  title: 'Sprinted, or ran', art: 'S842', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl who sprinted to the door, and a faded copy of her that only ran, with the question why sprinted written above',
  words: [
    'The sentence said she sprinted to the door. Lena\'s teacher asked what sprinted was doing there. Lena said it meant ran. Then why not write ran, the teacher asked. Lena did not know, and it bothered her all the way home.',
    'She tried the swap at her desk. She ran to the door. It was still a sentence, still true, still a girl and a door. But something had leaked out of it, and she could not say what.',
    'Then she could. Sprinted said the girl was afraid, or late, or desperate, that something was chasing her or waiting for her. Ran said nothing about why. The plainer word kept the fact and dropped the feeling. What was lost in the swap was exactly what the writer had chosen the word for.',
    'She tried it on other words. Whispered carries a secret that said does not. Strolled carries time to spare that walked does not. Each word had the same bare meaning as its plain cousin and a different feeling riding on top.',
    'Her teacher showed her that sentences do it too. She sprinted to the door. It was locked. Two short sentences, and the reader\'s heart speeds up. Stretch the same fact into one long, winding sentence and the panic drains away.',
    'To find what a word is doing, swap it for a plainer one. What is lost is its effect. Sprinted carries fear and urgency that ran leaves behind, and a writer who chose it was telling you something without saying it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['credible-sources'] = {
  about: 'two web pages about the same medicine, and the one that showed its work',
  more: [{ serial: 'S846', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a library computer reading a bright page that promises a cure' }, { serial: 'S847', after: 1, alt: 'A shop button hiding at the bottom of the nameless page' }, { serial: 'S2354', after: 2, alt: 'The page with an author, a date and a list of sources' }, { serial: 'S2355', after: 3, alt: 'A second browser tab searching who runs the bright page, a company name found' }, { serial: 'S2356', after: 4, alt: 'A list of studies on the plain page with two titles checked off' }],
  title: 'The page with no name', art: 'S845', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a library computer with two web pages side by side, one with an author and date, one blank where they should be',
  words: [
    'Theo found two pages about the same vitamin. One was plain, with an author, a date and a list of studies at the bottom. The other was bright and confident, promised the vitamin cured colds, and had no name on it anywhere. Theo believed the bright one. It sounded surer.',
    'His teacher asked him to find out who had written it. He could not. When it was written. He could not. Where its claims came from. It did not say. Then she asked him to scroll to the bottom, where a button sold the vitamin.',
    'The plain page showed its work: an author with a reason to know, a date, evidence he could follow to its source. The bright page showed none of that, and the one thing it did show was who benefited if he believed it. Sure is not the same as credible. Sure is cheap.',
    'Then she taught him a trick fact-checkers use. Do not keep reading the page to decide if it is good. Open a new tab and search for who runs it. Theo found that the bright page belonged to the company that sold the vitamin.',
    'The plain page had its own checks to pass. Its date mattered, because science changes, and it was only two years old. Its author worked at a university and had nothing to sell. And the studies it listed were real; he found two of them.',
    'A credible source shows its work: an author, a date, evidence you can follow, and a reason to know. Then ask who benefits if you believe it. The page with no name had a reason to sound sure.',
  ],
};
STORIES['punnett-squares'] = {
  about: 'a raffle with four tickets, and the odds of brown eyes',
  more: [{ serial: 'S849', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning at a family photo, brown-eyed parents and a blue-eyed little brother' }, { serial: 'S850', after: 1, alt: 'Two parents each holding two cards, B and b' }, { serial: 'S2360', after: 2, alt: 'The grid showing BB, Bb, Bb and bb' }, { serial: 'S2361', after: 3, alt: 'Several genes drawn as threads, all leading to one eye' }, { serial: 'S2362', after: 4, alt: 'Gregor Mendel in a monastery garden counting yellow and green peas' }],
  title: 'Four tickets', art: 'S848', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a kitchen table with a two-by-two grid drawn on paper, four squares filled in',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s parents both have brown eyes, and her little brother has blue. Rosa wanted to know how that was possible. A friend said her brother must be adopted. Rosa knew he was not.',
    'Her biology teacher drew a raffle. Each parent has two cards for eye color and gives one at random. Both her parents carry a capital B, for brown, and a small b, for blue. Brown is dominant: one B is enough to show.',
    'Four tickets in the raffle: BB, Bb, Bb and bb. Three of the four show brown. One in four shows blue, because it got a small b from each parent. Her brother drew the one ticket. Nothing strange about it. Just odds.',
    'Her teacher added an honest warning. Real eye color is shaped by several genes working together, so real families do not follow the square exactly. The square works cleanly for traits set by a single gene, like the pea colors Gregor Mendel counted in his garden in the 1860s.',
    'Mendel crossed plants that each carried one yellow and one green allele, and yellow was dominant. He counted thousands of seeds, and they came out close to three yellow for every green, just as the four tickets predict.',
    'Each parent gives one of two alleles; capital is dominant, small is recessive. A Punnett square shows the odds for each child, not a promise about any one of them. Three tickets in four is not a guarantee, and one in four is not a mystery.',
  ],
};
STORIES['respiration-and-photosynthesis'] = {
  about: 'a leaf and a lung, running the same reaction in opposite directions',
  more: [{ serial: 'S852', after: 1, alt: 'A notebook with the two reactions side by side, the same words on opposite sides of the arrow' }, { serial: 'S853', after: 2, alt: 'A leaf with arrows: carbon dioxide and water in, sugar and oxygen out' }, { serial: 'S2363', after: 3, alt: 'A whiteboard showing six carbon dioxide and six water making one sugar and six oxygen' }, { serial: 'S2364', after: 4, alt: 'A cutaway of a leaf cell showing both chloroplasts and mitochondria' }, { serial: 'S2365', after: 5, alt: 'A lung where respiration burns sugar with oxygen and gives back carbon dioxide and water, the arrows reversed from the leaf' }],
  title: 'The leaf and the lung', art: 'S851', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy breathing out toward a plant in a sunny garden, arrows drawn between his chest and its leaves',
  words: [
    'Kai learned photosynthesis in the morning and respiration after lunch, and by the bus ride home he had them tangled together. Both had sugar. Both had oxygen. Both had carbon dioxide. He could not remember which one made what.',
    'He wrote them side by side and noticed something. The same words appeared in both, but on opposite sides of the arrow.',
    'The leaf takes carbon dioxide and water, uses light, and makes sugar and oxygen. The lung takes that oxygen, the body burns the sugar with it for energy, and out come carbon dioxide and water. The plant builds the fuel and the animal spends it.',
    'His teacher wrote the balanced version: six carbon dioxide plus six water, with light, make one sugar and six oxygen. Respiration runs the same balance backward. The overall equations mirror each other, though the steps inside the cell are quite different.',
    'Then she caught the most common mistake. Plants breathe too. Their cells have mitochondria, the same energy factories ours have, and they burn their own sugar day and night. In daylight, a leaf simply builds much more sugar than it burns. At night, with no light, it only burns, and it gives off carbon dioxide like any animal.',
    'Photosynthesis makes sugar and oxygen from carbon dioxide, water and light. Respiration burns sugar with oxygen for energy and gives back carbon dioxide and water. They are the same equation read in opposite directions, and every breath connects the two.',
  ],
};
STORIES['evidence-for-evolution'] = {
  about: 'three witnesses who never met, telling the same story',
  more: [{ serial: 'S855', after: 0, alt: 'A girl and her cousin arguing in a museum lobby' }, { serial: 'S856', after: 1, alt: 'A courtroom with three witnesses who never met, each pointing to the same map' }, { serial: 'S2366', after: 2, alt: 'A fossil of an ancient whale with small hind legs' }, { serial: 'S2367', after: 3, alt: 'An arm, a flipper and a wing side by side, the same bones shaded' }, { serial: 'S2368', after: 4, alt: 'Two finches on an island rock, one with a deeper beak cracking a hard seed' }],
  title: 'Three witnesses', art: 'S854', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a museum between a fossil case, a display of an arm, a flipper and a wing, and a DNA model',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s cousin said evolution was just a guess, because nobody was there to see it. Maya did not know how to answer. She had not been there either.',
    'Her teacher asked how a court decides what happened when nobody in the room saw it. Witnesses. And the strongest case is three witnesses who never met, questioned separately, telling the same story. A single witness can be wrong; three who agree are hard to dismiss.',
    'Fossils are one witness: older rocks hold simpler forms, and in between are creatures like whales with tiny hind legs. Bones are another: an arm, a flipper and a wing share one bone plan under the skin. DNA is the third: the most closely related animals share the most letters. Three lines of evidence, gathered by different people in different ways, and they agree.',
    'The DNA witness can even overrule looks. A dolphin looks like a shark, but its DNA sits with the mammals, closest to the hippos. The bones agree: under the flipper is a hand.',
    'And some change can be watched. On one Galápagos island, scientists measured finch beaks for decades. After a drought left only hard seeds, the next generation had deeper beaks. Germs that outlast a medicine are the same story, told fast.',
    'Fossils, homologous structures and DNA are separate lines of evidence, gathered in different ways, and they point to the same history. Nobody needs to have been there when three witnesses who never met agree.',
  ],
};
STORIES['cell-division'] = {
  about: 'a recipe book copied two ways, once whole and once by halves',
  more: [{ serial: 'S858', after: 0, alt: 'A boy looking at a healing cut on his finger, a bandage beside it' }, { serial: 'S859', after: 1, alt: 'A full photocopy of the book, forty-six pages, two identical stacks' }, { serial: 'S2369', after: 2, alt: 'Four thin half-books of twenty-three pages each' }, { serial: 'S2370', after: 3, alt: 'Two cooks swapping recipe cards across a table' }, { serial: 'S2371', after: 4, alt: 'One cell dividing into two, then two into four, beside a stop sign' }],
  title: 'The recipe book, copied twice', art: 'S857', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a kitchen counter with a thick recipe book, a photocopier beside it',
  words: [
    'Jamal cut his finger and a week later the skin was whole again, made of new cells. He also learned that a baby starts from two cells, one from each parent. If cells just copied themselves, how did the copy know when to be whole and when to be half?',
    'His teacher used a recipe book. Every one of Jamal\'s cells holds the same book: forty-six pages, his chromosomes. To heal a cut, a cell photocopies all forty-six and splits in two. Both new cells have the whole book. That is mitosis.',
    'Meiosis is different. It makes egg and sperm cells, and it copies only half the book, twenty-three pages, into each of four cells. Then a half-book from each parent joins to make a whole new one. Forty-six pages again, but a book nobody has ever read before.',
    'Before the halves are made, each pair of matching chromosomes trades pieces, the way two cooks might swap a few recipe cards. So every egg and every sperm carries its own shuffle, and brothers and sisters get different books unless they are identical twins.',
    'Mitosis runs all the time, which is why skin heals and children grow. When it runs without stopping, ignoring the signals that should halt it, the result is cancer, which is why doctors study cell division so closely.',
    'Mitosis makes two identical cells for growth and repair. Meiosis makes four cells with half the chromosomes, for eggs and sperm, so two halves can make a new whole. One book, copied two ways, for two different jobs.',
  ],
};
STORIES['protein-synthesis'] = {
  about: 'a library book that never leaves the building, and the photocopy that does',
  more: [{ serial: 'S861', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning at a textbook that says DNA never leaves the nucleus' }, { serial: 'S862', after: 1, alt: 'The chained book, DNA, in a room marked nucleus' }, { serial: 'S2372', after: 2, alt: 'A workbench reading the copy three letters at a time, beads threaded into a chain' }, { serial: 'S2373', after: 3, alt: 'A chain of beads folding into a tight, knotted shape' }, { serial: 'S2374', after: 4, alt: 'Round red blood cells beside a few bent into sickle shapes' }],
  title: 'The book that stays', art: 'S860', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a library photocopier copying one page from a large reference book chained to a desk',
  words: [
    'Priya read that DNA holds the instructions for every protein in the body, and that DNA never leaves the nucleus. So how did the instructions get to where the proteins were built? A recipe locked in a room cannot cook anything.',
    'Her teacher pointed at the reference book chained to the library desk. You cannot take it home. You can photocopy one page. The copy goes to the kitchen; the book stays safe.',
    'DNA is the chained book. Transcription photocopies one recipe, one gene, into a strip of RNA that can leave the nucleus. Translation happens at the ribosome, which reads the RNA three letters at a time, and each three-letter word names one amino acid. Bead by bead, the amino acids link into a protein.',
    'Each three-letter word is called a codon, and there are twenty kinds of amino acid to fetch. A few codons mean stop. When the stop word comes, the chain is finished, and it folds itself into the shape that lets it do its job.',
    'One letter matters. In sickle cell disease, a single changed letter in the gene for hemoglobin swaps one amino acid, and the protein behaves differently enough to bend red blood cells out of shape. One typo in the photocopy, and the cooking changes.',
    'Transcription copies a gene into RNA; translation reads that RNA three letters at a time to build a protein. The book stays in the library, and the photocopy does the cooking.',
  ],
};
STORIES['carbon-and-nitrogen-cycles'] = {
  about: 'one carbon atom riding the air, a leaf, a rabbit and a fox, and never getting off',
  more: [{ serial: 'S864', after: 0, alt: 'A boy holding a sandwich in a meadow, looking up at the air in disbelief' }, { serial: 'S865', after: 1, alt: 'The carbon atom in a leaf, then in a rabbit, then breathed back out' }, { serial: 'S2375', after: 2, alt: 'Bacteria at a plant root turning air nitrogen into food' }, { serial: 'S2376', after: 3, alt: 'Ancient ferns turning into a seam of coal underground, and smoke rising from a chimney above' }, { serial: 'S2377', after: 4, alt: 'A green algae bloom on a river beside a farm field after rain' }],
  title: 'The atom on the bus', art: 'S863', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a meadow watching a rabbit nibble a leaf, a small drawn atom hopping from air to leaf to rabbit',
  words: [
    'Theo asked where the carbon in his sandwich had come from. The bread, his teacher said. The wheat. And before the wheat? The air. Theo did not believe that bread was made of air.',
    'She followed one carbon atom. It rode the air as carbon dioxide. A wheat leaf pulled it in and built it into sugar. A rabbit ate the leaf and breathed the carbon back out. A different leaf caught it. A fox ate the rabbit. The atom never got off the bus.',
    'Nitrogen rides a different loop. Most of the air is nitrogen, but plants cannot use it straight from the sky. Bacteria in the soil change it into a form roots can drink, and animals eat the plants. When things die and rot, bacteria hand the nitrogen back to the soil and the air.',
    'Some carbon got off the bus for a very long time. Plants buried millions of years ago became coal and oil, locking their carbon underground. Burning them puts that old carbon back into the air all at once, which is why the air now holds more carbon dioxide than it did two hundred years ago.',
    'Farmers add nitrogen to fields as fertilizer. When rain washes the extra into rivers, it feeds algae instead, and the algae can grow so thick that it uses up the oxygen fish need.',
    'Carbon and nitrogen circle between air, soil, water and living things. Plants take carbon from the air, bacteria make nitrogen usable, and nothing is ever used up. The atoms in the sandwich have been riding the bus for billions of years.',
  ],
};
STORIES['homeostasis'] = {
  about: 'a body that works like a thermostat, sensing a change and undoing it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S867', after: 1, alt: 'A girl adjusting the hallway thermostat to 70' }, { serial: 'S868', after: 2, alt: 'Sweat on a hot day, cooling the skin' }, { serial: 'S2378', after: 3, alt: 'A teenager drinking water after a long run, a drawn kidney holding on to water' }, { serial: 'S2379', after: 4, alt: 'A person checking blood sugar and taking insulin at a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S2380', after: 5, alt: 'Shivering on a cold day as negative feedback keeps the temperature steady' }],
  title: 'The thermostat inside', art: 'S866', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl adjusting a wall thermostat, a faint drawing of a body beside it with the same dial on its chest',
  words: [
    'On the hottest day of the year Ana\'s temperature was close to 98.6, and on the coldest day it was close to 98.6, within about a degree both times. She had never once thought about it. Then her teacher asked how a body knows.',
    'Ana thought about the thermostat in the hallway. Set it to 70. When the room gets warm, the cooling comes on. When it gets cold, the heat comes on. The room drifts, the thermostat notices, and it pushes the other way.',
    'That is homeostasis, and the push is called negative feedback: sense a change, act to undo it. Too hot, sweat, and the sweat cools you. Too cold, shiver, and the shivering warms you. Then stop. Blood sugar works the same way, with insulin after a meal and glucagon between them.',
    'Water works the same way. Sweat all afternoon and your blood gets a little saltier; your brain notices, makes you thirsty, and tells your kidneys to hold on to water. Drink a lot and the kidneys let more go.',
    'When a loop breaks, the body needs help. In type 1 diabetes the body stops making insulin, so blood sugar climbs and stays high, and people take insulin as medicine to close the loop by hand.',
    'Homeostasis keeps the inside steady by negative feedback: sense a change, act to undo it. Temperature, water and blood sugar all run on it. The thermostat in the hallway is the body\'s oldest idea.',
  ],
};
STORIES['analysis-paragraph'] = {
  about: 'a quotation that proved nothing until the sentence after it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S870', after: 0, alt: 'A margin note that says so? beside a two-sentence paragraph' }, { serial: 'S871', after: 1, alt: 'The quote about the house that held its breath, with nothing after it to say what it proved' }, { serial: 'S2399', after: 2, alt: 'The added sentence, glowing, connecting the two' }, { serial: 'S2400', after: 3, alt: 'The word held circled in a quotation, an arrow drawn to the word waiting' }, { serial: 'S2401', after: 4, alt: 'A long quotation trimmed to four words woven into a sentence' }],
  title: 'The sentence after the quote', art: 'S869', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a desk with a paragraph on her screen, a quotation in the middle and a blank line beneath it',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s paragraph was two sentences long. Claim: the narrator is lonely. Quote: the house held its breath. She had found the perfect line and she was done. Her teacher wrote one word in the margin: so?',
    'So? The quote proved it. The house held its breath. That was loneliness. Rosa read it again and saw that the quote did not say lonely anywhere. It said house and breath. She was the one who had connected them, in her head, and never on the page.',
    'She wrote the missing sentence. A house that holds its breath is waiting for someone, and so is the narrator; the author makes the building feel what she cannot say. Claim, quotation, and then a sentence saying what the quotation shows. The third sentence was the analysis. The first two were setup.',
    'Her teacher showed her how to squeeze more from the line. Pick the single word doing the most work. Held means waiting, not just silent; something that holds its breath expects something to happen. Rosa added that, and her paragraph grew teeth.',
    'She also learned to cut the quote down. The whole sentence ran twelve words, and only four mattered, so she wove them into her own sentence instead of setting them on a shelf by themselves.',
    'An analysis paragraph is a claim, a quotation, and a sentence saying what the quotation shows. The quotation alone proves nothing; the sentence after it does the proving. Rosa had skipped the only sentence that was hers.',
  ],
};
STORIES['narrative-with-a-turn'] = {
  about: 'an ordinary evening, a phone that rang, and the writer who learned to slow down there',
  more: [{ serial: 'S873', after: 0, alt: 'A four-sentence story on a page with a note: you drove past the turn' }, { serial: 'S874', after: 1, alt: 'A clock face with ten seconds stretched into an hour' }, { serial: 'S2402', after: 2, alt: 'A hand reaching for the ringing phone, the clock stopped' }, { serial: 'S2403', after: 3, alt: 'Dinner dishes and a math book, everything ordinary' }, { serial: 'S2404', after: 4, alt: 'A crossed-out line on a draft that says I felt scared' }],
  title: 'The phone rang', art: 'S872', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a kitchen table with homework as a phone rings on the counter, the moment frozen',
  words: [
    'Kai wrote about the night his family got the news, and the whole thing took four sentences. Dinner. Homework. The phone rang. It was Grandma, and she was okay. His teacher said the story had a turn and he had driven straight past it.',
    'A turn? Kai said the phone rang and that was that. But when he thought back, the ten seconds between the ring and the voice had lasted an hour. He had written them in three words.',
    'He wrote it again. Everything ordinary first: the dishes, the math book, his brother humming. Then the phone, and he slowed all the way down. The second ring. His mother\'s hand stopping over the plate. The way nobody moved. Then the voice, and the way the kitchen changed color. The ending showed what had changed: he did the dishes without being asked.',
    'His teacher gave the two speeds names. Summary moves fast: we ate dinner. Scene moves slowly, second by second, with the senses turned up: the ring, the scrape of a fork, the smell of the soap. A good story uses summary to reach the turn and scene to stay there.',
    'Kai tried one more thing. He cut the sentence that said he felt scared. The stopped hand and the silent kitchen already said it, and the reader believed it more for not being told.',
    'A narrative builds toward a turn, slows down at it, and lets the ending show what changed. The turn is the reason the story exists, and it deserves the most words, not the fewest.',
  ],
};
STORIES['rhetorical-analysis'] = {
  about: 'a speech taken apart into what it argued and how, by a boy who agreed with every word',
  more: [{ serial: 'S876', after: 0, alt: 'A boy getting his paper back with the note: how was it made?' }, { serial: 'S877', after: 2, alt: 'The transcript marked: a librarian\'s twenty years, a child\'s story, a chart' }, { serial: 'S2405', after: 3, alt: 'A city council table a week before a budget vote, a speaker at the podium' }, { serial: 'S2406', after: 4, alt: 'The speech drawn as three blocks in order, child, librarian, chart, with an arrow suggesting a new order' }, { serial: 'S2407', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook listing what the speech argues, which appeals it uses, and whether they fit the claim and the audience' }],
  title: 'Taking it apart', art: 'S875', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in an auditorium with a speech transcript, three colored highlighters laid across it',
  words: [
    'Sam agreed with the speech completely. Fund the library. He wrote that it was a great speech and turned it in. His teacher handed it back and said she had not asked whether he agreed. She had asked how it was made.',
    'How it was made? It was made of words, and the words were right. Sam did not see what else there was to say.',
    'Three questions. What does it argue? Fund the library. Which appeals does it use? A librarian\'s twenty years, for ethos; a child who learned to read there, for pathos; a chart of circulation against budget, for logos. Do the appeals fit the claim? Mostly. The child was moving, but the chart did the real work, and the speech leaned harder on the child. Sam still agreed. Now he could say why the speech worked and where it wobbled.',
    'His teacher added a fourth question: who was it for? The speech went to a city council a week before the budget vote, so the chart mattered more than the child. A council votes on numbers, and the speaker had misjudged her room.',
    'Sam also looked at the order. The speech opened with the child, moved to the librarian, and ended on the chart. Leaving the strongest evidence for last, when the room was tired, was a choice he would have made differently.',
    'A rhetorical analysis asks what a text argues, which appeals it uses, and whether they fit the claim and the audience. Judge the making, not whether you agree. Agreeing is a feeling; analysis is a job.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-shape-of-the-earth'] = {
  about: 'a cracked shell on a soft-boiled egg, and the planet under a girl\'s feet',
  more: [{ serial: 'S879', after: 0, alt: 'A girl at breakfast stopping with her spoon over a cracked egg' }, { serial: 'S880', after: 2, alt: 'Two shell pieces pushed together, one riding up over the other' }, { serial: 'S2381', after: 3, alt: 'A hand beside a ruler showing a few centimeters, labeled one year of plate motion' }, { serial: 'S2382', after: 4, alt: 'A chain of Hawaiian volcanoes drawn over a glowing hot spot, the oldest island worn low' }, { serial: 'S2383', after: 5, alt: 'A world map with mountain ranges and ocean ridges drawn along the cracks' }],
  title: 'The cracked egg', art: 'S878', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at breakfast holding a soft-boiled egg with its shell cracked into plates',
  words: [
    'Maya cracked her breakfast egg all over and stopped with the spoon in the air. The shell was in pieces, but the pieces still covered the egg, sliding a little on the soft inside. Her father said she was looking at the Earth.',
    'The Earth was not an egg, Maya said. It was rock all the way down. Her father asked why, then, there were mountains in some places and earthquakes in others and not everywhere.',
    'Because the crust is cracked into plates, riding on hot, soft rock beneath. Where two pieces push together, one rides up: mountains. Where they grind past each other, they stick and jerk: earthquakes. Where they pull apart, the soft inside rises and hardens: new ocean floor. Mountains sit along the cracks, not in the middle of the pieces.',
    'The plates move about as fast as fingernails grow, a few centimeters a year. That is too slow to feel and fast enough, over millions of years, to open an ocean. The Atlantic is still widening today.',
    'There is one famous exception. Hawaii sits in the middle of the Pacific plate, far from any crack. Beneath it is a hot spot, a plume of heat that punches through the plate. As the plate slides over it, the volcanoes line up in a chain, oldest at one end.',
    'The Earth\'s crust is broken into plates, and mountains, earthquakes and new ocean floor happen where they meet. Mountain ranges and volcano chains mostly follow the plate edges, which is why a map of them looks like a cracked shell.',
  ],
};
STORIES['climate-and-biomes'] = {
  about: 'three dials that set a climate, turned one at a time on a family road trip',
  more: [{ serial: 'S882', after: 0, alt: 'A boy in shorts at breakfast on the coast and in a coat at dinner in the mountains' }, { serial: 'S883', after: 1, alt: 'Three dials on the dashboard turning as the car climbs from green valley to snow, his mother explaining' }, { serial: 'S2384', after: 2, alt: 'A coast town under mild skies next to a desert inland' }, { serial: 'S2385', after: 3, alt: 'Clouds dropping rain on one side of a mountain and a desert on the far side' }, { serial: 'S2386', after: 4, alt: 'A tall tropical mountain drawn in bands: rainforest, forest, grassland, snow' }],
  title: 'Three dials', art: 'S881', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a car on a mountain road, three drawn dials on the dashboard for latitude, height and distance to the sea',
  words: [
    'Jamal\'s family drove from the coast into the mountains in one day, and he wore shorts at breakfast and a coat at dinner. Same country, same date. He asked why the weather had changed so much in three hundred miles.',
    'His mother said three dials had turned. Jamal thought weather was just weather. She asked him to name what had changed about where they were.',
    'Farther from the sea, so the mild air that the ocean lends a coast was gone. Higher up, and temperature drops about six degrees Celsius for every thousand meters. And on a longer trip, farther from the equator, where the sun strikes at a slant. Latitude, elevation and nearness to water. Turn those three dials and you set a climate, and the climate sets what grows, which is the biome.',
    'The mountains had one more trick. Wet air from the sea rises up the near slope, cools and drops its rain. By the time it crosses the top it is dry, so the far side lies in a rain shadow, often a desert.',
    'The dials set the biome. Hot and wet makes rainforest, hot and dry makes desert, cold and dry makes tundra, and the in-betweens make grassland and forest. Climb high enough on a mountain in the tropics and you can walk through several biomes in a single day.',
    'Latitude, elevation and nearness to water decide climate, and climate decides the biome. Temperature drops about six degrees Celsius per thousand meters of height, which is why one road can pass through summer and winter.',
  ],
};
STORIES['reading-maps'] = {
  about: 'a map as a shrunken photograph, and the hour that lived in every fifteen degrees',
  more: [{ serial: 'S885', after: 0, alt: 'A girl with a four-centimeter line on a map and a single sandwich' }, { serial: 'S886', after: 1, alt: 'A ruler on the map: one centimeter, ten kilometers' }, { serial: 'S2387', after: 2, alt: 'A globe with time zones drawn as slices' }, { serial: 'S2388', after: 3, alt: 'A time zone map with lines zigzagging along borders and China in one color' }, { serial: 'S2389', after: 4, alt: 'Greenland and Africa drawn at their true sizes side by side, Africa far larger' }],
  title: 'The shrunken photograph', art: 'S884', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl with a paper map and a ruler on a kitchen table, a clock and a globe beside her',
  words: [
    'Lena measured her hike on the map. Four centimeters. Her father said that was forty kilometers and she would need more than a sandwich. Lena did not see how four centimeters of paper could be forty of anything.',
    'A map is a photograph that has been shrunk, he said, and the scale tells you by how much. One centimeter on the map, ten kilometers on the ground. Four centimeters, forty kilometers. Lena put the hike off.',
    'Then she noticed the map\'s edge marked degrees of longitude, and asked what those were for. The Earth turns all the way around, 360 degrees, in a day, so it turns fifteen degrees every hour. Every fifteen degrees east, the clocks read an hour later. The map held distances and time in the same lines.',
    'Real time zones bend, though. They follow country and state borders, so a town does not split its clock down the middle of Main Street. China is wide enough for about five zones and keeps a single clock for the whole country.',
    'Then her father showed her the one lie every flat map tells. Peeling a round Earth flat stretches something. On a common map Greenland looks about as big as Africa, but Africa is about fourteen times larger.',
    'Scale turns a distance on the map into a distance on the ground. The Earth turns fifteen degrees of longitude every hour, so time zones roughly follow the lines. A map is a shrunken photograph with a clock printed on it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['people-and-places'] = {
  about: 'a city block, a ranch, and the arithmetic of where people are',
  more: [{ serial: 'S888', after: 0, alt: 'Ten people on a ranch the size of a town' }, { serial: 'S889', after: 1, alt: 'Two thousand people on one block, windows stacked high' }, { serial: 'S2390', after: 2, alt: 'A line climbing for two centuries, labeled the share of people in cities' }, { serial: 'S2391', after: 3, alt: 'A notebook showing 12 births minus 8 deaths per thousand' }, { serial: 'S2392', after: 4, alt: 'A crowded city skyline at night beside the words eight billion' }],
  title: 'The block and the ranch', art: 'S887', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy looking from a dense apartment block toward a distant ranch under a wide sky',
  words: [
    'Owen\'s cousin lived on a ranch with ten people for miles. Owen lived on a block with two thousand. His cousin said the ranch was bigger. Owen said the city was. They were both right about different things, and neither knew the word for it.',
    'The word was density: people divided by area. The ranch had more land. The block had more people per square mile, by a factor of thousands. Same country, two ways to live in it.',
    'Then the class looked at how places grow. Natural increase is births minus deaths, per thousand people a year. The urban share is the fraction living in cities, and it has been climbing for two centuries as farms need fewer hands and factories and offices need more. Owen\'s block was the whole trend in one street.',
    'They worked one out. A country with 12 births and 8 deaths for every thousand people a year grows by 4 per thousand, four tenths of a percent, before anyone moves in or out. Migration adds or subtracts the rest.',
    'The world passed eight billion people in 2022, and a little over half of them now live in cities. In 1800, fewer than one person in ten did. The trend on Owen\'s street is the trend of the whole planet.',
    'Density is people divided by area. Natural increase is births minus deaths per thousand. The urban share is city people over all people, and most of the world now lives on the block, not the ranch.',
  ],
};
STORIES['resources-and-work'] = {
  about: 'a paycheck that keeps coming, a jar of coins that does not, and the four kinds of work',
  more: [{ serial: 'S891', after: 0, alt: 'A girl between a paycheck envelope and a shrinking jar of coins' }, { serial: 'S892', after: 1, alt: 'A forest regrowing after a cut and a wind turbine turning' }, { serial: 'S2393', after: 2, alt: 'A coal seam, used once, empty behind it' }, { serial: 'S2394', after: 3, alt: 'A fishing boat with a nearly empty net over a once-crowded sea' }, { serial: 'S2395', after: 4, alt: 'An old copper wire becoming a coil inside a new phone' }],
  title: 'The paycheck and the jar', art: 'S890', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a kitchen table between a paycheck envelope and a jar of coins, a window onto a forest and a mine',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s mother got a paycheck every two weeks. Rosa had a jar of coins that got smaller every time she reached in. Her teacher said the world\'s resources came in those two kinds too.',
    'Rosa did not see how a forest was like a paycheck. Then she cut a Christmas tree with her uncle, and he pointed at the seedlings he had planted where last year\'s trees had stood.',
    'Trees regrow. Wind keeps blowing. Sunlight arrives every morning. Those are renewable, the paycheck that keeps coming. Coal, oil and copper are the jar: used once, and the jar gets lighter. And the work people do with resources comes in four kinds: primary takes them from the ground, secondary makes things from them, tertiary provides services, quaternary works with knowledge.',
    'Her uncle added a catch. A renewable resource is only renewable if you use it no faster than it comes back. Fish are renewable, but catch them faster than they breed and a fishery can collapse in a few years.',
    'The jar can be stretched, too. Copper can be recycled again and again, so a wire in an old house can become a wire in a new phone. Recycling does not refill the jar, but it slows how fast it empties.',
    'Renewable resources come back if they are used no faster than they return; nonrenewable ones are used once. Work is primary, secondary, tertiary or quaternary, from the mine to the factory to the shop to the lab, and a country\'s mix of the four says a lot about its economy.',
  ],
};
STORIES['regions-of-the-world'] = {
  about: 'a map drawn with eyes closed, and the geographer who could',
  more: [{ serial: 'S894', after: 0, alt: 'A boy pointing at Brazil on the wall map while the teacher waits' }, { serial: 'S895', after: 2, alt: 'A blank outline of the world with a few capitals pinned' }, { serial: 'S2396', after: 3, alt: 'A map with Canberra, Ottawa and Brasília starred and Sydney, Toronto and Rio crossed out as capitals' }, { serial: 'S2397', after: 4, alt: 'A closet with shelves labeled with world regions, countries stacked on each' }, { serial: 'S2398', after: 5, alt: 'The same map filling in: Cairo, Paris, Brasília, Ottawa, Mexico City' }],
  title: 'The map behind your eyes', art: 'S893', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with his eyes shut drawing a rough world map on a whiteboard while classmates watch',
  words: [
    'The teacher asked Diego to close his eyes and point to Egypt on the wall map. He pointed at Brazil. Then she asked which sea lay between Italy and Africa. Diego had no idea there was one.',
    'He said he could look anything up in two seconds. She agreed. Then she asked how he would know a news story was wrong if he could not picture where it happened.',
    'A geographer carries a map behind the eyes: which countries share a border, which capital goes with which flag, what sea a ship would cross. Mexico City, Ottawa, Brasília, Paris, Berlin, Cairo, Nairobi, Beijing, Tokyo, Canberra. Diego started with ten capitals and one sea, the Mediterranean, and by spring he could draw the world badly with his eyes closed, which is the whole idea.',
    'The traps taught him most. The capital of Australia is Canberra, not Sydney. Canada\'s is Ottawa, not Toronto. Brazil\'s is Brasília, not Rio de Janeiro. The biggest city is often not the capital.',
    'Then he grouped the countries into world regions, the way a closet has shelves: Latin America, Europe, North Africa and the Middle East, Sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia, East Asia. A new country went on a shelf, and its neighbors came with it.',
    'A geographer carries a mental map of countries, capitals and features. Looking it up tells you where something is; knowing it lets you notice when something is wrong. Draw the world badly from memory, then fix it, and it stays.',
  ],
};
STORIES['angle-relationships'] = {
  about: 'two sticks crossed on a table, and the angles that had to match',
  more: [{ serial: 'S897', after: 0, alt: 'A girl and her brother betting over two crossed chopsticks at a dinner table' }, { serial: 'S898', after: 1, alt: 'The crossed sticks with the two opposite angles shaded the same color' }, { serial: 'S2408', after: 2, alt: 'A triangle cut from paper, its three corners torn off and lined up flat' }, { serial: 'S2409', after: 3, alt: 'Two parallel chopsticks crossed by a third, matching angles in matching colors' }, { serial: 'S2410', after: 4, alt: 'A triangle with one side extended, inside angles of 70 and 50 and an outside angle of 120' }],
  title: 'The bow tie', art: 'S896', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl crossing two chopsticks on a table, the four angles between them drawn faintly',
  words: [
    'Ana crossed two chopsticks on the table and her brother bet her that the angle on the left was bigger than the one on the right. She measured. They were the same. She twisted the sticks and measured again. Still the same, and she could not see why.',
    'Two sticks make four angles, like a bow tie. The two across from each other always matched, no matter how she twisted them. Not roughly. Exactly. That felt like a trick.',
    'It was not a trick. Any two angles side by side along one stick add to 180, a straight line. The left angle and the top angle add to 180. The right angle and the top angle add to 180. So left and right are both 180 minus the top: equal. Then she tore the three corners off a paper triangle and laid them side by side. A straight line. 180.',
    'Her brother laid a third chopstick across two parallel ones. Now there were eight angles, and they came in matching sets. Angles in the same corner of each crossing were equal, and the ones zigzagging across between the parallels were equal too. Parallel lines copy their angles.',
    'One more came from the torn triangle. Extend one side of a triangle, and the outside angle equals the two far inside angles added together. Ana checked it with a protractor: 70 and 50 inside, 120 outside.',
    'Opposite angles are equal. Angles on a straight line add to 180. The three angles of a triangle add to 180. Three facts, and the first two prove the third if you draw one line and look.',
  ],
};
STORIES['similar-triangles'] = {
  about: 'a photo and its enlargement, and the number that made them the same shape',
  more: [{ serial: 'S900', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a print shop measuring a triangle in a small photo' }, { serial: 'S901', after: 1, alt: 'A 3-4-5 triangle beside a 6-8-10 triangle, the same angles marked' }, { serial: 'S2411', after: 2, alt: 'A ruler on both pictures, one side four inches on the photo and eight on the poster' }, { serial: 'S2412', after: 3, alt: 'A man and a tree casting shadows of 4 feet and 20 feet across a lawn' }, { serial: 'S2413', after: 4, alt: 'Four small photos laid edge to edge to cover the enlarged poster' }],
  title: 'The enlargement', art: 'S899', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a print shop counter holding a small photo next to its poster-sized enlargement',
  words: [
    'Owen had a photo enlarged into a poster, and the poster looked exactly like the photo, only big. He measured a triangle in the picture: 3, 4 and 5 inches. On the poster it was 6, 8 and 10. He had guessed the shop added three to each side. Then it would have been 6, 7 and 8.',
    'It was not. Every side had been doubled. And the angles, when he checked them with a protractor, had not changed at all. The shape was the same. Only the size had moved.',
    'That is what similar means. Same angles, same shape, every length multiplied by the same number, the scale factor. From the photo to the poster the factor was 2. From the poster back to the photo it was one half. Find one pair of matching sides, divide, and you have the factor for every other pair.',
    'His uncle used the same idea to measure a tree without climbing it. Owen, who is 6 feet tall, cast a 4-foot shadow while the tree cast a 20-foot one. The sun made two similar triangles, so the tree was 6 over 4 times 20: 30 feet tall.',
    'Area did something stranger. The poster\'s sides were doubled, but it took four photos to cover it, because area grows by the scale factor squared. Triple the sides and you need nine.',
    'Same angles means the same shape. Find the scale factor from one pair of matching sides, then multiply every side by it. An enlargement is a triangle wearing a bigger coat.',
  ],
};
STORIES['transformations'] = {
  about: 'a picture moved on a wall, flipped in a mirror and turned a quarter, in the language of coordinates',
  more: [{ serial: 'S903', after: 0, alt: 'A girl straightening a crooked picture on a wall marked with a faint grid' }, { serial: 'S904', after: 1, alt: 'A picture held against the mirror on a door, its reflection flipped' }, { serial: 'S2414', after: 2, alt: 'The picture slid right, every point shifted the same amount' }, { serial: 'S2415', after: 4, alt: 'A photocopier printing the picture at 200 percent, twice as big' }, { serial: 'S2416', after: 5, alt: 'The picture turned a quarter turn, its corners swapped' }],
  title: 'The picture on the wall', art: 'S902', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding a framed picture against a wall marked with a faint grid, a mirror on the door beside it',
  words: [
    'Lena hung a picture and her father said it was crooked. She slid it right. Then she turned it a quarter turn for fun and held it against the mirror on the door. Her homework that night had the same three moves with numbers on them, and she did not recognize them.',
    'The homework said translate, reflect, rotate. Lena had been doing exactly those things with a picture frame and a mirror an hour earlier.',
    'Slide it: add to the coordinates, the same amount to every point. Flip it across a mirror: change one sign, x for a vertical mirror, y for a horizontal one. Turn it a quarter turn counterclockwise around the center of the grid: (x, y) becomes (-y, x). Every point obeys the same rule at once, which is why the picture stays a picture.',
    'Her father pointed out what the three moves had in common. The picture never stretched, shrank or bent. Slides, flips and turns keep every length and angle the same, so the picture before and after is congruent, the same shape and size.',
    'Then Lena tried a fourth move the mirror could not do: she photocopied the picture at 200 percent. Every coordinate doubled, (x, y) became (2x, 2y), and the picture came out similar instead of congruent, the same shape but bigger.',
    'Translate by adding to the coordinates. Reflect by flipping one sign. Rotate a quarter turn by sending (x, y) to (-y, x). The frame does not care what you call the moves; the coordinates do.',
  ],
};
STORIES['right-triangle-trig'] = {
  about: 'a boy standing at one corner of a triangle, naming the sides from where he stood',
  more: [{ serial: 'S906', after: 0, alt: 'A boy chalking a big right triangle on the playground' }, { serial: 'S907', after: 1, alt: 'The three sides labeled from his corner: opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse' }, { serial: 'S2417', after: 2, alt: 'SOH CAH TOA written on the ground in chalk' }, { serial: 'S2418', after: 3, alt: 'A teacher 20 meters from an oak tree, sighting up at a 35-degree angle' }, { serial: 'S2419', after: 4, alt: 'A chalk triangle with a 30-degree corner, the short side half as long as the slanted one' }],
  title: 'Standing at the corner', art: 'S905', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy standing at one corner of a large triangle chalked on the ground, pointing across at the far side',
  words: [
    'Sam could never remember which side was opposite and which was adjacent, so he chalked a big right triangle on the playground and stood in one corner. His teacher said that was the whole secret. The names depend on where you stand.',
    'From his corner, one side lay across from him: opposite. One side ran beside him along the ground: adjacent. The long slanted side, across from the right angle, was the hypotenuse no matter where he stood.',
    'Sine was opposite over hypotenuse. Cosine was adjacent over hypotenuse. Tangent was opposite over adjacent. SOH CAH TOA. He walked to the other sharp corner and the opposite and adjacent sides swapped names, because he had swapped corners. The ratios followed the angle he stood at, which was the point.',
    'Then his teacher gave the ratios a job. She stood 20 meters from the old oak by the gym and measured the angle up to its top: 35 degrees. The height was the opposite side and 20 meters the adjacent, so tangent did it: 20 times the tangent of 35 degrees, about 14 meters.',
    'Some angles give neat answers. In a right triangle with a 30-degree angle, the side opposite it is exactly half the hypotenuse, so the sine of 30 degrees is one half. Sam checked it with a tape measure on his chalk triangle, and the chalk agreed.',
    'Sine is opposite over hypotenuse, cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, tangent is opposite over adjacent. The side names depend on which angle you stand at, and the ratios belong to that angle.',
  ],
};
STORIES['arcs-and-sectors'] = {
  about: 'a quarter of a pizza, and the fraction that ran the whole circle',
  more: [{ serial: 'S909', after: 0, alt: 'A girl cutting one quarter out of a pizza while her brother watches' }, { serial: 'S910', after: 1, alt: 'The quarter slice with its crust edge highlighted' }, { serial: 'S2420', after: 2, alt: 'A string laid along the crust of a quarter slice beside a ruler' }, { serial: 'S2421', after: 3, alt: 'A 12-inch pizza with its crust length and area written on the box' }, { serial: 'S2422', after: 4, alt: 'A circle with a 60-degree wedge shaded, one sixth' }],
  title: 'A quarter of the pizza', art: 'S908', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a pizzeria cutting one quarter from a whole pizza, a ninety-degree angle drawn at the center',
  words: [
    'Rosa cut a quarter out of the pizza and her little brother asked how much crust he was getting. Rosa said a quarter, obviously. He asked how much pizza. A quarter. He asked how she knew both without measuring anything.',
    'She had not thought about it. The cut made a ninety-degree angle at the center. Ninety out of the whole 360 was a quarter. So a quarter of everything: crust, sauce, cheese, area.',
    'That was the rule. Angle over 360 is the fraction of the circle. Multiply the circumference by it and you get the arc, the crust. Multiply the area by it and you get the sector, the slice. Her brother measured the crust with a string to check, and it came out a quarter.',
    'Then they did it with numbers. The pizza was 12 inches across, so the whole crust was pi times 12, about 37.7 inches, and a quarter of it was about 9.4. The whole pizza was pi times 6 squared, about 113 square inches, and the quarter slice was about 28.',
    'Her brother wanted a sixty-degree slice instead. Sixty over 360 is one sixth: about 6.3 inches of crust and about 19 square inches of pizza. He decided the quarter was the better deal.',
    'Angle over 360 is the fraction of the circle. Multiply the circumference by that fraction for the arc and the area by it for the sector. One angle at the center runs the whole slice.',
  ],
};
STORIES['spotting-bias'] = {
  about: 'an article where every fact was true and the middle was missing',
  more: [{ serial: 'S912', after: 0, alt: 'A girl checking facts in a newspaper article with a highlighter at a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S913', after: 1, alt: 'Charged words in the article circled: slammed, admitted, so-called' }, { serial: 'S2423', after: 2, alt: 'A list of people quoted, with an empty line where the other side should be' }, { serial: 'S2424', after: 3, alt: 'A headline and first paragraph highlighted, a small number buried near the end' }, { serial: 'S2425', after: 4, alt: 'A neighborhood newsletter beside the newspaper, parents quoted in its columns' }],
  title: 'The missing middle', art: 'S911', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a kitchen table with a newspaper article, a wide blank gap drawn through its middle',
  words: [
    'Lena checked every fact in the article about the new park and every one was true. So she wrote that it was unbiased, and her teacher wrote back that true was not the same as fair. Lena had no idea how a page of true facts could lean.',
    'She read it again looking for a lie and found none. Then she read it looking for what was not there. The cost of the park was in the first paragraph. The cost of doing nothing was nowhere. The mayor had slammed the plan; the planners had admitted a delay; the so-called experts had spoken.',
    'Bias is a lean, not a lie. It lives in three places: the facts left out, the charged words that carry a verdict, and the voices missing from the page. Every person quoted opposed the park. Not one family who would use it had been asked. The middle of the story was simply gone.',
    'Placement leans too. The cost of the park sat in the headline and the first paragraph, where most readers stop. The number of families who had asked for it appeared once, near the end, after the last photo.',
    'The fix was a second source. A neighborhood newsletter covered the same meeting, quoted three parents and the planner, and listed the cost of doing nothing: a flooded lot the city paid to pump every spring. Reading both together, Lena finally had the whole story.',
    'Bias is a lean, not a lie. Look for missing facts, charged words, missing voices and what gets buried at the end. A page can be true in every sentence and still tell only half the story, and the half it leaves out is the bias.',
  ],
};
STORIES['paraphrasing'] = {
  about: 'the same cargo moved into a different truck, with nothing dropped and nothing added',
  more: [{ serial: 'S915', after: 0, alt: 'A teacher counting facts in two paragraphs with a red pen, five against three' }, { serial: 'S916', after: 1, alt: 'One box left behind on the dock, circled' }, { serial: 'S2426', after: 2, alt: 'The original sentence and the paraphrase side by side, facts numbered' }, { serial: 'S2427', after: 3, alt: 'A sentence wearing a disguise: the same shape with every word swapped' }, { serial: 'S2428', after: 4, alt: 'A box on a truck with a shipping label that names the author' }],
  title: 'A different truck', art: 'S914', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a loading dock moving boxes from one truck to another, counting them on a clipboard',
  words: [
    'Marcus paraphrased the paragraph and was proud of it, because not a single word matched the original. His teacher counted the facts in each. The original had five. Marcus\'s had three, plus one the author had never said.',
    'He had thought a paraphrase was about changing the words. It was about keeping the cargo. He had moved the load into a new truck and dropped two boxes on the dock, and somehow picked up a box that was not his.',
    'So he checked fact by fact. Who: kept. When: dropped, put back. How many: dropped, put back. The claim about why, which he had added because it seemed obvious: not in the original, out. Now the new truck carried exactly what the old one had, in different words.',
    'His teacher showed him one more trap. Swapping each word for a synonym while keeping the author\'s sentence shape is not a paraphrase; it is the original in a disguise. Read the passage, put it away, write it from memory, and then check.',
    'And a paraphrase still needs its source. New words do not make an idea yours. Marcus added the author\'s name, the way a truck carries a shipping label. Borrowed cargo still says where it came from.',
    'A paraphrase keeps every fact and adds none. Check yours against the original, fact by fact, the way a driver checks a manifest. Different words, same cargo, the source named, and nothing left on the dock.',
  ],
};
STORIES['complex-characters'] = {
  about: 'a character who wanted two things that could not both be had, and the reader who tracked which won',
  more: [{ serial: 'S918', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning at a novel, a note in the margin that says inconsistent' }, { serial: 'S919', after: 1, alt: 'The character on one page choosing safety, on the next choosing her brother' }, { serial: 'S2429', after: 2, alt: 'A tally of scenes, one column for each want' }, { serial: 'S2430', after: 3, alt: 'Two friends side by side on a page, one torn in two directions, one simply smiling' }, { serial: 'S2431', after: 4, alt: 'The character on the last page choosing her brother without a lie' }],
  title: 'Two wants', art: 'S917', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl reading about a main character pulled between her brother and staying safe, drawn as a tug-of-war rope above the page',
  words: [
    'Rosa said the main character was inconsistent. In one chapter she risked everything for her brother and in the next she lied to stay safe. Rosa called that bad writing. Her teacher said it was the point of the book.',
    'A simple character wants one thing. A complex character wants two things that pull against each other, and cannot have both. This one wanted to keep her brother and wanted to stay alive, and the story was built from the scenes where she had to pick.',
    'Rosa went back and made a tally. Chapter three: brother. Chapter five: safety. Chapter nine: brother, at a cost. By the end the pattern was the story. Safety kept winning until it did not, and the scene where the brother won for good was the climax. She had not been inconsistent. She had been torn.',
    'Her teacher pointed to the girl\'s best friend, who wanted only one thing, to be liked, and never changed. A character like that is a foil: set beside the complex one, she makes the struggle easier to see.',
    'And the girl did change. By the last chapter she no longer lied to stay safe; she had decided her brother was worth the risk. A character who changes like that is dynamic, and the change was the story\'s answer to its own question.',
    'A complex character wants two things that pull against each other. Name both, then watch which wins in each scene. The scenes where the tally flips are where the story lives.',
  ],
};
STORIES['symbols'] = {
  about: 'a green light mentioned four times, and what it meant each time',
  more: [{ serial: 'S921', after: 0, alt: 'A boy noticing a green light on the first page of a novel' }, { serial: 'S922', after: 1, alt: 'Four bookmarks in a novel, each at a page with the same light' }, { serial: 'S2432', after: 2, alt: 'The light seen from four distances, the last one very close and ordinary' }, { serial: 'S2433', after: 3, alt: 'A blue car in a sentence with a note beside it: usually just a blue car' }, { serial: 'S2434', after: 4, alt: 'A road, a snowy field and a dove drawn side by side as familiar symbols' }],
  title: 'The green light', art: 'S920', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a dark dock looking across water at a small green light, the novel in his hand',
  words: [
    'Owen noticed a green light at the end of a dock mentioned on page one, and thought nothing of it. Then it came back on page forty, and again on ninety. By the fourth time he was sure the author was doing it on purpose, and he had no idea why.',
    'A light is a light. But nobody mentions the same light four times by accident. Repetition was the signal. The question was what the thing stood for, and the answer changed with what the character was going through each time.',
    'On page one the character reached toward it: hope, a future he wanted. Later, when he had almost reached it, the light was just a bulb on a dock, smaller than it had looked. By the end it stood for the way wanting a thing can be better than having it. The light never changed. His situation did, and so did the meaning.',
    'His teacher warned him not to see symbols everywhere. A symbol earns the name by coming back, by being noticed by the characters, or by sitting at a key moment. A blue car mentioned once is usually just a blue car.',
    'Some symbols travel from book to book: a road for a life, winter for an ending, a dove for peace. The green light was the author\'s own, which is why Owen had to work out its meaning from the story itself.',
    'A symbol is a thing that stands for an idea. Repetition is the signal, and the character\'s situation each time tells you the meaning. Four green lights, one bulb, four different things.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ionic-and-covalent'] = {
  about: 'two ways to share a toy, and the ways atoms hold on to each other',
  more: [{ serial: 'S924', after: 0, alt: 'Two small cousins arguing over a toy truck while their mother holds up two fingers' }, { serial: 'S925', after: 1, alt: 'Sodium handing its electron to chlorine, the two sticking' }, { serial: 'S2438', after: 2, alt: 'Two hydrogens and an oxygen holding electrons between them' }, { serial: 'S2439', after: 3, alt: 'A pan of melting sugar beside a salt shaker and a thermometer reading 800' }, { serial: 'S2440', after: 4, alt: 'A copper wire with a sea of electrons drawn flowing around its atoms' }],
  title: 'Two ways to share', art: 'S923', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two small children with a toy, one handing it over and two others holding it together, atoms drawn faintly above them',
  words: [
    'Theo watched his little cousins fight over a toy until their mother stepped in with two rules. Either one of you gives it up, or you both hold it at the same time. His chemistry homework that night had the same two rules with electrons instead of a truck.',
    'Sodium and chlorine were the first kind. Sodium handed its outer electron to chlorine and let go. Now one was positive and one was negative, and opposites attract, so they stuck. That was ionic, and it made table salt.',
    'Two hydrogens and an oxygen were the second kind. Nobody gave anything up. They held the electrons between them, both hands on the toy, and that holding was the bond. That was covalent, and it made water. Metal with nonmetal tends to give; nonmetal with nonmetal tends to share.',
    'The two kinds behave differently. Salt melts only at about 800 degrees Celsius and, dissolved in water, carries electricity. Sugar, held together by sharing, melts in a pan at home and carries none.',
    'Metals have a third way. Their atoms toss their outer electrons into a shared pool that flows around all of them at once, which is why a copper wire carries current and a spoon conducts heat.',
    'Ionic bonds give electrons, covalent bonds share them, and metals pool them. Metal plus nonmetal is ionic, nonmetal plus nonmetal is covalent. Two rules for a toy, and three for most of the substances around you.',
  ],
};
STORIES['balancing-equations'] = {
  about: 'a pile of bricks taken apart and rebuilt, with every brick accounted for',
  more: [{ serial: 'S927', after: 0, alt: 'A boy frowning at H₂ + O₂ → H₂O with one oxygen circled as missing' }, { serial: 'S928', after: 2, alt: 'Two red pairs and one blue pair before rebuilding' }, { serial: 'S2441', after: 3, alt: 'A gas stove flame with methane and oxygen becoming carbon dioxide and water written beside it' }, { serial: 'S2442', after: 4, alt: 'An old sealed glass vessel on a balance scale in an eighteenth-century laboratory' }, { serial: 'S2443', after: 5, alt: 'Two new pieces, each made of two red and one blue, and no bricks left on the floor' }],
  title: 'Bricks in, bricks out', art: 'S926', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on the floor with red and blue building bricks, taking a small structure apart and rebuilding it',
  words: [
    'Kai wrote hydrogen plus oxygen makes water, H₂ + O₂ → H₂O, and felt done. His teacher counted the atoms. Two oxygen on the left, one on the right. Kai had lost an oxygen atom somewhere between the arrow\'s two ends.',
    'Atoms do not vanish. Whatever goes into a reaction comes out of it, just rearranged. Kai thought about his brother\'s building bricks. Take a structure apart and you can build something new, but you never end up with fewer bricks.',
    'So he counted bricks. Two H₂ and one O₂: four hydrogen bricks, two oxygen bricks. Rebuild them into two H₂O: four hydrogen, two oxygen. 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. He had changed the big numbers in front, how many of each piece, and never the small numbers inside, which would have changed what the piece was.',
    'His teacher gave him a harder one: methane burning in a stove, CH₄ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O. Kai counted carbon first, one on each side. Four hydrogen on the left meant 2H₂O on the right. That made four oxygen on the right, so he needed 2O₂ on the left. CH₄ + 2O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O.',
    'The rule behind the counting is old. In the late 1700s Antoine Lavoisier weighed chemicals before and after reacting them in sealed containers, and the total mass never changed. Balancing an equation is his scale, written in letters.',
    'Balanced means every atom counted the same on both sides. Change the big numbers in front, never the small numbers inside. Bricks in, bricks out, and nothing left on the floor.',
  ],
};
STORIES['acids-and-bases'] = {
  about: 'lemon juice, soap and water on a ruler that ran from 0 to 14',
  more: [{ serial: 'S930', after: 0, alt: 'A girl at a kitchen counter wincing after an accidental taste of lemon juice' }, { serial: 'S931', after: 1, alt: 'The strip with lemon at 2, water at 7, soap at 9' }, { serial: 'S2444', after: 2, alt: 'Vinegar and baking soda fizzing in a bowl, meeting in the middle' }, { serial: 'S2445', after: 3, alt: 'Three glasses of purple cabbage water turned pink, purple and green' }, { serial: 'S2446', after: 4, alt: 'A body outline with the stomach marked 2 and the blood marked 7.4' }],
  title: 'The ruler from 0 to 14', art: 'S929', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a kitchen counter with a lemon, a bar of soap and a glass of water lined up beside a color strip',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s kitchen had three liquids on the counter and she tasted two of them by accident. Lemon juice bit. Soapy water tasted awful and felt slippery. Plain water tasted like nothing. She had just met the whole pH scale.',
    'Below 7 is acid, like the lemon. Exactly 7 is neutral, like water. Above 7 is base, like soap. Maya assumed 2 was only a little more sour than 3.',
    'Each step is ten times. Lemon juice at 2 is ten times more acidic than something at 3 and a hundred times more than something at 4. And an acid and a base cancel each other toward 7: she poured vinegar onto baking soda, it fizzed, and what was left was closer to neutral than either had been.',
    'In a lab nobody tastes anything; that is what indicators are for. Maya boiled red cabbage and used the purple water. Lemon juice turned it pink, baking soda water turned it green, and plain water left it purple.',
    'Her own body runs on the ruler too. Her stomach sits near 2, strong enough to break down food, while her blood is held just above 7, at about 7.4, and her body works hard to keep it there.',
    'Below 7 is acid, 7 is neutral, above 7 is base. Each step is ten times, and an acid and a base cancel toward 7. The ruler runs from lemon to soap with water in the middle.',
  ],
};
STORIES['moles-and-molar-mass'] = {
  about: 'a dozen eggs, a dozen grapes, and the chemist\'s dozen',
  more: [{ serial: 'S933', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a grocery scale with a dozen eggs in one pan and a dozen grapes in the other' }, { serial: 'S934', after: 1, alt: 'Twelve eggs and twelve grapes, same count, different weight' }, { serial: 'S2447', after: 2, alt: 'A water molecule with its atoms labeled and their masses added' }, { serial: 'S2448', after: 3, alt: 'A big sip of water beside a beach, with 6.02 × 10²³ written in the sand' }, { serial: 'S2449', after: 4, alt: 'A kitchen scale reading 36 grams under a small glass of water' }],
  title: 'A dozen for chemists', art: 'S932', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a grocery scale weighing a dozen eggs and a dozen grapes side by side',
  words: [
    'At the store, Jamal weighed a dozen eggs and a dozen grapes. Same count, wildly different mass. His mother said chemists have a dozen too, only enormous, and it works the same way.',
    'A mole is a fixed count of particles, about six hundred billion trillion of them. A mole of hydrogen atoms and a mole of oxygen atoms have the same count and different mass, like the eggs and the grapes.',
    'The mass of one mole is the molar mass, in grams, and you read it off the periodic table. Hydrogen is about 1, oxygen about 16. For water, add up the atoms in the formula: two hydrogens, 2, plus one oxygen, 16. Eighteen grams in a mole of water. A dozen tells you how many; the scale tells you how heavy.',
    'Scientists write the count as 6.02 × 10²³, Avogadro\'s number. A mole of water is 18 grams, about 18 milliliters, roughly a big sip. By most estimates it holds more molecules than there are grains of sand on all the beaches of the Earth.',
    'Converting goes both ways. Jamal weighed 36 grams of water: 36 divided by 18 is 2 moles. Two moles times 6.02 × 10²³ is about 1.2 × 10²⁴ molecules, in a glass he could drink in one gulp.',
    'A mole is a fixed count, and molar mass is the grams in one mole. Add up the atoms in the formula, each times its mass, and divide grams by molar mass to get moles. A dozen eggs and a dozen grapes taught the whole idea.',
  ],
};
STORIES['reaction-types'] = {
  about: 'things that snap together, split apart, swap partners or burn, sorted by counting',
  more: [{ serial: 'S936', after: 0, alt: 'A girl facing a list of twenty reactions on a whiteboard, looking lost' }, { serial: 'S937', after: 1, alt: 'A and B snapping into AB for synthesis, then AB splitting into A and B for decomposition' }, { serial: 'S2450', after: 2, alt: 'Five labeled bins on a bench: snap, split, swap one, swap two, burn' }, { serial: 'S2451', after: 3, alt: 'Two clear liquids poured together making a white cloud that sinks' }, { serial: 'S2452', after: 4, alt: 'A candle burning under a glass jar, oxygen drawn arriving from the air' }],
  title: 'Snap, split, swap, burn', art: 'S935', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a lab bench with four small cards showing pieces joining, splitting, swapping and burning',
  words: [
    'Rosa faced a list of twenty reactions and one instruction: sort them. They looked like alphabet soup. Her teacher told her to ignore the letters and count what went in and what came out.',
    'Two things in, one thing out: A and B snap into AB. Synthesis. One thing in, two out: AB splits into A and B. Decomposition. Suddenly the list had shape.',
    'One element and a compound in, and the element takes a partner\'s place: single replacement. Two compounds in and they swap partners: double replacement. And anything with oxygen going in and carbon dioxide and water coming out was combustion, the thing burning. Twenty reactions, five bins, sorted by counting. Her teacher said to watch the pieces, not the names.',
    'Then she gave Rosa one of each from real life. Iron and oxygen snap into rust. Hydrogen peroxide splits into water and oxygen, the fizz on a cut. Zinc dropped in acid takes hydrogen\'s place and bubbles hydrogen gas out. And two clear solutions mixed can swap partners and make a cloud of solid that sinks.',
    'The candle on her desk was combustion: wax and oxygen in, carbon dioxide and water out, with heat and light besides. When Rosa put a jar over it and the oxygen ran low, the flame went out.',
    'Synthesis joins, decomposition splits, replacements swap, and combustion burns with oxygen. Count what goes in and what comes out, and the type names itself.',
  ],
};
STORIES['gas-laws'] = {
  about: 'a balloon squeezed in a fist, left in a hot car and shut in the freezer',
  more: [{ serial: 'S939', after: 0, alt: 'The balloon squeezed to half its size, straining' }, { serial: 'S940', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher holding up three balloons, one small, one normal, one fat' }, { serial: 'S2453', after: 2, alt: 'The same balloon shrunken after an hour in the freezer' }, { serial: 'S2454', after: 3, alt: 'A thermometer marked in Celsius and kelvins side by side, 27 beside 300' }, { serial: 'S2455', after: 4, alt: 'A puffed-up bag of chips on a car dashboard on a mountain road' }],
  title: 'The balloon', art: 'S938', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy squeezing a balloon in a parked car, the balloon puffed larger in the heat',
  words: [
    'Diego squeezed a balloon in his fist and it pushed back harder the smaller he made it. He left another in the hot car and came back to find it fat and tight. He put a third in the freezer and it shriveled. Three balloons, three surprises, one gas.',
    'His science teacher said the same rules ran all three. Diego thought a balloon was just rubber and air.',
    'Squeeze a gas and its pressure rises, because the same molecules hit the walls more often: halve its space and its pressure doubles, so pressure times volume stays the same. Heat a gas and the molecules move faster, so it expands if it can, or its pressure rises if it cannot. Cool it and both fall. The balloons had been telling him the gas laws in the language of rubber.',
    'The numbers only work in kelvins, which count from absolute zero, about minus 273 degrees Celsius. A balloon at 27 degrees Celsius is at 300 kelvins. Warm it to 57 degrees, 330 kelvins, and it grows by a tenth, because 330 is a tenth more than 300.',
    'Diego saw the laws everywhere after that. Car tires read low on a cold morning. A bag of chips puffs up on a mountain road, where the air outside presses less. And a spray can says never to heat it, because its pressure would climb with nowhere to go.',
    'Squeeze a gas and its pressure rises; pressure times volume stays the same. Heat a gas and it expands or its pressure rises; cool it and the reverse. Use kelvins for the math. Three balloons is a whole chapter.',
  ],
};
STORIES['concentration'] = {
  about: 'lemonade that was too strong, and the two ways to fix it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S942', after: 0, alt: 'Four spoons of sugar going into two glasses' }, { serial: 'S943', after: 1, alt: 'A fraction drawn on a napkin: spoons on top, glasses on the bottom' }, { serial: 'S2456', after: 2, alt: 'Water poured in, the same sugar spread through more liquid' }, { serial: 'S2457', after: 3, alt: 'A liter of salt water poured into a two-liter jug, the label changing from 0.5 to 0.25 molar' }, { serial: 'S2458', after: 4, alt: 'Undissolved sugar at the bottom of a glass of iced tea beside a steaming kettle' }],
  title: 'How strong the lemonade is', art: 'S941', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl tasting lemonade at a kitchen counter, a bag of sugar and a pitcher of water beside her',
  words: [
    'Lena put four spoons of sugar into two glasses of lemonade and it was undrinkable. Her brother said take some sugar out. Her mother said add water. Both worked, and Lena wanted to know why two opposite moves fixed the same problem.',
    'Four spoons in two glasses was two spoons per glass. That number, how much stuff per how much liquid, was the strength. You could lower it by cutting the top of the fraction or by raising the bottom.',
    'Chemists call the strength molarity: moles of solute divided by liters of solution. Add water and the solute is unchanged, the same four spoons, but it is spread through more liquid, so the concentration drops. Lena added water. Four spoons in four glasses: one spoon per glass, and the lemonade was right.',
    'Her chemistry teacher did it with numbers. Half a mole of salt dissolved to make one liter is 0.5 molar. Add water until there are two liters and it is 0.25 molar: the same salt, twice the liquid, half the strength.',
    'There is also a limit. Stir in sugar and at some point it stops dissolving and sinks to the bottom: the solution is saturated. Warm water holds more sugar than cold, which is why iced tea is sweetened while it is still hot.',
    'Molarity is moles of solute divided by liters of solution. Adding solvent lowers the concentration while the solute stays the same. Too strong means too much per liter, and more liters is the gentlest fix.',
  ],
};
STORIES['literary-analysis'] = {
  about: 'a thesis about the whole book, and the difference between a claim and a summary',
  more: [{ serial: 'S945', after: 0, alt: 'A summary sentence crossed out: the book is about a boy and a river' }, { serial: 'S946', after: 1, alt: 'A new thesis written boldly: the river is the only place the boy can be honest' }, { serial: 'S2477', after: 2, alt: 'A thesis sentence with three quotations lined up under it' }, { serial: 'S2478', after: 3, alt: 'A raft scene bookmarked with a note: the lie he takes back' }, { serial: 'S2479', after: 4, alt: 'A margin note that says present tense: the boy speaks' }],
  title: 'A claim, not a summary', art: 'S944', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a library with a novel and a page of notes, one sentence at the top crossed out and rewritten',
  words: [
    'Ana\'s thesis was that the novel was about a boy who goes down a river and learns about friendship. Her teacher said that was the back cover. Ana said it was true. Her teacher agreed it was true. Nobody could argue with it, and that was the problem.',
    'A summary tells what happens. A thesis makes a claim about what it means, one that a reader could push back on. Ana tried again: the river is the only place the boy can be honest, and every lie he tells happens on land.',
    'Now she had something to prove. She found three quotations, one from the raft, two from towns. After each she wrote a sentence saying what it showed: that the boy speaks plainly on the water and performs on shore. The essay had a spine. The back cover did not.',
    'Her teacher found the hard case: one scene where the boy lies on the raft. Ana did not hide it. She wrote that the lie on the raft is the one he takes back, which only made the pattern clearer. Answering the scene that seems to break a thesis makes it stronger.',
    'She also learned the small rule that makes an analysis sound sure of itself: write about the book in the present tense. The boy speaks plainly on the water, not spoke, because the book says it every time someone opens it.',
    'A literary analysis is a thesis about the text, quotations as evidence, and a sentence after each saying what it shows. The thesis is a claim someone could argue with, not a summary nobody could.',
  ],
};
STORIES['sourced-argument'] = {
  about: 'an argument that named where every fact came from, and the one that did not',
  more: [{ serial: 'S948', after: 0, alt: 'The boy at the podium as a classmate asks where a fact came from' }, { serial: 'S949', after: 1, alt: 'A sleep study, a district report and a newspaper stacked on a desk' }, { serial: 'S2480', after: 2, alt: 'Two cards side by side, one with a source line and one blank at the bottom' }, { serial: 'S2481', after: 3, alt: 'A forum post with no author crossed out beside a signed newspaper story' }, { serial: 'S2482', after: 4, alt: 'A final page listing every source at the end, with believable written at the top' }],
  title: 'Where did you get that?', art: 'S947', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a podium with index cards, each card carrying a small source line at the bottom',
  words: [
    'Diego argued that the school should switch to later start times and gave four strong facts. A classmate asked where the third one came from. Diego said he had read it somewhere. The room went quiet, and the fact he could not source was the one everyone remembered.',
    'The other three had come from a sleep study, a district report and a newspaper, and he knew it. But he had not said so. On paper they looked the same as the one he had picked up who knows where.',
    'He rewrote every card with a source line at the bottom: the sleep study\'s authors and year, the district\'s own transportation report. The fourth fact he could not trace, so he cut it, and the argument got stronger by getting shorter. Naming the source let the audience check him, which is why they believed him.',
    'His teacher added a second question: is it a good source? The district report was recent and official. The newspaper story quoted a doctor by name. A forum post with no author, however well written, would have been the same as no source at all.',
    'The final paper named each source in a sentence and listed them all at the end, so anyone could find them. It took Diego an extra hour. It was the hour that made his teacher write believable at the top.',
    'Every piece of evidence names its source. That is what separates an argument from an opinion. A fact you cannot trace is not evidence yet, and cutting it makes the case better, not worse.',
  ],
};
STORIES['reflective-essay'] = {
  about: 'what a girl believed before, what changed it, and the gap in between',
  more: [{ serial: 'S951', after: 0, alt: 'A diary-like draft with Monday and Tuesday at the start of each line' }, { serial: 'S952', after: 1, alt: 'The first paragraph: what happened, plainly' }, { serial: 'S2483', after: 2, alt: 'An old man in a green cap at a shop counter, the aunt greeting him by name' }, { serial: 'S2484', after: 3, alt: 'Her aunt pouring coffee for the old man, who never pays' }, { serial: 'S2485', after: 5, alt: 'The last paragraph: what it meant, with the word before circled' }],
  title: 'Before and after', art: 'S950', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl writing about the summer she worked at her aunt\'s shop, her draft reading like a diary',
  words: [
    'Priya wrote about the summer she worked at her aunt\'s shop, and it read like a diary. Monday, this happened. Tuesday, that happened. Her teacher said it was a good record and not yet a reflection. Priya asked what the difference was.',
    'A record tells what happened. A reflection tells what it meant, and the meaning lives in a gap. What did you believe before? What do you believe now? What, exactly, happened in between?',
    'Before the summer she believed customers were an interruption. After it she believed the customers were the job. The change happened on a slow Wednesday when an old man came in every day for a week and bought nothing, and her aunt greeted him by name each time. Priya told what happened, then told what it meant, and the essay stopped being a diary.',
    'Her teacher pushed her to slow down on that Wednesday. What did the old man wear? What did her aunt say, word for word? Priya wrote the green cap, the same question about the weather, and her aunt pouring him coffee he never paid for. The details carried the meaning so she did not have to announce it.',
    'The hardest sentence to write was the one that admitted she had been wrong. Her first draft ended with a lesson for the reader. Her last draft ended with what she did differently on the next busy Saturday, which was look up.',
    'A reflective essay tells what happened, then what it meant. Say what you believed before, what you believe now, and name the moment that changed it. The gap between the two is the essay.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ancient-civilizations'] = {
  about: 'a river that flooded on schedule, and the surplus that let people build, rule and write',
  more: [{ serial: 'S954', after: 1, alt: 'Dark flood mud left on a field after the Nile went down, a farmer planting' }, { serial: 'S955', after: 2, alt: 'Granaries full of grain, a scribe counting jars with marks on clay' }, { serial: 'S2459', after: 3, alt: 'A clay tablet of laws about canals and water beside an irrigation ditch' }, { serial: 'S2460', after: 4, alt: 'A map with four great rivers marked: the Nile, the Tigris and Euphrates, the Indus and the Yellow River' }, { serial: 'S2461', after: 5, alt: 'A city wall rising beside the fields, workers who are not farming' }],
  title: 'The river that kept time', art: 'S953', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a riverbank watching a wide river rise over its banks onto dark fields',
  words: [
    'Kai learned that the first cities grew on rivers and assumed it was for the drinking water. His teacher asked why, then, they grew on the rivers that flooded every year and not on the calm ones.',
    'The floods came on schedule. The Nile rose every summer and left dark, rich mud behind. A farmer on that mud grew more than his family could eat, year after year. The extra was the surplus, and the surplus changed everything.',
    'With a surplus, not everyone had to farm. Some could build walls, some could rule, some could pray, and some had to keep track of whose grain was in which storehouse. In Mesopotamia, writing began as that accounting: marks on clay for jars and sheep. Only later did anyone use it for a story or a law.',
    'Rivers also needed rules. A canal that watered one farm could drain another, so someone had to decide who got the water and when. Some of the oldest written laws, like Hammurabi\'s code in Babylon around 1750 BC, settle exactly those arguments.',
    'The same pattern grew up on other rivers, each on its own: the Tigris and Euphrates, the Indus and the Yellow River. People who never met found the same answer to the same problem.',
    'Civilization began on flooding rivers where a surplus freed people to build, rule and write. Writing started as accounting, and cities started as storehouses with walls. The river kept the time, and the people kept the count.',
  ],
};
STORIES['greece-and-rome'] = {
  about: 'everyone votes, then some vote, then one rules, and the founders who studied all three',
  more: [{ serial: 'S957', after: 0, alt: 'A girl in a museum between three stone reliefs, puzzled' }, { serial: 'S958', after: 1, alt: 'Athenian citizens voting by show of hands on a hillside' }, { serial: 'S2462', after: 2, alt: 'Three circles on a board labeled everyone, some and one' }, { serial: 'S2463', after: 3, alt: 'The Capitol dome in Washington beside a drawing of an ancient Roman hill' }, { serial: 'S2464', after: 4, alt: 'A Roman senate chamber, then the same chamber with one man on a raised chair' }],
  title: 'Everyone, some, one', art: 'S956', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a museum hall between a Greek assembly relief, a Roman senate relief and a bust of an emperor',
  words: [
    'Rosa read that Athens had a democracy, Rome had a republic, and then Rome had emperors. She could not see why a class needed three words for governments that were all just old.',
    'Her teacher drew three circles. In Athens every citizen voted on every law himself, in a crowd on a hill: direct democracy. A citizen, though, meant a free adult man, a small share of the people living there. In the Roman republic citizens chose senators and consuls to vote for them: representation. Then in 27 BC one man, Augustus, kept the titles and took the power.',
    'Everyone votes, then some vote, then one rules. Rosa asked which one America was. The founders had read all three stories, and they designed against the last one. They chose representatives like Rome, elected by the many like Athens, with power split so that no Augustus could gather it up.',
    'Rome also left its words behind. Senate, veto, citizen and republic all come from Latin. The Capitol in Washington is named for a Roman hill.',
    'Rosa noticed how Augustus did it: not by abolishing the Senate, but by keeping it and emptying it. The old names stayed on the doors while the decisions moved to one man. Historians point to that as a warning: a government can keep its old names while the power moves somewhere else.',
    'Athens invented direct democracy, Rome built a republic that became an empire in 27 BC, and the American founders designed against that ending. Three governments, one lesson about how power moves from everyone to some to one.',
  ],
};
STORIES['middle-ages-and-renaissance'] = {
  about: 'land for loyalty, then a plague that loosened every knot, then a press',
  more: [{ serial: 'S960', after: 0, alt: 'A boy picturing Europe after Rome as empty ruins, a question mark over them' }, { serial: 'S961', after: 1, alt: 'A lord handing land to a knight who kneels' }, { serial: 'S2465', after: 2, alt: 'A king sealing a long document while barons stand around him in 1215' }, { serial: 'S2466', after: 3, alt: 'A printing press turning out pages, a crowd reading' }, { serial: 'S2467', after: 4, alt: 'Pamphlets pouring off a press in 1517 and carried away by riders' }],
  title: 'Land, plague, press', art: 'S959', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a castle courtyard looking at a knight, a plague-emptied village and a printing press in three archways',
  words: [
    'When Rome fell, Marcus assumed Europe fell into chaos and stayed there for a thousand years. His teacher said the years were not empty. They were held together by a deal.',
    'The deal was feudalism: a lord gave land to a knight, the knight gave loyalty and soldiers, and peasants worked the land for protection. Everyone was tied to someone above them. It was not fair, but it held, and it held for centuries.',
    'Even inside the deal, limits grew. In 1215 English barons forced King John to seal Magna Carta, which said that even a king had to follow the law. It helped mostly the barons at first, but the idea outlived them.',
    'Then in 1347 the Black Death arrived and killed at least a third of Europe, and suddenly there were too few workers for the land. A peasant could leave and be paid elsewhere. The knots loosened. Into that loosened world came the Renaissance, a rebirth of learning, and around 1450 the printing press, which put books in the hands of people who had never owned one.',
    'The press changed religion too. In 1517 Martin Luther wrote objections to the church, and printers copied them so fast that they spread across Germany in weeks. A monk with a press could now argue with an empire.',
    'Feudalism held Europe together after Rome; the Black Death of 1347 loosened it; the Renaissance and the printing press of about 1450 spread ideas faster than any lord could stop. Land, then plague, then press.',
  ],
};
STORIES['age-of-revolutions'] = {
  about: 'one idea, three revolutions, and the different endings it got',
  more: [{ serial: 'S963', after: 0, alt: 'A girl with three books open side by side in a library' }, { serial: 'S964', after: 1, alt: 'America testing the Enlightenment idea in 1776, a quill signing its declaration' }, { serial: 'S2468', after: 2, alt: 'The same idea drawn as one seed landing in three kinds of ground: a steady field, a burning square in Paris and a freed island' }, { serial: 'S2469', after: 3, alt: 'A woman writing her own declaration by candlelight in 1791' }, { serial: 'S2470', after: 4, alt: 'Toussaint Louverture on horseback before a crowd of free people in Haiti' }],
  title: 'One idea, three endings', art: 'S962', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a library with three books open: a declaration, a guillotine sketch and a slave rebellion',
  words: [
    'Ana read about the American, French and Haitian revolutions in one week and assumed they were three copies of the same event. Her teacher asked her to find the sentence all three began with.',
    'It was an Enlightenment idea: rights come first, and governments exist only by the consent of the governed. America tested it in 1776 and wrote a constitution that held. France tested it in 1789 and fell into terror, then an emperor. Haiti\'s enslaved people tested it in 1791, and theirs became the only slave revolt in history to found a nation.',
    'Same idea, three endings. Ana asked why. Her teacher said an idea is a seed, and the ground it lands on matters: who holds the army, how deep the hunger runs, whether the old order fights back or leaves. The seed was the same. The soil was not.',
    'The idea also showed its own gaps. The American declaration said all men are created equal while slavery stayed legal for almost ninety more years. France\'s Declaration of the Rights of Man left women out, and in 1791 Olympe de Gouges answered with a declaration of her own.',
    'Haiti\'s revolution, led at first by Toussaint Louverture, ended in 1804 with a new nation where no one could be enslaved, the first country in the Americas to end slavery for good.',
    'The Enlightenment said rights come first and governments come from consent; America tested it in 1776, France in 1789 and Haiti in 1791, with three different endings. One idea can grow into a republic, a terror or a freedom, depending on the ground.',
  ],
};
STORIES['world-wars'] = {
  about: 'two wars twenty years apart, and the peace between them that made the second',
  more: [{ serial: 'S966', after: 0, alt: 'A boy between two framed photos: a great-grandfather in a 1918 uniform and a grandfather in a 1944 uniform' }, { serial: 'S967', after: 1, alt: 'The peace of 1919 that punished Germany with debt and shame, signed at a long table' }, { serial: 'S2474', after: 2, alt: 'A memorial wall with too many names to read' }, { serial: 'S2475', after: 3, alt: 'The United Nations flag raised in 1945 over a crowd' }, { serial: 'S2476', after: 4, alt: 'The winners helping rebuild their old enemies after the second war, new streets rising in Germany and Japan' }],
  title: 'Twenty years apart', art: 'S965', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy walking between rows of white headstones with two dates, 1918 and 1945, on a stone at the end',
  words: [
    'Theo\'s great-grandfather had fought in the First World War and his grandfather in the Second, and Theo thought that was just bad luck in one family. His history teacher said the two wars were one story with an intermission.',
    'The First, from 1914 to 1918, ended with a peace that punished Germany with debt and shame. The debt, and then the worldwide Depression of the 1930s, wrecked its economy. The shame gave a man with a plan an audience. In 1939 the Second began.',
    'The Second, from 1939 to 1945, killed around seventy million people by many estimates. Six million of them were Jews murdered in the Holocaust, not on battlefields but in camps built for the purpose; the Nazis also murdered Roma, disabled people and others.',
    'After the first war, the League of Nations was meant to keep the peace, but the United States never joined, and it had no way to enforce anything. After the second, the United Nations began in 1945 with the great powers inside it.',
    'When the second war ended, the winners remembered 1919 and did the opposite. They helped rebuild their enemies instead of punishing them, and those countries have not gone to war with each other since.',
    'The First World War, 1914 to 1918, made the peace that led to the Second, 1939 to 1945. Around seventy million people died in the Second, six million of them Jews in the Holocaust. The lesson the world took from the intermission was that how a war ends decides whether the next one starts.',
  ],
};
// Grades 11 and 12, told for readers.
STORIES['quadratic-formula'] = {
  about: 'a machine that finds where an arch touches the ground, and what it says when the arch never does',
  more: [{ serial: 'S969', after: 0, alt: 'A boy filming a friend\'s bike jump at a skate park' }, { serial: 'S970', after: 1, alt: 'The formula written out, the part under the root circled' }, { serial: 'S2486', after: 2, alt: 'Three arches: one crossing the ground twice, one touching once, one floating above' }, { serial: 'S2487', after: 3, alt: 'A graph of the arch crossing the ground at 0.76 and 5.24' }, { serial: 'S2488', after: 4, alt: 'The top of the arch marked at 3 across and 2.5 meters up, halfway between the landings' }],
  title: 'Where the arch lands', art: 'S968', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a skate park sketching the arch of a jump, two marks where it meets the ground',
  words: [
    'Owen filmed a friend\'s bike jump and traced the arch on paper. He wanted to know exactly where the wheels left the ground and where they landed. Factoring the equation got him nowhere; the numbers were not friendly.',
    'His teacher said there was a machine for exactly this, and it worked on every quadratic, friendly or not. Owen had seen it and been scared of it: x equals minus b, plus or minus the square root of b squared minus four a c, all over two a.',
    'He fed the machine his a, b and c. Out came two numbers: the takeoff and the landing. Then the teacher pointed at the part under the root. Positive, two answers, the arch crosses the ground twice. Zero, one answer, the arch just kisses it. Negative, no real answer, the arch never comes down at all. The machine told him how many landings to expect before it told him where.',
    'His arch was y = −0.5x² + 3x − 2, in meters. The part under the root was 9 minus 4 times −0.5 times −2, which is 5: positive, so two answers. The machine gave about 0.76 and 5.24, takeoff and landing, about four and a half meters apart.',
    'The highest point sat exactly halfway between them, at 3, where the arch reached 2.5 meters. That always works: the top of an arch sits midway between its two landings.',
    'The quadratic formula solves every quadratic, and the part under the root tells you how many answers to expect: two, one or none. Owen\'s friend landed twice on paper and once in real life, which is a different lesson.',
  ],
};
STORIES['multiplying-binomials'] = {
  about: 'a garden bed with four patches, and the multiplication hiding in its shape',
  more: [{ serial: 'S972', after: 0, alt: 'A girl who had to multiply x plus 3 by x plus 2 and wrote x squared plus 6, half the garden missing' }, { serial: 'S973', after: 1, alt: 'The bed labeled x + 3 along one side and x + 2 along the other' }, { serial: 'S2489', after: 2, alt: 'The four patches: x squared, 2x, 3x and 6' }, { serial: 'S2490', after: 3, alt: 'A square bed with sides of x + 3 split into four patches, two middle patches of 3x' }, { serial: 'S2491', after: 4, alt: 'Two patches canceling each other out, leaving x² − 9' }],
  title: 'Four patches', art: 'S971', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl standing over a rectangular garden bed divided into four patches by two paths',
  words: [
    'Lena had to multiply (x + 3) by (x + 2), and she wrote x squared plus 6, because x times x and 3 times 2 seemed like the whole job. Her answer was missing half the garden.',
    'Her father showed her the vegetable bed. One side was x plus 3 feet long, the other x plus 2, and two paths split it into four patches. Every patch was part of the bed. Lena had counted only the two corners.',
    'First: x times x, the big patch. Outer: x times 2. Inner: 3 times x. Last: 3 times 2. Four patches: x squared, 2x, 3x and 6. Then the two middle patches were the same kind and joined: x squared plus 5x plus 6. Every piece in the first set of parentheses had to meet every piece in the second.',
    'Her father added a trap. (x + 3) squared is not x² + 9; it is (x + 3)(x + 3), four patches again: x² + 6x + 9. The two middle patches are why squaring a sum is never just squaring the pieces.',
    'And one special pair of factors makes patches disappear. (x + 3)(x − 3) gives x² − 3x + 3x − 9, and the middle patches cancel: x² − 9. That was the difference of squares, which she had met from the other direction when factoring.',
    'Every piece in the first set of parentheses multiplies every piece in the second. First, Outer, Inner, Last, then join the like terms. A garden bed with four patches is a multiplication you can walk around.',
  ],
};
STORIES['sequences'] = {
  about: 'a staircase with even steps, a staircase whose steps kept doubling, and the rule for step one hundred',
  more: [{ serial: 'S975', after: 0, alt: 'A boy on a staircase to the roof counting steps' }, { serial: 'S976', after: 1, alt: 'Even steps numbered, with the same rise written on each' }, { serial: 'S2492', after: 2, alt: 'Steps doubling in height, 2, 4, 8, 16, drawn to scale' }, { serial: 'S2493', after: 3, alt: 'The numbers one to a hundred in a line, the first and last joined by an arc labeled 101' }, { serial: 'S2494', after: 4, alt: 'A jar with the same coins added each month beside a jar growing faster with interest' }],
  title: 'Two staircases', art: 'S974', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a staircase looking up, a second staircase drawn beside it with steps growing taller',
  words: [
    'Kai counted the stairs to the roof: every step rose seven inches, and the fifth step was thirty-five inches up. He asked how high the hundredth would be and started multiplying before his brother told him there was a rule.',
    'A staircase with even steps is an arithmetic sequence: add the same amount each time. The nth step is the first step plus the rise times one less than n: a + (n - 1)d. Step one hundred was 7 + 99 × 7. He did not need to climb.',
    'Then his brother drew a different staircase where every step was twice the height of the one before: 2, 4, 8, 16. That was geometric: multiply by the same amount each time, and the nth term is a times r to the n minus 1. Step twenty on that staircase was over a million inches. Nobody was climbing that.',
    'Step one hundred on the even staircase was 700 inches up, more than fifty-eight feet. Then his brother asked for a total and told him a trick a schoolboy named Gauss is said to have found. Pair the first number with the last and the second with the next-to-last, and every pair adds to the same amount.',
    'One plus a hundred is a hundred and one, and there are fifty such pairs, so the numbers from one to a hundred add up to 5,050. Savings work both ways too: putting in the same amount each month is arithmetic, and earning interest on a growing total is geometric.',
    'Arithmetic: add the same amount each time, and the nth term is a + (n - 1)d. Geometric: multiply by the same amount, and the nth term is a times r to the n minus 1. Find the rule once, and step one hundred is a line of arithmetic, not a climb.',
  ],
};
STORIES['logarithms'] = {
  about: 'how many doublings it takes, and the number that answers it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S978', after: 0, alt: 'A girl folding bread dough on a floured counter, counting layers' }, { serial: 'S979', after: 2, alt: 'Eight becoming 16, 32, 64, with the doublings counted' }, { serial: 'S2495', after: 3, alt: 'A seismograph line growing ten times taller with each magnitude step' }, { serial: 'S2496', after: 4, alt: 'A pH strip beside a magnitude scale, both marked in powers of ten' }, { serial: 'S2497', after: 5, alt: '2 to the x equals 64 written above log base 2 of 64 equals 6' }],
  title: 'How many doublings', art: 'S977', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a kitchen counter folding dough, a chalk tally of doublings beside her',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s bread recipe said to fold the dough until it had sixty-four layers. She started with one layer, folded, and had two. Folded again, four. She wanted to know how many folds would get her to sixty-four without folding sixty-four times to find out.',
    'Two to the something equals sixty-four. She knew the base, 2, and the result, 64. What she wanted was the exponent, and that was the one thing the equation would not hand her.',
    'That question has a name. A logarithm is the exponent you were looking for. Two to the x equals sixty-four means log base 2 of 64 equals x, and x is six, because six doublings take one to sixty-four. Rosa folded six times. Log is just how many doublings, said out loud.',
    'Her uncle, who studied earthquakes, used base ten. Each whole step on the magnitude scale means the ground shakes about ten times harder. A magnitude 6 shakes ten times more than a 5 and a hundred times more than a 4, so the scale counts tenfoldings.',
    'The pH scale Rosa had learned in chemistry was a log too, each step ten times. Logarithms turn huge ranges into small, friendly numbers, which is why scientists reach for them whenever something grows by multiplying.',
    'A logarithm is the exponent you were looking for. b to the x equals y means log base b of y equals x. Every log question is how many doublings, or triplings, or tenfoldings, it takes.',
  ],
};
STORIES['absolute-value'] = {
  about: 'every place that is four steps from three, in both directions',
  more: [{ serial: 'S981', after: 0, alt: 'A boy looking at a half-right mark on his homework' }, { serial: 'S982', after: 1, alt: 'A number line with 3 marked and two spots four away' }, { serial: 'S2498', after: 2, alt: 'The two cases written out: x minus 3 equals 4, and equals minus 4' }, { serial: 'S2499', after: 3, alt: 'A number line with the stretch between minus 1 and 7 shaded' }, { serial: 'S2500', after: 4, alt: 'A card reading the absolute value of x equals minus 2, stamped no solution' }],
  title: 'Four steps from three', art: 'S980', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy standing on a numbered sidewalk at 3, looking four squares to his left and four to his right',
  words: [
    'The problem said the absolute value of x minus 3 equals 4, and Diego solved it: x is 7. He checked: 7 minus 3 is 4. Done. His teacher marked it half right, and Diego could not see what half he had missed.',
    'He stood on the sidewalk at square 3 and walked four steps forward to 7. Then he thought about absolute value: it is distance from zero, with no direction. Four steps from three could go the other way too.',
    'Four steps back landed on minus 1. The absolute value of minus 1 minus 3 is the absolute value of minus 4, which is 4. Two answers, one in each direction. The way to catch both is to split the equation into a positive case and a negative case, solve each, and check each.',
    'His teacher turned the equals sign into less than. Every place fewer than four steps from three lies between minus 1 and 7. So the absolute value of x minus 3 is less than 4 whenever x is between minus 1 and 7.',
    'Then she wrote one with no answers at all: the absolute value of x equals minus 2. A distance can never be negative, so no number works. Diego wrote no solution and got it fully right for the first time.',
    'Absolute value is distance from zero, so an absolute value equation usually has two answers, one in each direction. Split into a positive case and a negative case, solve both, check both. Four steps from three is two places.',
  ],
};
STORIES['counterclaims'] = {
  about: 'the objection that got to the room before the argument did, and the writer who learned to say it first',
  more: [{ serial: 'S984', after: 0, alt: 'A teacher asking a question from the back of a classroom as a girl holds her essay' }, { serial: 'S985', after: 1, alt: 'A draft with the strongest objection written in at the top, in the writer\'s own words' }, { serial: 'S2501', after: 2, alt: 'A balance scale with the objection on one pan and the answer on the other' }, { serial: 'S2502', after: 3, alt: 'Sentence starters on a board: it is true that, granted, some will say' }, { serial: 'S2503', after: 4, alt: 'A claim card rewritten smaller: try it for one year and measure' }],
  title: 'The objection that got there first', art: 'S983', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a lectern, a raised hand in the audience already up before she has begun',
  words: [
    'Priya\'s essay argued for a four-day school week and she was sure of it. Her teacher read it and asked one question: what about parents who work five days? Priya had an answer. It was not in the essay, and the question had gotten to the room before her argument did.',
    'She had left the objection out on purpose, because putting it in felt like arguing against herself. That was backward. Every reader had already thought of it. Leaving it out did not remove it; it just let the reader win the point for free.',
    'So she named it first, in its strongest form: many families cannot cover a fifth day, and a four-day week shifts a cost onto them. Then she answered it, with the district\'s own survey and a plan for the fifth day. A rebuttal weighs the objection. It does not pretend it away, and it does not wave it off with a sentence.',
    'Her teacher showed her the words that carry it: it is true that, granted, some will say. A concession admits what is right in the other side. Priya conceded the cost, then showed that the plan covered it.',
    'She also qualified her claim. Instead of saying every district should move to four days, she argued that hers should try it for one year and measure the results. A smaller claim was easier to defend, and the objection had less to push against.',
    'Name the best objection, then answer it. A rebuttal weighs the objection; it does not pretend it away. The strongest thing you can do to an argument against you is to make it yourself, better than they would, and then meet it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['satire'] = {
  about: 'an essay that praised something terrible with a straight face, and the boy who took it literally',
  more: [{ serial: 'S987', after: 0, alt: 'A boy horrified at a line in an old pamphlet in a library' }, { serial: 'S988', after: 1, alt: 'A line of the pamphlet in its calm, reasonable tone' }, { serial: 'S2504', after: 2, alt: 'Two arrows: what is exaggerated, and the real target behind it' }, { serial: 'S2505', after: 3, alt: 'An old title page reading A Modest Proposal, 1729, beside a crowded, hungry street in old Dublin' }, { serial: 'S2506', after: 4, alt: 'A parody news headline being shared on a phone by someone who thinks it is real' }],
  title: 'A straight face', art: 'S986', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy reading an old pamphlet in a library, his expression shifting from shock to a slow grin',
  words: [
    'Marcus read an old essay that calmly proposed solving poverty by selling children as food, with recipes. He was horrified and said so. His teacher asked whether he thought the author had meant it. Marcus said the author had written it, so obviously he meant it.',
    'Then Marcus noticed how reasonable the tone was. Numbers, costs, polite phrases. Nobody who meant it would be that calm. The calm was the point.',
    'That is satire: criticism delivered by exaggerating with a straight face. The exaggeration was the recipe. The real target was the people who talked about the poor as numbers and never as people. To read satire, find what is exaggerated, then find who is really being hit, and it is never the thing on the surface.',
    'The essay was A Modest Proposal, written by Jonathan Swift in 1729, when the poor of Ireland were starving under absent English landlords. Swift was furious, and the calm voice was how he showed it. He never says so outright, which is exactly the point.',
    'Satire has a risk built in: some readers never notice the straight face. Modern news parodies are still shared by people who think they are real. The better the satire, the more carefully it has to be read.',
    'Satire criticizes by exaggerating with a straight face. Find what is exaggerated, then find the real target. The calm tone is the tell, and the target is standing behind the joke, not in it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['sentence-structure'] = {
  about: 'a long sentence that carried a reader down a hill, and the short one at the bottom that stopped her',
  more: [{ serial: 'S990', after: 0, alt: 'A girl in a reading nook pressing a hand to her chest after a short sentence' }, { serial: 'S991', after: 1, alt: 'A long sentence drawn as a slope' }, { serial: 'S2507', after: 2, alt: 'A short sentence drawn as a wall' }, { serial: 'S2508', after: 3, alt: 'Three short sentences built the same way, drawn in a row like drumbeats' }, { serial: 'S2509', after: 4, alt: 'A car pulling away down a road, a girl waving from a porch' }],
  title: 'The long one, then the short one', art: 'S989', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a reading nook with a page where one long winding sentence is followed by three short words',
  words: [
    'Lena read a paragraph where one sentence ran for four lines, winding through the woods and the rain and the lost dog and the road. Then the next sentence was three words. He was gone. She felt it in her chest and did not know why.',
    'She had been taught that a sentence was a container for a fact. Long and short were just sizes. But the long one had carried her somewhere and the short one had stopped her cold, and that was not about the facts.',
    'Long sentences flow and build; they gather momentum and pull the reader along. Short sentences stop and hit. The writer had used the long one to get her moving and the short one to make her walk into a wall. The shape of a sentence does something to your reading before you understand a word of it.',
    'Her teacher showed her parallel structure next: I came, I saw, I conquered. Three sentences built the same way, so the reader feels the pattern and expects the next beat. Breaking the pattern on purpose is how a writer surprises.',
    'Then Lena tried it herself, a paragraph about the day her grandmother moved away. One long sentence for the packing, one long sentence for the drive, and then two words. She waved.',
    'Long sentences flow and build. Short sentences stop and hit. When a sentence moves you, ask what its shape did, because the writer chose the shape before the words.',
  ],
};
STORIES['enough-evidence'] = {
  about: 'a big claim resting on a small pile, and the two questions that are not the same question',
  more: [{ serial: 'S993', after: 0, alt: 'A judge at a science fair asking a boy about his poster' }, { serial: 'S994', after: 1, alt: 'One relevant study, alone, under a sweeping claim' }, { serial: 'S2510', after: 2, alt: 'The same claim, shrunk to fit the pile, and a taller pile beside it' }, { serial: 'S2511', after: 3, alt: 'Two groups of plants side by side, one near a speaker and one in silence' }, { serial: 'S2512', after: 4, alt: 'Two posters from two schools side by side, one showing faster growth and one not' }],
  title: 'A big claim on a small pile', art: 'S992', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a science fair table with a large poster claim balanced on a very small stack of papers',
  words: [
    'Owen\'s poster said that a certain music made plants grow faster. His evidence was one study of twelve plants over two weeks, and it was a real study about exactly that. A judge asked whether the evidence was enough. Owen said it was relevant. She said that was a different question.',
    'Relevant means it bears on the claim. Sufficient means there is enough of it to hold the claim up. His one study was relevant. Whether twelve plants could carry the words all plants and faster was another matter.',
    'The bigger the claim, the more evidence it needs. Owen could shrink the claim to fit the pile: in one small study, these plants grew faster. Or he could grow the pile to fit the claim, with more studies, more plants, more weeks. What he could not do was leave a big claim on a small pile and call it proved.',
    'The judge asked about other plants too. Had any grown in silence, in the same light and water, for comparison? Without a control group, faster than what? Owen had to admit his twelve plants had never had anyone to race.',
    'The next spring he ran it again with forty plants, half with music and half in silence, and asked a friend at another school to run the same test. His plants grew a little faster; hers did not. His new poster said the evidence was mixed, and the judges praised it for saying so.',
    'Relevant and sufficient are two different questions. The bigger the claim, the more evidence it needs. Match the size of the claim to the size of the pile, in either direction.',
  ],
};
STORIES['energy-kinds'] = {
  about: 'a ball held high, dropped, and the energy that changed shape without going anywhere',
  more: [{ serial: 'S996', after: 0, alt: 'A girl holding a ball over a balcony railing while her brother watches' }, { serial: 'S997', after: 1, alt: 'The ball at the top, still, with potential energy drawn as a full bar' }, { serial: 'S2516', after: 2, alt: 'The ball near the ground, fast, the bar now labeled kinetic' }, { serial: 'S2517', after: 3, alt: 'A ball landing in a courtyard with a small bounce and a puff of dust' }, { serial: 'S2518', after: 4, alt: 'A ball on a balcony 10 meters up with 49 joules written beside it' }],
  title: 'The ball on the balcony', art: 'S995', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a balcony holding a ball over the railing, a faint arrow showing its fall',
  words: [
    'Priya held a ball over the balcony railing and asked her brother where its energy was. It was not moving. It was not doing anything. He said it had none. She let go.',
    'By the time it hit the courtyard it was moving fast, and moving things have energy. So where had the energy been while the ball sat still in her hand?',
    'In its position. A ball held high has potential energy, stored by being up where gravity can pull it. As it falls, that energy turns into kinetic energy, the energy of motion, a little more with every meter. At the top, all potential. At the bottom, nearly all kinetic, apart from a little given to the air on the way down.',
    'When the ball landed, its energy did not vanish either. It became a thud you could hear, a little warmth in the ball and the ground, and a small bounce. Energy is never used up; it changes form.',
    'Her brother wanted numbers. A half-kilogram ball held 10 meters up stores about 49 joules: mass times gravity times height, 0.5 times 9.8 times 10. Just before landing, nearly all 49 had become energy of motion.',
    'The same bookkeeping runs everything. Food holds chemical energy, a battery holds electrical energy and the sun sends light, and every bit of it ends up somewhere, usually as a little warmth.',
    'Kinetic is energy of motion; potential is energy of position. One turns into the other, and energy is never destroyed, only changed. The ball in her hand was not empty. It was loaded.',
  ],
};
STORIES['waves'] = {
  about: 'a rope shaken at one end, slowly and then fast, and what the waves did',
  more: [{ serial: 'S999', after: 0, alt: 'A slow shake: a few long waves on the rope' }, { serial: 'S1000', after: 1, alt: 'A boy timing waves on a rope with his phone' }, { serial: 'S2519', after: 2, alt: 'A fast shake: many short waves, the same speed down the rope' }, { serial: 'S2520', after: 3, alt: 'A rope with waves 5 meters long at two shakes a second and 2 meters long at five' }, { serial: 'S2521', after: 4, alt: 'A speaker pushing sound waves through the air beside a rainbow of light' }],
  title: 'The rope', art: 'S998', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy shaking one end of a long rope tied to a fence, waves running down its length',
  words: [
    'Marcus tied a rope to the fence and shook the free end. Slow shakes sent long, lazy waves down the rope. Fast shakes sent lots of short ones. He assumed the fast ones were also traveling faster.',
    'He timed them with his phone. A wave took the same time to reach the fence whether he shook slowly or fast. The speed had not changed. Only the length of each wave had.',
    'That is because the rope decides the speed, not the hand. What the hand decides is the frequency, how many waves per second. Speed equals wavelength times frequency, so at a fixed speed, a higher frequency has to come with a shorter wavelength. More waves per second, less rope per wave.',
    'He worked it out with numbers. If waves ran down the rope at 10 meters per second and he shook it twice a second, each wave was 5 meters long. Five shakes a second made waves 2 meters long, and the speed stayed 10.',
    'The same rule runs sound and light. Sound travels through air at about 343 meters per second, so a high note has short waves and a low note long ones. The rope moved up and down as its waves passed, but sound pushes and pulls the air along its path instead.',
    'Speed equals wavelength times frequency. At a fixed speed, higher frequency means shorter waves. The rope was never in a hurry; the hand just chopped the same speed into smaller pieces.',
  ],
};
STORIES['electricity'] = {
  about: 'a water pipe, a pump and a kink, and the electricity that behaves the same way',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1002', after: 0, alt: 'A girl watering a garden as the hose kinks and the flow drops to a trickle' }, { serial: 'S1003', after: 1, alt: 'A pump pushing water: more push, more flow' }, { serial: 'S2522', after: 2, alt: 'A kinked hose: same push, less flow' }, { serial: 'S2523', after: 3, alt: 'A 9-volt battery wired to a 3-ohm resistor, 3 amperes flowing' }, { serial: 'S2524', after: 4, alt: 'A water wheel turning in a stream beside a glowing bulb, the flow the same on both sides' }],
  title: 'The water pipe', art: 'S1001', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl watering a garden with a hose, a kink in it, a small drawn battery and wire beside her',
  words: [
    'Ana was watering the garden when the hose kinked and the flow died to a trickle. Her dad turned the tap up and the trickle grew. Then he unkinked the hose and it gushed. He said she had just seen Ohm\'s law with water.',
    'Electricity in a wire, he said, is water in a pipe. Voltage is the push from the pump. Current is how much flows. Resistance is the kink.',
    'Turn up the push and more flows: more voltage, more current. Kink the pipe and less flows for the same push: more resistance, less current. Voltage equals current times resistance. A thin wire is a kinked hose; a thick one is wide open.',
    'Then they did it with numbers. A 9-volt battery across a 3-ohm resistor pushes 3 amperes, because 9 divided by 3 is 3. Double the resistance to 6 ohms, and the current falls to 1.5 amperes.',
    'Her dad pointed out one thing the hose gets right that surprises people. A bulb does not use up current any more than a water wheel uses up water. The same current flows out as flows in; the bulb takes energy, not charge. One more surprise: the electrons in a wire drift slower than a snail crawls. A lamp still lights the instant you flip the switch, because the push races down the wire at close to the speed of light.',
    'Voltage equals current times resistance. More push means more flow; more resistance means less. A pump, a pipe and a kink are the whole law. The garden hose had explained a whole chapter.',
  ],
};
STORIES['momentum'] = {
  about: 'a bike and a truck at the same speed, and why one is easy to stop',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1005', after: 0, alt: 'A hand stopping the bike easily' }, { serial: 'S1006', after: 1, alt: 'A bike and a delivery truck at the same speed, the difference between them their mass' }, { serial: 'S2525', after: 2, alt: 'Two carts colliding on a track, their totals written before and after' }, { serial: 'S2526', after: 3, alt: 'A car and a baseball with their momentum numbers written above them' }, { serial: 'S2527', after: 4, alt: 'An airbag opening in a car, the stop stretched out and gentle' }],
  title: 'The bike and the truck', art: 'S1004', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a crosswalk watching a bicycle and a truck roll toward the same stop line at the same speed',
  words: [
    'Sam watched a bike and a delivery truck coast toward the same stop line at the same slow speed. He could have stopped the bike with one hand. Nobody was stopping the truck with anything.',
    'Same speed. Different everything else. Sam could see it before he could name it. The difference was mass, and the thing that mass and speed make together has a name.',
    'Momentum is mass times velocity. The truck had about a hundred times the mass, so about a hundred times the momentum at the same speed, and stopping it takes a hundred times the push. When things collide, the total momentum before equals the total after: a fast cart hitting a slow one hands over exactly what it loses.',
    'Numbers make it plain. A 1,000-kilogram car at 20 meters per second carries 20,000 units of momentum. A baseball thrown at 40 meters per second carries about 6, because it weighs only 0.15 kilograms.',
    'Stopping takes a force for a time, and the longer the time, the smaller the force. That is why airbags and seatbelts stretch a crash over a little more time: the same momentum stops, but it pushes on the body far less. A catcher pulls the mitt back as the ball arrives for the same reason.',
    'Momentum is mass times velocity. In a collision, total momentum before equals total momentum after. The truck and the bike had the same speed and not the same momentum, which is why you stop one with a hand.',
  ],
};
STORIES['work-and-power'] = {
  about: 'the same box carried up the stairs slowly, then run, and what changed',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1008', after: 0, alt: 'A girl carrying a box slowly up a flight of stairs' }, { serial: 'S1009', after: 1, alt: 'The box lifted a set height: force times distance' }, { serial: 'S2528', after: 2, alt: 'A stopwatch beside the stairs, the same work in less time' }, { serial: 'S2529', after: 3, alt: 'Two stopwatches by the stairs, one at 10 seconds and one at 3' }, { serial: 'S2530', after: 4, alt: 'An old steam engine beside a working horse pulling the same load' }],
  title: 'The same box, twice', art: 'S1007', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl carrying a box up a flight of stairs, a second faded image of her running the same stairs',
  words: [
    'Lena carried a box up the stairs slowly and then, on a dare, ran the same box up the same stairs. She was far more tired the second time and assumed she had done more work. Her physics teacher said no.',
    'Work is force times distance. The box weighed the same and the stairs were the same height, so the work, the energy she had put into the box by lifting it, was identical both times. The box did not care how fast it arrived.',
    'What changed was power: work divided by time, measured in watts. The same work in half the time is twice the power, and her legs had felt the difference. A strong engine and a weak one can both lift the box; the strong one lifts it faster.',
    'Her teacher put numbers on it. The box weighed about 10 kilograms and the stairs rose 3 meters, so each trip gave the box about 300 joules. Walking took 10 seconds, about 30 watts. Running took 3 seconds, about 100 watts.',
    'Her legs had also lifted her own body both times, which is why stairs tire you even empty-handed. James Watt, whose name the unit carries, compared his steam engines to horses; one horsepower is about 746 watts.',
    'Work is force times distance, and it is how energy gets into a thing. Power is work divided by time, in watts. The box got the same energy both trips; Lena spent it at two different rates.',
  ],
};
STORIES['series-and-parallel'] = {
  about: 'two strings of holiday lights, and why one bulb could darken a whole string',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1011', after: 0, alt: 'Two strings of holiday lights on a porch, one all dark and one with a single bulb out' }, { serial: 'S1012', after: 1, alt: 'One path drawn through every bulb in a row' }, { serial: 'S2534', after: 2, alt: 'Separate paths to each bulb, one bulb dark and the rest lit' }, { serial: 'S2535', after: 3, alt: 'Two bulbs in series glowing dimly beside the same two in parallel glowing brightly' }, { serial: 'S2536', after: 4, alt: 'A tiny bypass wire inside a holiday bulb closing the gap where the filament broke' }],
  title: 'Two strings of lights', art: 'S1010', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a porch with two strings of holiday lights, one dark along its whole length and one with a single bulb out',
  words: [
    'Two strings of lights went up on the porch, and one bulb burned out on each. On the first string, every light went dark. On the second, one bulb went dark and the rest kept shining. Kai wanted to know why the same failure had two different results.',
    'The first string was wired in series: one path through every bulb. Break it anywhere and the whole path is broken. The second was in parallel: a separate path to each bulb, so a break in one path leaves the others whole.',
    'Series also adds up resistance, every bulb adding to the drag, so the more bulbs you add, the dimmer each one glows. Parallel gives each bulb its own road, so each gets the full voltage, and adding more paths actually lowers the total resistance. Houses are wired in parallel for both reasons: one lamp out does not darken the kitchen.',
    'His uncle drew it with numbers. Two 6-ohm bulbs in series on a 12-volt battery make 12 ohms, so 1 ampere flows through both. The same two in parallel each get the full 12 volts and 2 amperes apiece, and they glow much brighter.',
    'Many newer light strings hide a tiny bypass wire inside each bulb that takes over when the filament breaks. That is why a single dead bulb does not always darken a series string anymore.',
    'Series is one path, so one break darkens everything and resistances add. Parallel is separate paths, so the rest stay lit and each path gets the full voltage. One dead bulb tells you which kind of string you bought.',
  ],
};
STORIES['synthesis-essay'] = {
  about: 'an essay organized by its reasons instead of its sources, and what happened when two sources disagreed',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1014', after: 0, alt: 'An outline with one paragraph per source, crossed out' }, { serial: 'S1015', after: 1, alt: 'Four sources drawn as witnesses on a stand, the reasons written above them as the case' }, { serial: 'S2555', after: 2, alt: 'A new outline with one paragraph per reason, sources mixed under each' }, { serial: 'S2556', after: 3, alt: 'A sentence beginning according to the district budget, the source named inside it' }, { serial: 'S2557', after: 4, alt: 'A source that disagrees kept on the desk and answered in the margin' }],
  title: 'By reasons, not by sources', art: 'S1013', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a desk with four sources fanned out and an outline that lists three reasons instead of four sources',
  words: [
    'Rosa had four sources about school gardens and wrote four paragraphs: what source A said, what source B said, and so on. It read like a book report on four books. Her teacher said a synthesis was not a parade of sources.',
    'Rosa had organized by where the evidence came from. She needed to organize by what it proved. The sources were witnesses; the reasons were the case.',
    'Three reasons, three paragraphs, sources mixed inside each wherever they helped. And in the second paragraph, two sources disagreed about cost. Rosa did not report both and move on. She judged between them: one had a budget from a real district and the other an estimate from a blog, and she said which she trusted and why.',
    'Her teacher showed her how a source enters a sentence: according to the district budget, the garden cost less than one field trip a year. The source is named, the fact is used, and Rosa\'s voice stays in charge of the paragraph.',
    'She kept one source that cut against her, a teacher who worried that gardens take time from reading. Rosa answered it with her third reason, and the essay read as fair because it was. Her teacher called it the most persuasive paragraph in the essay.',
    'Organize by your reasons, not by your sources. Where sources disagree, judge between them and say why. A synthesis is your argument with their evidence in it, not their arguments in a row.',
  ],
};
STORIES['literary-argument'] = {
  about: 'a claim about a whole book, proved three times from three parts of it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1017', after: 0, alt: 'A boy frowning at a single scene from chapter two, a question mark beside it' }, { serial: 'S1018', after: 1, alt: 'Three bookmarks: beginning, middle, end' }, { serial: 'S2558', after: 2, alt: 'A rival reading written on a card and answered beneath it' }, { serial: 'S2559', after: 3, alt: 'A long block quote trimmed down to a few words woven into a sentence' }, { serial: 'S2560', after: 4, alt: 'A reader flipping back through a novel with new eyes' }],
  title: 'Three times, from three places', art: 'S1016', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a library table with a novel open in three places, a thesis on a card above them',
  words: [
    'Sam\'s claim was that the novel\'s narrator could not be trusted. He proved it with one scene from chapter two, and stopped. His teacher asked whether the narrator lied in chapter two or in the book.',
    'A claim about a whole work needs evidence from across the whole work. One scene proves a scene. Sam went back and found the narrator shading the truth in the middle and again at the end, in different ways.',
    'Then he did the hard part. The strongest reading against his was that the narrator was not lying but mistaken, an honest fool. Sam answered it: a fool gets things wrong at random; this narrator got things wrong only when the truth was unflattering. A pattern like that is a choice.',
    'His teacher pushed him on quotations too. Long block quotes let the book do his arguing. Sam trimmed each one to the few words that proved his point and wove them into his own sentences.',
    'The conclusion asked the last question: so what? If the narrator shades the truth only when it flatters him, then the book is really about vanity, and every page the reader believed becomes a page to reread.',
    'A literary argument is a disputable claim about the whole work, proved with evidence from across it, and it answers the strongest reading against yours. Three places and one rival reading make a claim about a book instead of a claim about a page.',
  ],
};
STORIES['op-ed'] = {
  about: 'thirty seconds of a stranger\'s attention, and the four things an op-ed does with them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1020', after: 0, alt: 'A girl\'s op-ed opening with 1962 while a reader\'s thumb scrolls past' }, { serial: 'S1021', after: 1, alt: 'A hook line at the top, the reader\'s eyes caught' }, { serial: 'S2561', after: 2, alt: 'The last line, an ask, with a phone and a ballot beside it' }, { serial: 'S2562', after: 3, alt: 'A lifeguard chair beside the pool, a whistle hanging from it' }, { serial: 'S2563', after: 4, alt: 'A city meeting room on a Tuesday night, the chairs filling up' }],
  title: 'Thirty seconds', art: 'S1019', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl reading a newspaper op-ed at a kitchen table while a stranger on a train, drawn faintly, reads the same page',
  words: [
    'Ana wrote an op-ed for the town paper about the closing pool, and it began with the pool\'s history since 1962. Her teacher said the average reader gives an op-ed thirty seconds, and Ana had spent them all on the year 1962.',
    'A stranger on a train, a coffee going cold, a thumb ready to scroll. That was her reader. The reader did not owe her the thirty seconds. She had to earn every one of them.',
    'Hook: last Saturday, forty kids stood outside a locked gate. Position: the pool should stay open, and here is how. Evidence: the cost, the alternatives, the summer drowning numbers from the county. Ask: come to Tuesday\'s meeting, and here is the room. Four moves, five hundred words, nothing about 1962.',
    'Her teacher added one more question: why should they listen to her? Ana had been a lifeguard at that pool for two summers, and one sentence saying so gave her words weight.',
    'She also gave the other side one fair sentence: the city is right that the pool loses money every year. Then she showed that closing it would cost more, in bus rides to the next town and in children swimming in the river instead.',
    'An op-ed is a hook, a position, evidence and an ask, all for a reader who gives you thirty seconds. Earn the first five with the hook, and spend the rest telling them exactly what to do.',
  ],
};
STORIES['gilded-age-and-progressives'] = {
  about: 'gold on the outside, iron underneath, and the reformers who scraped the gold off',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1023', after: 0, alt: 'Gilded meaning gold on the outside and something cheaper underneath, a gold-leaf frame around an iron plate' }, { serial: 'S1024', after: 1, alt: 'A mansion ceiling in gold leaf above a crowded tenement room' }, { serial: 'S2537', after: 2, alt: 'Muckrakers digging underneath, the novel The Jungle in 1906 showing readers what went into their meat' }, { serial: 'S2538', after: 3, alt: 'A food inspector, a broken trust, a tax form and a ballot for senators' }, { serial: 'S2539', after: 4, alt: 'Women marching with banners for the vote before 1920' }],
  title: 'Gold on the outside', art: 'S1022', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a street with a gilded mansion on one side and a smoking factory tenement on the other',
  words: [
    'Kai\'s book called the decades after 1877 the Gilded Age, and he thought gilded meant golden. His teacher asked him to look up gilded. Gold on the outside, something cheaper underneath.',
    'Railroads and steel made fortunes that built mansions with gold leaf on the ceilings. The same railroads and mills paid twelve-hour days to workers who lived six to a room, and children worked the looms. The gold was real. So was what it covered.',
    'Journalists called muckrakers dug underneath. In 1906 Upton Sinclair\'s novel The Jungle showed readers what went into their meat, and within months Congress passed laws to inspect it. In 1911 a fire at the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory killed 146 workers behind locked doors, and New York rewrote its safety rules.',
    'The Progressives were the people who scraped at the gold. Food safety laws after the packing houses were exposed. Antitrust to break the giant companies. The income tax so the fortunes paid a share, and the direct election of senators so money could not simply buy a seat, both amendments in 1913. Not a revolution; a set of repairs.',
    'The biggest repair came last. Women had organized for the vote for about seventy years, and in 1920 the Nineteenth Amendment won it for them. Children came off the looms more slowly, state by state.',
    'Railroads and industry made fortunes and misery after 1877; the Progressives answered with food safety, antitrust, the income tax, the direct election of senators and, by 1920, the vote for women. Gilded means gold over iron, and reform means looking underneath.',
  ],
};
STORIES['depression-and-new-deal'] = {
  about: 'one worker in four with no job, and the government that decided to be the employer',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1026', after: 0, alt: 'A girl picturing her own street with one worker in four out of a job' }, { serial: 'S1027', after: 1, alt: 'A stock ticker in 1929, then a shuttered bank' }, { serial: 'S2540', after: 2, alt: 'A dust storm rolling over a farmhouse on the southern plains' }, { serial: 'S2541', after: 3, alt: 'A work crew building a dam, a Social Security card beside it' }, { serial: 'S2542', after: 4, alt: 'A bank door with a notice that savings are insured' }],
  title: 'One in four', art: 'S1025', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a museum exhibit showing a 1933 bread line, four figures with one shaded',
  words: [
    'Rosa read that in 1933 a quarter of American workers had no job, and tried to picture it in her own town. Her father, her uncle, her neighbor, the mail carrier: one of the four with nothing. Then the next four. Then every four.',
    'The crash of 1929 had set it off. Banks failed, savings vanished, factories closed because nobody could buy, and nobody could buy because the factories closed. The old answer was to wait. Waiting made it worse.',
    'Farms suffered twice. Across the southern plains, years of drought on plowed-up grassland turned into the Dust Bowl, and storms of dirt buried fences and houses. Hundreds of thousands of people packed up and headed west.',
    'The New Deal decided the government would hire people itself: dams, roads, parks, murals and trails, millions of jobs where the market had none. And in 1935 it created Social Security, so that being old would not mean being poor. Not everything worked. Enough did to change what people expected their government to do.',
    'Some changes quietly outlasted everything. After 1933, federal insurance protected ordinary savings when a bank failed, and the bank runs that had wiped out families mostly stopped.',
    'The 1929 crash left a quarter of workers jobless by 1933. The New Deal put people to work and created Social Security in 1935. The Depression is the reason the country still argues about what a government owes the people in a bad year.',
  ],
};
STORIES['america-in-world-war-two'] = {
  about: 'a Sunday morning that ended an argument, and the home front that won a war',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1029', after: 1, alt: 'A family at a kitchen radio on the morning of December 7, 1941' }, { serial: 'S1030', after: 2, alt: 'Landing craft at dawn on a beach, June 6, 1944' }, { serial: 'S2543', after: 3, alt: 'Women on a factory line building aircraft' }, { serial: 'S2544', after: 4, alt: 'A family with suitcases at a camp gate in 1942' }, { serial: 'S2545', after: 5, alt: 'Tuskegee Airmen standing beside their fighter planes' }],
  title: 'The morning that ended the argument', art: 'S1028', cast: [],
  alt: 'A family gathered around a kitchen radio on a Sunday morning, a newspaper with a harbor photo on the table',
  words: [
    'Theo asked why America waited two years to join a war that had started in 1939. His grandmother said the country had been arguing about it every day, and then one morning the argument was over.',
    'December 7, 1941. Pearl Harbor. By evening there was nothing left to argue about. The next day the country was at war, and the question changed from whether to how.',
    'How was everyone. Sixteen million Americans served, about ten million of them drafted, and on June 6, 1944, D-Day, they landed on the beaches of France.',
    'At home, women built the planes and ships in numbers no one had planned for, and rationing meant a family counted sugar and gasoline. Posters called the women Rosie the Riveter.',
    'The home front had a dark side too. In 1942 the government forced about 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry, most of them American citizens, into camps. In 1988 the country formally apologized and paid the survivors.',
    'Black Americans served in segregated units, among them the Tuskegee Airmen, and came home asking why freedom abroad was not yet freedom at home. Victory came in 1945, in Europe in May and in the Pacific in August, after atomic bombs fell on Hiroshima and Nagasaki.',
    'Pearl Harbor, December 7, 1941, brought America into the war. D-Day was June 6, 1944. Victory came in 1945. At home, women went to work and families rationed, and the country that had argued for two years turned itself into a machine in four.',
  ],
};
STORIES['cold-war'] = {
  about: 'two powers that never fired at each other directly, and the week in 1962 when they almost did',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1032', after: 1, alt: 'Two powers competing everywhere else: in chess, in space and through wars in Korea and Vietnam' }, { serial: 'S1033', after: 2, alt: 'Sputnik circling the Earth in 1957' }, { serial: 'S2546', after: 3, alt: 'A missile on a truck under palm trees, ships in a ring around an island' }, { serial: 'S2547', after: 4, alt: 'Students crouching under their desks in a school drill' }, { serial: 'S2548', after: 5, alt: 'A wall coming down in 1989, people on top of it' }],
  title: 'Two powers, no shots', art: 'S1031', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy wondering how a war could last from 1945 to 1991 without the two sides fighting, two powers shaded on a world map',
  words: [
    'Jamal could not understand how a war could last from 1945 to 1991 without the two sides ever fighting. His teacher said that was the whole strangeness of it. Both had bombs that could end the other, so neither could afford to start.',
    'Instead they competed everywhere else: through wars in Korea and Vietnam, in space, in chess, in who could build the bigger stockpile. Every crisis was a test of nerve, and the closest call came in October 1962.',
    'Space became a race too. The Soviets launched Sputnik, the first satellite, in 1957, and put the first person in orbit in 1961. In 1969 Americans walked on the moon, and the race had its most famous finish line.',
    'In 1962 Soviet missiles were found in Cuba, ninety miles from Florida. For thirteen days the world waited for a mistake. Ships met at sea and turned around. A deal was made, some of it in secret, and the missiles left.',
    'The strangest part was how the peace held. Each side kept tens of thousands of warheads, enough to destroy the other many times over, and both knew it. Schoolchildren practiced hiding under their desks. The fear was the brake.',
    'Twenty-seven years after Cuba, the Berlin Wall came down, and in 1991 the Soviet Union dissolved without a shot fired between the two.',
    'From 1945 to 1991, two nuclear powers never fought each other directly. The closest call was Cuba in 1962. It ended with the Soviet Union\'s collapse in 1991, and the lesson of thirteen days is how much of peace is nerve.',
  ],
};
STORIES['civil-rights-movement'] = {
  about: 'courts, feet, crowds and laws, and the order they came in',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1035', after: 0, alt: 'Maya and her teacher laying Brown, Montgomery and the March on Washington on a line of eleven years' }, { serial: 'S1036', after: 2, alt: 'An empty bus seat, 1955' }, { serial: 'S2549', after: 3, alt: 'Nine students walking into Central High School past soldiers in 1957' }, { serial: 'S2550', after: 4, alt: 'A crowd of a quarter of a million in Washington in 1963, then the Civil Rights Act signed into law in 1964' }, { serial: 'S2551', after: 5, alt: 'Marchers crossing a bridge in Selma in 1965' }],
  title: 'Courts, feet, crowds, laws', art: 'S1034', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a museum hall passing four displays: a courtroom, a bus, a crowd at a monument, a signed law',
  words: [
    'Maya knew the names, Brown and Montgomery and the March on Washington, and thought they had all happened in one summer. Her teacher laid them on a line. 1954. 1955. 1963. 1964. 1965. Eleven years, in a particular order.',
    'The courts came first. Brown v. Board in 1954 said separate schools were not equal. A ruling is a piece of paper until people move.',
    'Then feet: on December 1, 1955, Rosa Parks refused to give up her seat on a Montgomery bus. The people of Montgomery then walked to work for more than a year rather than ride the back of the bus. A young minister named Martin Luther King Jr. led the boycott.',
    'Rulings met resistance. In 1957 nine Black students needed federal troops to walk into Central High School in Little Rock. In 1960 four college students sat at a whites-only lunch counter in Greensboro and would not leave, and sit-ins spread across the South.',
    'Then crowds: a quarter of a million in Washington in 1963. Then laws: the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965 put the ruling into the country\'s hands. Each step made the next one possible.',
    'The Voting Rights Act came after the march from Selma in 1965, when marchers were beaten on a bridge and the country watched on television. Within a few years, Black voter registration in the South rose sharply.',
    'Brown in 1954, Montgomery in 1955, Washington in 1963, then the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965. Courts, feet, crowds, laws. The order is the lesson: a ruling starts it, and people finish it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['recent-america'] = {
  about: 'the shocks a generation was born into, and the country that kept voting through all of them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1038', after: 0, alt: 'Kai\'s parents listing the internet, September 11, the 2008 crash, 2009 and the pandemic' }, { serial: 'S1039', after: 1, alt: 'The internet changing everything, a dial-up computer becoming a laptop while the shape of the country stands' }, { serial: 'S2552', after: 2, alt: 'A teenager scrolling a smartphone in 2010' }, { serial: 'S2553', after: 3, alt: 'The 2008 crash costing jobs and homes, then the pandemic closing schools and Kai finishing the year at a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S2554', after: 4, alt: 'The Capitol dome at night, lawmakers inside counting votes' }],
  title: 'The shocks', art: 'S1037', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy asking his parents what had happened in the world since they were his age, their list pinned on a photo wall',
  words: [
    'Kai asked his parents what had happened in the world since they were his age, and they started listing. The internet in every house. September 11, 2001. The 2008 crash. The first Black president in 2009. The pandemic in 2020. Kai had been born into the middle of the list.',
    'Each one had felt, at the time, like the thing that would change everything. Some did. The internet did. The rest changed a lot and left the shape of the country standing.',
    'In 2007 the smartphone put the internet in a pocket, and within a few years most teenagers carried one. News, friends and arguments all moved onto the same small screen.',
    'The 2008 crash cost millions of people their jobs and homes, and unemployment reached 10 percent in 2009. The pandemic closed schools across the country in the spring of 2020, and Kai finished that year at a kitchen table.',
    'Through all of it, the same things kept happening on schedule: elections every two years, a census every ten, courts in session. In January 2021 a crowd broke into the Capitol while Congress was counting the electoral votes. Congress finished the count that night, and the new president took office on the appointed day.',
    'The internet, September 11, 2001, the 2008 crisis, the first Black president in 2009 and the 2020 pandemic. Through all of it, the country kept voting, counting and handing over power on schedule, even when that was tested, which is the quiet half of recent history.',
  ],
};
STORIES['function-shifts'] = {
  about: 'a picture moved on a wall, and which part of the formula moved it which way',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1041', after: 0, alt: 'A girl sliding a framed graph the wrong way along a wall' }, { serial: 'S1042', after: 1, alt: 'f(x - 3) drawn to the right of f(x), the minus sending it right' }, { serial: 'S2564', after: 2, alt: 'Outside the parentheses, plus 2 lifting every point up by two, while inside the parentheses moves the picture sideways' }, { serial: 'S2565', after: 3, alt: 'A parabola with its lowest point moved from (0, 0) to (3, 2)' }, { serial: 'S2566', after: 4, alt: 'A graph flipped upside down beside one stretched to twice its height' }],
  title: 'The picture, moved', art: 'S1040', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding a framed graph against a wall, faint copies of it shifted up and to the right',
  words: [
    'Lena had the graph of f(x) and was told to draw f(x - 3). She slid it three to the left, because minus meant left. Every point was wrong. The teacher\'s answer key had it three to the right, and Lena thought the key was wrong.',
    'She tested it. At x = 3, f(x - 3) is f(0). So the new graph does at 3 what the old one did at 0. The old point had moved right by three. Minus inside the parentheses went right. It felt backward until she saw why: to get the same input, x had to be bigger.',
    'Outside the parentheses was the easy half: f(x) + 2 lifts every point up by two, and minus 2 drops it. Inside the parentheses moves the picture sideways, and the sign is reversed. Up and down are what you expect. Left and right are the opposite of what you expect, every time.',
    'Her teacher put both together. The graph of x squared has its lowest point at (0, 0). The graph of (x - 3) squared + 2 has the same shape with its lowest point moved to (3, 2): right three, up two.',
    'Then two more moves. A minus sign in front, -f(x), flips the graph upside down across the x-axis. Multiplying by 2, 2f(x), stretches every height to double. Each part of the formula does one job, and you can read them off one at a time.',
    'Outside the parentheses moves the graph up or down. Inside the parentheses moves it sideways, and minus goes right. A picture moved on a wall follows the same rules, and the sideways one is the one to check twice.',
  ],
};
STORIES['composite-functions'] = {
  about: 'two machines in a row, and the order that changed the answer',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1044', after: 0, alt: 'Two boys comparing answers, 13 and 16, beside the same two machines' }, { serial: 'S1045', after: 1, alt: 'The machines with g first and f second, inside out' }, { serial: 'S2567', after: 2, alt: 'The same machines swapped, a different number coming out' }, { serial: 'S2568', after: 3, alt: 'A jacket price tag with a coupon and a tax line applied in two different orders' }, { serial: 'S2569', after: 4, alt: 'A thermometer reading 100 degrees Celsius and 212 Fahrenheit, arrows going both ways' }],
  title: 'Two machines in a row', art: 'S1043', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy feeding a number into the first of two machines on a bench, the output rolling into the second',
  words: [
    'Owen had two machines. One doubled a number; one added three. He fed in five and got thirteen. His friend fed in five and got sixteen. Same machines, same number, different answers, and both of them were sure they had done it right.',
    'They had used the machines in different orders. Owen doubled first, then added three: ten, then thirteen. His friend added three first, then doubled: eight, then sixteen. The order was the whole difference.',
    'That is a composite function. f(g(x)) means apply g first, then f, working from the inside out. If g doubles and f adds three, f(g(5)) is thirteen. Swap them and g(f(5)) is sixteen. The machines do not care which order you like; the answer does.',
    'It happens at the checkout. A coupon takes 10 dollars off, and tax adds 8 percent. On a 50-dollar jacket, coupon first and then tax comes to 43.20; tax first and then coupon comes to 44.00. Two machines, two orders, eighty cents apart.',
    'Some pairs of machines undo each other. One turns Celsius into Fahrenheit, and the other turns it back. Feed 100 through both and 100 comes out, because each is the other\'s inverse. Cooks use that pair every time a recipe from another country lists its oven in Celsius.',
    'f(g(x)) means apply g first, then f. Work from the inside out, and the order matters. Two machines in a row are two different machines depending on which one you feed first.',
  ],
};
STORIES['unit-circle'] = {
  about: 'a walk around a circle of radius one, reading the sine and cosine off the ground',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1047', after: 0, alt: 'A girl walking a chalk circle on a playground, stopping to measure' }, { serial: 'S1048', after: 1, alt: 'Her position at 30 degrees, x and y marked as cosine and sine' }, { serial: 'S2570', after: 2, alt: 'The circle divided into quarters with the signs of x and y in each' }, { serial: 'S2571', after: 3, alt: 'A right triangle inside the circle with sides 0.87 and 0.5 and a slanted side of 1' }, { serial: 'S2572', after: 4, alt: 'A string laid once around the circle, measuring about 6.28' }],
  title: 'A walk around the circle', art: 'S1046', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl walking a large circle chalked on a playground, its center marked, a right triangle drawn from her spot to the center',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s teacher chalked a circle with radius one on the playground and asked her to walk it, stopping every so often. At each stop she was to say how far right of center she was and how far up. Maya did not see what that had to do with trigonometry.',
    'At thirty degrees she was about 0.87 to the right and exactly 0.5 up. Her teacher said: cosine 30 and sine 30. Maya looked down at her feet. The x was the cosine. The y was the sine. The whole table she had memorized was just where she stood.',
    'She learned five stops by heart: 0, 30, 45, 60 and 90 degrees. Then she kept walking. In the second quarter she was left of center, so cosine went negative while sine stayed positive. In the third both were negative. In the fourth, x was positive again and y still down. The quarter she stood in set the signs.',
    'Her teacher asked her to square both numbers at thirty degrees and add them: 0.75 plus 0.25 is 1. It works at every stop, because each stop makes a right triangle with a slanted side of length one. Sine squared plus cosine squared is always one.',
    'A whole lap was about 6.28 along the chalk, because the edge of a circle with radius one is 2 pi long. Mathematicians measure turns in those lengths, called radians, so a full turn is 2 pi and a quarter turn is pi over 2.',
    'On the unit circle, x is cosine and y is sine. Know 0, 30, 45, 60 and 90 degrees, and let the quarter set the signs. Everything after that is walking.',
  ],
};
STORIES['half-life'] = {
  about: 'a clock that halved instead of ticking, and the sample that was almost never gone',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1050', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a lab bench frowning at a sample and a clock' }, { serial: 'S1051', after: 1, alt: 'A tray at the start, then half, then a quarter, then an eighth' }, { serial: 'S2573', after: 2, alt: 'The formula with one half raised to the number of half-lives' }, { serial: 'S2574', after: 3, alt: 'An ancient bone beside a clock showing two halvings of carbon-14' }, { serial: 'S2575', after: 4, alt: 'A few last atoms scattered on an almost empty tray' }],
  title: 'The halving clock', art: 'S1049', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a lab bench with a tray of glowing pieces, half of them dimmed, a clock on the wall',
  words: [
    'Sam had a sample with a half-life of ten years and a question: how much would be left after thirty. He reasoned that if half went in ten years, all of it would be gone in twenty, and after thirty there would be less than nothing.',
    'His teacher asked what half of a half was. A quarter. And half of that? An eighth. The sample did not lose the same amount each decade. It lost half of whatever was left.',
    'So the clock ticks in halvings. Thirty years is three half-lives. Halve three times: one half, one quarter, one eighth. Amount left equals the start times one half to the power of the number of half-lives. An eighth remained, and after a hundred years about a thousandth would.',
    'Scientists use the same clock to date old things. Living things take in carbon-14, which has a half-life of about 5,730 years. A bone with a quarter of its carbon-14 left has been through two half-lives, so it is about 11,460 years old.',
    'On paper the halving never reaches zero. A real sample is made of whole atoms, though, so eventually the last one does decay; for any sample big enough to hold, that takes an extremely long time.',
    'Count the half-lives, then halve that many times. Amount left is the start times one half to the power of the number of half-lives. A halving clock gets close to zero long before it gets there.',
  ],
};
STORIES['end-behavior'] = {
  about: 'what the ends of a graph do when the middle stops mattering, settled by two facts',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1053', after: 0, alt: 'A girl plotting points on a messy graph until she gives up at x equals nine' }, { serial: 'S1054', after: 1, alt: 'A girl crossing out every term of a long polynomial except the highest power' }, { serial: 'S2576', after: 2, alt: 'An even-power graph with both ends up beside another with both ends down' }, { serial: 'S2577', after: 3, alt: 'A giant x to the fourth beside a small 50 x cubed at x equals 1,000' }, { serial: 'S2578', after: 4, alt: 'An odd-power graph, one end down and one end up' }],
  title: 'What the ends do', art: 'S1052', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a desk with four sketched graphs, the middles scribbled over and only the far ends showing',
  words: [
    'Rosa had a polynomial with six terms and was asked what its graph did far to the left and far to the right. She started plotting points and gave up somewhere around x equals nine. The middle was a mess of hills.',
    'Her teacher told her to cross out every term but one. Far from zero, the highest power is so much bigger than the rest that the rest stop mattering. The ends belong to that one term.',
    'Two facts settle it. Is the highest power even or odd? Even means both ends go the same way; odd means they go opposite ways. Is its coefficient positive or negative? Positive sends the right end up; negative sends it down. Her six-term mess was x to the fourth with a minus sign: even, negative, both ends down. The middle could do what it liked.',
    'Numbers showed her why. At x = 1,000, x to the fourth is a trillion, while 50 x cubed is only 50 billion, twenty times smaller. Every other term shrinks next to the leader as x grows.',
    'Her teacher drew one odd case to finish: x cubed. Far left it plunges down; far right it climbs. Flip the sign to minus x cubed, and the ends swap. The middle can bend all it likes.',
    'The highest power rules the ends. Even or odd, positive or negative: those two facts settle the shape far from the middle, and the middle is a separate question.',
  ],
};
STORIES['two-sources'] = {
  about: 'two witnesses to one event, and the reader who stopped asking which was lying',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1056', after: 0, alt: 'A girl frowning over two accounts of a factory fire' }, { serial: 'S1057', after: 1, alt: 'Two columns: where they agree, where they differ, what each adds' }, { serial: 'S2579', after: 2, alt: 'Two views of one fire: a worker at a locked stairwell door, and an owner counting doors on the plans in an office' }, { serial: 'S2580', after: 3, alt: 'A factory worker and an owner seen side by side, each with something at stake' }, { serial: 'S2581', after: 4, alt: 'A fire marshal\'s report on a desk, three of four doors marked locked' }],
  title: 'Two witnesses', art: 'S1055', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a table with two accounts of the same event side by side, a line drawn where they overlap',
  words: [
    'Maya read two accounts of the same factory fire, one by a worker and one by the owner, and they disagreed about how many exits were open. She decided one of them was lying and spent an hour trying to work out which.',
    'Her teacher asked her to hold both in mind instead of picking. Where did they agree? The date, the floor, the smoke. Where did they differ? The exits. What did each add that the other could not? The worker knew the stairwell; the owner knew the building plans.',
    'The difference was not a lie. The worker had run for the door she knew, found it locked, and remembered one exit. The owner counted the doors on the plans and remembered four. Two views of one fire, from the stairwell and from the office, and the truth needed both.',
    'Her teacher asked one more question: what did each witness have at stake? The owner could be blamed if doors were locked; the worker had lost friends. Neither had to be lying for their stake to shape what they remembered.',
    'Then Maya found a third witness, the fire marshal\'s report written that week. Three of the four doors had been locked from outside. The worker was right about her door, and the owner was right that the plans showed four.',
    'Hold both sources in mind. Ask where they agree, where they differ, what each adds and what each had at stake. A difference is often two views of one thing, and the job is to stand where each witness stood.',
  ],
};
STORIES['assumptions'] = {
  about: 'the step the writer skipped, found by asking what would have to be true',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1059', after: 0, alt: 'A boy reading a letter to the editor about closing a skate park, puzzled' }, { serial: 'S1060', after: 1, alt: 'Hunting for a missing sentence: the claim that the park should close at the top, two injuries below, a gap between' }, { serial: 'S2582', after: 2, alt: 'The missing stair drawn in dotted lines, labeled' }, { serial: 'S2583', after: 3, alt: 'Two bars comparing injuries per visitor at a skate park and a football field' }, { serial: 'S2584', after: 4, alt: 'A boy writing a caveat into his own reply, a question mark beside it' }],
  title: 'The skipped step', art: 'S1058', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a desk with an argument written as stairs, one stair missing between two others',
  words: [
    'The letter argued that the town should close the skate park because there had been two injuries there this year. Marcus felt something was wrong with the argument and could not find the bad sentence. Every sentence was true. Two injuries, one park.',
    'His teacher told him to stop hunting for a false sentence and hunt for a missing one. What would have to be true for two injuries to prove the park should close?',
    'It would have to be true that two injuries in a year is a lot. Marcus looked it up: the football field had had nine. That was the assumption, the step the writer skipped, and once it was written down it wobbled. The letter was not lying. It was standing on a stair that was not there.',
    'Even nine was not the whole picture, because the two places had different crowds. The skate park had about 200 visits a week and the field about 400. Per visit, the park\'s injury rate was less than half the field\'s.',
    'His teacher told him to check his own argument the same way. His claim, that the park was safe enough to keep, assumed the injury reports were complete. He asked the town whether minor injuries were counted, and wrote that caveat into his reply.',
    'An assumption is the step the writer skipped. Ask what would have to be true for the reason to support the claim, write that step down, and see whether it holds. The weakest part of an argument is usually the sentence nobody wrote.',
  ],
};
STORIES['precise-words'] = {
  about: 'four ways to cross a room, and the one word that said which',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1062', after: 0, alt: 'A girl writing that her character walked to the principal\'s office' }, { serial: 'S1063', after: 1, alt: 'Four figures: a stroll, a march, a trudge, a plain walk' }, { serial: 'S2585', after: 2, alt: 'One sentence with the word swapped four times, four different boys' }, { serial: 'S2586', after: 3, alt: 'Three people careful with money, one called thrifty with praise, one frugal and one cheap with scorn' }, { serial: 'S2587', after: 4, alt: 'An open thesaurus with a long fancy word crossed out and a short one circled' }],
  title: 'Walk, stroll, march, trudge', art: 'S1061', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl watching four people cross a hallway in four different ways',
  words: [
    'Rosa wrote that her character walked to the principal\'s office. Her teacher asked how. Rosa said he walked. It was in the sentence. Her teacher said the sentence told her he had gone from one place to another and nothing else. Rosa had never thought of a verb as a choice.',
    'Was he strolling, in no hurry, sure it was nothing? Marching, angry, ready to argue? Trudging, dreading every step? Each was a walk. Each was a different boy and a different story.',
    'Near-synonyms are not the same word wearing different clothes. Each one names a slightly different thing. Rosa chose trudged, and the sentence now carried his dread all the way down the hall without a word about how he felt. Nouns work the same way: a dog could be anything, but a limping terrier is one animal.',
    'Words carry feelings as well as meanings. Thrifty, frugal and cheap all mean careful with money, but thrifty praises, frugal describes and cheap insults. Choosing one is choosing a side, and a careful writer picks the one that matches the feeling on purpose.',
    'Her teacher warned her about the opposite mistake: reaching into a thesaurus for the fanciest word. Perambulated is not better than walked. The precise word is the one that is exactly right, not the one that is longest.',
    'Near-synonyms name slightly different things. The precise word names exactly what happened, and it can carry a paragraph of feeling in one verb. Walk is a category; trudge is a boy.',
  ],
};
STORIES['author-choices'] = {
  about: 'a novel that began after the war instead of before it, and what that choice decided',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1065', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at the first page of a novel that starts the day a soldier comes home' }, { serial: 'S1066', after: 1, alt: 'The same story sketched with an earlier starting point' }, { serial: 'S2588', after: 2, alt: 'Choices marked on the story: ending at the first good night\'s sleep, the letter from a dead friend moved to the middle' }, { serial: 'S2589', after: 3, alt: 'A wife watching her husband flinch at a whistling kettle in a kitchen' }, { serial: 'S2590', after: 4, alt: 'A timeline of the story with its start, its end and its order marked as choices' }],
  title: 'Why it starts here', art: 'S1064', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a novel open at the first page, a faint timeline behind him with the story starting late along it',
  words: [
    'Kai noticed that the novel began the day the soldier came home and never showed the war at all. He thought the author had skipped the interesting part. His teacher asked what the book would have been if it had started at the front.',
    'A war story. Battles, fear, the friends who did not come back. Instead it was a story about a kitchen, a wife who did not know what to say, and a man who flinched at the kettle. Starting late made the war a shadow instead of a subject.',
    'Every choice worked that way. Ending at the first good night\'s sleep instead of a year later said the story was about the first step, not the recovery. Putting the letter from the dead friend in the middle instead of at the start meant the reader learned the reason for the flinching only after watching it for a hundred pages.',
    'Point of view was a choice too. The book was told by the wife, not the soldier, so the reader saw the flinching from outside, the way she did, and understood it only as late as she did.',
    'Where a story begins, where it ends, whose eyes it uses and the order of its parts are the author deciding what it means.',
    'Where a story begins, where it ends, and the order of its parts all shape its meaning. Ask what it would mean if the choice had gone the other way, and the author\'s reasons come into view.',
  ],
};
STORIES['rock-cycle'] = {
  about: 'a rock that was never finished, seen from a mountain path',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1068', after: 0, alt: 'A boy on a mountain path holding up three rocks that look nothing alike' }, { serial: 'S1069', after: 1, alt: 'Lava cooling into dark rock' }, { serial: 'S2591', after: 2, alt: 'Obsidian, sandstone and striped gneiss lined up on a boulder' }, { serial: 'S2592', after: 3, alt: 'Layers of sand pressing into stone, then bending under heat' }, { serial: 'S2593', after: 4, alt: 'A single grain of sand traveling a loop: sea, layer, mountain, river' }],
  title: 'Never finished', art: 'S1067', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a mountain path holding three rocks: a glassy dark one, a layered sandy one and a banded one',
  words: [
    'On the hike Kai picked up three rocks that looked nothing alike: one dark and glassy, one sandy and layered, one banded like ribbon candy. He asked which one was the real rock. His aunt said all three, and that each had been the others.',
    'The glassy one was igneous, cooled from a melt. The layered one was sedimentary, pressed from pieces of older rock that had washed into a sea. The banded one was metamorphic, changed by heat and pressure deep underground without melting.',
    'She gave the three their names. The glassy one was obsidian, lava cooled so fast that no crystals had time to grow. The sandy one was sandstone, and the banded one was gneiss, which had started life as granite and been squeezed until its minerals lined up in stripes.',
    'Then she pointed at the mountain. The sedimentary rock had been made from the wreckage of an older mountain. Bury it deep and it becomes metamorphic. Melt it and it comes back as igneous. Weather it and its pieces make new layers. Any kind can become any other, given time, and none of them is the finished one.',
    'None of it is quick. A grain of sand washed into the sea today might sit in a layer for a hundred million years before it is buried, cooked, lifted into a mountain and weathered loose again. Kai\'s three rocks were three photographs from one very long film.',
    'Igneous cooled from a melt, sedimentary was pressed from pieces, metamorphic was changed by heat and pressure. Each kind can become any other. A rock is a stage, not a destination.',
  ],
};
STORIES['climate-and-weather'] = {
  about: 'one rainy Tuesday, thirty years of Tuesdays, and the difference between them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1071', after: 0, alt: 'A girl on a porch in April snow, seeing her breath' }, { serial: 'S1072', after: 1, alt: 'One cold day circled on a calendar' }, { serial: 'S2594', after: 2, alt: 'Thirty years of Tuesdays stacked into one average line' }, { serial: 'S2595', after: 3, alt: 'A thirty-year line with the cold day as a single dot on it' }, { serial: 'S2596', after: 4, alt: 'A hot October day circled beside the cold April one, both single dots' }],
  title: 'One Tuesday and thirty years', art: 'S1070', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a porch in the rain looking at a thirty-year chart of the town\'s weather',
  words: [
    'It snowed in April, and Ana\'s neighbor said that proved the climate was not warming. Ana thought that sounded right. It was cold. She could see her breath.',
    'Her science teacher asked whether one cold day proved anything about thirty years. Weather is what the sky does today. Climate is what it does on average over decades. A snowy Tuesday is weather. It says nothing about climate either way, hot or cold.',
    'Scientists use thirty years for a reason. Thirty years of Tuesdays washes out the cold snaps and the heat waves and leaves the pattern underneath. When they say a place has a warm climate, they mean the average of decades, not last week. A desert can have a cold night and still be a desert.',
    'Ana looked at the town\'s thirty-year chart. The line wandered up and down, week to week, like the weather. But the whole line had drifted, and the April snow was one dot on it. One dot can no more bend the line than one tall kid can change the average height of a class.',
    'Her teacher added the other half of the lesson. One hot day in October proves nothing either. Ana\'s neighbor had drawn a big conclusion from a small sample, and it is an easy mistake to make in either direction.',
    'Weather is today; climate is decades of average. A single day, hot or cold, tells you nothing about climate. Look at the line, not the dot.',
  ],
};
STORIES['life-of-a-star'] = {
  about: 'a campfire that lasts billions of years, and the two ways it goes out',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1074', after: 0, alt: 'A boy watching a campfire burn down under a sky full of stars' }, { serial: 'S1075', after: 1, alt: 'A star drawn as a balance: fusion pushing out, gravity pulling in' }, { serial: 'S2597', after: 2, alt: 'A sun-like star swelling red, then shrinking to a small white ember' }, { serial: 'S2598', after: 3, alt: 'A heavy star blowing apart in a bright shell, a dense remnant at the center' }, { serial: 'S2599', after: 4, alt: 'The carbon, oxygen and iron in a hand traced back to a dying star' }],
  title: 'The long campfire', art: 'S1073', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy beside a campfire at night looking up at a sky full of stars',
  words: [
    'Owen watched the campfire burn down and asked whether stars burned down too. His uncle said yes, just slower. A star is a fire that lasts billions of years, and like a campfire, what happens at the end depends on how big it was.',
    'While it lasts, a star fuses hydrogen into helium in its core, and that fusion holds it up against its own gravity. When the hydrogen runs low, the balance breaks.',
    'The sun is about 4.6 billion years old and roughly halfway through its hydrogen. In about five billion years it will swell, and the Earth will not be a comfortable place. Nobody needs to worry this week.',
    'A star like the sun swells into a red giant, sheds its outer layers, and settles into a white dwarf, a hot ember slowly cooling forever. A much heavier star goes the other way: it explodes as a supernova, scattering the elements that will make planets, and leaves behind a neutron star or a black hole. Same fuel, different ending, decided by mass.',
    'The explosions matter to us. The carbon in Owen\'s hand, the oxygen he was breathing and the iron in his blood were all made inside stars and scattered when they died. The campfire was burning atoms that had been made in stars.',
    'A star fuses hydrogen into helium while it lasts. A sun-like star ends as a white dwarf; a heavy star explodes and leaves a neutron star or a black hole. How the fire ends depends on how much wood there was.',
  ],
};
STORIES['human-impact'] = {
  about: 'a planet treated the way a doctor treats a patient: measure, find the cause, act',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1077', after: 0, alt: 'A doctor reading a chart while a girl watches, a small globe on the desk' }, { serial: 'S1078', after: 1, alt: 'A river measured year by year, a line rising' }, { serial: 'S2600', after: 2, alt: 'A factory outflow, then a filter, then the line falling' }, { serial: 'S2601', after: 3, alt: 'A lake with acid rain readings improving year by year on a chart' }, { serial: 'S2602', after: 4, alt: 'The ozone hole over Antarctica in satellite maps, shrinking across the years' }],
  title: 'The way a doctor does it', art: 'S1076', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a clinic watching a doctor read a chart, a small globe on the desk with its own chart beside it',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s uncle is a doctor, and she asked him why a lesson on the environment kept talking about measurement. He said measurement is the whole job. You do not treat a patient by how you feel about them.',
    'A doctor measures the change: temperature, blood pressure, the numbers over time. Then finds the cause, then acts on the cause, not the symptom, and then measures again to see whether the action worked.',
    'People change the Earth in measurable ways with measurable causes. A river warms; the readings show it. The cause is found upstream, a plant\'s outflow. The action is a filter or a rule. The next year\'s readings say whether it worked. Rosa had thought the environment was a matter of caring. It was a matter of charts.',
    'The method has worked at the size of a country. Acid rain was measured falling on lakes in the 1970s and 1980s, traced to sulfur from power plants, and cut by rules passed in 1990. The readings in the lakes have been improving since.',
    'It has worked at the size of the planet. A hole in the ozone layer was measured over Antarctica in the 1980s, traced to certain refrigerant gases, and the world agreed in 1987 to phase them out. The measurements now show the hole slowly closing.',
    'People change the Earth in measurable ways with measurable causes. Measure the change, find the cause, act on the cause, then measure again. The planet is a patient, and the chart is how you know.',
  ],
};
STORIES['earths-layers'] = {
  about: 'a peach, and the earthquake waves that mapped what nobody could dig to',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1080', after: 0, alt: 'A boy asking about the middle of the Earth as his mother hands him a peach' }, { serial: 'S1081', after: 1, alt: 'The peach in cross-section: skin, flesh, pit' }, { serial: 'S2603', after: 2, alt: 'A peach whose skin is as thin as paper, next to the Earth with its crust drawn to scale' }, { serial: 'S2604', after: 3, alt: 'Earthquake waves bending through the layers on a diagram' }, { serial: 'S2605', after: 4, alt: 'A shadow zone on the far side of the Earth where sideways waves never arrive' }],
  title: 'The peach', art: 'S1079', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy cutting a peach in half at a kitchen counter, a diagram of the Earth beside it',
  words: [
    'Kai asked how anyone knew what the middle of the Earth was made of when the deepest hole ever drilled went only twelve kilometers. His mother handed him a peach and a knife.',
    'The skin is the crust, thin and cool, the part everything lives on. The flesh is the mantle, thick and slow-moving, hot enough to flow over millions of years. The pit is the core, and it has two parts: a liquid outer core and a solid inner one.',
    'The peach exaggerates the skin. The crust is only about 35 kilometers thick under the continents and thinner under the oceans, on a planet 6,400 kilometers from surface to center. On a real peach the skin would be as thin as a few sheets of paper.',
    'Nobody dug there. Earthquakes did the mapping. Their waves travel at different speeds through different materials, and some kinds cannot pass through liquid at all. By timing waves arriving around the world, scientists drew the layers they could never see. The plates ride on the mantle, and the churning liquid core makes the magnetic field that turns a compass.',
    'One kind of wave, the sideways-shaking kind, never arrives on the far side of the world from a quake, because it cannot cross liquid. That shadow is how the liquid outer core was found. In 1936 a Danish scientist, Inge Lehmann, noticed waves bouncing off something inside it and found the solid inner core.',
    'Crust, mantle, outer core, inner core. Earthquake waves mapped the layers, the plates ride the mantle, and the liquid core makes the magnetic field. A peach and a seismograph are the whole picture.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ocean-currents'] = {
  about: 'a conveyor belt of heavy water, and why one coast is mild and another is not',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1083', after: 0, alt: 'Two cousins at the same latitude, one in a light jacket and one in a heavy coat' }, { serial: 'S1084', after: 1, alt: 'Wind pushing surface water in a great loop' }, { serial: 'S2606', after: 2, alt: 'Palm trees on a mild coast at the same latitude as a snowy one across the ocean' }, { serial: 'S2607', after: 3, alt: 'Cold salty water sinking near the pole and creeping along the bottom' }, { serial: 'S2608', after: 4, alt: 'Warm Pacific water sloshing east in an El Niño year, weather shifting with it' }],
  title: 'The conveyor belt', art: 'S1082', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a harbor wall in a light jacket, a map behind her with warm and cold currents drawn as arrows',
  words: [
    'Lena\'s cousin in a city at the same latitude as hers shivered through winters twice as cold, and Lena wanted to know why. Same distance from the equator, same sun. Her geography teacher pointed at the sea.',
    'Wind drives the surface currents, pushing warm water from the tropics toward the poles in great loops. Lena\'s coast sat beside a warm current. Her cousin\'s sat beside a cold one coming back down.',
    'The most famous of the warm currents is the Gulf Stream, which carries water heated in the tropics up the Atlantic coast and across toward Europe. Partly thanks to it, towns on the far side of that ocean grow palm trees at the latitude of Canadian snow.',
    'Underneath, a slower engine runs. Near the poles, water gets cold and salty and heavy, and it sinks, crawling along the ocean floor for centuries before rising somewhere warm. Surface and deep together make a conveyor belt that carries heat around the planet, which is why coasts have the weather they do.',
    'The belt can shift. Every few years the warm water of the Pacific sloshes east in what is called an El Niño, and rain, drought and fish move with it around the world. When a current moves, so does the weather that rode on it.',
    'Wind drives surface currents; cold salty water sinks and drives the deep ones. Currents carry heat, which is why two coasts at the same latitude can have different winters. The sea is a heating system with a very long loop.',
  ],
};
STORIES['natural-resources'] = {
  about: 'a forest cut slower than it grows, and the rate that decides which side of the line a resource is on',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1086', after: 0, alt: 'A boy comparing a textbook list to a photo of a shrinking rainforest' }, { serial: 'S1087', after: 1, alt: 'An hourglass for oil, millions of years, beside a forest regrowing in decades' }, { serial: 'S2609', after: 2, alt: 'Coal, oil and ore on one side, sunlight, wind, forests and fish on the other' }, { serial: 'S2610', after: 3, alt: 'Trees planted faster than they are cut, the forest thickening' }, { serial: 'S2611', after: 4, alt: 'Fishing boats tied up in a Newfoundland harbor after the cod fishery closed in 1992' }],
  title: 'Slower than it grows', art: 'S1085', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a managed forest with a cut stump beside rows of young trees',
  words: [
    'Sam\'s textbook listed forests under renewable and oil under nonrenewable, and he asked why, then, the rainforest was disappearing. If it was renewable, how could it run out?',
    'Renewable means it comes back on a human timescale. Oil takes millions of years, so it does not. A forest can regrow in decades, so it can. But can is not the same as does.',
    'The lists are long on both sides. Coal, oil, natural gas and metal ores took millions of years to form, so a used tank of gasoline is simply gone. Sunlight, wind, forests, fish and fresh water come back, some in a day and some in a decade.',
    'His teacher showed him two forests. One was cut slower than it grew, and it would stand forever. The other was cut faster than it grew, and it was a coal mine with leaves. The same was true of a lake pumped faster than rain refilled it. For forests and water, the rate of use decides which side of the line they land on.',
    'The line can be crossed at sea too. Off Newfoundland, cod were caught faster than they bred for decades, and in 1992 the fishery collapsed and was closed. Thirty years later it had only partly come back. A renewable resource had been spent like a nonrenewable one.',
    'Renewable comes back on a human timescale; nonrenewable does not. For forests and water, the rate of use decides which side of the line they land on. A forest cut slower than it grows is renewable; the same forest cut faster is not.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-big-bang'] = {
  about: 'raisins in rising dough, and the three clues that the universe is still rising',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1089', after: 0, alt: 'A girl watching raisin dough rise on a counter, the raisins spreading apart' }, { serial: 'S1090', after: 1, alt: 'Two raisins close together moving apart slowly, two far apart moving apart fast' }, { serial: 'S2612', after: 2, alt: 'Two engineers puzzled at a horn-shaped antenna in 1965' }, { serial: 'S2613', after: 3, alt: 'A sky glowing faintly everywhere, and a chart of the elements' }, { serial: 'S2614', after: 4, alt: 'A loaf with an edge beside a universe with none, stretching everywhere at once' }],
  title: 'Raisins in the dough', art: 'S1088', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl watching a ball of raisin dough rise on a kitchen counter, the raisins spreading apart',
  words: [
    'Ana\'s grandmother set raisin dough to rise, and Ana watched the raisins drift apart as the loaf swelled. Her grandmother said the sky did the same thing, and that the raisins had helped a lot of astronomers think.',
    'Every raisin sees every other raisin moving away, and the far ones move away faster, because there is more dough between them stretching. That is exactly what galaxies do: the farther they are, the faster they recede. If everything is spreading out now, it was once all together.',
    'The first clue was found in 1929, when Edwin Hubble measured galaxies and saw the pattern. The second came by accident in 1965, when two engineers could not get rid of a faint hiss in their antenna and finally realized the hiss was coming from the whole sky.',
    'Two more clues agree. A faint glow fills the whole sky evenly, the cooled-down light of a time when the universe was small and hot. And the mix of elements in old stars, mostly hydrogen with a fixed share of helium, is what the first minutes of such a beginning would cook. Three lines of evidence, gathered three ways.',
    'Her grandmother added the part the dough gets wrong. The loaf has an edge and a middle, and the universe has neither. It is not raisins flying out into a kitchen; it is the dough itself stretching everywhere at once, for about 13.8 billion years so far.',
    'Galaxies recede faster the farther they are; a faint glow fills the sky; the mix of elements matches. Three lines of evidence point to a universe that began small and hot and is still rising, like dough.',
  ],
};
STORIES['research-paper'] = {
  about: 'a question, what the sources say, and the answer that was the writer\'s own',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1092', after: 0, alt: 'A girl at a library table with twelve sources and no question' }, { serial: 'S1093', after: 1, alt: 'A card with one question written large above the pile of sources' }, { serial: 'S2615', after: 2, alt: 'Sources sorted by what the question needed: measurements, the actual standard, and background' }, { serial: 'S2616', after: 3, alt: 'The final answer paragraph, built on the sources but in her own voice' }, { serial: 'S2617', after: 4, alt: 'A list of sources at the end of a paper, each one also named in the text above' }],
  title: 'The question, the sources, the answer', art: 'S1091', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a library table with a question written large on a card and a fan of sources below it',
  words: [
    'Ana had twelve sources for her paper on the town\'s water and no idea what the paper was about. She had read everything. She had highlighted everything. She had a pile, and a pile is not a paper.',
    'Her teacher asked what her question was. Ana did not have one; she had a topic. Water. A topic is a pile. A question is a spine: is the town\'s tap water safe to drink, and by whose standard?',
    'The question also told her what to read next. She now wanted measurements, not opinions, and the actual standard, not a news story about it. Her twelve sources became twenty, and then, after a hard afternoon of sorting, nine.',
    'With a question, every source suddenly had a job or did not. Three gave background. Four gave the measurements. Two disagreed about the standard, and she judged between them. The rest went back on the shelf. The last section was her own answer, built on the sources but hers: yes, by the federal standard, and no, by the stricter one, and here is what the gap means.',
    'She cited every source she used, in the text and in a list at the end, and she quoted almost none of them. Paraphrase in her own voice, with the source named beside it, kept the paper hers. She saved quotation for the one sentence of the standard she wanted exact.',
    'A research paper is a question, what the sources say, and your own answer built on them. Every source is cited and every source does a job. A pile becomes a paper the moment it has a question.',
  ],
};
STORIES['personal-essay'] = {
  about: 'one small true moment that showed who a boy was, better than a list of everything he had done',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1095', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a desk with a long list of achievements and a teacher who has learned nothing from it' }, { serial: 'S1096', after: 1, alt: 'A boy fixing old radios with his grandfather on a Saturday morning, nothing on any list' }, { serial: 'S2618', after: 2, alt: 'A boy and his grandfather at a workbench, fixing a radio' }, { serial: 'S2619', after: 3, alt: 'An essay beginning in the middle of a Saturday, a radio open on a bench' }, { serial: 'S2620', after: 4, alt: 'A sentence only one boy could have written: hot solder on a cold morning' }],
  title: 'One small moment', art: 'S1094', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a desk with a long list of achievements crossed out and one short paragraph beside it',
  words: [
    'Sam\'s college essay listed everything: captain, honor roll, volunteer hours, the robotics award. It was all true, and his English teacher said she had learned nothing about him. She could have read the same list on a form.',
    'She asked him what he did on Saturday mornings. He fixed old radios with his grandfather, and had for six years, and had never once put that on a list because it was not an achievement.',
    'He wrote about one Saturday: the radio that would not come back, his grandfather\'s hands slowing down, the moment Sam realized he was now the one holding the soldering iron. Four hundred words, no awards. His teacher said now she knew who he was. The list had been what he had done; the radio was who he was.',
    'The essay had a shape as well as a subject. It began in the middle of the Saturday, with the radio open on the bench, and it never explained who Sam was; it let the reader watch and work it out. The last line was his grandfather\'s, telling him to try it again.',
    'His teacher gave him one more test for every sentence: could anyone else have written it? Captain of the team could be anyone. The smell of hot solder on a cold morning was his.',
    'A personal essay is one story, told well, that shows who you are. A small moment beats a list of achievements, because the list says what you did and the moment shows how you are.',
  ],
};
STORIES['letter-to-an-editor'] = {
  about: 'one point, one ask, one page, for a stranger with a coffee going cold',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1098', after: 0, alt: 'A girl proudly holding a three-page letter that makes six points' }, { serial: 'S1099', after: 1, alt: 'A three-page draft with six points, marked down to one' }, { serial: 'S2621', after: 2, alt: 'Five crossed-out points, each with a neighbor\'s name beside it for another week' }, { serial: 'S2622', after: 3, alt: 'The final page with its evidence and one bold ask at the end' }, { serial: 'S2623', after: 4, alt: 'A letter signed with a name, a street and the number of the bus she rides' }],
  title: 'One point, one ask', art: 'S1097', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl folding a single typed page at a kitchen table, an envelope addressed to a newspaper',
  words: [
    'Lena\'s letter to the editor made six points about the new bus schedule and ran three pages. She was proud of its completeness. Her teacher said the editor would print none of it, and if he did, nobody would finish it.',
    'A letter to the editor is read by a busy stranger with a coffee going cold. That stranger will give it one page and will remember one thing. Six points is zero points.',
    'Cutting was the hard part. Every one of the six points was true, and dropping five felt like lying by leaving things out. Her teacher said no: the other five could each have their own letter, on their own week, from a different neighbor.',
    'Lena picked the strongest: the last bus now leaves before the night shift ends. She backed it with one piece of evidence, the shift times at the hospital and the timetable side by side. She ended with one specific ask: move the last departure to 11:40. One page. The paper printed it, and the transit board read it aloud at its next meeting.',
    'The letter carried her name, her street and the fact that she rode that bus every day. An editor prints letters from people who can be found and who have a stake. Complaints with no name are set aside.',
    'One point, backed by evidence, and one specific ask, on a page a busy stranger will read. A letter that makes one point can win it; a letter that makes six is a letter about the writer.',
  ],
};
STORIES['principles-of-the-constitution'] = {
  about: 'a handful of ideas under everything, found by asking why each rule was there',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1101', after: 0, alt: 'A boy giving up on memorizing the Constitution somewhere in Article Two' }, { serial: 'S1102', after: 1, alt: 'The words We the People with an arrow pointing up at the whole document' }, { serial: 'S2624', after: 2, alt: 'A short list: popular sovereignty, limited government, separation of powers, checks and balances, federalism, individual rights' }, { serial: 'S2625', after: 3, alt: 'Two houses of Congress, a veto stamp and a short list of powers, each tagged with its idea' }, { serial: 'S2626', after: 4, alt: 'A crown on one side and an empty treasury on the other, a new document between them' }],
  title: 'The ideas underneath', art: 'S1100', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a museum looking at the Constitution in its case, a few large words glowing behind the glass',
  words: [
    'Theo tried to memorize the Constitution the way he had memorized state capitals and gave up around Article Two. There were too many rules and they did not seem to belong to each other.',
    'His teacher asked him to stop reading the rules and ask, of each one, why it was there. Why does Congress have two houses? Why can the President veto? Why does the whole thing start with We the People?',
    'The answers kept coming back to a handful of ideas. The people are the source of power. The government is limited to what the document allows. Power is split three ways and each part can check the others. Power is shared with the states. And some rights belong to individuals and cannot be voted away. Six ideas, written in 1787. Every rule was one of them wearing a specific coat.',
    'Theo tested the idea on the rules that had stumped him. Two houses of Congress: separation of powers, so that no single group writes the laws. The veto: checks and balances. A list of what Congress may do, and no more: limited government.',
    'The ideas came from hard experience. The writers had just fought a king who answered to no one, and then lived under a first government so weak it could not pay its own soldiers. The Constitution was built between those two failures.',
    'The Constitution rests on a few principles. The people are the source of power, the government is limited, power is separated and checked, shared with the states, and some rights are the person\'s own. Learn the six ideas and the rules explain themselves.',
  ],
};
STORIES['three-branches'] = {
  about: 'three teams with three jobs, and what happens when one of them reaches too far',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1104', after: 0, alt: 'A girl at a whiteboard trying to pass a law as President, and failing' }, { serial: 'S1105', after: 1, alt: 'Three circles labeled by job: make the laws, carry them out, decide what they mean' }, { serial: 'S2627', after: 2, alt: 'A veto stamped on a bill, then a two-thirds vote overriding it' }, { serial: 'S2628', after: 3, alt: 'A court striking a law, and a Senate confirming a judge' }, { serial: 'S2629', after: 4, alt: 'Three slow-turning gears, each one able to stop the others' }],
  title: 'Three teams', art: 'S1103', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a whiteboard with three circles labeled by their jobs, arrows running between them',
  words: [
    'Rosa thought the President ran the country. Her teacher asked her to try to pass a law as President. Rosa could not; only Congress could. Then, as Congress, to enforce one. She could not; that was the President\'s. Then to decide what one meant. The courts.',
    'Three teams, three jobs. Congress makes the laws, the President carries them out, and the courts decide what they mean. No team could do another\'s job, and that was the first half of the design.',
    'The second half was reach. The President can veto a law; Congress can override with two thirds. The courts can strike a law down; the President appoints the judges and the Senate confirms them. Congress can impeach either. Each team can stop the others from going too far, which means nobody gets to run the country alone.',
    'Rosa asked what happened when a team reached too far anyway. Her teacher pointed to 1803, when the Supreme Court first struck down an act of Congress and claimed the power to say what the Constitution means. That power is not written in the document; the Court took it, and the other branches let it stand.',
    'The design is slow on purpose. Three teams that must agree get less done than one team that answers to nobody. The writers thought that was a fair price for keeping any one team from running the country.',
    'Congress makes the laws, the President carries them out, and the courts decide what they mean. Each branch can check the others, so no one branch can take over. The design is three teams that need each other.',
  ],
};
STORIES['federalism'] = {
  about: 'a school and a school district, and which one decides what',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1107', after: 0, alt: 'A boy in a school office reading two signs, one from the district and one from the principal' }, { serial: 'S1108', after: 1, alt: 'A list of powers only the national government holds: coining money, the army, treaties' }, { serial: 'S2630', after: 2, alt: 'Two circles overlapping: taxes, roads and laws in the shared middle' }, { serial: 'S2631', after: 3, alt: 'A list held by the states: schools, police, licenses' }, { serial: 'S2632', after: 4, alt: 'A state handing powers down to a city and a county, the way a district hands them to a school' }],
  title: 'The school and the district', art: 'S1106', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in a school office looking at two signs: one from the district office and one from the principal',
  words: [
    'Owen\'s school could set its own dress code but could not change the school year, and he wanted to know who decided which decisions were whose. His principal said the district set some things, the school set others, and a few were shared.',
    'The country works the same way. The national government holds the enumerated powers, the ones the Constitution names: coining money, raising an army, making treaties, running the mail. Everything not named is reserved to the states: schools, police, marriage, driver\'s licenses.',
    'Some powers overlap. Both can tax; both can build roads; both can make laws, and when they conflict, the national law wins. That is federalism: power shared between the national government and the states, with a list for one, the rest for the other, and a rule for the overlap.',
    'The overlap is where the arguments live. Speed limits, drinking ages and school tests have all been argued over as state business or national business, and the answers have moved over time. The Tenth Amendment says that what is not given to the national government stays with the states or the people.',
    'Owen\'s principal added the part the textbook skipped: a state can hand power down too. Cities and counties get their powers from their state, the way his school got its dress code from the district.',
    'Power is shared between the national government and the states. The national government holds the enumerated powers, the states hold the reserved ones, and some are concurrent. A school and its district are the same arrangement in miniature.',
  ],
};
STORIES['bill-of-rights'] = {
  about: 'a fence around the government, and which side of it a person stands on',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1110', after: 0, alt: 'A girl reading the first five words of the First Amendment: Congress shall make no law' }, { serial: 'S1111', after: 1, alt: 'The First Amendment as five gates: speech, press, religion, assembly, petition' }, { serial: 'S2633', after: 2, alt: 'A police officer at a door with a warrant, the Fourth Amendment' }, { serial: 'S2634', after: 3, alt: 'A fence around the national government, then extended around the states after 1868' }, { serial: 'S2635', after: 4, alt: 'The Ninth and Tenth Amendments as two rails at the end of the fence' }],
  title: 'The fence', art: 'S1109', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on courthouse steps, a low fence drawn around the building with her standing outside it',
  words: [
    'Maya thought the Bill of Rights was a list of things people were allowed to do. Her teacher said to read the first words of the First Amendment. Congress shall make no law. It was a list of things the government could not do.',
    'That flipped it. A fence around the government, not a permission slip for the citizen. Ratified in 1791, ten amendments, each one a rail.',
    'The First protects religion, speech, press, assembly and petition. The Fourth stops searches without a warrant. The Fifth and Sixth guarantee due process and a fair, speedy, public trial with a lawyer. The Eighth forbids cruel punishment. Maya stood on the outside of the fence, where the rules did not reach her, and that was the point of building it.',
    'For its first century the fence stood only around the national government; Congress shall make no law meant Congress. After the Fourteenth Amendment in 1868, the courts slowly held the states to most of the same rails, one case at a time.',
    'The Ninth Amendment says the list is not complete, and the Tenth says that powers not given to the national government stay with the states or the people. Maya\'s teacher called them the two rails people forget.',
    'The first ten amendments, ratified in 1791, list what the government may never do to a person. The First Amendment protects religion, speech, press, assembly and petition; the Fourth through Eighth protect the accused. The fence is around the government, and the person is outside it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['how-a-bill-becomes-law'] = {
  about: 'an obstacle course with seven stations, and the bill that had to clear every one',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1113', after: 0, alt: 'A boy with an idea for a law, expecting a form to fill in' }, { serial: 'S1114', after: 1, alt: 'A committee table with the bill under study, most bills stopping here' }, { serial: 'S2636', after: 2, alt: 'A class simulation of the obstacle course, Kai\'s idea stopped at the second station' }, { serial: 'S2637', after: 3, alt: 'A tall stack of bills introduced beside a small stack that became law' }, { serial: 'S2638', after: 4, alt: 'A Senate floor where debate runs on until sixty senators vote to end it' }],
  title: 'The obstacle course', art: 'S1112', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on capitol steps holding a rolled bill, a course of seven hurdles drawn behind him',
  words: [
    'Kai had an idea for a law and assumed a bill was a form you filled in. His civics teacher drew an obstacle course on the board with seven stations and said most bills fall at the second one.',
    'Introduced by a member. Studied in committee, where most die quietly. Passed by one house. Passed by the other, usually in a slightly different form. Agreed as one text by a conference of both. Sent to the President, who signs or vetoes. And a veto overridden only by two thirds of each house.',
    'Seven stations, and the course was slow on purpose. A law that would bind everyone had to survive everyone\'s objections first. Kai\'s idea got to the second station in the class simulation and stopped there, which his teacher said was the most realistic thing that happened all week.',
    'The numbers make the point. In a two-year Congress, thousands of bills are introduced and only a few hundred become law. Most are never voted on at all.',
    'The Senate adds a hurdle of its own. Debate there can go on until sixty senators vote to end it, so a bill with a simple majority can still stall. Kai\'s teacher called it an obstacle course with a swamp in the middle.',
    'A bill is introduced, studied in committee, passed by one house and then the other, agreed as one text, and signed or vetoed, with a veto overridden by two thirds. The course is long by design, and the committee is where most bills end.',
  ],
};
STORIES['elections-parties-and-voting'] = {
  about: 'two rounds, 270 to win, and the map that decides the second',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1116', after: 0, alt: 'A girl watching election returns as the anchor keeps saying 270' }, { serial: 'S1117', after: 1, alt: 'A primary ballot with several names from one party' }, { serial: 'S2639', after: 2, alt: 'The Electoral College map with 270 marked as the line' }, { serial: 'S2640', after: 3, alt: '538 electors drawn as 435 plus 100 plus 3, with Maine and Nebraska split by district' }, { serial: 'S2641', after: 4, alt: 'Campaign buses crowded into a handful of close states on a map' }],
  title: 'Two rounds, 270', art: 'S1115', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl watching election returns on a living room television, a map of states filling in with two colors',
  words: [
    'Ana watched the election returns and could not understand why the anchor kept saying 270 when the vote counts were in the millions. Her father said she was watching the second round of a two-round contest.',
    'The first round was the primaries, months earlier, when each party\'s voters chose that party\'s candidate from several. The second was the general election, when everyone chose between the winners.',
    'And for President the second round is counted by state. Each state has electors, its House seats plus two, and in most states whoever wins the state takes all of them. 270 is a majority of the 538. The millions were real votes, but the map was the scoreboard, and the map had to reach 270.',
    'The 538 come from 435 House seats, 100 senators and three electors for Washington, D.C., which has had them since 1961. Two states, Maine and Nebraska, split their electors by district instead of giving all of them to the state\'s winner.',
    'Because of the map, a candidate can win the most votes across the country and still lose, which has happened a handful of times, most recently in 2016. That is why campaigns spend their last weeks in a few close states, and why Ana\'s father said the anchor was counting the right thing.',
    'Primaries choose each party\'s candidate, and the general election chooses the winner. A president is chosen by the Electoral College, 270 of 538, mostly state by state, which is why the night is a map.',
  ],
};
STORIES['scarcity-and-opportunity-cost'] = {
  about: 'sixty dollars, two wants, and the cost that was not on the price tag',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1119', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a counter with sixty dollars, shoes on one side and concert tickets on the other' }, { serial: 'S1120', after: 1, alt: 'One sixty-dollar bill that cannot be two things at once' }, { serial: 'S2642', after: 2, alt: 'An evening at practice beside an unwritten essay' }, { serial: 'S2643', after: 3, alt: 'The shoes bought, the concert poster fading behind' }, { serial: 'S2644', after: 4, alt: 'The opportunity cost named out loud before the choice: a road here, a school not built there' }],
  title: 'Sixty dollars, two wants', art: 'S1118', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a shop counter with sixty dollars, a pair of shoes on one side and concert tickets on the other',
  words: [
    'Marcus had sixty dollars and two things he wanted: the shoes and the concert. Each cost sixty. He bought the shoes and told his sister they were a good deal. She asked what they had cost him. Sixty dollars, he said. She said no.',
    'Scarcity means there is never enough of everything. Sixty dollars could not be both. So every choice gives something up, and the thing given up is a cost that does not appear on any tag.',
    'It works for time as much as for money. An evening spent at practice is an evening not spent on the essay, and the cost of the practice is the essay, whether or not anyone writes it on a tag.',
    'The opportunity cost of the shoes was the concert, the best thing he did not choose. Not everything he could have bought, just the next best. When his sister asked whether the shoes were worth a concert, he found the question harder than when she had asked whether they were worth sixty dollars.',
    'His sister said the trick is to name the cost before you choose, not after. Governments do it with budgets, and the question is always the same: a road here is a school not built there. Whoever names the opportunity cost out loud makes the better choice.',
    'Scarcity means there is never enough of everything, so every choice gives something up. The opportunity cost of a choice is the next best thing you did not choose. Sixty dollars was the price; the concert was the cost.',
  ],
};
STORIES['competition-and-markets'] = {
  about: 'two lemonade stands on one street, and what the second one did to the first',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1122', after: 0, alt: 'One stand alone, a high price on its sign' }, { serial: 'S1123', after: 1, alt: 'Two stands, prices lower and cups bigger on both' }, { serial: 'S2648', after: 2, alt: 'Three lemonade signs visible from one corner, the stand with smaller cups losing its line' }, { serial: 'S2649', after: 3, alt: 'Five posts holding up a stand: private property, free choice, profit, competition, limited government' }, { serial: 'S2650', after: 4, alt: 'Two stands agreeing to charge the same two dollars, a deal against the customer, crossed out' }],
  title: 'Two stands on one street', art: 'S1121', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a lemonade stand looking down the block at a rival stand with a bigger sign',
  words: [
    'Rosa ran the only lemonade stand on her street and charged two dollars a cup. Then a boy opened a stand at the other end for one dollar. Rosa was furious. Her father said the street had just gotten a market.',
    'With one stand, Rosa set the price and the customers took it. With two, the customers had a choice, and the choice was a vote. Rosa dropped to a dollar fifty and added ice. The boy added lemons. Both stands got better, and the price stayed down.',
    'The next week a third stand opened, and the boy\'s cups got smaller. Rosa did not have to match him. Customers could see three signs from the corner, and the stand that cut quality lost its line by lunchtime.',
    'That is what competition does inside free enterprise: private property, free choice, the profit motive that made both of them try, and limited government that let them. Competition keeps prices down and quality up because every customer can walk to the other end of the block.',
    'Her father added the warning. If the two stands had agreed to both charge two dollars, that would not have been competition but a deal against the customer, and for real businesses it is against the law. Markets only work while the customer keeps the choice.',
    'Free enterprise means private property, free choice, the profit motive, competition and limited government. Competition keeps prices honest and quality rising, because the customer can always walk. Two stands are a market; one is a toll.',
  ],
};
STORIES['money-banking-and-the-fed'] = {
  about: 'a promise everyone accepts, a bank that lends it out, and the Fed that sets its price',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1125', after: 0, alt: 'A boy at a bank counter asking why a piece of paper is worth anything' }, { serial: 'S1126', after: 1, alt: 'A dollar with three labels: exchange, account, store' }, { serial: 'S2651', after: 2, alt: 'Germany in 1923: printed money piled in a wheelbarrow, the promise broken' }, { serial: 'S2652', after: 3, alt: 'A deposit lent out at interest, and a dial marked by the Fed' }, { serial: 'S2653', after: 4, alt: 'A bank vault with most of its shelves empty, an insurance sign on the door' }],
  title: 'A promise everyone accepts', art: 'S1124', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy asking his grandfather why a piece of paper is worth anything, a promise everyone accepts',
  words: [
    'Owen asked why a piece of paper was worth anything. His grandfather said it was a promise everyone accepted, and that a promise everyone accepts is the most useful invention in the world.',
    'Money does three jobs. It is a medium of exchange, so a farmer does not need a barber who wants eggs. It is a unit of account, so a car and a pencil are measured on one scale. It is a store of value, so today\'s work can buy next year\'s food.',
    'The promise only works while people trust it. When a government prints far more money than there are goods to buy with it, prices race and the promise breaks. Germany in 1923 and Zimbabwe in 2008 both watched paper money become worthless in months.',
    'Banks take the promises in as deposits and lend most of them out, charging interest, the price of borrowing. And the Federal Reserve sets the basic level of that price: lower it and borrowing gets cheap and the economy speeds up; raise it and things cool. A dial on the price of a promise.',
    'Because banks lend out most of what they hold, no bank has everyone\'s money in the vault at once. That is why deposits are insured, and why the Fed stands behind banks in a panic. Its two jobs are steady prices and as many people working as the economy can hold.',
    'Money is a medium of exchange, a unit of account and a store of value. Banks lend deposits and charge interest, the price of borrowing, and the Fed moves that price to speed up or cool the economy. The paper is a promise, and the Fed sets its rent.',
  ],
};
STORIES['gdp-inflation-and-unemployment'] = {
  about: 'the country\'s paycheck, the price of a sandwich, and the people who wanted work',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1128', after: 0, alt: 'A girl in a diner reading three numbers in one headline' }, { serial: 'S1129', after: 1, alt: 'A paycheck labeled GDP with this year and last year side by side' }, { serial: 'S2654', after: 2, alt: 'A sandwich menu with the price rising, and a line of people at a job board' }, { serial: 'S2655', after: 3, alt: 'A growth number with inflation already taken out, beside a few people between jobs' }, { serial: 'S2656', after: 4, alt: 'Three dials linked by strings: jobs, prices and interest rates' }],
  title: 'The country\'s paycheck', art: 'S1127', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl in a diner with a newspaper showing three numbers, a menu with an old price crossed out beside her',
  words: [
    'The news gave three numbers in one breath, GDP up two percent, inflation at four, unemployment at five, and Ana\'s uncle said the country was doing fine. Ana did not know whether any of the three was good.',
    'GDP is the country\'s paycheck: the value of everything it produces in a year, and its growth rate is this year\'s change divided by last year\'s total. Two percent meant the paycheck grew a little.',
    'Inflation is the sandwich. Four percent meant the same sandwich cost four percent more than last year, so a paycheck that grew two percent bought less. And unemployment counts people who want work and cannot find it, as a share of those working or looking. Five in a hundred wanted a job and had none. Three numbers, three stories, and fine depended on which one you were living in.',
    'Ana\'s teacher added two corrections. Reported growth is usually already adjusted for inflation, so two percent meant the country really made more. And unemployment is never zero in a healthy economy, because people are always between jobs; four or five percent has been ordinary.',
    'The three numbers pull on each other. When almost everyone is working, wages and prices tend to rise, and when the Fed raises interest rates to cool prices, jobs can grow scarce. The central bank aims for inflation of about two percent a year.',
    'GDP is the value of everything a country produces in a year, and its growth rate is the change divided by last year. Inflation is the rise in prices, and unemployment is the share of the labor force looking for work. The paycheck, the sandwich and the line at the job board.',
  ],
};
// College, and the last kindergarten one.
STORIES['mean-median-mode'] = {
  about: 'six roommates, one rich friend, and three honest averages that disagreed',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1131', after: 0, alt: 'Six monthly budgets in a row, one far taller' }, { serial: 'S1132', after: 1, alt: 'A thick wallet dragging a mean marker far to the right along a number line' }, { serial: 'S2660', after: 2, alt: 'The same six sorted, a finger on the middle two' }, { serial: 'S2661', after: 3, alt: 'One grocery bill split six ways, the mean written on each share' }, { serial: 'S2662', after: 4, alt: 'A town where one mansion lifts the average income while the median stays put' }],
  title: 'The rich friend', art: 'S1130', cast: [],
  alt: 'Six young adults around an apartment kitchen table, receipts spread out, one of them holding a much thicker wallet',
  words: [
    'Six roommates compared what they spent on food in a month: 180, 200, 200, 220, 250 and 900. Jamal announced the average was 325 and said nobody was overspending. The four people under 250 disagreed with him loudly.',
    'The 900 belonged to Dev, who ate out every night. One number had dragged the mean to a place where nobody actually lived. Jamal was right about the arithmetic and wrong about the roommates.',
    'The median told a different truth. Sort them, take the middle: 210. Dev could spend nine thousand and the median would not move. The mode, the most common, was 200. Three honest averages, and the mean was the only one Dev could pull.',
    'Which average to report depends on the question. If the six of them split one grocery bill evenly, the mean is exactly what each pays, Dev included. If the question is what a typical roommate spends, the median answers it, and the mean misleads.',
    'News reports use the same trick in both directions. Average income in a town can rise because one billionaire moved in, while the median household never saw a dollar. When a number is called the average, the first question is which one.',
    'Mean adds and divides. Median is the middle. Mode is the most common. Extreme values pull the mean toward them, and the median ignores them, which is why a rich friend makes the mean lie and leaves the median alone.',
  ],
};
STORIES['spread'] = {
  about: 'two classes with the same average and nothing else in common',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1134', after: 0, alt: 'Priya planning the same review for two sections that both averaged 75 on the midterm' }, { serial: 'S1135', after: 1, alt: 'One row of scores bunched tightly around 75' }, { serial: 'S2663', after: 2, alt: 'Range as biggest minus smallest: ten in one room, sixty in the other, the average hiding both' }, { serial: 'S2664', after: 3, alt: 'A typical distance from the average marked as three points in one room and twenty in the other' }, { serial: 'S2665', after: 4, alt: 'The second room split into groups, each with its own review' }],
  title: 'Same average, different rooms', art: 'S1133', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman at a lecture hall door looking at two rows of test scores with the same average written under each',
  words: [
    'Priya taught two sections and both averaged 75 on the midterm, so she planned the same review for each. The first class nodded through it. The second class was half lost and half bored, and she could not understand why the same lesson had landed so differently.',
    'She looked past the average. The first section\'s scores ran from 70 to 80: everyone in the same place. The second ran from 40 to 100: a few who needed everything and a few who needed nothing. The average had hidden the room.',
    'The spread is what the average hides. Range is biggest minus smallest, ten in one room and sixty in the other. A bigger spread means a more varied set, and a more varied set needs a different kind of teaching, not a better version of the same one.',
    'Statisticians measure spread more carefully than range, because range depends on only two students. The standard deviation is roughly how far a typical score sits from the average: about three points in the first room and closer to twenty in the second.',
    'Priya split the second section into groups and gave each group the review it needed. The averages were still 75 on the next test. The spread in the second room had shrunk by half, which was the number that had actually changed.',
    'The average hides the spread. Range is biggest minus smallest, and a bigger spread means a more varied set. Two rooms with the same average can be two different problems.',
  ],
};
STORIES['probability'] = {
  about: 'a spinner with three winning slices, two spins, and the odds of getting nothing',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1137', after: 0, alt: 'A young man spinning a wheel while a friend insists he is due' }, { serial: 'S1138', after: 1, alt: 'The spinner with three of eight slices shaded' }, { serial: 'S2666', after: 2, alt: 'Two losses in a row: five eighths times five eighths, about 39 percent' }, { serial: 'S2667', after: 3, alt: 'Two spins side by side with one winning slice lit, 61 percent written beside them' }, { serial: 'S2668', after: 4, alt: 'A two-dollar ticket beside a five-dollar prize and a small minus sign' }],
  title: 'Three slices', art: 'S1136', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man at a game night table spinning a wheel with eight slices, three shaded as winners',
  words: [
    'The spinner at game night had eight slices and three won a prize. Marcus figured his chance was three in eight and spun. Nothing. His friend said he was due. Marcus spun again. Nothing. He was starting to believe in due.',
    'The spinner had no memory. Three in eight, every spin, no matter what the last one did. The two spins were independent, and being due was a feeling, not a probability. A coin that has landed heads five times is still a coin.',
    'So what were the odds of two losses in a row? Not winning was one minus three eighths: five eighths. Two independent events multiply: five eighths times five eighths, about 39 percent. Two misses was not bad luck. It was the single most likely thing that could happen.',
    'The other question is a win somewhere in the two spins. That is one minus two misses, about 61 percent, which is why the friend felt sure and why the feeling was still not a promise.',
    'The prize was five dollars and each spin cost two. Three eighths of five is about 1.88, so every spin lost about twelve cents on average. The spinner was never due, and over a long night it was never a bargain either. Casinos are built on that twelve cents.',
    'Probability is favorable outcomes over all outcomes. Independent events multiply, and not happening is one minus happening. The spinner is never due, and two misses were the favorite all along.',
  ],
};
STORIES['compound-interest'] = {
  about: 'a snowball rolled downhill, and the account that grew the same way',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1140', after: 0, alt: 'Lena expecting fifty dollars a year on a thousand at five percent, her statement showing 1,102.50' }, { serial: 'S1141', after: 1, alt: 'A statement: interest paid on the balance, then on the balance plus interest' }, { serial: 'S2669', after: 2, alt: 'Compound interest at five percent: a thousand dollars curving to 1,629 after ten years and 4,322 after thirty' }, { serial: 'S2670', after: 3, alt: 'A snowball doubling on a slope marked in years, 14 at five percent and 7 at ten' }, { serial: 'S2671', after: 4, alt: 'The same curve drawn as a debt, rising against a credit card' }],
  title: 'The snowball', art: 'S1139', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman rolling a snowball down a hill, it growing with every turn, a bank statement drawn beside it',
  words: [
    'Lena put a thousand dollars in an account paying five percent and expected fifty dollars a year, forever. After year one she had 1,050. After year two she had 1,102.50, and the extra two-fifty confused her. Where had it come from?',
    'From the fifty. In year two the account paid five percent on 1,050, not on 1,000. The interest had earned interest. She thought of a snowball rolled downhill: it picks up snow in proportion to how big it already is, so the bigger it gets, the faster it grows.',
    'That is compound interest. The amount is the principal times one plus the rate, raised to the number of years. At five percent, a thousand dollars is about 1,629 after ten years and 4,322 after thirty. The last ten years add more than the first twenty, because by then the snowball is large.',
    'A quick rule says how long doubling takes: divide 72 by the rate. At five percent, about fourteen years; at ten, about seven. Lena\'s thousand would be two thousand a little after her fourteenth year without her adding a cent.',
    'The snowball rolls downhill on debts too. A credit card at twenty percent doubles what is owed in under four years if nothing is paid, by the same rule. The formula does not care which side of it you are on.',
    'Compound interest pays interest on the interest. Amount is principal times one plus the rate, to the power of the years. The snowball\'s secret is time, not the hill.',
  ],
};
STORIES['correlation-causation'] = {
  about: 'ice cream sales and drownings that rose together every summer, and the four ways that can happen',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1143', after: 0, alt: 'A young man writing that ice cream causes drowning, and a professor\'s one-word reply: how?' }, { serial: 'S1144', after: 1, alt: 'Two lines on a chart rising and falling together across a year' }, { serial: 'S2672', after: 2, alt: 'Four small diagrams: A to B, B to A, a third thing to both, and a shrug' }, { serial: 'S2673', after: 3, alt: 'A bar of chocolate beside a Nobel medal, a bank vault between them' }, { serial: 'S2674', after: 4, alt: 'A crowd split by coin flip, ice cream for one half and none for the other' }],
  title: 'Ice cream and the pool', art: 'S1142', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man at a poolside snack bar looking at a chart where two lines rise together',
  words: [
    'Kai found a chart where ice cream sales and pool drownings rose and fell together across the year, and he wrote that ice cream causes drowning. His professor wrote back one word: how?',
    'Kai could not say how. He tried it the other way: drownings cause ice cream sales. That was worse. But the lines really did move together; that part was not in dispute.',
    'Together is not because. There are four doors. A causes B. B causes A. Something else causes both. Or coincidence. The third door had a name here: summer. Hot weather sells ice cream and fills pools, and pools with more people in them have more accidents. Ice cream was innocent.',
    'The third door has a name, a confounder, and it hides in famous places. Countries that eat more chocolate have won more Nobel prizes, which says nothing about chocolate and a lot about wealth, which buys both chocolate and universities.',
    'The way through the doors is an experiment. Give ice cream to half a random crowd and none to the other half, count the accidents, and the sun can no longer take sides. That is why medicine tests drugs with a coin flip, and why the coin, not the doctor, chooses who gets the pill.',
    'Correlation is not causation. When two things move together, hold four doors open: A causes B, B causes A, something causes both, or coincidence. The ice cream was standing next to the real cause.',
  ],
};
STORIES['thesis-statements'] = {
  about: 'a fight worth picking, between a thesis too broad to hold and one too obvious to matter',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1146', after: 0, alt: 'A thesis that says technology changed society, drawn as an ocean' }, { serial: 'S1147', after: 1, alt: 'A thesis that says phones are common, drawn as a pebble' }, { serial: 'S2675', after: 2, alt: 'A third thesis circled, with a dean shown arguing back' }, { serial: 'S2676', after: 3, alt: 'Six lecture courses circled on a course list, a spreadsheet beside them' }, { serial: 'S2677', after: 4, alt: 'A thesis written before the research and rewritten after, smaller and truer' }],
  title: 'A fight worth picking', art: 'S1145', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman at a dorm desk with three thesis drafts, the first too big, the second too small, the third circled',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s first thesis was that technology has changed society. Her professor said she could not prove it in a lifetime. Her second was that most students own phones. He said nobody would argue and nobody would care. She had gone from an ocean to a pebble.',
    'A thesis is a claim someone could dispute. Too broad and it cannot be proved; too obvious and there is nothing to prove. The good one lived in between, where a reader would say prove it and mean it.',
    'Her third: phone bans in lecture halls raise attendance but not grades, and the university is measuring the wrong thing. Now there was a fight. A dean would argue. The data would have to be found. The essay had somewhere to go.',
    'She narrowed it once more, so the fight was one she could win: at her own university, in the six lecture courses that banned phones last year. A thesis about the whole world could not be tested; a thesis about six courses could be, by a student with a spreadsheet.',
    'The professor added that a thesis is a promise, not a vow. She wrote it before the research and rewrote it after, when two of the six courses turned out to have raised grades too. The final sentence was smaller and truer, and it still picked a fight.',
    'A thesis is a claim someone could dispute, not a topic. Too broad and too obvious are both weak, in opposite directions. Find the sentence a smart reader would push back on, and write toward the pushback.',
  ],
};
STORIES['academic-structure'] = {
  about: 'the sentence that explains why the evidence counts, and how often it goes missing',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1149', after: 0, alt: 'A professor\'s circle around the gap between a table and a claim, with a question written beside it' }, { serial: 'S1150', after: 1, alt: 'Twelve streets with bike lanes highlighted on a city map' }, { serial: 'S2678', after: 2, alt: 'Claim, evidence, and an empty slot labeled warrant' }, { serial: 'S2679', after: 3, alt: 'New streetlights on a timeline, arriving eight months after the deaths began falling' }, { serial: 'S2680', after: 4, alt: 'The slot filled, the paragraph now standing' }],
  title: 'The sentence that explains why', art: 'S1148', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man at a seminar table with a paragraph on his laptop, a blank line highlighted between a quotation and the next claim',
  words: [
    'Theo\'s paper claimed that the city\'s new bike lanes had cut traffic deaths, and his evidence was a table showing deaths down eleven percent. He moved on to the next point. His professor circled the gap between them and wrote: why does this count?',
    'Theo thought the table spoke for itself. It did not. Deaths had fallen eleven percent in the whole city, and the bike lanes ran on twelve streets. Nothing on the page connected the number to the lanes.',
    'The missing sentence was the warrant. He went back to the data and found that deaths on the twelve streets fell thirty percent while the rest of the city fell four. Now he could write it: the drop was concentrated where the lanes went in, which is what you would see if the lanes were the cause. Claim, evidence, warrant. The third one had done all the work.',
    'A warrant can be attacked, and a good paper attacks its own first. The twelve streets had also gotten new streetlights that year. Theo checked: the lights went in eight months after the deaths had already started falling, so the lanes stayed the better explanation, and he said so in the paper.',
    'He also gave every number its backing: the city\'s own crash reports, cited by year, so a reader could pull them. Claim, evidence, warrant, backing, and the alternative faced head-on. The paragraph was twice as long and finally stood up.',
    'An academic argument is a claim, evidence, and a warrant that says why the evidence counts. The warrant is the part most often missing, because the writer already believes it and forgets the reader does not.',
  ],
};
STORIES['numbers-in-prose'] = {
  about: 'a number with no clothes on, and the three questions that dress it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1152', after: 0, alt: 'A young woman at a newsroom desk asking fifty percent of what' }, { serial: 'S1153', after: 1, alt: 'The headline: crime up 50 percent, and beneath it, from 2 to 3' }, { serial: 'S2681', after: 2, alt: 'Three questions on a sticky note: of what, compared with when, out of how many' }, { serial: 'S2682', after: 3, alt: 'Two neighborhoods side by side: 3 burglaries in 4,000 homes against 9 in 16,000' }, { serial: 'S2683', after: 4, alt: 'A number dressed properly: three burglaries, four thousand homes, one more than last year' }],
  title: 'A number with no clothes on', art: 'S1151', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman at a newsroom desk reading a headline with a large percentage and no base',
  words: [
    'The headline said burglaries in the neighborhood were up fifty percent, and Ana\'s roommates started talking about locks. Ana asked one question: fifty percent of what?',
    'She found the numbers. Two burglaries last year, three this year. Fifty percent, and also one more house. The headline was true, and it had walked into the room with no clothes on. One more house on one street had become a wave.',
    'A number needs three things to be judged. Percent of what: the base. Compared with when: last year, or a bad year chosen to flatter? Out of how many: three burglaries among four thousand homes. Dressed, the number was quiet. Naked, it had sounded like a wave.',
    'Rates need clothes too. Three burglaries in four thousand homes is less than one in a thousand. A neighborhood four times the size with nine burglaries is not three times as dangerous but slightly safer. Per home, per person, per year: a number means nothing until it says per what.',
    'Ana wrote the number up properly for the roommates: three burglaries this year among four thousand homes, one more than last year, and fewer per home than the neighborhood next door. The locks stayed as they were. So did the roommates\' sleep, which was the point of dressing the number.',
    'Percent of what, compared with when, out of how many. A number without its base cannot be judged, and a headline that leaves the base out is asking you not to.',
  ],
};
STORIES['logical-consistency'] = {
  about: 'a speech that called a plan the cheapest option and the most expensive ever, four minutes apart',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1155', after: 0, alt: 'A speaker at a town hall podium, a young man in the audience writing two quotes side by side' }, { serial: 'S1156', after: 1, alt: 'Two of the claims joined by a line and a question mark' }, { serial: 'S2684', after: 2, alt: 'A list of a speech\'s main claims, numbered' }, { serial: 'S2685', after: 3, alt: 'A bridge priced per year of use beside its total price, a rescue attempt that fails' }, { serial: 'S2686', after: 4, alt: 'A speech and a printed plan held side by side, two mismatches circled' }],
  title: 'Cheap, and the most expensive ever', art: 'S1154', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man in a town hall audience with a notebook, two contradictory quotes written side by side',
  words: [
    'At the town hall, the speaker said the new bridge was the cheapest option on the table. Four minutes later he called it the most expensive project in the county\'s history. Owen wrote both down and nobody else seemed to notice.',
    'Each sentence sounded fine on its own. The speaker was fluent, confident and applauded. But the two claims could not both be true at once, and an argument that contradicts itself has not proved anything, however good it sounds a sentence at a time.',
    'Owen listed the speech\'s main claims and held them side by side. Cheapest option; most expensive ever. Safe for a century; must be replaced in thirty years. Three pairs that could not coexist. He asked about the first pair during questions, and the speaker had no answer, because there was none.',
    'Owen also tried to rescue the speaker, because a fair listener does. Cheapest could mean cheapest per year of use and most expensive could mean the largest total, and both might be true of a bridge built to last. But the speaker had said thirty years, not a century, and the rescue failed on the third pair.',
    'The printed plan on the table contradicted the speech twice more, so Owen held the two documents side by side the same way. Consistency is not only inside one speech; it is between the speech and the paper it waves.',
    'Consistent means every claim can be true at once. List the main claims, then hold them side by side, because a contradiction hides easily across four minutes and not at all across one line.',
  ],
};
STORIES['primary-and-secondary-sources'] = {
  about: 'a letter from the trenches and a textbook about the trenches, and what each one knows',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1158', after: 0, alt: 'The letter, mud-stained, dated 1916' }, { serial: 'S1159', after: 1, alt: 'Primary: a hand writing in a trench; secondary: a historian a century later with hundreds of letters' }, { serial: 'S2687', after: 2, alt: 'The textbook page with a map and a casualty table' }, { serial: 'S2688', after: 3, alt: 'A soldier writing to his mother while a censor waits to read the letter' }, { serial: 'S2689', after: 4, alt: 'An officer\'s weather log kept two miles behind the line, the source of the dry day' }],
  title: 'The letter and the textbook', art: 'S1157', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman in an archive holding an old handwritten letter, a modern textbook open on the table beside it',
  words: [
    'Maya had a letter from her great-great-grandfather written in the trenches in 1916 and a textbook chapter on the same battle, and they did not agree about the weather. The textbook said the day was dry. The letter complained of mud to the knees.',
    'The letter was a primary source: made at the time, by someone there. The textbook was secondary: made a century later by someone looking back over hundreds of letters and maps. Each knew something the other could not.',
    'The letter knew the mud in one trench on one morning. The textbook knew the whole front and the whole month, and the average day had been dry. Neither was wrong. Maya learned to ask of every source who made it, when, and from where they were standing, and to want both kinds in the room.',
    'A primary source can also be wrong, and often is, because the person there saw one corner and had reasons of their own. Her great-great-grandfather wrote to a mother he wanted to reassure and to a censor he knew would read it first. Maya read the letter for what it was allowed to say.',
    'A secondary source can be wrong too, when it leans on the wrong primaries or on none. The textbook\'s dry day traced back to an officer\'s weather log kept two miles behind the line. The best histories show their sources, so a reader can walk back to the trench.',
    'A primary source was made at the time by someone there; a secondary source was made later, looking back. Ask of every source who made it, when, and why. The letter knows the mud; the textbook knows the month.',
  ],
};
STORIES['counting-time'] = {
  about: 'why 1750 is the eighteenth century, and the year that does not exist',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1161', after: 0, alt: 'A young man\'s essay with 1750 circled and a professor\'s note beside it' }, { serial: 'S1162', after: 1, alt: 'The years 1701 to 1800 bracketed and labeled 18th century' }, { serial: 'S2690', after: 2, alt: 'A timeline crossing from 1 BC straight to AD 1 with no zero' }, { serial: 'S2691', after: 3, alt: 'A calendar page for 1999 with a party hat, and the real century line drawn at the end of 2000' }, { serial: 'S2692', after: 4, alt: 'A British calendar for September 1752 with eleven days missing' }],
  title: 'The century off by one', art: 'S1160', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man at a library table with a long timeline, a gap drawn where year zero would be',
  words: [
    'Sam wrote that 1750 was in the seventeenth century, because it started with seventeen. His professor circled it. Sam was sure, and he was wrong by exactly one century, the way everyone is once.',
    'The first century was the years 1 to 100. So the second began in 101, and the eighteenth ran from 1701 to 1800. The hundreds digit plus one. 1750 was the eighteenth century, and so was 1800, and 1801 began the nineteenth.',
    'Then Sam tried to count from 50 BC to AD 50 and got a hundred years. There is no year zero; 1 BC steps straight to AD 1. So the count is the two numbers added, minus one: ninety-nine years. Two small traps, and every date in the course had walked past both.',
    'Decades have the same trap. The 1990s ran from 1990 to 1999, ten years, but the twentieth century ended on the last day of 2000, not 1999, because the first century started at year 1. The world threw its party a year early, and historians let it.',
    'Sam also learned to read dates the way they were written. Many old documents used the year of a king\'s reign or a calendar that started in March, and the calendar itself skipped eleven days in Britain in 1752. A date is a claim that needs a source too.',
    'The years 1701 to 1800 are the 18th century: hundreds digit plus one. There is no year zero, so from a BC year to an AD year you add the two and subtract one. Time counts differently than a ruler does.',
  ],
};
STORIES['cause-and-effect'] = {
  about: 'dry wood and a match, and why the match gets all the credit',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1164', after: 0, alt: 'A professor asking what the wood had been doing for twenty years before the match' }, { serial: 'S1165', after: 1, alt: 'Years of drought drawn as a stack of dry seasons' }, { serial: 'S2693', after: 2, alt: 'One spark at the top of the stack, and a forest fire on the horizon' }, { serial: 'S2694', after: 3, alt: 'Europe drawn as dry wood with the shooting removed, another spark hovering above it' }, { serial: 'S2695', after: 4, alt: 'A list of causes in order of weight, the trigger at the top and the alliances underneath' }],
  title: 'Dry wood and a match', art: 'S1163', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman crouched at a campfire, a stack of dry wood and a single struck match',
  words: [
    'Lena\'s essay said the war started because an archduke was shot. Her professor asked whether every shooting starts a war. Lena said this one did. He asked what the wood had been doing for the twenty years before the match.',
    'Long-term causes build for years: alliances that tied every country to a fight, arms races, empires rubbing at the edges. Those were the dry wood. The shooting was the trigger, the spark that would have gone out on a wet pile.',
    'And effects come in kinds too. Short-term: mobilization within weeks. Long-term: empires gone, borders redrawn, a peace that grew the next war. Lena rewrote her thesis. The match lit the fire; the wood decided how big it would be.',
    'Her professor gave her a test for a cause: would the effect have happened without it? Take away the shooting, and Europe\'s wood was still dry; some other spark would likely have found it. Take away the alliances, and the shooting stays a murder in one city.',
    'History rarely has one cause, so Lena listed hers in order of weight and said which was which. Necessary causes had to be there; the trigger only had to be first. Her essay was longer and, for the first time, could be argued with.',
    'Causes come in kinds: long-term causes build for years, a trigger is the spark, and effects can be short-term or long-term. Give the match its due and the wood its share.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-big-turns'] = {
  about: 'eight hinges the world swung on, and how to tell a hinge from a headline',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1167', after: 0, alt: 'A museum hall of eight doorways, most events drifting past as weather' }, { serial: 'S1168', after: 1, alt: 'A plow, a clay tablet, a ballot, a printing press' }, { serial: 'S2696', after: 2, alt: 'The hinge test: a battle that changes who rules beside a hinge that changes what a life is' }, { serial: 'S2697', after: 3, alt: 'A map with farming arriving in different valleys thousands of years apart' }, { serial: 'S2698', after: 4, alt: 'A student holding a headline from his own decade up to the hinge test' }],
  title: 'Eight hinges', art: 'S1166', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man walking a museum hall with eight doorways, each opening on a different age',
  words: [
    'Marcus asked what made an event important enough for the course, since the world had thousands of events a year. His professor said most events are weather. A few are hinges: after them, the door does not swing the same way again.',
    'Farming turned wanderers into villagers. Writing let a thought outlive its thinker. Republics put power in more hands. Printing made a book cheaper than a horse. The Columbian exchange traded crops, animals and diseases across an ocean. Industry moved work from muscle to engine. The atomic age put the end of the world within reach. The web put every library in every pocket.',
    'The test for a hinge was the same each time: did people afterward live in a way that was impossible before? A battle changes who rules. A hinge changes what a life is. Marcus started sorting the headlines of his own decade with the same question.',
    'The hinges did not swing evenly. Farming reached some valleys thousands of years before others, printing spread in decades, the web in years. A hinge is a change in what is possible, and the possible arrives on different days in different places.',
    'Marcus tried a candidate from his own decade and argued it both ways. A new way to move money might be a hinge or might be a faster horse; he would not know for fifty years. Historians, his professor said, are the only people who get to grade a hinge, and they grade slowly.',
    'Farming, writing, republics, printing, the Columbian exchange, industry, the atomic age and the web are the great turns. Each changed what a human life could be, which is the test that separates a hinge from a headline.',
  ],
};
STORIES['writing-history'] = {
  about: 'bricks and a house, and the difference between a fact and a claim',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1170', after: 0, alt: 'A stack of bricks labeled facts, no shape yet' }, { serial: 'S1171', after: 1, alt: 'A thesis sentence pinned above a pile of index cards' }, { serial: 'S2699', after: 2, alt: 'The bricks built into a small house with one sentence over the door' }, { serial: 'S2700', after: 3, alt: 'A second historian\'s house beside hers, with export figures answering it' }, { serial: 'S2701', after: 4, alt: 'Bricks each stamped with a report, a page and a year' }],
  title: 'Bricks and a house', art: 'S1169', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman at a study desk with a pile of index cards on one side and a single sentence pinned above them',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s history paper was twelve pages of facts, all of them correct, in date order. Her professor gave it back and said it was a pile of bricks. Rosa asked what was wrong with bricks. He said nothing, and that nobody lives in a pile.',
    'A fact is not a claim. A thesis is a sentence somebody could disagree with, and the facts are what you build it out of. Rosa had the materials and no house.',
    'She wrote the sentence: the famine was a policy failure before it was a crop failure. Now the bricks had somewhere to go, and some of them did not belong. Then she matched her words to her evidence: certainly where the record was thick, probably where it was thin, and one paragraph that said plainly what could not be known. The house stood, and it was honest about its weak wall.',
    'Her professor also wanted the other builder\'s house. A historian who read the same bricks had argued the crop, not the policy. Rosa gave that reading a paragraph and answered it with the export figures, which showed grain leaving the country during the hunger.',
    'Every brick got its address: the report, the page, the year. A claim without a source is a brick nobody can inspect, and a house nobody can inspect is a house nobody has to believe.',
    'A fact is not a claim; a thesis is a sentence somebody could disagree with. Match the strength of your words to the strength of the evidence, so the house stands on what the bricks can hold.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ending-sounds'] = {
  about: 'a cat said very slowly, and the little tuh at the end',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1173', after: 0, alt: 'The boy saying cat fast, the end sound blurred' }, { serial: 'S1547', after: 2, alt: 'The boy with his tongue tapping behind his teeth' }, { serial: 'S1548', after: 4, alt: 'A boy saying the end of a word with his lips popping, a toy dog and a cup on the kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S1549', after: 5, alt: 'A bus drawn with a hissing sss at its end' }],
  title: 'The sound at the end', art: 'S1172', cast: [],
  alt: 'A small boy saying a word slowly with a cat on his lap, three letters floating from his mouth',
  words: [
    'Sam could hear the start of cat, cuh, but he could not hear the end. It went by too fast. Cat, and it was gone.',
    'His dad told him to say it slowly. Sam said cat. Slower, said his dad, like a snail.',
    'Caaat. There it was, a little tuh at the very end. His tongue tapped behind his teeth to make it.',
    'Then came bus. Busss. The last sound was sss, a hiss like a snake.',
    'Then dog, with guh at the end. After that came cup, and his lips popped on the puh at the very end.',
    'Sam said every word in the kitchen slowly. Pan ended in nnn, spoon ended in nnn, and fork ended in kuh. He found every ending.',
    'Say the word slowly. The last sound is the ending sound.',
  ],
};
