STORIES['dictionary-skills'] = {
  about: 'a word that hid on the wrong page until a girl read the words at the top',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1176', after: 0, alt: 'A girl paging through the wrong part of a huge dictionary, a picture of a giraffe on the page' }, { serial: 'S1177', after: 1, alt: 'A dad and his daughter at a kitchen table, the dad spelling a word out on his fingers' }, { serial: 'S1866', after: 2, alt: 'Dictionary pages fanned out like a deck of cards, the girl flipping toward the back' }, { serial: 'S1867', after: 3, alt: 'The girl pointing at the two top corners of a dictionary page' }, { serial: 'S1868', after: 4, alt: 'The girl with her finger on the right entry halfway down a dictionary page, smiling' }],
  title: 'The wrong page', art: 'S1175', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl flipping through a thick dictionary at a library table, frustrated, one finger holding a page',
  words: [
    'Priya needed the word jungle for her report and opened the dictionary to G, because the word starts with the same sound as giraffe. Nothing. She flipped forward and back until the pages blurred. The dictionary was enormous, and the word was hiding.',
    'Her dad asked how jungle was spelled. J, U, N, G, L, E, she said. Then why look in G? Because jungle and giraffe start with the same sound, she said. But not the same letter. A dictionary does not care how a word sounds. It only cares how it is spelled.',
    'So she went to J. But J still had pages and pages. Her dad showed her the second letter trick. Words that start with the same letter are sorted by the next one: bat, bed, bin. JA came first, then JE, JI, JO, and last, JU.',
    'Then she noticed two words printed in bold at the top of every page. She had never once looked at them. They were guide words: the first and last words on that page.',
    'One page said jumpy at the top left and junk at the top right. Jungle comes after jumpy, because N comes after M. It comes before junk, because G comes before K. So it had to be on that page, and there it was.',
    'A dictionary is sorted by the first letter, then the second, then the third. Guide words name a page\'s first and last entries, so you never have to read a page to know if your word is on it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['simple-machines'] = {
  about: 'a piano too heavy to lift, and the ramp that did not make it any lighter',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1179', after: 0, alt: 'Two movers straining to lift the piano straight up, unable to' }, { serial: 'S1180', after: 2, alt: 'The long ramp beside the tall truck bed, the piano halfway up' }, { serial: 'S1887', after: 3, alt: 'A dolly stacked with boxes, a crowbar prying open a crate and a flag rising up a pole next door' }, { serial: 'S1888', after: 4, alt: 'A long lever handle pushed down far to lift a heavy crate a little' }, { serial: 'S1889', after: 5, alt: 'The six simple machines in a row: a ramp, a wedge, a screw, a lever, a wheel and axle, and a pulley' }],
  title: 'The piano', art: 'S1178', cast: [],
  alt: 'Movers rolling an upright piano up a long ramp into a truck, a girl watching from the driveway',
  words: [
    'The movers could not lift Grandma\'s piano into the truck. Two of them strained until their faces went red, and the piano rose about an inch. Nina watched from the driveway, sure the piano was staying.',
    'Then they pulled a long metal ramp from the truck and laid it against the back. They rolled the piano up it, slowly, and it went in. Nina did not understand. The piano weighed the same as it had a minute ago. Where had the weight gone?',
    'Nowhere, the mover said. The ramp made a trade. Instead of lifting the piano straight up four feet, they pushed it along twelve feet of ramp. Less force, more distance. The work, the push times the distance, stayed about the same.',
    'That ramp is a simple machine, an inclined plane. Nina counted the others that day. The dolly under the boxes rolled on wheels and axles. The crowbar that opened a crate was a lever. Next door, a flag went up its pole on a pulley.',
    'Every one made a job easier without an engine, and every one made the same trade. A long lever handle moves far so the other end can move a little and push hard.',
    'There are six simple machines: the inclined plane, the wedge, the screw, the lever, the wheel and axle, and the pulley. None of them gives you something for nothing.',
    'Machines do not make jobs smaller. They make them possible, by stretching a hard push into a long, easy one.',
  ],
};
STORIES['coordinate-plane'] = {
  about: 'a treasure map, two numbers, and the boy who walked them in the wrong order',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1182', after: 0, alt: 'A treasure map with 3, 2 written on it beside a small X' }, { serial: 'S1183', after: 1, alt: 'The same boy at a different corner after walking north first' }, { serial: 'S1979', after: 2, alt: 'The boy walking three paths east, then two north, to a marked corner' }, { serial: 'S1980', after: 3, alt: 'A boy beside a flag on a lamppost four blocks north of the park gate' }, { serial: 'S1981', after: 4, alt: 'A grid with an x-axis along the bottom and a y-axis up the side, two points marked' }],
  title: 'Three east, two north', art: 'S1181', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a treasure map in a park, a grid of paths drawn on the ground, a small X in the distance',
  words: [
    'The treasure map said the prize was at 3, 2 and nothing else. Jamal stood at the start of a park laid out in straight paths and had no idea what the two numbers wanted from him. Three what? Two what?',
    'He tried walking two paths and then three and found himself at a bench with nothing under it. He tried three and then two in every direction and found another bench. The map was either broken or he was.',
    'His sister took the map. Across first, then up, she said. Three blocks east along the bottom, then two blocks north. The corner she reached had a small stone with an X on it. Two numbers, one point, and the order was the whole secret.',
    'The map had a second clue: 0, 4. Jamal laughed. Zero across meant no walking east at all. He walked four blocks straight north from the start and found a flag on a lamppost. The start of the park, where both numbers are zero, is called the origin.',
    'His sister wrote the pairs the way mathematicians do, in parentheses with a comma: (3, 2) and (0, 4). The first number goes along the x-axis, across the bottom. The second goes up the y-axis. Every point on the plane has exactly one address.',
    'The coordinate plane works exactly like that park. The first number says how far across, the second says how far up, and swapping them lands you somewhere else. Two numbers, one point, always across first.',
  ],
};
STORIES['watersheds'] = {
  about: 'a crumpled paper bag, a cup of water, and the corner where every drop ended up',
  more: [{ serial: 'S1185', after: 0, alt: 'A storm drain on a hilly street with a creek at the bottom of the hill' }, { serial: 'S1186', after: 1, alt: 'A close look at the creases of the bag, drops running down them like creeks' }, { serial: 'S2204', after: 2, alt: 'A river map with every stream feeding one river' }, { serial: 'S2205', after: 3, alt: 'Creeks joining a wide river that becomes a lake in a city and runs on to the sea' }, { serial: 'S2206', after: 4, alt: 'An oily puddle and a lawn sprinkler washing down a curb into a storm drain' }],
  title: 'The crumpled bag', art: 'S1184', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy pouring water from a cup over a crumpled paper bag, the water running along the creases',
  words: [
    'Kai lived on a street with a storm drain and a creek at the bottom of the hill, and he had never once wondered where the creek came from. Then his teacher handed him a crumpled paper bag and a cup of water.',
    'He poured a little water on the top of the bag. It ran along the creases, joined other trickles, and gathered in one low corner. He poured on the other side. Different creases, same corner. He could not make the water go anywhere else.',
    'The creases were ridges. The folds between them were creeks. Every drop that fell on the bag ran downhill to the same river, and all the land that drained to that corner was its watershed. His street, his storm drain, the creek: one bag, one corner.',
    'His teacher showed a real map. In Austin, Texas, creeks such as Shoal Creek and Barton Creek run into the Colorado River, which widens into Lady Bird Lake downtown and flows on to the Gulf of Mexico. Every creek on the map had its own little watershed inside the big one.',
    'That is why oil dripped in a driveway or fertilizer washed off a lawn matters. Rain carries it down the creases too, into the storm drain and the creek, and what happens on the hill shows up in the river.',
    'A watershed is all the land that drains to one river, and upstream becomes downstream. What you pour on the top of the bag ends up in the corner, which is why a town cares what happens on the hill above it.',
  ],
};
