// The plain AI course, grade 6 (2026-09-29, pass FT): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['learning-from-examples'] = {
  about: 'a bird feeder camera that learned squirrels from two hundred labeled clips',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2723', after: 1, alt: 'A girl and her grandfather at a kitchen table labeling clips of birds and squirrels, two mugs of cocoa' }, { serial: 'S2724', after: 2, alt: 'Two hundred small clips flowing into a box that keeps only the shared shapes of birds and squirrels' }, { serial: 'S2725', after: 3, alt: 'A feeder door swinging shut just before a squirrel reaches the seed, a girl cheering' }, { serial: 'S2726', after: 4, alt: 'A blue jay with a puffed-up tail landing on the feeder as the door closes on it' }, { serial: 'S2727', after: 5, alt: 'A girl labeling twenty clips of blue jays while her grandfather watches' }],
  title: 'The bird feeder that learned', art: 'S2722', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl aiming an old phone at a bird feeder in a backyard while a squirrel hangs from it',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s grandfather was tired of squirrels emptying the bird feeder, so Maya pointed an old phone at it and set it to record every visitor. By Sunday she had two hundred short clips, and a program that was supposed to sort them into birds and squirrels.',
    'The program came with no rules in it. It could not be told that squirrels have bushy tails, because nobody had written a place to type that. What it wanted instead was examples. Maya sat at the kitchen table and labeled clip after clip, bird, squirrel, bird, bird, until her grandfather brought her cocoa and took a turn.',
    'When the labeling was done, the program did something Maya could not see. It went through the two hundred clips and kept whatever the birds shared and whatever the squirrels shared, and dropped the rest: the time of day, the light, the particular sunflower seed. What was left, the program called a model.',
    'On Monday the model met its first new clip and made a guess. Squirrel, it said, 94 out of 100, and the feeder\'s little door swung shut before the squirrel reached the seed. Maya cheered. Her grandfather asked how the program knew, and she found she could not quite say. It had never told her its rule.',
    'It had never told her, because it had no rule to tell. It had a pattern, pulled from her two hundred labels, and the pattern was only as good as the clips. When a blue jay with a puffed-up tail landed, the door swung shut on a bird. The model had learned that a big tail means squirrel, and nobody had shown it a jay.',
    'So Maya labeled twenty jays, and the model learned again. That, her grandfather said, is what the machine really is: not a thinker, but a keeper of patterns, and you are the one who decides which examples it sees.',
  ],
};
STORIES['wrong-and-sure'] = {
  about: 'a chatbot that gave the old iron bridge a date, a builder and a flood that never happened',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2729', after: 1, alt: 'A family at dinner, a sister asking a question while a boy looks up from his plate' }, { serial: 'S2730', after: 2, alt: 'A librarian opening a drawer of old newspapers, a boy reading a yellowed page about the bridge' }, { serial: 'S2731', after: 3, alt: 'Millions of pages about bridges pouring into a machine that writes one likely word at a time' }, { serial: 'S2732', after: 4, alt: 'A confident paragraph on a screen with the shape of a plaque, a question mark drawn over it' }, { serial: 'S2733', after: 5, alt: 'A report with two columns, how it looked and how it was, a teacher writing in the margin' }],
  title: 'The bridge that was never built', art: 'S2728', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a laptop with a chatbot answer on screen and an old iron bridge in a framed photo on the wall',
  words: [
    'Theo\'s report on the old iron bridge was due Friday, and the chatbot answered his first question in four seconds. The bridge, it said, was finished in 1887 by an engineer named Samuel Wray, and it had survived the flood of 1913 with only one cracked pier.',
    'It sounded wonderful. It sounded like a plaque. Theo typed the paragraph straight into his report, and he might have handed it in that way if his sister had not asked, over dinner, who Samuel Wray was. Theo did not know. He had not asked the machine how it knew, and it had not said.',
    'The next day the librarian pulled a drawer of old newspapers. The bridge was real, and it was finished in 1889, not 1887. There was no engineer named Wray in any issue; the builder was a company from Pittsburgh, and the flood of 1913 had not cracked a pier. It had washed away the road on the far bank.',
    'Theo felt tricked, until the librarian explained what the chatbot had done. It had read millions of pages about bridges and learned what a paragraph about a bridge usually looks like: a date, a name, a flood. Then it had built one for his bridge, one likely word at a time, with no way to check any of it.',
    'Sounding sure was part of the pattern, she said, because nearly every page it learned from had been written with confidence. The number it had for Samuel Wray was not a fact. It was the shape of a fact.',
    'Theo rewrote the report from the newspapers and put the chatbot\'s version beside it in a box, with a title: how it looked, and how it was. His teacher gave the box its own grade. The most useful question, she wrote in the margin, is the one he asked too late: how do you know?',
  ],
};
STORIES['fair-examples'] = {
  about: 'a kitchen speaker that understood Priya and not her grandmother, and the pile of voices that explained why',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2735', after: 1, alt: 'A girl speaking slowly into the speaker in her grandmother\'s accent, the speaker showing a puzzled light' }, { serial: 'S2736', after: 2, alt: 'A teacher at a board drawing thousands of hours of voices flowing into a speaker' }, { serial: 'S2737', after: 3, alt: 'A pile of recordings from one city, one empty space where a grandmother\'s voice should be' }, { serial: 'S2738', after: 4, alt: 'A grandmother reading a page of sentences to the speaker while a girl holds up a timer' }, { serial: 'S2739', after: 5, alt: 'A row of very different people each handed a microphone, the machine finally hearing the ones it heard least' }],
  title: 'The voice it never heard', art: 'S2734', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl and her grandmother at a kitchen counter with a small round speaker between them',
  words: [
    'The kitchen speaker understood Priya perfectly. It played her songs, set her timers and told her the weather in a cheerful voice. When her grandmother asked it for the same things, it played the wrong song, set no timer, and once answered a question about rain with the price of rice.',
    'Her grandmother laughed it off, but Priya did not. She spoke to the speaker in her grandmother\'s accent, slowly, and it failed for her too. So it was not the words. It was the voice: the shape of the vowels, the rhythm, the way the sentence rose at the end.',
    'Priya\'s science teacher explained where a speaker learns to listen. It is trained on recordings, thousands of hours of people talking, each with the words written out. It keeps the patterns those voices share. If nearly all of the voices came from one place, the patterns fit that place and stumble everywhere else.',
    'Nothing in the speaker was against her grandmother. She had simply been missing, along with everyone who sounded like her, from the pile of recordings someone had collected years before. It had been collected in a city where hardly anyone spoke the way she did. The unfairness was in the pile, not in the machine.',
    'So Priya did the one thing that changes a model. She found the setting that let the speaker learn a new voice, sat her grandmother in front of it, and had her read a page of sentences. Then another. By the third page the timer worked. By the fifth, the speaker knew the difference between rain and rice.',
    'A machine that is right for most people can still be wrong for the people it heard least. The fix is never a faster machine. It is asking who is missing, and adding them.',
  ],
};
STORIES['staying-in-charge'] = {
  about: 'Dev, a science-fair poster, and the postcard rule for anything typed into a machine',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2741', after: 1, alt: 'A mother leaning over a boy\'s shoulder at a laptop, a postcard held up beside the screen' }, { serial: 'S2742', after: 2, alt: 'A boy deleting his address from a text box, a trail of footprints fading behind a laptop' }, { serial: 'S2743', after: 3, alt: 'A picture of the solar system made of thousands of tiny paintings, a boy writing a credit line on the poster under it' }, { serial: 'S2744', after: 4, alt: 'A boy answering quiz questions with the laptop closed, writing a paragraph by hand' }, { serial: 'S2745', after: 5, alt: 'A boy with a ribbon beside his poster as two judges nod' }],
  title: 'The postcard rule', art: 'S2740', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a laptop with a glowing picture of the solar system on screen, a blank poster board and a glue stick beside him',
  words: [
    'Dev\'s science-fair poster needed a picture of the solar system and a paragraph to go under it, and the AI tool on his mother\'s laptop offered both in under a minute. The picture was gorgeous. The paragraph was smooth. Dev was already reaching for the glue stick.',
    'Then the tool asked, helpfully, for his full name, his school and his home address, so it could write him a cover letter to the judges. Dev had typed the first two before his mother looked over his shoulder and said the postcard rule. Anything you type into that box, she said, leaves this laptop and may be kept. Would you write it on a postcard?',
    'He deleted the school and the address, and kept the first name only. The cover letter could wait; the judges did not need a letter from a machine anyway. What went into the box was part of his digital footprint now, and a footprint is a trail you cannot rake away.',
    'The picture was the next problem. It looked like a painting, because it was made from millions of paintings and photos that people had made, and those people had names. Dev could not find them, so he wrote on the poster where the picture came from and what tool had made it, the way a book lists its sources.',
    'The paragraph he threw away. Not because it was wrong, but because it was not his, and the judges would ask him questions the paragraph could not answer for him. He asked the tool to quiz him on the planets instead, closed the laptop, and wrote the paragraph from his own head, twice, until it was better than the smooth one.',
    'Nothing private goes in, the work inside gets its credit, and the thinking stays yours. Dev won a ribbon. The judges said his answers were the best part.',
  ],
};
// Computer science for grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-29, pass FU): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['inside-the-machine'] = {
  about: 'a laptop that would not wake, and the four parts a repair tech checked in order',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2747', after: 0, alt: 'A power light glowing on a dark screen, a fan spinning behind a vent' }, { serial: 'S2748', after: 2, alt: 'A technician holding a small memory stick beside an empty slot inside the laptop' }, { serial: 'S2749', after: 3, alt: 'A laptop screen showing an operating system starting, a browser and a game waiting as icons' }, { serial: 'S2750', after: 4, alt: 'A finger on the keyboard, a flow drawn on a whiteboard: input, processing, output, storage' }, { serial: 'S2751', after: 5, alt: 'A girl carrying her repaired laptop out of the shop, her essay open on the screen' }],
  title: 'The laptop that would not wake', art: 'S2746', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenager and a repair technician at a workbench with an open laptop, its drive and memory sticks laid out beside it',
  words: [
    'Ines\'s laptop had died on the night before her essay was due, or so it seemed. The power light came on, the fan spun, and the screen stayed black. The repair tech, a woman named Dolores, did not panic. She said the machine had four kinds of part and she would check them in order.',
    'The fan meant the CPU had power, so the processor was probably fine. The black screen could be the screen itself, a peripheral, or it could be that the operating system never started, because starting is the operating system\'s first job. Dolores plugged in an outside monitor. Still black. So the trouble was earlier than the screen.',
    'She opened the case and pulled a memory stick out of its slot. It was loose, which was enough. Memory holds what the CPU is working on right now, she said, and with a bad stick the CPU has nowhere to put its first thought. Nothing in memory survives a power cut anyway, so the essay was never there.',
    'The essay was on the drive, the storage that keeps files when the power is off. She reseated the stick and pressed the button. The operating system started, checked the hardware, shared out the CPU and memory, and the desktop appeared with a browser and a game waiting as icons. Those were applications, and they would ask it for the screen and the files.',
    'Dolores drew the cycle on the whiteboard by the counter: input from the keyboard, processing in the CPU, output to the screen, storage for what should last. Primary storage is memory, fast and forgetful. Secondary storage is the drive, slower and stubborn. Every machine in the shop, phone or server, was the same picture.',
    'Ines opened the essay. It was exactly where she had left it, because storage does not forget. She did not know yet that the next thing she would learn was how the drive held her sentences as numbers.',
  ],
};
STORIES['counting-in-binary'] = {
  about: 'eight lanterns on a harbor wall that could send any number up to 255',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2753', after: 1, alt: 'A boy and a harbor master looking at a row of eight lantern hooks, each hook labeled with a power of two' }, { serial: 'S2754', after: 2, alt: 'A boy adding the places of lit lanterns on a slate: eight, two and one' }, { serial: 'S2755', after: 3, alt: 'Lanterns being lit from the largest place down as a boy works out thirteen' }, { serial: 'S2756', after: 4, alt: 'A row of lanterns rolling over like an odometer, the rightmost going dark and the next lighting' }, { serial: 'S2757', after: 5, alt: 'A boy at a laptop where eight small squares stand for one byte of a photograph' }],
  title: 'The eight lanterns', art: 'S2752', cast: [],
  alt: 'Eight lanterns in a row on a stone harbor wall at night, some lit and some dark, a boat waiting offshore',
  words: [
    'The old harbor wall had eight iron hooks, and Tomas asked the harbor master what they were for. Lanterns, she said. Before radio, the keepers used them to tell boats a number: which berth to take, how many hours until the tide. Eight lanterns, and any number from zero to two hundred and fifty-five.',
    'Tomas did not believe eight lanterns could say that many things until she explained the hooks. The one on the right was worth one. The next was worth two, then four, then eight, sixteen, thirty-two, sixty-four and one hundred and twenty-eight, each hook twice the one to its right. A lit lantern meant that place was on. A dark one meant off.',
    'To read a number you added the lit places. Lit, dark, lit, lit on the four right-hand hooks was eight and two and one: eleven. Tomas wrote it on a slate as 1011 and saw what a computer sees, a row of switches.',
    'To send a number you worked from the biggest hook down. Thirteen? One hundred and twenty-eight was too big, and so was everything down to sixteen. Eight fit, with five left over, and then four fit, with one left over. Two did not fit, but one did, so thirteen was 1101, and Tomas lit the lanterns himself.',
    'Then she showed him counting. One lantern lit meant one; for two, the first went dark and the second lit; for three, both lit; for four, the third lit and the first two went dark. Every time the right-hand lantern would have to show two, it went dark and passed one to the left. A car odometer does the same, rolling nine back to zero and turning the next wheel.',
    'That night Tomas opened a photograph on his laptop and thought about the wall. Every dot of color was three numbers, each held in eight switches. The whole picture was lanterns, millions of them, lit and dark, and the machine read them the way he had.',
  ],
};
STORIES['variables-and-decisions'] = {
  about: 'a vending machine program that counted change with a modulus and failed at the edges',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2759', after: 0, alt: 'A whiteboard listing four variables with their types: price, paid, change, done' }, { serial: 'S2760', after: 1, alt: 'A calculation on a screen: change divided by twenty-five, and the remainder left over' }, { serial: 'S2761', after: 2, alt: 'A flow of a program: a loop that keeps asking for coins while paid is less than price' }, { serial: 'S2762', after: 3, alt: 'A cardboard vending machine giving too many coins as two students stare at the screen' }, { serial: 'S2763', after: 5, alt: 'A test list on paper with edge cases checked off: exact change, zero, a large bill' }],
  title: 'The machine that gave change', art: 'S2758', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two students at a laptop beside a cardboard vending machine model, coins spread on the table',
  words: [
    'The workshop\'s vending machine was cardboard, but the program that ran it had to be real. Rosa and Ben started with the variables, and their teacher made them name the type of each. Price was an integer in cents. Paid was an integer too. Change was an integer, and done was a boolean, true when the machine had finished.',
    'Change was the interesting part. If change was 85 cents, how many quarters? The whole part of 85 divided by 25 was 3, the integer division. What was left, 85 % 25, was 10: the modulus, the remainder. Ten cents was a dime, and the remainder of 10 % 10 was zero, so no pennies. Every coin was one more division and one more remainder.',
    'The program was a sequence with a loop in it. While paid was less than price, ask for another coin and add it. When the loop ended, a conditional decided: if paid was greater than price, give change; else give nothing. Then set done to true.',
    'It worked on the first three tries and failed on the fourth. Ben paid the exact price and the machine gave him a quarter. They read the conditional again. It said greater than or equal, not greater than, and one wrong symbol had turned exact change into a gift.',
    'So they tested at the edges on purpose: exact change, zero paid, a twenty-dollar bill for a fifty-cent drink, and a price of zero. The last one made the loop end before it began, which was right, and the big bill made 1950 cents of change, seventy-eight quarters, which was also right and also silly. They added a rule about bills.',
    'Rosa kept the test list taped inside the cardboard door. A program, she wrote at the bottom, does exactly what it says, so say exactly what you mean, and check the edges before anyone else does.',
  ],
};
STORIES['working-like-a-programmer'] = {
  about: 'an app that crashed, a map library with a license, and a password that had leaked',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2765', after: 0, alt: 'A student reading an error message and printing a variable at the suspect line' }, { serial: 'S2766', after: 1, alt: 'An input file being cut in half again and again until one line remains' }, { serial: 'S2767', after: 2, alt: 'A license page for an open-source map library, with the words use, change and share marked' }, { serial: 'S2768', after: 3, alt: 'A phone showing a one-time code beside a laptop login screen, a password manager open' }, { serial: 'S2769', after: 4, alt: 'A polished web page with a question written over it: who wrote this, when, and why' }],
  title: 'The license on the map', art: 'S2764', cast: [],
  alt: 'A student at a laptop showing a crashed school app, a printed license page and a phone with a code beside it',
  words: [
    'The club\'s app for finding lost water bottles crashed every Tuesday, and Kwame was the one who read the error. It named a line, so he printed the variables at that line and saw that the date was empty on Tuesdays, when the janitor\'s list came in blank. That was the bug: not the app, but an empty input the app had never been told about.',
    'Finding it had taken a method. He had shrunk the input, cutting the list in half and running the app again, until one line remained and the crash stayed with it. Then he looked up the function in its reference, which said in plain words that it could not take an empty date. A minute of reading beat an hour of guessing.',
    'The map in the app came from a library, and the library came with a license. Kwame read it because his teacher had made reading licenses a habit: open source, use it, change it, share what you change under the same rules. A different map they had wanted was freeware, free to use but not to change, and one old atlas was public domain, nobody\'s and everybody\'s. They cited the library on the about page.',
    'Then the club\'s login stopped working, and the reason arrived by email: their shared password, a word and a year, had leaked from another site. Kwame set a new one, twelve random characters kept in a password manager. He turned on the second step too, a code on the club phone, so a leaked password alone would open nothing.',
    'That week a fast, polished page claimed the school would close for a snow day. It looked official. It was not, and it had no author, no date and an ad for a game at the bottom. Kwame taught the club his three questions for any page: who wrote it, when, and what do they gain.',
    'The app ran the next Tuesday without a crash. The bug had been small, the license had been read, the password was long, and nobody had believed the snow day.',
  ],
};
// Economics and personal finance, K to 2 (2026-09-29, pass FV): four stories at the kindergarten length with five pictures each.
STORIES['wants-needs-and-choices'] = {
  about: 'Rosa, one coin, and the balloon she let go',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2771', after: 1, alt: 'A girl looking from a red balloon to a sparkly ring, one coin in her hand' }, { serial: 'S2772', after: 2, alt: 'A big brother kneeling beside a girl and holding up one finger' }, { serial: 'S2773', after: 3, alt: 'A girl sliding a sparkly ring onto her finger as she hands the coin to a man' }, { serial: 'S2774', after: 4, alt: 'A girl at the fair gate looking back at the balloons, her brother beside her' }],
  title: 'One coin, two wants', art: 'S2770', cast: [],
  alt: 'A small girl holding one shiny coin between a table of balloons and a table of sparkly rings at a school fair',
  words: [
    'Rosa had one shiny coin for the school fair. She walked past the balloons, and then past the sparkly rings, and stopped.',
    'She wanted a balloon. She wanted a ring too. But the coin could buy only one thing.',
    'Her big brother Luis knelt down beside her. You have to choose, he said. When you pick one, you let the other go.',
    'Rosa held the coin for a long time. Then she chose the ring, because a balloon can pop and a ring can stay. She gave the coin to the man and slid the ring on her finger.',
    'At the gate she saw the balloons again, and she felt a little sad. Luis said that was normal. Giving something up is part of choosing.',
    'Needs come first, and wants wait. One coin, two wants. You pick one and let the other go.',
  ],
};
STORIES['earning-income'] = {
  about: 'Kofi, a dog named Pepper, and the coins he earned',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2776', after: 1, alt: 'A neighbor woman handing a boy a leash and counting rules on her fingers' }, { serial: 'S2777', after: 2, alt: 'A woman placing five coins in a boy\'s open hand while a dog wags beside them' }, { serial: 'S2778', after: 3, alt: 'An aunt handing a boy a card with two coins inside, a jar on the table' }, { serial: 'S2779', after: 4, alt: 'A boy flying a new kite, a jar of coins on the porch behind him' }],
  title: 'The dog walk', art: 'S2775', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy walking a small brown dog on a leash past a neighbor\'s gate, a kite in a shop window behind him',
  words: [
    'Kofi wanted a kite, and kites cost coins. Miss Ito next door had a dog named Pepper who needed a walk every day.',
    'I will walk Pepper, Kofi said. Miss Ito said yes, and she said the job had rules. Be on time, hold the leash tight, and bring Pepper home dry.',
    'Every day Kofi came at four. Every day Pepper pulled, and Kofi held on. At the end of the week Miss Ito gave him five coins. That was income, money he had earned.',
    'On Sunday his aunt came to visit and gave him two coins in a card. Kofi put them in the jar too. But those were a gift, his aunt said. You did not work for them.',
    'By the end of the month the jar held enough for the kite. Kofi knew which coins he had earned and which had been given. Both bought the kite, but only one had been work.',
    'Work earns income. A gift is not income. Skills like being on time get you the job.',
  ],
};
STORIES['spending-and-saving'] = {
  about: 'Amara, a jar with a scooter on the front, and two coins a week',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2781', after: 1, alt: 'A girl dropping one coin into a jar and keeping one coin in her other hand' }, { serial: 'S2782', after: 2, alt: 'A jar filling with coins, the scooter picture taped on the front' }, { serial: 'S2783', after: 3, alt: 'A girl taking three coins out of the jar to buy a birthday card' }, { serial: 'S2784', after: 4, alt: 'A girl riding a red scooter down a street at sunset' }],
  title: 'Two coins a week', art: 'S2780', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding an empty jar with a picture of a red scooter taped to the front',
  words: [
    'Amara wanted a red scooter. It cost more coins than she had ever seen in one place. So her mother gave her a jar and a plan.',
    'Every week Amara earned three coins for setting the table. Two coins went in the jar, and one coin was hers to spend. Putting coins in the jar was a deposit.',
    'The jar filled slowly. Two, then four, then six, then eight. Amara taped a picture of the scooter on the front, and the picture got closer every week.',
    'One day she took three coins out to buy a birthday card for her friend. Taking coins out is a withdrawal. The jar went down, and then it went up again.',
    'By the end of the summer the jar was full. Amara spent it on the scooter. She rode it up and down the street until the sun went down.',
    'Spending is now. Saving is for later. A deposit puts money in, a withdrawal takes it out, and saving adds up.',
  ],
};
STORIES['trading-and-markets'] = {
  about: 'a trading day of stickers and marbles, and a school store with prices',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2786', after: 1, alt: 'A boy handing two stickers to a girl and receiving one blue marble' }, { serial: 'S2787', after: 2, alt: 'A boy holding out a sticker while another shakes his head at a toy car, a teacher pointing at a coin' }, { serial: 'S2788', after: 3, alt: 'A school store counter with a pencil and a small ball, each with a price tag' }, { serial: 'S2789', after: 4, alt: 'A boy buying a pencil at the school store and holding up one coin left over' }],
  title: 'The sticker market', art: 'S2785', cast: [],
  alt: 'Three children at a classroom table with piles of stickers, marbles and tiny toy cars',
  words: [
    'On Friday the class had a trading day. Diego brought stickers, Lena brought marbles, and Sam brought tiny toy cars.',
    'A trade is a swap, Miss Ray said. You give one thing and get one thing. Diego gave Lena two stickers and got one blue marble.',
    'Then Sam wanted a sticker, but Diego did not want a car. They had nothing to swap. That is why people use money, Miss Ray said. Everyone takes money.',
    'After lunch the class went to the school store, which is a market with a roof. A pencil cost 2 coins. A small ball cost 4. Diego had 3 coins in his pocket.',
    'He could buy the pencil and have 1 coin left, but he could not buy the ball yet. Not enough coins means save more, or choose something else. Diego chose the pencil and kept saving.',
    'A trade is a swap. A market is where buyers meet sellers. The price is the coins it takes.',
  ],
};
// Economics and personal finance, grades 3 to 5 (2026-09-29, pass FW): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['skills-and-income'] = {
  about: 'a girl who could sharpen blades, a windstorm, and a shelf of chainsaw blades that went up in price',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2791', after: 1, alt: 'A grandfather showing a girl how to lift a blade off a mower in a garage' }, { serial: 'S2792', after: 2, alt: 'A boy doing arithmetic on the back of a flyer, a mower behind him' }, { serial: 'S2793', after: 3, alt: 'A hardware store shelf with a dozen chainsaw blades and a price tag raised by four dollars' }, { serial: 'S2794', after: 4, alt: 'A delivery truck refilling the shelf of blades, the price tag coming back down' }, { serial: 'S2795', after: 5, alt: 'A boy at a grinder in winter, sparks flying, his grandfather nodding' }],
  title: 'The blade sharpener', art: 'S2790', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl sharpening a mower blade on a workbench grinder while a boy watches, sparks flying',
  words: [
    'Priya and her cousin Jonah both mowed lawns on Elm Street that summer, and both charged the same: six dollars an hour. By July, Jonah had noticed something that did not seem fair. Priya was earning almost twice as much as he was.',
    'The difference was not the mowing. It was what Priya had learned from her grandfather the winter before, on the workbench in his garage. She could take a mower blade off, sharpen it on the grinder, and balance it so the mower did not shake. Nobody else on Elm Street could do that.',
    'So the neighbors paid her for two things. Six dollars an hour to mow, and eight dollars for every blade she sharpened, which took her fifteen minutes. Jonah worked out the arithmetic on the back of a flyer and stared at it. A skill, his grandfather told him, is money that shows up every hour you use it.',
    'In August a windstorm came through and knocked down half the branches on the street. Everyone needed a chainsaw blade at once, and the hardware store had twelve. The price on the shelf went up by four dollars overnight. The blades had not changed. How many there were, and how many people wanted them, had.',
    'Priya understood it before Jonah did. When something is scarce and wanted, it costs more; when the truck refills the shelf, the price comes back down. She did not raise her own prices for the neighbors, but she understood why the store had.',
    'That winter Jonah spent his Saturdays in the garage, learning the grinder. Skills are human capital, his grandfather said, and human capital is the only thing you carry to every job you will ever have. By the next summer, Elm Street had two blade sharpeners, and both of them earned for two things.',
  ],
};
STORIES['planned-spending-and-credit'] = {
  about: 'Marcus, a planned bike, a loan from his sister, and a friend who decided in the aisle',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2797', after: 1, alt: 'A teenage sister writing twenty-four on a slip of paper while a boy nods' }, { serial: 'S2798', after: 2, alt: 'A boy leaving a store with a remote-control car and candy, three dollars left in his hand' }, { serial: 'S2799', after: 3, alt: 'A boy mowing a neighbor\'s yard while his sister counts dollars on the porch' }, { serial: 'S2800', after: 4, alt: 'Two boys talking beside a bike, one drawing a line down a page into two halves' }, { serial: 'S2801', after: 5, alt: 'A boy writing a shopping list at a kitchen table before going to the store' }],
  title: 'The bike that cost forty-four dollars', art: 'S2796', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy looking at a blue bike with a sale tag in a shop window, a jar of dollars in his hands',
  words: [
    'Marcus had planned the bike for a month. He knew the model, the color, and the price: sixty dollars, marked down from eighty for one week in June. He had forty dollars in his jar, and the week would end on Saturday.',
    'His sister Dani, who was in high school and had a job, offered him a loan. Twenty dollars, she said, and you pay me back twenty-four by the end of summer. The four dollars was interest, the price of borrowing. Marcus thought about it overnight, wrote down how he would earn the twenty-four, and said yes.',
    'His friend Theo went to the same store that Saturday with thirty dollars and no plan. He came out with a remote-control car that had been by the register, a bag of candy, and three dollars. The car broke on Tuesday. The bike Theo had been talking about since April was still sixty dollars, and he had three.',
    'Marcus rode home. Every Saturday in July he mowed Miss Okafor\'s yard for six dollars, and every Saturday he handed Dani a share. By the middle of August he had paid back all twenty-four, and the bike was his with nothing owed on it.',
    'Theo asked how it had worked. Marcus explained the two halves of it. The plan had told him what he wanted and what it cost before he walked in, so the register had no power over him. And the loan had been for something planned that he could pay back, with the interest counted before he said yes.',
    'Credit is a tool, Dani told them both. Used for a plan, it gets you the bike. Used in the aisle, it turns a want into a debt, and the debt outlives the want. Theo wrote a list before his next trip to the store.',
  ],
};
STORIES['expenses-and-profit'] = {
  about: 'a cookie table with a fixed five dollars, variable flour and a napkin of arithmetic',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2803', after: 1, alt: 'Bowls of flour, sugar, butter and chocolate chips on a counter beside four trays of cookies' }, { serial: 'S2804', after: 2, alt: 'A man with three children buying the last fifteen cookies at half price' }, { serial: 'S2805', after: 3, alt: 'A girl doing arithmetic on a napkin at a kitchen table, her mother checking it' }, { serial: 'S2806', after: 4, alt: 'Three jars labeled spend, save and shelter, a girl dividing dollars among them' }, { serial: 'S2807', after: 5, alt: 'A girl sketching next year\'s plan on a napkin: five dozen cookies and no cousin' }],
  title: 'The cookie table', art: 'S2802', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl behind a fair table of cookies under a hand-lettered sign, a five-dollar receipt taped to the table',
  words: [
    'Ana signed up for a table at the spring fair to sell cookies, and the first thing she learned was that the table itself cost money. Five dollars for the day, whether she sold one cookie or a hundred. Her mother called it a fixed expense: it did not move.',
    'The flour, sugar, butter and chocolate chips were different. Every dozen cookies she baked used up more of them, so the more she planned to sell, the more they cost. Those were variable expenses, and they grew with the batch. Four dozen cookies cost her twelve dollars in ingredients.',
    'She priced the cookies at one dollar each and set out forty-eight of them under a sign. By two o\'clock she had sold thirty-one. Her cousin ate two, which counted as expenses of a sort. At the end of the day she sold the last fifteen for fifty cents each to a man with three children.',
    'At the kitchen table that night she did the arithmetic on a napkin. Money in: thirty-one dollars plus seven dollars and fifty cents, thirty-eight fifty. Expenses: five for the table, twelve for ingredients, seventeen. Profit: twenty-one dollars and fifty cents. Her mother made her do it twice, and it came out the same.',
    'Ana divided the profit the way she divided her allowance, before it could disappear: ten to spend, eight to save, and three fifty for the animal shelter jar. The deciding took a minute and the money stayed where she had put it.',
    'Next year, she said, she would bake five dozen and skip the cousin. Fixed expenses stay the same, variable ones grow with what you sell, and profit is what is left when both are paid. A napkin and a pencil are enough to run a table.',
  ],
};
STORIES['savings-taxes-and-budgets'] = {
  about: 'a first paycheck with twelve dollars missing, and a budget six dollars over',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2809', after: 1, alt: 'A pay stub with gross income at the top and net income at the bottom, taxes listed in small print' }, { serial: 'S2810', after: 2, alt: 'A father and son in a car passing a road crew, a school, a park with a pool and a fire station' }, { serial: 'S2811', after: 3, alt: 'A budget on a kitchen table with income on the left and expenses on the right' }, { serial: 'S2812', after: 4, alt: 'A boy packing his own lunch, a budget on the fridge with six dollars crossed out' }, { serial: 'S2813', after: 5, alt: 'A boy at a bank counter opening a savings account, a jar left behind on a closet shelf' }],
  title: 'The paycheck with a hole in it', art: 'S2808', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage boy staring at his first paycheck beside a pool snack bar, a calculator in his other hand',
  words: [
    'Leo\'s first paycheck from the swimming pool snack bar was supposed to be one hundred dollars: ten hours at ten dollars an hour. The check said eighty-eight. He read it three times and then took it to his father, sure that someone had made a mistake.',
    'Nobody had. One hundred dollars was his gross income, what he had earned. Eighty-eight was his net income, what he took home. The twelve dollars in between were taxes: some for income tax, some for payroll tax, listed in small print down the side of the stub.',
    'Leo wanted to know where the twelve dollars had gone. His father drove him past the answer on the way home. The road they were driving on, the school, the park with the pool, and the fire station on the corner: taxes pay for the things everyone shares.',
    'At the kitchen table they made a budget for the summer. Income on the left: eighty-eight dollars every two weeks. Expenses on the right: a bus pass, lunches, and the phone plan Leo had promised to pay for himself. The right side came to ninety-four.',
    'A budget balances when the expenses fit inside the income, his father said, and this one did not. Six dollars over. Leo had three ways to close it: spend less, earn more, or move something to next month. He packed his lunches, and the six dollars vanished.',
    'The rest went into a savings account instead of the jar in his closet, because the bank kept it safe and paid a little interest for leaving it there. By the end of the summer the account held more than he had put in, and Leo knew exactly where every dollar had gone.',
  ],
};
// Economics and personal finance, grades 6 to 8 (2026-09-29, pass FX): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['accounts-and-cards'] = {
  about: 'a first checking account, a debit card understood wrong for four days, and a register',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2815', after: 1, alt: 'A girl holding up her phone showing twelve times twelve, her mother nodding' }, { serial: 'S2816', after: 2, alt: 'A debit card at a grocery store register, an arrow drawn from the card to an account' }, { serial: 'S2817', after: 3, alt: 'A bank statement with an overdraft fee circled, headphones and a movie ticket beside it' }, { serial: 'S2818', after: 4, alt: 'A small notebook register with a balance after every line, matching a bank app on a phone' }, { serial: 'S2819', after: 5, alt: 'A girl opening a door drawn on a page, a register held in front of it' }],
  title: 'The card that was not money', art: 'S2814', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl and her mother at a bank counter, two fee sheets side by side, a paycheck in hand',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s first paycheck from the pool came with a piece of advice from her mother: put it in a checking account, not a drawer. So they visited two banks on the same afternoon. The first charged twelve dollars a month unless five hundred dollars stayed in the account. The second charged nothing at all.',
    'Twelve dollars a month is a hundred and forty-four a year, Maya said, doing it on her phone. Her mother said that was the whole lesson and they should open the account at the second bank, which they did, with a hundred and ten dollars from the pool.',
    'The debit card arrived a week later, and Maya understood it wrong for exactly four days. She thought of it as a card that paid for things. Her mother corrected her at the grocery store: it was a card that took her money, out of her account, right then. Nothing was borrowed. What left the account was hers.',
    'On the fifth day she bought a ninety-dollar pair of headphones, then lunch, then a movie, and the bank charged her thirty-two dollars for spending more than she had. An overdraft fee, the statement said. The most expensive lunch of her life, her mother said, and she was not joking.',
    'That night Maya started a register in a small notebook: every deposit added, every withdrawal and every transfer subtracted, and the balance written after each line. One hundred ten, minus ninety for the headphones, minus twelve for lunch, minus fourteen for the movie, minus the thirty-two dollar fee, plus sixty from the next pay. Twenty-two dollars, and it matched the bank to the penny.',
    'The card, she decided, was not money. It was a door to the account, and the register was the only way to know what was behind the door before she opened it. She never paid the fee again.',
  ],
};
STORIES['credit-history-and-loans'] = {
  about: 'two cousins, the same used car, and loans at five and nine percent',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2821', after: 1, alt: 'A loan officer reading a credit report on a screen, accounts and payment history listed' }, { serial: 'S2822', after: 2, alt: 'A phone bill and a credit card statement stamped paid on time, beside another with four months unpaid' }, { serial: 'S2823', after: 3, alt: 'A desk pad with two columns of interest arithmetic, two thousand against thirty-six hundred' }, { serial: 'S2824', after: 4, alt: 'A hand crossing out a six-year loan on a desk pad and circling four years' }, { serial: 'S2825', after: 5, alt: 'A young man setting bills to pay on the due date on his phone, a car key on the seat beside him' }],
  title: 'The seven-year shadow', art: 'S2820', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two young men at a car dealership desk, two loan papers with different rates',
  words: [
    'Two cousins, Dev and Marcus, walked into the same dealership on the same Saturday to buy the same kind of used car. They walked out with loans that were not the same at all. Dev\'s was at five percent. Marcus\'s was at nine.',
    'The difference was not the car and not the cousins\' jobs, which paid about the same. It was a report neither of them had ever seen. The lender had pulled each cousin\'s credit report: the accounts they had, whether they had paid on time, how much they owed, and who else had asked to look.',
    'Dev had a phone plan and a small credit card, both paid on the day they were due, for three years. Marcus had the same card and had let it slide for four months two years earlier, when he was between jobs. That was still on the report. Most of what goes on one, the loan officer said, stays for about seven years.',
    'On a ten-thousand-dollar loan over four years, the numbers were plain. Five percent simple interest is five hundred a year, two thousand in all. Nine percent is nine hundred a year, thirty-six hundred. Marcus would pay sixteen hundred dollars more for the same car because of four months two years ago.',
    'The loan officer offered Marcus a longer loan, six years instead of four, to make the monthly payment smaller. Dev did the arithmetic on the desk pad: nine percent for six years was fifty-four hundred dollars of interest. Smaller each month, and eighteen hundred more in all. Marcus took the four years.',
    'A credit report is a reputation with numbers, Dev said on the drive home. It takes years to build and one bad season to dent, and the dent shows for seven. Marcus set every bill to pay itself the day it came due, and the next report, in time, said so.',
  ],
};
STORIES['interest-that-compounds'] = {
  about: 'twin sisters, a hundred dollars each, and the year the one-dollar gap started to grow',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2827', after: 1, alt: 'Two jars, one marked simple and one marked compound, ten percent written on each' }, { serial: 'S2828', after: 2, alt: 'A ledger page showing one hundred twenty beside one hundred twenty-one, a girl laughing' }, { serial: 'S2829', after: 3, alt: 'A flat road and a steepening hill drawn side by side on a ledger page' }, { serial: 'S2830', after: 4, alt: 'A grandfather writing seventy-two divided by ten on a chalkboard, the years to double beneath it' }, { serial: 'S2831', after: 5, alt: 'Two girls switching a jar label from simple to compound, their grandfather smiling' }],
  title: 'Two ways to grow a hundred dollars', art: 'S2826', cast: [],
  alt: 'Twin girls at a kitchen table with a grandfather, two hundred-dollar bills and a ledger between them',
  words: [
    'On their twelfth birthday, the twins Leah and Noor each got a hundred dollars from their grandfather and the same deal. He would pay them interest for leaving it with him, ten percent a year, but they had to choose how.',
    'Leah chose simple interest. Ten dollars a year, every year, on the hundred she started with. Flat and easy to count. Noor chose compound interest. Ten percent a year on whatever the pile had grown to, including the interest already earned.',
    'After one year they had the same: one hundred ten each. After two, Leah had one hundred twenty and Noor had one hundred twenty-one, because Noor\'s second year earned ten percent of one hundred ten, which is eleven. Leah laughed at the one dollar. Noor did not.',
    'After three years it was one hundred thirty against one hundred thirty-three and change. Their grandfather kept the account in a ledger, and every birthday the gap was wider than the year before. Simple interest was a flat road. Compound interest was a hill that kept getting steeper.',
    'Their grandfather showed them the rule of seventy-two. Divide seventy-two by the rate and you get about the years to double: at ten percent, seven years or so. Noor\'s hundred would be two hundred by nineteen and four hundred by twenty-six, without adding a dollar. Leah\'s would take twenty years to reach three hundred.',
    'Leah switched at fourteen, and Noor let her keep the gap she had already lost, which was not much yet. The lesson, their grandfather said, was not the ten percent. It was the time. The earliest dollars do the most work, and the same arithmetic runs against you on a debt.',
  ],
};
STORIES['budgets-taxes-and-college'] = {
  about: 'a first paycheck read in percents, a drill with tax added, a napkin of net worth, and a college target',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2833', after: 1, alt: 'An aunt drawing a budget as a pie chart in percents on a whiteboard at the garden center' }, { serial: 'S2834', after: 2, alt: 'A hardware store register showing a fifty-dollar drill plus four dollars of sales tax' }, { serial: 'S2835', after: 3, alt: 'A napkin with two columns, assets and what is owed, and net worth circled at the bottom' }, { serial: 'S2836', after: 4, alt: 'A boy comparing the cost of a two-year college and a four-year school on a laptop, five ways to pay listed beside it' }, { serial: 'S2837', after: 5, alt: 'A folder thick with napkins, a boy adding one more' }],
  title: 'The paycheck and the percent', art: 'S2832', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage boy reading his paycheck at a garden center, a plant cart behind him',
  words: [
    'Sam\'s summer job at the garden center paid two hundred dollars every two weeks, and the first thing he learned was that two hundred was not what arrived. Income tax came out before the paycheck was written. What arrived was one hundred seventy-six.',
    'His aunt Rosa, who ran the garden center, showed him how to read the money in percents instead of dollars. Twenty-four out of two hundred is twelve percent to tax. Then she had him build a budget the same way. Forty-four dollars to savings, which was twenty-five percent of the take-home, sixty-two for the bus and lunches, and the rest, seventy, to spend.',
    'The register at the hardware store taught him the other tax. A fifty-dollar drill had eight percent sales tax added at the counter: four dollars, fifty-four in all. He had counted out exactly fifty and had to put it back.',
    'Rosa also had him write his net worth on a napkin, which took two minutes. A bike worth two hundred and a savings account of three hundred: five hundred of assets. Forty dollars he owed his cousin: one liability. Net worth, four hundred sixty. She said it would be the first of many napkins.',
    'What he was saving for was college. Rosa laid out the five ways to pay: savings, grants, scholarships, student loans and work-study, and she made him look up the numbers. A two-year college near home was less than a third of the four-year school he had first named. Divide the first year by the months until it starts, she said, and that is the target.',
    'Forty-four dollars every two weeks was not much. But Sam had done the arithmetic on lifetime income too, and the jobs that needed the two years of training paid enough more each year that the savings looked cheap. He kept the napkins in a folder, and the folder got thick.',
  ],
};
// Personal finance, grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-29, pass FY): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['paychecks-and-statements'] = {
  about: 'a first full-time paycheck, the stub that explained the missing money, and three papers that keep a money life honest',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2839', after: 1, alt: 'A pay stub with gross pay at the top and a column of deductions, FICA circled' }, { serial: 'S2840', after: 2, alt: 'A benefits section of a pay stub showing a health insurance contribution and a retirement match' }, { serial: 'S2841', after: 3, alt: 'A bank statement and a handwritten list side by side, two coffee charges circled' }, { serial: 'S2842', after: 4, alt: 'An income statement and a net worth statement on a kitchen table, a bicycle behind' }, { serial: 'S2843', after: 5, alt: 'A goal card on a fridge: an emergency fund by March, a number and a date' }],
  title: 'The stub that told the truth', art: 'S2838', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man reading a pay stub in a break room, a calculator and a coffee beside him',
  words: [
    'Andre\'s first full-time paycheck was for two weeks at 15 dollars an hour, eighty hours, and he had done the multiplication in his head a dozen times: 1,200 dollars. The deposit that landed in his account on Friday was 1,028.20. He took the stub to the break room and read it like a contract.',
    'The stub had two columns. Gross pay, 1,200. Then the deductions: Social Security at 6.2 percent, 74.40; Medicare at 1.45 percent, 17.40; those two together were FICA, 91.80. Federal income tax withheld, 80.00. Net pay, 1,028.20, exactly what had landed. The bank had made no mistake at all; the government had simply made its claim.',
    'Below the taxes was a third section he had not expected, and it made him feel better. The company put 60 dollars toward his health insurance every two weeks. Starting next month it would match whatever he sent to a retirement account, up to 3 percent of his pay. None of that was in his net pay, and all of it was pay.',
    'That night he reconciled his bank statement against his own list, the way the course had shown him: every deposit, every card swipe, every transfer, line against line. Two lines did not match. A coffee shop had charged him twice on the ninth, 4.75 and 4.75. He called the next morning and the second one came back.',
    'Then he wrote an income statement for the month, income on top and expenses beneath, and a net worth statement under that. A bike worth 500 and 900 dollars in savings against 300 dollars owed to his brother: a net worth of 1,100. It was not much. It was a number, and a number can be moved.',
    'His goal went on the fridge in the form the course insisted on: 1,500 dollars in an emergency fund by March, 125 a month, specific, measurable and dated. Gross is what you earn, he told his brother, net is what arrives, and the stub in between is where the story is.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-true-cost-of-credit'] = {
  about: 'a storefront loan at fifteen per hundred, a credit union loan at eighteen percent, and a credit report with a mistake on it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2845', after: 1, alt: 'A woman doing arithmetic on a fogged bus window: fifteen times twenty-six' }, { serial: 'S2846', after: 2, alt: 'A credit union desk with a small loan paper showing eighteen percent over six months' }, { serial: 'S2847', after: 3, alt: 'A credit report with one wrong phone account line circled, a score climbing beside it' }, { serial: 'S2848', after: 4, alt: 'A notebook page of loan words: revolving, installment, secured, unsecured, fixed, variable' }, { serial: 'S2849', after: 5, alt: 'A car pulling away with its transmission fixed, a note on the dashboard reading cash today' }],
  title: 'Fifteen dollars per hundred', art: 'S2844', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman outside a storefront lender with a cash today sign, a bus stop and a broken-down car behind her',
  words: [
    'The sign in the storefront said cash today, and Renata needed 300 dollars today, because the transmission had not asked whether payday was Friday. The clerk explained the terms in one breath: 15 dollars per 100, due in two weeks, no credit check. Forty-five dollars for two weeks seemed almost polite.',
    'She did the arithmetic on the bus. Fifteen percent for two weeks. There are twenty-six two-week periods in a year, so the yearly rate, the APR, was 15 times 26: about 390 percent. A credit card charging 24 percent had looked outrageous to her a month before. The storefront charged sixteen times that.',
    'The credit union three blocks away had a different door. A small installment loan, 300 dollars at 18 percent APR over six months, cost her about 16 dollars in interest in all, and it built a record. The clerk there asked about the three Cs without calling them that. Character meant whether she paid her bills; capacity meant her income against her debts; collateral she had none of, so the rate was a little higher.',
    'The loan officer also pulled her credit report, and there was the surprise: a phone account she had closed two years before was listed as unpaid. Errors are common, the officer said, and lenders act on them. Renata disputed it in writing that week, and a month later the line was gone and her score had climbed.',
    'She learned the vocabulary while she waited. Revolving credit, which you can borrow again as you repay, like a card. Installment credit, a fixed amount in fixed payments, like her loan. Secured loans, backed by something the lender can take, and unsecured ones, backed by a promise, which cost more. Fixed rates and variable ones. Grace periods, late fees, cash advance fees.',
    'The transmission got fixed either way. The difference was 45 dollars against 16, a record that helped her against a record that would have hurt, and a habit of reading the whole page. Cash today, she decided, is the most expensive phrase in the language.',
  ],
};
STORIES['saving-investing-and-risk'] = {
  about: 'an emergency fund built a paycheck at a time, three shelves for money with a date on it, and a retirement match',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2851', after: 1, alt: 'A mechanic\'s invoice for a transmission being paid from an emergency fund, a young man calm' }, { serial: 'S2852', after: 2, alt: 'Three shelves labeled savings account, certificate of deposit and money market fund, a toy car on the middle one' }, { serial: 'S2853', after: 3, alt: 'A retirement plan form with a four percent match, a young man doing the arithmetic twice' }, { serial: 'S2854', after: 4, alt: 'A seesaw with bonds low on one end and single stocks high on the other, a mutual fund at the middle' }, { serial: 'S2855', after: 5, alt: 'A young man and his aunt at a kitchen table with a chart of four doublings, the first thousand marked' }],
  title: 'The first thousand', art: 'S2850', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man moving ten percent of a paycheck into a savings account on his phone, a car outside the window',
  words: [
    'Malik\'s emergency fund began as a rule, not a number: 10 percent of every paycheck into a savings account he could reach but did not touch. His pay was 2,000 dollars a month, so 200 went in, and his expenses were 1,400, so the course\'s three months came to 4,200. It took him twenty-one months to get there.',
    'In month nineteen the fund earned its keep. The transmission on his car went, 1,300 dollars, and for the first time in his life a broken car was an errand instead of a loan. He paid it from the fund, and started filling it again the next payday.',
    'With the fund full, the next goal was a down payment on a better car in three years, and for money with a date on it the course had three shelves. A savings account, low interest and instantly reachable. A certificate of deposit, more interest for agreeing to leave the money for a fixed term. A money market fund, in between. He put the car money in a two-year certificate.',
    'Then his employer offered a retirement plan with a match: put in 4 percent of pay and the company added 4 percent. Malik did the arithmetic twice. Every 80 dollars he put in became 160 before the market did anything, an instant return no stock could promise. He signed up on the spot.',
    'The plan\'s menu made him read the seesaw of risk and return. Bonds, loans to companies and governments, steady and low. Stocks, shares of ownership, high on both ends. A mutual fund spread across hundreds of companies, sitting in the middle because they do not all fall at once. At twenty-three, with forty years ahead, he chose the fund.',
    'The rule of 72 said his money at 7 percent would double about every ten years: four doublings before he retired, each bigger than the last. The first thousand had been the hardest. It always is, his aunt said. The last thousand arrives on its own.',
  ],
};
STORIES['insurance-scams-and-college'] = {
  about: 'a burst pipe, a renter\'s policy with a deductible, a scam call about a student loan, and school paid for in the cheapest order',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2857', after: 1, alt: 'A renter\'s insurance claim form showing a five hundred dollar deductible and the insurer\'s share' }, { serial: 'S2858', after: 2, alt: 'Four cards on a table labeled avoid, reduce, retain and transfer, a water alarm beside them' }, { serial: 'S2859', after: 3, alt: 'A phone showing an unknown caller, a young woman shaking her head, textbooks drying on a rack' }, { serial: 'S2860', after: 4, alt: 'A stack of papers: grants and scholarships on top, a work-study card, a small loan at the bottom, a FAFSA form' }, { serial: 'S2861', after: 5, alt: 'A sheet listing three schools by cost, graduation rate and starting pay, a community college circled' }],
  title: 'The deductible and the phone call', art: 'S2856', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman in a soaked apartment holding a dripping laptop, water on the floor',
  words: [
    'The pipe above Sofia\'s apartment burst on a Tuesday in January, and by the time the landlord shut the water off, her laptop, her textbooks and most of her clothes were soaked. The landlord\'s insurance covered the building. It did not cover a single thing she owned.',
    'Her renter\'s policy did. She had bought it for 14 dollars a month because the course had made her list what she would have to replace and price it, and the total had scared her. The claim came to 2,600 dollars. The deductible was 500. She paid the 500; the insurer paid 2,100, up to a policy limit she never came near.',
    'The course had given her four words for risk, and she could see them all in one week. Avoid: keep the laptop off the floor from now on. Reduce: a renter\'s alarm that shuts the water off. Retain: the 500 she had paid herself, from savings. Transfer: the 2,100 the insurer paid, because she had paid premiums every month.',
    'The phone call came while she was still drying textbooks. A recorded voice said her student loan had been approved for forgiveness and she need only confirm her Social Security number to claim it. Every scam has the same tell, urgency plus secrecy, and this one had both. She hung up and reported the number to the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau.',
    'Her actual student loan was the smallest part of how she paid for school, and she kept it that way on purpose. The order the course taught: grants and scholarships first, because they need no repaying; work-study second; loans last, and only what a starting salary in her field could carry. The FAFSA, filed the fall before, had opened the first two doors.',
    'She had chosen the community college for the first two years after comparing three schools on cost, graduation rate and the starting pay of graduates in her program. The arithmetic still held. A burst pipe was a bad week. Without the policy, the fund and the order, it would have been a bad decade.',
  ],
};
// College computer science (2026-09-29, pass FZ): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['inside-a-running-program'] = {
  about: 'a stack overflow, a wall of numbered pigeonholes, and a pile of bookmarks that nobody took off',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2863', after: 1, alt: 'A professor drawing a wall of numbered pigeonholes with a slip of paper in each' }, { serial: 'S2864', after: 2, alt: 'An array drawn as holes 1000, 1004 and 1008 with an arrow computing the address of element i' }, { serial: 'S2865', after: 3, alt: 'A pile of bookmarks beside the wall, one added for every call, one removed for every return' }, { serial: 'S2866', after: 4, alt: 'A pile of bookmarks reaching the ceiling, a single wrong character circled in the code' }, { serial: 'S2867', after: 5, alt: 'A student writing which hole and which bookmark on a sticky note stuck to her laptop' }],
  title: 'The box at address 1000', art: 'S2862', cast: [],
  alt: 'A student at a laptop showing the words stack overflow, a wall of numbered pigeonholes drawn on a whiteboard behind her',
  words: [
    'Ines\'s program crashed with two words she had never seen: stack overflow. It was the third week of her first systems course and the program was forty lines long. She had read it a dozen times without finding anything that looked like a stack, or full.',
    'Her professor did not look at the code. He drew a wall of numbered pigeonholes on the board, one thousand to one thousand and forty, and put a slip of paper in each. Memory, he said, is this wall. A variable is a name for a hole. A program is a clerk with a list of orders: fetch the next order, decode what it asks, execute it, and again, billions of times a second.',
    'Then he drew an array of integers starting at hole 1000. Element 0 lives at 1000, he said, element 1 at 1004, element 2 at 1008: four bytes each, so the address of element i is 1000 plus 4 times i. The clerk does not walk the wall; the clerk computes the number and reaches. That is why arrays are fast.',
    'And the stack? He drew a pile of bookmarks beside the wall. When a function calls another, the clerk drops a bookmark on the pile: where to come back to, and the values it was holding. When the function returns, the bookmark comes off. Your function, he said, calls itself, and nothing ever takes a bookmark off.',
    'Ines saw it then. Her function had a case that was supposed to stop the calls, and a typo had made it never true. Each call added a bookmark, none removed one, and the pile hit the ceiling, which is all a stack overflow is: bookmarks past the room the machine gave them.',
    'She fixed the one character and the program ran in a blink. The lesson she kept was not the fix. It was the wall: text becomes instructions, instructions move values between numbered holes, and a pile of bookmarks remembers who called whom. Every bug after that, she found by asking which hole and which bookmark.',
  ],
};
STORIES['counting-steps'] = {
  about: 'a guessing game between one and a thousand, handshakes that grow four times when the people double, and a roster search that went from two thousand steps to twelve',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2869', after: 1, alt: 'A student halving a number line with each question, the range shrinking to a single number' }, { serial: 'S2870', after: 2, alt: 'A chart comparing a thousand steps against ten and a billion against thirty-one' }, { serial: 'S2871', after: 3, alt: 'Sixteen people shaking hands in a circle with one hundred twenty lines between them' }, { serial: 'S2872', after: 4, alt: 'A student sorting a club roster once and then searching it by halving on a laptop in a dormitory' }, { serial: 'S2873', after: 5, alt: 'The number three hundred and twelve on a board with one sentence written beneath it' }],
  title: 'Guess my number', art: 'S2868', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teaching assistant at a whiteboard with the numbers one to one thousand, students calling out questions',
  words: [
    'The teaching assistant opened the lab with a game. I am thinking of a number between one and one thousand, she said, and you may ask yes-or-no questions. The first student started at one and worked upward, and the room groaned by the time he reached forty.',
    'The second student asked whether it was above five hundred. No. Above two hundred and fifty? Yes. Above three hundred and seventy-five? No. Ten questions later she had it: three hundred and twelve. Each question had thrown away half of what was left, and 1,024 is two to the tenth, so ten questions always suffice.',
    'That, the assistant said, is the whole of the lecture. Searching an unsorted list looks at every item in the worst case: n steps, linear. Searching a sorted list by halving takes about log2 of n, plus one, and doubling the list adds one step. On a thousand items the difference is a thousand against ten. On a billion, it is a billion against thirty-one.',
    'Then she turned it around with handshakes. Four people shaking every hand: six handshakes. Eight people: twenty-eight. Sixteen: one hundred and twenty. Double the people and the handshakes go up about four times, because the count is n times n minus one over two. Any algorithm that compares every pair grows that way, n squared, and it is the reason the simplest sorts die on large inputs.',
    'One student, Priya, went back to her dormitory and found her own club roster program, which searched an unsorted list of two thousand names for every lookup. She sorted the list once and searched by halving. The lookups went from two thousand steps to twelve, and the page that had taken a breath to load now loaded before she lifted her finger.',
    'The number the assistant had been thinking of was written on the board at the end: three hundred and twelve, and beneath it, in smaller letters, the only sentence Priya copied down. A faster machine buys you one more doubling. A better algorithm buys you all of them.',
  ],
};
STORIES['shapes-of-data'] = {
  about: 'an order app that served the last order first, and the shape of data that fixed it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2875', after: 1, alt: 'A student comparing a stack of plates on a shelf with a line at a counter, a one-line bug on his screen' }, { serial: 'S2876', after: 2, alt: 'Three numbered plates pushed onto a stack, the nine coming off first, a queue giving back the three' }, { serial: 'S2877', after: 3, alt: 'A program walking a list of ten thousand customers from the front to find one name' }, { serial: 'S2878', after: 4, alt: 'A coat check where a ticket number points straight to a hook, a hash table drawn beside it' }, { serial: 'S2879', after: 5, alt: 'Three questions taped above a desk: how often, how many, does order matter' }],
  title: 'The plates and the line', art: 'S2874', cast: [],
  alt: 'A campus café with a line to the door, an order screen showing the newest order at the top',
  words: [
    'The campus café\'s new order app went live on a Monday and the line reached the door by ten. Orders were not being lost. They were being served in the wrong order: whoever had ordered last got their coffee first, and the student who had ordered at eight-fifteen was still waiting at nine.',
    'Diego, who had written the app over the summer, found the bug in one line. He had stored the orders in a stack: the last one pushed was the first one popped, like plates on a shelf. A line at a counter is a queue: first in, first out. He had built the plates when he needed the line.',
    'Push 3, push 7, push 9, then take one, his roommate said, testing him. Nine from a stack, three from a queue. Diego changed one structure and the café ran in order by lunchtime. The undo button in the same app, he noticed, was a stack on purpose: the last change is the first undone.',
    'The second complaint was slower to see. Looking up a customer\'s rewards balance took longer every week, because the app walked a list of every customer from the front until it found the name. Ten customers, no problem. Ten thousand, and every lookup was a hike.',
    'He replaced the list with a hash table, which turns a name into a slot by arithmetic. A coat check does the same, turning a ticket number into a hook without searching the rack. Key 47 in a table of ten slots goes to slot 7; a name goes to a slot computed from its letters. Lookups became one step, however many customers signed up.',
    'By the end of the term Diego kept three questions taped above his desk, the ones the course said choose the shape of any data. What do I do most often, how many will there be, and does the order matter? A stack, a queue, a list, a hash table and a tree each answer them differently, and the café never lost the line again.',
  ],
};
STORIES['recursion-and-limits'] = {
  about: 'eleven thousand photographs in folders inside folders, a function that asked the same question of something smaller, and a question no program can answer',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2881', after: 1, alt: 'A diagram of a folder holding files and smaller folders, the smallest folder marked as the base case' }, { serial: 'S2882', after: 2, alt: 'Three nesting dolls opened in a row beside a toy folder of twelve files' }, { serial: 'S2883', after: 3, alt: 'Factorial of four written as a chain of five calls multiplying back up to twenty-four' }, { serial: 'S2884', after: 4, alt: 'A machine reading a tape of another program with a question mark above it' }, { serial: 'S2885', after: 5, alt: 'A student writing the count eleven thousand and forty beside a door with no lock and no door' }],
  title: 'The folder inside the folder', art: 'S2880', cast: [],
  alt: 'A student at a laptop with a tree of folders inside folders drawn across the screen',
  words: [
    'Wen had eleven thousand photographs in folders inside folders inside folders, and the assignment was to count them without opening a single folder by hand. Her first program counted the files in the top folder and stopped, because it did not know how deep the folders went.',
    'The answer was to ask the same question of something smaller. To count a folder: count its own files, then add the count of each folder inside it. The smallest folder, with no folders in it, is the base case, the one you can answer directly. That is recursion, and it fits any data shaped like a tree.',
    'She tested it on a toy: a folder with three files and two folders of four and five files. Three plus four plus five, twelve. Then she ran it on the eleven thousand, and it finished before her tea was cold. Her roommate said it was like counting nesting dolls: open one, count it, ask the same of the doll inside.',
    'Factorial, the course pointed out, is the same trick with numbers: factorial of n is n times factorial of n minus 1, and factorial of 0 is 1. So factorial of 4 is 4 times 3 times 2 times 1, 24, in five calls. Leave out the base case and the calls never stop, the bookmarks pile up, and the stack overflows.',
    'That word, stop, led Wen to the strangest thing in the course. She asked whether a program could be written that reads any other program and says whether it will ever finish. In 1936 Alan Turing proved that no such program can exist, not for lack of speed or memory but because the general answer is impossible. It is called the halting problem.',
    'The count came to eleven thousand and forty. The proof she could not stop thinking about had no number in it at all. Some doors, the course said, are not locked. There is no door. Knowing where those are, Wen decided, is what separates a computer scientist from someone who can code.',
  ],
};
// Art and music appreciation, K to 2 (2026-09-29, pass GE): four stories at the kindergarten length with five pictures each.
STORIES['lines-shapes-and-colors'] = {
  about: 'Nia, a pumpkin, and the orange that two colors made',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2887', after: 1, alt: 'An art teacher putting dabs of red and yellow on a plate and handing a girl a brush' }, { serial: 'S2888', after: 2, alt: 'A girl stirring red and yellow paint into bright orange, laughing' }, { serial: 'S2889', after: 3, alt: 'Small plates of green and purple paint beside a painted pumpkin with a green stem' }, { serial: 'S2890', after: 4, alt: 'A painted pumpkin with a zigzag mouth, triangle eyes and a curved smile' }],
  title: 'The orange that was not there', art: 'S2886', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at an art table with a box of red, yellow and blue paint and no orange, a pumpkin on the table',
  words: [
    'Nia wanted to paint a pumpkin, and there was no orange paint in the whole box. There was red, yellow and blue, and that was all.',
    'Her art teacher did not look worried at all. He put a dab of red on the plate, and a dab of yellow beside it. Then he handed Nia the brush.',
    'Nia mixed them, round and round. The red and the yellow turned into orange, bright as a real pumpkin. She laughed out loud. Two colors had made a third one.',
    'After that she could not stop. Blue and yellow made green for the stem. Red and blue made purple for the sky at night. Every mix was a small surprise.',
    'For the pumpkin\'s face she drew a zigzag mouth and two triangle eyes. She added a curved line for a smile under the zigzag. Lines and shapes, her teacher said, are the bones of a picture.',
    'Red, yellow and blue can mix into many new colors. Lines and shapes hold the picture together.',
  ],
};
STORIES['what-a-picture-says'] = {
  about: 'a class, a big picture of a boat, and the two questions that open any picture',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2892', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher pointing at the painted boat on the river while three children call out what they see' }, { serial: 'S2893', after: 2, alt: 'Two children in front of the picture, one calm and smiling, one looking sad at the lonely boat' }, { serial: 'S2894', after: 3, alt: 'A cup with a painted bird in a coffee shop window and a quilt hanging over a fence' }, { serial: 'S2895', after: 4, alt: 'A teacher holding up a cup with a bird on it, children listening' }],
  title: 'The picture on the wall', art: 'S2891', cast: [],
  alt: 'A class of children looking up at a big picture of a boat on a blue river with a red sun going down',
  words: [
    'The class walked to the library to see the big picture on the wall. It showed a boat on a wide blue river, with a red sun going down.',
    'What does it show, asked Miss Lopez. A boat, said Omar, and Ana added the river, and Kofi added the red sun. All of them were right.',
    'How does it make you feel, she asked next. Omar said calm, because the water was so smooth and still. Ana said sad, because the boat was alone out there. Both of you are right, said Miss Lopez, and a picture can be two things at once.',
    'On the walk back, Kofi began to see art all around him. On a cup in the coffee shop window. On a street sign with a painted bird. On a quilt hung over a fence.',
    'People make art to show what they love, Miss Lopez said, and to make plain things beautiful. A cup does not need a bird on it. Someone wanted one there.',
    'Ask a picture what it shows and how it feels. Then say your idea, and listen to a friend say theirs.',
  ],
};
STORIES['beat-and-sound'] = {
  about: 'Ben, a heartbeat that never sped up, and the rhythm of his own name',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2897', after: 1, alt: 'A class marching around a rug in time while the teacher taps a drum' }, { serial: 'S2898', after: 2, alt: 'A boy clapping the three parts of his name, quick, quick, slow' }, { serial: 'S2899', after: 3, alt: 'A teacher playing a flute high and soft, a big drum low and loud beside her' }, { serial: 'S2900', after: 4, alt: 'A quiet classroom after the song, a boy still feeling the beat in his chest' }],
  title: 'The drum and the heartbeat', art: 'S2896', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with a hand on his chest feeling his heartbeat, a music teacher with a drum beside him',
  words: [
    'Ben put his hand on his chest the way the music teacher showed him. Thump. Thump. Thump. While he sat still, it kept the same steady pace.',
    'That is a steady beat, said the teacher. Music has one too. She tapped a drum, tap, tap, tap, tap. The class marched around the rug in time.',
    'Then she asked Ben to clap his name. Ben-ja-min, three claps, quick, quick, slow. That was rhythm, the pattern of the words on top of the beat. Ana\'s name had two claps, and Kofi\'s had two.',
    'The teacher played a bird song on the flute, high and soft. Then she hit the big drum, low and loud. Fast and slow, loud and soft, high and low, she said. Listen for each one.',
    'When the song stopped, nobody moved. The quiet was part of the music too. Ben could still feel the beat in his chest, going on and on.',
    'The steady beat is always the same. The rhythm is the pattern of the words on top.',
  ],
};
STORIES['voices-and-instruments'] = {
  about: 'a hello said five ways, three families of instruments, and a good audience on Monday',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2902', after: 1, alt: 'Children whispering hello with hands cupped, then calling it across the room' }, { serial: 'S2903', after: 2, alt: 'A silent class with mouths closed and eyes bright, saying hello inside their heads' }, { serial: 'S2904', after: 3, alt: 'A guitar, a flute and a drum on a table, children with eyes closed guessing' }, { serial: 'S2905', after: 4, alt: 'Grown-ups clapping at the end of a spring concert while children bow' }],
  title: 'Five voices', art: 'S2901', cast: [],
  alt: 'A music teacher holding up five fingers while a class says hello together',
  words: [
    'On Friday the music room had a game. Say hello five ways, said the teacher. Everyone said hello in a speaking voice first, all together.',
    'Next they sang hello, high and sweet. After that they whispered it, so soft it was almost silence. They called it too, as if across a big yard. Last, the teacher said, now say it with your inner voice.',
    'Nobody made a sound. Every child said hello inside their own head, and every child smiled. Five voices, the teacher said, and you have them all.',
    'After the game she played sounds, and the class guessed with their eyes closed. A guitar has strings you pluck, and a flute sounds when you blow. A drum sounds when you hit it. Three families of instruments, and each one had its own sound.',
    'On Monday the grown-ups came to hear the spring songs. The children sat still, listened with their ears, and clapped at the end. That, said the teacher, is what a good audience does.',
    'Five voices, three families of instruments, and two rules for an audience: listen, and clap at the end.',
  ],
};
// Art and music appreciation, grades 3 to 5 (2026-09-29, pass GF): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['principles-of-design'] = {
  about: 'forty gray birds, one red one, and the principles that fixed a poster',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2907', after: 1, alt: 'A poster of gray birds all the same size and distance apart, looking like wallpaper' }, { serial: 'S2908', after: 2, alt: 'A boy painting one bird red among the gray ones, every eye in the room turning to it' }, { serial: 'S2909', after: 3, alt: 'A thin curved cloud line leading from the corner of the poster to the small red bird' }, { serial: 'S2910', after: 4, alt: 'A large dull barn painted low in the right corner, balancing the small bright bird on the left' }, { serial: 'S2911', after: 5, alt: 'A second-place ribbon pinned beside a judge\'s handwritten note on a poster' }],
  title: 'The red bird in the gray sky', art: 'S2906', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy in front of a poster of forty identical gray birds in a gray sky, an art teacher beside him',
  words: [
    'Tomas had painted a whole sky of gray birds for the school poster contest, forty of them, and the poster looked like a wet sidewalk. His art teacher, Miss Delgado, stood beside it for a long time and then asked one question. Where does your eye go first?',
    'Nowhere, Tomas admitted. The birds were all the same size, the same gray, the same distance apart. Repetition, Miss Delgado said, is a fine principle, but it needs a partner. A picture with only repetition is wallpaper.',
    'She handed him a brush loaded with red. One bird, she said, any bird. He painted the third bird from the left red, and the poster changed while the paint was still wet. Every eye that came near it went to the red bird first. That is emphasis, she said. A focal point.',
    'Then she showed him contrast. The red bird was small against the big gray sky, bright against dull, one against many, and each difference made the bird louder. Movement came next: he added a thin line of cloud that curved from the corner of the poster to the red bird, and the eye followed it like a road.',
    'Balance was the last fix. The red bird sat on the left, so the right side felt empty, until Tomas painted a large, dull barn low in the corner. A big dull thing balanced a small bright thing, and the poster stopped tipping. Proportion he left alone; the birds were the right size for the sky.',
    'The poster won second place, and Tomas kept the judge\'s note more than the ribbon. The elements are the building blocks, it said. The principles are how you stack them. Your red bird knows that.',
  ],
};
STORIES['art-across-time-and-place'] = {
  about: 'a cave wall, a wave and a quilt, and one question that opened all three',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2913', after: 1, alt: 'A copy of a Lascaux cave wall with running horses painted over the bumps of the rock' }, { serial: 'S2914', after: 2, alt: 'A great wave of hooked lines curling over three small boats with a mountain far behind' }, { serial: 'S2915', after: 3, alt: 'A story quilt with a painted family on a rooftop at night and words written along the border' }, { serial: 'S2916', after: 4, alt: 'A docent on a school bus counting museum jobs on her fingers: illustrator, graphic artist, photographer, architect' }, { serial: 'S2917', after: 5, alt: 'A girl writing one question on the inside cover of a sketchbook' }],
  title: 'Three pictures, one question', art: 'S2912', cast: [],
  alt: 'A class following a museum docent through a gallery, three rooms marked on a paper map',
  words: [
    'The museum had three rooms on Priya\'s class list, and the docent gave the class one question to carry through all of them: what did the artist most want you to see?',
    'The first room held a copy of a cave painting from Lascaux, in France, horses running across a wall that was painted about seventeen thousand years ago. The painter had used the bumps of the rock for the horses\' bellies. Priya decided the main idea was movement: a whole herd, going somewhere, fast.',
    'The second room held a print by Hokusai, made in Japan in the 1830s, a great wave curling over three small boats with a mountain far behind. The main idea, said Kofi, was that the sea was bigger than anybody. Priya thought it was the boats, still rowing. The docent said both, and that the wave was made of a hundred hooked lines, repetition doing the work of fear.',
    'The third room held a story quilt by Faith Ringgold, painted cloth with a border of sewn squares and a story written along the edge. A family on a rooftop at night, in a city. The main idea was closeness, the class agreed: everyone on one blanket under one sky.',
    'On the bus Priya asked the docent what her job was called. A museum educator, she said. Then she counted the other jobs the class had passed that day. An illustrator had drawn the map of the rooms; a graphic artist had made the signs; a photographer had shot the catalog; an architect had built the light into the ceiling.',
    'Three pictures, three places, seventeen thousand years apart, and one question that worked on all of them. Priya wrote it on the inside cover of her sketchbook, where she would see it every time she opened it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['families-and-forms'] = {
  about: 'four families named with the eyes shut, a color called timbre, and a form drawn with three letters',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2919', after: 1, alt: 'Four sounds drawn as pictures on a board: a flute, a trumpet, a violin bow and a drumstick' }, { serial: 'S2920', after: 2, alt: 'A flute and a trumpet playing the same note, two different colors of sound drawn as waves' }, { serial: 'S2921', after: 3, alt: 'A conductor pointing at mezzo forte on a page while students play more softly' }, { serial: 'S2922', after: 4, alt: 'A boy drawing the letters A, B, A on his program while the conductor holds it up to the room' }, { serial: 'S2923', after: 5, alt: 'A boy walking home with a concert program in his pocket, letters on the front' }],
  title: 'The concert with the eyes closed', art: 'S2918', cast: [],
  alt: 'Third graders sitting in on an orchestra rehearsal with their eyes closed, a conductor holding up a flute',
  words: [
    'The fourth-grade orchestra let the third graders sit in on a rehearsal, and the conductor, Dana Reyes, began with a game. Close your eyes, she said, and tell me the family.',
    'The first sound was air moving through a tube, high and clear. Woodwind, said Leo, before anyone else, and it was a flute. The second was lips buzzing into metal, bright as a shout: brass, a trumpet. The third was a bow drawn across a string, and the fourth was a stick on a drum skin. Strings and percussion. Four families, four sounds, and he had named them all with his eyes shut.',
    'Then she played the same note on the flute and the trumpet, one after the other. Same note, she said, different color. That color is timbre, and it is how you know a friend\'s voice on the phone.',
    'The orchestra played its piece next, and Dana Reyes stopped them twice to point at the page. Here is mezzo forte, she said, a little loud, not all the way. Here is piano, soft. The students who had played it loud all the way through looked at their shoes and then played it again, better.',
    'Leo noticed something on the third time through. The beginning came back at the end, the same tune after a different middle. He drew three letters on his program, A, B, A, and showed his neighbor. The conductor saw it and held it up to the room. That, she said, is the form of the piece, and this third grader just drew it.',
    'Four families you can tell apart with your eyes shut, a color called timbre, signs for how loud and how fast, and a shape you can draw with letters. Leo went home with the program in his pocket and the letters on the front.',
  ],
};
STORIES['listening-with-a-purpose'] = {
  about: 'a mariachi concert in a San Antonio park, four musical events with the right words, and a fifth from a grandmother',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2925', after: 1, alt: 'A girl with her homework page open to four blank lines, ready to listen with a purpose' }, { serial: 'S2926', after: 2, alt: 'Trumpets lifting their bells as they take the tune from the violins, a girl writing timbre' }, { serial: 'S2927', after: 3, alt: 'Dancers spinning in front of the stage as the song breaks into a gallop' }, { serial: 'S2928', after: 4, alt: 'A grandmother humming beside a girl in a standing crowd at the end of the concert' }, { serial: 'S2929', after: 5, alt: 'A homework page with five musical events, each with its word, the last one added by hand' }],
  title: 'The night the mariachi played', art: 'S2924', cast: [],
  alt: 'A mariachi band of trumpets, violins, a guitar and a round-backed guitarrón on a park stage at night',
  words: [
    'The park in San Antonio held a free concert on the last Friday of every month. In October the band was a mariachi from the high school: trumpets, violins, a guitar and a big round-backed guitarrón.',
    'Sofia\'s music teacher had given the class homework for the night: listen with a purpose. Write down four musical events, she had said, and name each one with the right word.',
    'The first event came quickly. The violins carried the tune for a verse, and then the trumpets took it, bright and hard, and the whole sound changed color. A change of timbre, Sofia wrote. The second came when the chorus returned after the verse, the same tune again. A change of form.',
    'The third song began slowly and then broke into a gallop, and the dancers in front of the stage began to spin. A change of tempo. And in the last song the band dropped to almost nothing, one guitar and one voice, before everyone came back at once. A change of dynamics, from piano to forte in a single bar.',
    'Between songs Sofia noticed the manners of the crowd. Nobody clapped between the verses of a long song, phones stayed dark, and when the last chord rang, the whole park stood up. Her grandmother, standing beside her, hummed a song she had learned as a girl in Monterrey. Sofia wrote a fifth event that was not on the list: a folk song, passed from person to person.',
    'Four events, five with her grandmother\'s, each with the right word beside it. Listening with a purpose, Sofia found, did not make the music smaller. It made it hers.',
  ],
};
// Art and music appreciation, grades 6 to 8 (2026-09-29, pass GG): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['the-four-step-critique'] = {
  about: 'an eleven-word review, a sculpture of bicycle parts, and the three steps a critic had skipped',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2931', after: 1, alt: 'A white welded figure of bicycle parts with one arm raised and a red chain at the heart, on a low black base' }, { serial: 'S2932', after: 2, alt: 'A notebook page of arrows: a diagonal arm, one red spot, rust against white, a low base' }, { serial: 'S2933', after: 3, alt: 'A boy sketching a figure made of bicycle parts among students wheeling bicycles into a school hall' }, { serial: 'S2934', after: 4, alt: 'A boy writing the fourth step of a review at a desk, the sculpture visible through a doorway' }, { serial: 'S2935', after: 5, alt: 'A letter from a sculptor pinned to the school paper\'s board beside a four-hundred-word review' }],
  title: 'The review that skipped three steps', art: 'S2930', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding an eleven-word review in front of a tall white sculpture welded from bicycle parts',
  words: [
    'Dev\'s first review for the school paper was eleven words long. The new sculpture in the front hall is weird and I do not like it. The arts editor, who was also the art teacher, handed it back with one comment. You skipped three steps.',
    'Describe first, she said. Dev went and stood in front of the thing for ten minutes. A figure made of welded bicycle parts, taller than a person, one arm raised, painted white except for a red chain where the heart would be. Rusted in places. Set on a low black base near the doors.',
    'Analyze: how is it organized? The raised arm made a diagonal that pulled the eye up. The red chain was the only color, emphasis in one spot. The rust and the white were contrast. The base was low, so the figure loomed. Dev wrote it all down and noticed that none of it was weird. It was arranged.',
    'Interpret: what did the artist mean? A body built from bicycle parts, with a chain for a heart, in a school hall full of students who rode to school. The narrative, Dev decided, was motion: a figure made of the things that move us. The red chain was a symbol, the heart as a working part.',
    'Evaluate came last, and it came out differently than it had the first time. The sculpture succeeded at what it tried to do, Dev wrote, which was to make something still look like it was about to move. He still did not love it, and he said so, with his reasons: the rust felt like an accident rather than a choice.',
    'The review ran at four hundred words with all four steps in order. The sculptor read it, wrote to the paper, and said the rust was on purpose, and also that it was the first review of her work she had learned something from.',
  ],
};
STORIES['art-history-and-power'] = {
  about: 'a gray painting with no red in it, sixty small panels, and a building that believed knowledge deserved a temple',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2937', after: 1, alt: 'A docent gesturing at gray figures, a screaming horse and a bare light bulb in a wide print' }, { serial: 'S2938', after: 2, alt: 'A girl asking a question in front of a gray painting, a single red swatch held up beside it for contrast' }, { serial: 'S2939', after: 3, alt: 'Small bright flat panels of families with suitcases in crowded train stations, hung in a row' }, { serial: 'S2940', after: 4, alt: 'A museum building with columns and a dome, a class listening to a teacher on the steps' }, { serial: 'S2941', after: 5, alt: 'A girl writing one sentence in a notebook on a school bus' }],
  title: 'The wall that answered a war', art: 'S2936', cast: [],
  alt: 'A history class standing before a full-size print of a gray painting of screaming figures under a bare bulb',
  words: [
    'The field trip was to the university museum, and Amara\'s history teacher had made a deal with the art teacher. One hour, two rooms, and every student would come back able to say how a picture answers an event.',
    'The first room held a full-size print of Guernica. Picasso painted it in 1937, the docent said, after German planes bombed the town of Guernica during the Spanish Civil War. The class stood in front of it for a long time. Gray figures, a screaming horse, a bare light bulb where the sun should have been. Nobody in the painting was winning.',
    'Amara asked why there was no red. Because red would let you look away, the docent said. Gray keeps you inside it. That was the first time Amara understood that a color could be a decision about the viewer.',
    'The second room held panels from Jacob Lawrence\'s Migration Series, painted between 1940 and 1941. Sixty small pictures showed Black families leaving the American South for the North, trains and suitcases and crowded stations in flat bright shapes. A universal theme, the docent said, the passage of time and identity, and also a specific history, told by a painter whose own parents had made the trip.',
    'On the way out they passed the museum building itself, columns and a dome, and the art teacher stopped them. Buildings answer questions too, she said. This one says the city believed knowledge deserved a temple. Then she reminded them of the rules they had kept all hour. No touching, no flash, low voices, room for the person behind you.',
    'On the bus Amara wrote the sentence the teachers had asked for. A picture answers an event by choosing what you are allowed to look away from. She was not sure it was right, but it was hers, and it had the vocabulary.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-language-of-the-score'] = {
  about: 'a page that looked like a fence with birds on it, fractions in the birds, and two dots that double a section',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2943', after: 1, alt: 'Note values drawn as fractions: a whole note, two halves, four quarters, eight beamed eighths, with rests beside them' }, { serial: 'S2944', after: 2, alt: 'A line of music with piano, a crescendo hairpin, forte, dots for staccato and a curved legato line' }, { serial: 'S2945', after: 3, alt: 'A boy playing on alone past two dots and a double bar while the rest of the band turns back' }, { serial: 'S2946', after: 4, alt: 'A sketch of three blocks marked with letters over a page of music, a boy tapping the return' }, { serial: 'S2947', after: 5, alt: 'A boy reading a page of music like a map, trumpet under his arm' }],
  title: 'Twelve bars and a repeat sign', art: 'S2942', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with a trumpet staring at a page of band music that looks like a fence with birds on it',
  words: [
    'Mateo could play the trumpet by ear before he could read a note, and the first page of band music looked to him like a fence with birds on it. The band director gave him one rule for the first week: every mark on the page is an instruction, and none of them is decoration.',
    'The birds were note values, and they were fractions. A whole note held four beats in 4/4 time; a half note two; a quarter one; the eighth notes, joined at the top by a beam, half a beat each. The empty spaces had marks too, rests, silences measured as carefully as the sounds. Mateo, who liked math, could count a bar before he could play it.',
    'Then the words. Piano under the first line, soft. A long hairpin opening to the right, crescendo, growing louder, until forte at the top of the hill. Allegro in the corner meant quick, and ritardando in the last bar meant slow down, as if the music were arriving. Dots over the notes meant staccato, short and separated; a curved line over a group meant legato, smooth.',
    'At the end of the eighth bar stood two dots before a double bar line. Mateo played on, alone, into silence, while the band went back to the beginning. A repeat sign, the director said, without looking up. The cheapest instruction in music. Two dots, and you play it twice.',
    'By the third rehearsal Mateo could see the form. The tune of the first eight bars came back after a different middle section, so the piece was ternary, ABA, and he felt the return coming. He learned that rondo would have gone out and come home more than once, ABACA, and that theme and variations would have changed the tune each time it came.',
    'The fence with birds turned into a page he could read the way he read a map. Every mark was an instruction. He played the repeat both times, soft where it said soft, and short where it said short.',
  ],
};
STORIES['judging-a-performance'] = {
  about: 'a shadow judge, five criteria that never ask whether you liked it, and a winner nobody had cheered for',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2949', after: 1, alt: 'A judging sheet with five criteria printed down the left: intonation, rhythm, tone, balance, expression' }, { serial: 'S2950', after: 2, alt: 'A boy playing loud and fast on stage while a girl marks a sheet with a frown' }, { serial: 'S2951', after: 3, alt: 'A boy with a button accordion on stage, a judge playing a short recording on a phone for a girl' }, { serial: 'S2952', after: 4, alt: 'An audience sitting quiet and still with phones dark while a player bows on stage' }, { serial: 'S2953', after: 5, alt: 'A flute player receiving a ribbon, a shadow judge\'s sheet held up beside the real one' }],
  title: 'The judge with five questions', art: 'S2948', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl with a clipboard sitting beside a contest judge at a table in a school auditorium',
  words: [
    'The district solo contest needed a student shadow judge, someone to sit beside the real judge and fill in a sheet that would not count. Lena, who had played cello for four years, said yes before she knew what the sheet asked.',
    'It asked five things. Intonation: were the notes in tune? Rhythm: was the beat steady and the timing right? Tone: was the sound full and clear? Balance: could every part be heard, the soloist against the piano? Expression: did the player follow the dynamics and the tempo the composer wrote? Five criteria, the judge said, and nowhere on the sheet does it ask whether you liked it.',
    'The first player was loud and fast and got a burst of applause from his friends in the back, and Lena, honest, marked him down. The rhythm rushed. The forte was there but the piano was not; he had played a whisper as a shout. The judge, without looking, marked the same.',
    'The fourth player brought a button accordion and a piece of conjunto from the Texas border, and Lena had never judged anything like it. The judge told her to do what she had done for the cello: compare it to the best example of its kind she knew. Lena did not know one, so the judge played her thirty seconds on her phone, and then Lena could hear that the player\'s tone was clear and his rhythm exact.',
    'Between players Lena watched the room as much as the stage. The good listeners were quiet and still, phones dark, applause only at the end. She realized the sheet was teaching her to hear the way the etiquette was teaching the room to listen, and that both were the same lesson.',
    'The winner was a flute player nobody had cheered for, in tune to the last note, soft where the page said soft. Lena\'s sheet, which did not count, matched the judge\'s on four of five.',
  ],
};
// Art and music appreciation, grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-29, pass GH): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['reading-artwork-with-precision'] = {
  about: 'a painting of an empty chair, a critique that started with a preference, and the four steps that fixed it',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2955', after: 1, alt: 'A first draft of a critique with the words I don\'t like it circled in red' }, { serial: 'S2956', after: 2, alt: 'A boy writing four numbered steps beside a painting: describe, analyze, interpret, evaluate' }, { serial: 'S2957', after: 3, alt: 'A gallery owner reading a page while a boy waits, the painting of the chair between them' }, { serial: 'S2958', after: 4, alt: 'A wilting flower on the windowsill in the painting, a pencil pointing at it' }, { serial: 'S2959', after: 5, alt: 'A photocopied zine cover with a blank square where a painting would go, a permission form beside it' }],
  title: 'The chair by the window', art: 'S2954', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage boy with a notebook in front of a small painting of an empty chair beside a window in a local gallery',
  words: [
    'The assignment was one page on one work in a gallery within a mile of school. Malik chose the smallest painting in the smallest gallery: an empty chair beside a window, a flower on the sill. His first draft took ten minutes and began with the truth. I do not like it.',
    'His teacher circled the sentence in red and wrote one word beside it: preference. Then she wrote four more. Describe. Analyze. Interpret. Evaluate. Do these first, she said, and then tell me whether you like it, if it still matters.',
    'Describe was easy and took longer than he expected: a wooden chair, a window with gray light, a sill, a flower with two petals down, nothing else. Analyze was the principles: the chair off center, the light the only bright value, the flower the only curve in a room of straight lines. Emphasis, contrast, and a lot of empty space doing work.',
    'Interpret was where the painting opened up for him. The chair was its content, and the absence in it was its meaning. Somebody is missed, he wrote, was the message. And the flower with two petals down was a metaphor, one thing standing for another, a life partly gone. He had four properties, and he could say which was which.',
    'Evaluate, he wrote last, is not whether I would have painted it. It is whether it did what it set out to do. It set out to make a room feel empty, and it did, because I noticed I had stopped breathing. The gallery owner read the page when he brought it back and asked if she could keep a copy.',
    'She said one more thing when he asked to put a photo of the painting on the cover of his zine. The painter was alive, the work was hers, and it was not public domain. Ask her, the owner said, and she will probably say yes. The asking is the point.',
  ],
};
STORIES['styles-themes-and-cultures'] = {
  about: 'two portraits four hundred years apart, the same three questions, and a career fair that came after',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2961', after: 1, alt: 'A seventeenth-century portrait of a merchant in black with a white collar, lit from one window' }, { serial: 'S2962', after: 2, alt: 'A contemporary portrait of a young woman in a hooded sweatshirt against a patterned gold background' }, { serial: 'S2963', after: 3, alt: 'Two portraits side by side with the same three questions written on a card beneath them' }, { serial: 'S2964', after: 4, alt: 'A museum conservator at a table repairing a crack in a painting with a fine brush' }, { serial: 'S2965', after: 5, alt: 'A girl at a career fair table with two brochures, one for conservation and one for curating' }],
  title: 'Two portraits, one wall', art: 'S2960', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage girl between two portraits on a museum wall, one dark and formal, one bright and contemporary',
  words: [
    'The museum had hung them on one wall on purpose. One was a portrait from a seventeenth-century Dutch studio, a merchant in black with a white collar. The other was painted three years ago: a young woman in a hooded sweatshirt against a background of gold pattern. Ines\'s class was told to ask both the same three questions.',
    'Who is shown. A man who wanted to be seen as sober, wealthy and serious, and paid for it. A woman who wanted to be seen as herself, in her own clothes, and was painted by a friend. How is the eye led. In the old portrait, by one window\'s light on a face. In the new one, by the gold pattern that pressed forward until the face was the only quiet place.',
    'What is the artist saying about the sitter. That is where the four hundred years fell away, Ines wrote, because both painters were saying the same thing: this person matters, look. The difference was the trend, the black cloth against the gold pattern, the window against the flat bright ground. The shared question was the tradition.',
    'A style, the docent said, is a family resemblance among works, and you have just found a family that crosses four centuries. She pointed out what the new portrait carried that the old one could not: the sitter\'s own heritage in the pattern behind her, chosen from her grandmother\'s cloth. Identity and heritage are characteristics too.',
    'On the way out, the museum had set up a career fair in the lobby for the visiting classes. Ines stopped at two tables. A conservator showed her a crack in a painting under a lamp and the brush she was mending it with. A curator showed her the plan for the next room, which pictures would hang beside which, and why.',
    'Same gallery, she thought, two jobs, and neither of them painted. She took both brochures. A weekend painter, the curator said when Ines asked, is the third career, and it pays nothing and never stops.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-elements-of-music'] = {
  about: 'a triad counted out on a piano, a round that turned into polyphony, and the earplugs Theo did not want to wear',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2967', after: 1, alt: 'A hand counting four keys up from C on a piano, then three more, landing on E and G' }, { serial: 'S2968', after: 2, alt: 'A chord drawn as a root, a third and a fifth stacked on a staff, the intervals counted in half steps' }, { serial: 'S2969', after: 3, alt: 'A class singing a round in three groups, three overlapping lines drawn on the board' }, { serial: 'S2970', after: 4, alt: 'A sheet of music with expression markings circled: piano, forte, allegro and a crescendo hairpin' }, { serial: 'S2971', after: 5, alt: 'A boy at a loud show with foam earplugs in, the stage lights blazing' }],
  title: 'Four half steps and three', art: 'S2966', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage boy at a classroom piano counting keys with one finger, a chord chart on the music stand',
  words: [
    'Music Appreciation met in the room with the old upright piano, and on the first day the teacher asked Theo to build a chord without knowing what one was. Start on C, she said. Count four keys up, black and white both. Then three more.',
    'Four up from C landed on E, and three more landed on G. Play all three, she said, and he did, and the room had a C major triad in it. Now start on A, she said, and go three up, then four: A, C, E. That is a minor triad, and you can hear the difference before you can name it.',
    'A triad is a root, a third and a fifth, she said, and the only thing that separates major from minor is which interval comes first. Intervals are counted in half steps. Chords are stacks of intervals. The whole of harmony is arithmetic you can do on your fingers, and then a lifetime of learning what the arithmetic feels like.',
    'The next week was texture. The class sang a song alone, one line, monophony. With the piano playing chords beneath it, homophony. As a round, three groups starting at different times, the three lines crossed and overlapped and the room filled. Polyphony, she said, is several independent lines at once, and you are singing it.',
    'The week after that she handed out a page of a score and had them read the expression markings out loud. Piano, soft; forte, loud; allegro, quick; the little hairpin that meant crescendo. Rhythm, meter, melody, harmony, key, texture, form, dynamics, timbre. Nine elements, she said, and a tenth for the listener.',
    'The tenth was hearing protection, and Theo learned it at a show that Friday, when his friend handed him foam earplugs and he refused, and then took them. Loud music damages hearing slowly and permanently, the teacher had said. He could still hear the top of the guitar on Monday, and he had decided to keep it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['music-in-society'] = {
  about: 'a song built from a song, the permission it needed, the numbers behind streaming, and a performance judged by criteria',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2973', after: 1, alt: 'A letter asking permission to use a recording, signed by three students at the bottom' }, { serial: 'S2974', after: 2, alt: 'A chart of streaming plays next to a small pile of coins' }, { serial: 'S2975', after: 3, alt: 'A band listening back to a recording of themselves beside an exemplary recording, a checklist of criteria between them' }, { serial: 'S2976', after: 4, alt: 'An audience at a school showcase with phones dark, standing to applaud at the last chord' }, { serial: 'S2977', after: 5, alt: 'A credit line printed on a track listing beside a page of criteria with a teacher\'s note' }],
  title: 'The song built from a song', art: 'S2972', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage girl at a laptop with a waveform on the screen, an old record sleeve beside it',
  words: [
    'Priya\'s band built their best song from a bar of an old record her grandfather owned, a horn line from 1962, looped under everything they played. It sounded like a hit. The music teacher listened once and asked a question that stopped the room. Whose recording is that?',
    'Sampling is quotation with sound, he said, and a recording is property as well as art. Quoting a book takes a citation; quoting a record takes permission. The label that owned the 1962 recording still existed, and the band wrote to it, three signatures at the bottom. They waited three weeks for a yes that came with one condition: credit on the track.',
    'While they waited, the teacher showed them the numbers behind the music they streamed every day. A play pays a fraction of a cent. A song played a million times earns a few thousand dollars, split among everyone who made it. Technology made the music repeatable, he said, and economics decides who eats. Both are part of the subject.',
    'Before the spring showcase, the band recorded a rehearsal and listened to it beside a recording the teacher called an exemplary model, a band the field agreed was fine. They judged by criteria, not by mood: intonation, rhythm, tone, phrasing, dynamics, balance, and whether the performance served the song. The bass was late; the singer was flat on the bridge. Both were fixable, and both were fixed.',
    'On the night, Priya watched the audience as much as she played. Phones dark. Nobody clapped between the verses. When the last chord rang, the whole room stood. She understood concert etiquette from the other side of it: it was the audience listening the way they would want to be listened to.',
    'The credit line went on the track as promised. Priya wrote the review of her own band that week, with criteria and evidence, and the teacher gave it back with one note. This is the job, he wrote. Vocation or avocation, this is what it is.',
  ],
};
// Philosophy, grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-29, pass GI): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['what-is-an-argument'] = {
  about: 'a penguin that could not fly, a valid argument that was still wrong, and the two questions that sort them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2979', after: 1, alt: 'A girl pointing at the word penguins on a board while the coach keeps the argument up' }, { serial: 'S2980', after: 2, alt: 'A well-built house standing on sand, drawn beside the words valid and sound' }, { serial: 'S2981', after: 3, alt: 'A girl checking a school alarm panel whose light is not red' }, { serial: 'S2982', after: 4, alt: 'A girl saying a hidden premise out loud while her friend shakes his head and the coach smiles' }, { serial: 'S2983', after: 5, alt: 'A paper penguin taped inside a binder with two questions written beneath it' }],
  title: 'The penguin in the syllogism', art: 'S2978', cast: [],
  alt: 'A debate coach at a whiteboard with a three-line argument about penguins, fourteen hands raised',
  words: [
    'The first thing the debate coach wrote on the board was an argument with a penguin in it. All birds can fly, and penguins are birds, so penguins can fly. Then she asked the room what was wrong with it, and fourteen hands went up.',
    'It is false, said Maya, because penguins cannot fly. The coach nodded and did not erase it. The conclusion is false, she said, and the argument is still valid. If the premises were true, the conclusion would have to be. The form is perfect. The trouble is a premise.',
    'That was the split Maya had never seen: valid is about the shape, sound is about the shape plus the truth. A valid argument with a false premise is a well-built house on sand. From then on she asked every argument two questions in order. Does the form hold, and are the premises true?',
    'The coach gave the two forms names. If P then Q, and P, so Q: modus ponens. If P then Q, and not Q, so not P: modus tollens. Maya tried the second on the school alarm. If the alarm were on, the panel light would be red, and the light was not red, so the alarm was not on. She checked the panel, and it was not.',
    'The hardest exercise came last. Her friend Dev said a classmate who was late did not care about the club, and the coach asked Maya to find the missing premise. People who care are never late, Maya said slowly. Nobody believes that, said Dev. Then the argument does not work, said the coach, and everyone heard the hidden premise fall.',
    'Maya taped the penguin to the inside of her binder. Not because it could fly, but because it had taught her the two questions, and because most bad arguments, she now knew, fail exactly one of them.',
  ],
};
STORIES['the-usual-mistakes'] = {
  about: 'a two-hour meeting about a pool, nine fallacies on four pages, and the one argument that deserved an answer',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2985', after: 1, alt: 'Two doors labeled close the pool and raise taxes, with a third door standing open behind them' }, { serial: 'S2986', after: 2, alt: 'A man pointing at a committee chair while a boy writes ad hominem on his pad' }, { serial: 'S2987', after: 3, alt: 'A confident speaker with half the room nodding, a boy writing how many is everybody' }, { serial: 'S2988', after: 4, alt: 'A boy at the microphone restating the case for closing while the first speaker nods and hands over a page of numbers' }, { serial: 'S2989', after: 5, alt: 'A summer pass pinned to a pool gate, four pages of notes beside it' }],
  title: 'The third door', art: 'S2984', cast: [],
  alt: 'A crowded town meeting about a swimming pool, a teenage boy taking notes on a legal pad',
  words: [
    'The town meeting about the pool was supposed to take twenty minutes and took two hours. Jonah, who had come for extra credit, filled four pages with the mistakes he had learned that week.',
    'The first speaker said the choice was simple: close the pool or raise taxes on everyone. False dilemma, Jonah wrote, because a third door was standing open the whole time, a summer pass that paid for itself. The second speaker said the pool committee wanted to turn the park into a water slide for tourists. Nobody on the committee had ever said that. Straw man.',
    'Then it got personal. A man in the back said the committee chair could not be trusted because she had once been late paying a parking ticket. Ad hominem, Jonah wrote, since a parking ticket says nothing about a budget. A woman said that if the town paid for a lifeguard this year it would be paying for a stadium within five. Slippery slope, no steps shown.',
    'The most persuasive speaker of the night was also the emptiest. Everybody I know wants the pool closed, he said, and half the room nodded. Jonah wrote appeal to popularity, and beside it, how many people is everybody?',
    'When Jonah\'s turn came he did the thing his teacher had drilled all week. He restated the strongest version of the case for closing, the pool lost money three summers running, and said it back until the first speaker agreed that was the claim. Then he asked for the numbers, because the person who makes a claim carries the burden of proof. The numbers, it turned out, included a roof repair that had been paid off in June.',
    'The pool stayed open with a summer pass. Jonah\'s four pages earned the extra credit and something better: he had heard nine fallacies in one room and answered the one argument that deserved an answer.',
  ],
};
STORIES['what-can-we-know'] = {
  about: 'a horoscope that could not be wrong, a forecast that could, a stopped clock and a car that would not start',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2991', after: 1, alt: 'A clear sky at four o\'clock beside a forecast that promised rain by three' }, { serial: 'S2992', after: 2, alt: 'A stopped clock reading the right time, a judge looking at it with new eyes' }, { serial: 'S2993', after: 3, alt: 'A car with its hood up, a dead battery, and a cartoon thief crossed out beside it' }, { serial: 'S2994', after: 4, alt: 'A girl awake at night thinking about dreaming and the sun rising' }, { serial: 'S2995', after: 5, alt: 'A volcano with a first-place ribbon next to a board about doubt that took second' }],
  title: 'The horoscope and the forecast', art: 'S2990', cast: [],
  alt: 'A science fair board with a horoscope and a weather forecast pinned side by side, a girl beside it',
  words: [
    'Lena\'s science fair project began as a joke. She pinned a horoscope and a weather forecast side by side and asked visitors which one could be wrong.',
    'The horoscope said she would face a challenge this week, and everyone agreed it could not be wrong, because every week has a challenge. The forecast said rain by three, and at four the sky was clear. It was wrong, and that, Lena explained to a judge, was the point. In the 1930s Karl Popper argued that a real claim says in advance what would count against it. The forecast risked something. The horoscope risked nothing, so it told you nothing.',
    'Her second board was about knowing. A stopped clock reads the right time twice a day. Glance at it at one of those moments and you have a true belief with a good reason, and you still do not know the time. Edmund Gettier, 1963, three pages. The judge said he had never thought of a clock that way.',
    'The third board was a car that would not start. A dead battery needs one assumption. A thief who drained it and left needs three. William of Ockham, in the 1300s, said to prefer the explanation with fewer assumptions, and Lena had tested it: the battery was dead. The car was her father\'s.',
    'That night, unable to sleep, she thought about Descartes asking in 1641 how he could know he was not dreaming. She thought about Hume in 1748, saying that the sun rising every morning was a habit and not a proof. It did not frighten her. Doubt, she had written on her first board, is not the enemy of knowledge; it is the tool that makes it.',
    'The project took second place, behind a volcano. Lena did not mind. The volcano could not be wrong either.',
  ],
};
STORIES['how-should-we-live'] = {
  about: 'a trolley problem set up with the salt and pepper, three thinkers at one table, and a cake cut fairly',
  more: [{ serial: 'S2997', after: 1, alt: 'A boy pushing the pepper mill switch while five sugar cubes sit on one track and one on the other' }, { serial: 'S2998', after: 2, alt: 'A grandmother holding her hands still, refusing the switch, a small lie written on a napkin' }, { serial: 'S2999', after: 3, alt: 'A father asking a question, a line drawn from cowardice through courage to recklessness' }, { serial: 'S3000', after: 4, alt: 'A boy cutting a cake into four equal pieces while everyone else waits to choose first' }, { serial: 'S3001', after: 5, alt: 'A passed quiz on a fridge beside an evenly cut cake' }],
  title: 'The switch and the cake', art: 'S2996', cast: [],
  alt: 'A family at a dinner table with a salt shaker and a pepper mill set up as a trolley problem',
  words: [
    'The trolley problem came to dinner on a Tuesday, because Ana had a philosophy quiz on Wednesday and needed to argue with someone.',
    'She set it up with the salt and pepper. A runaway trolley, five people on the track, one on the side track, and a switch in your hand. Her brother Tomas pulled the switch at once. Five is more than one, he said. That is consequentialism, Ana said, and Mill wrote it down in 1861: the greatest happiness for the greatest number.',
    'Her grandmother would not pull it. If I pull the switch, I have chosen to kill that one man, she said. Ana recognized Kant, 1785: never treat a person merely as a means, and act only on a rule you could will everyone to follow. Then she tried Kant on a small lie. Could you will a world where everyone lies whenever it is convenient? In that world nobody believes anyone, so the lie would not even work.',
    'Her father, who had said nothing, asked what kind of person pulls the switch and what kind does not. Ana said that was the oldest question of the three, Aristotle in the fourth century BC. Courage sits between cowardice and recklessness. Honesty is a habit you build by practice. The question is not the rule but the person.',
    'Dessert settled the last argument. There was one cake and four people, and Tomas reached for the knife. Ana stopped him with Rawls, 1971: the divider chooses last. Behind that veil of ignorance he cut four equal pieces, because the smallest one might be his.',
    'Ana passed the quiz. Tomas, who had never read a page of philosophy, cut the cake evenly for the rest of the year.',
  ],
};
// Philosophy, grades 6 to 8 (2026-09-29, pass GJ): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['questions-that-open'] = {
  about: 'a mock trial over whether a hot dog is a sandwich, and what kind of question that turned out to be',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3003', after: 1, alt: 'A girl holding up a sandwich and a hot dog side by side while a boy points at a taco' }, { serial: 'S3004', after: 2, alt: 'Two words on a board, definition and counterexample, a girl rewriting her definition' }, { serial: 'S3005', after: 3, alt: 'A teacher pointing at three labels, fact, opinion and reasoned view, with the last one circled' }, { serial: 'S3006', after: 4, alt: 'A drawing of Socrates in Athens asking a question while a student writes it down' }, { serial: 'S3007', after: 5, alt: 'A jury of students shrugging, then a lunch line with hot dogs' }],
  title: 'The hot dog court', art: 'S3002', cast: [],
  alt: 'A classroom set up as a courtroom with a hot dog on the judge\'s desk and a teacher as judge',
  words: [
    'The trial of the hot dog began at nine on a Friday, in Room 12, with Miss Ito as judge and the whole class split into two sides. The question was simple and impossible: is a hot dog a sandwich?',
    'The prosecution went first. A sandwich, said Priya, is filling between two pieces of bread, and a hot dog is filling in one piece of bread with a fold. Not a sandwich. The defense objected at once. By that definition, said Marcus, a wrap is not a sandwich, and neither is a taco.',
    'Miss Ito stopped them and wrote two words on the board: definition and counterexample. A definition says what a word covers, she said. A counterexample is a case that breaks it. The honest move was not to shout but to fix the definition. Priya tried again: a sandwich is a filling held by bread you can eat with your hands. Marcus said that made a hot dog a sandwich, and Priya, after a pause, said it might.',
    'Then came the part she had waited for. She asked what kind of question they had been arguing about. It was not a fact, since nobody could look it up. It was not an opinion, since Priya and Marcus had both given reasons and both changed a little. It was a reasoned view, she said, and a reasoned view is the thing philosophers argue about.',
    'She told them about Socrates, who walked around Athens about 2,400 years ago asking people what they meant by bravery, justice and friendship. He was put to death in 399 BC, partly for asking too much. His student Plato wrote the conversations down. That is why we can still read them, she said, and why a hot dog can still start a trial.',
    'The jury could not agree, and Miss Ito ruled that the correct verdict. Some questions are for sharpening the mind, she said, not for winning, and the class went to lunch, where hot dogs were served.',
  ],
};
STORIES['reasons-and-evidence'] = {
  about: 'a petition with a hundred signatures and no reason, a cousin\'s story, a fair sample and the four-word question',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3009', after: 1, alt: 'A boy comparing one cousin\'s story with a stack of studies about sleep' }, { serial: 'S3010', after: 2, alt: 'A teacher circling the word hawk in red on a page and writing true and useless' }, { serial: 'S3011', after: 3, alt: 'A boy asking every fourth person in a lunch line while tallying answers on a clipboard' }, { serial: 'S3012', after: 4, alt: 'A petition with a page of reasons, a line about jobs and a bus plan, handed to a teacher' }, { serial: 'S3013', after: 5, alt: 'A school board table with a survey on it beside an old petition' }],
  title: 'How do you know?', art: 'S3008', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a petition with a hundred signatures while a teacher asks for a reason',
  words: [
    'Theo\'s petition to start school an hour later had one hundred and four signatures and, his teacher pointed out, not a single reason. Students want it, he said, and she told him that a hundred signatures were a headcount. Give me a reason, she said.',
    'His first try came from his cousin, who went to a school that started at nine and said it was great. One story, said Miss Vega, is an anecdote, true and thin. His second try was better: studies of teenagers find that later starts mean more sleep and better grades. That is evidence, she said, because somebody could check it.',
    'Then she made him run the relevance test. Theo had written that the school should start later because the building was cold in the morning. True, she said, and what does it have to do with the claim? He crossed it out. He had also written that the mascot was a hawk, as a joke, and she circled it in red and wrote true and useless.',
    'The survey was the hard part. Theo asked twenty friends and all twenty agreed, which felt like proof until Miss Vega asked whether twenty friends were a fair sample of six hundred students. He went back and asked every fourth person in the lunch line, a hundred and fifty in all. Sixty-one percent wanted the later start, and thirty-nine did not, mostly because of after-school jobs.',
    'That number changed the petition. Theo added a line about the jobs, because a good argument includes the reason against it, and a plan for the buses, because the principal would ask. When he handed it in, Miss Vega asked the question she had been asking all month. How do you know? This time he had a page.',
    'The school board did not move the start time that year. It did ask for the survey, which is more than a hundred and four signatures had ever done.',
  ],
};
STORIES['disagreeing-well'] = {
  about: 'a three-day argument about a hamster, the rule that ended it, and a boy who changed his mind',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3015', after: 1, alt: 'A girl saying so you are saying while a boy shakes his head, then nods' }, { serial: 'S3016', after: 2, alt: 'The word charity written on a board above two versions of the same argument' }, { serial: 'S3017', after: 3, alt: 'A girl separating a person from an idea: the boy on one side, the claim about quiet houses on the other' }, { serial: 'S3018', after: 4, alt: 'A boy changing his mind with a smile while the teacher holds up a book of Plato\'s dialogues' }, { serial: 'S3019', after: 5, alt: 'Three class rules on a poster beside a hamster cage going home in a girl\'s arms' }],
  title: 'So you are saying', art: 'S3014', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two students glaring across a hamster cage while a teacher writes one rule on the board',
  words: [
    'The argument about the class hamster had lasted three days when Miss Hale stopped it with a single rule. Nobody could reply until they had said the other person\'s view back, and the other person had agreed that was it.',
    'Lila went first. So you are saying, she said to Ben, that the hamster should go home with a different student every weekend, because everyone should get a turn. Ben said no, that was not it. He was saying it should go home with whoever had a quiet house, because the hamster was a living thing and not a prize. Lila tried again, and this time he said yes.',
    'It was the first time in three days that either of them had understood the other, and Miss Hale wrote the word charity on the board. Arguing against the version you made up, she said, is easy and useless. Arguing against the version they mean is hard and worth something.',
    'The second rule was harder for Lila, who liked Ben and had spent three days annoyed at him. Separate the person from the idea, Miss Hale said. You can think a claim is wrong and think the person is smart and kind at the same time. Lila said the idea about quiet houses left out the students who had loud houses through no fault of their own. That, she said, was the reason she disagreed, and it was the reason and not Ben.',
    'Ben thought about it for a while and said she was right, and that a rota with a quiet-house check might do both. Somebody said he had lost. Miss Hale said that changing your mind for a better reason was the whole point. Plato had written whole books of people doing it, in Athens, in the 300s BC, and they ended better thinkers than they started.',
    'The class wrote three rules that afternoon: one voice at a time, reasons not volume, and say it back before you answer. The hamster, as it happened, went home with Lila first.',
  ],
};
STORIES['fair-and-unfair'] = {
  about: 'six hundred dollars, three sections, three rules for fair, and a test older than any of them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3021', after: 1, alt: 'Drummers, flutes and trumpets each holding a sign: equal, by need, by work' }, { serial: 'S3022', after: 2, alt: 'A band director writing fair by which rule on a whiteboard' }, { serial: 'S3023', after: 3, alt: 'Trumpet players pointing at a car wash photo while flute players point at three empty chairs' }, { serial: 'S3024', after: 4, alt: 'A band voting behind a curtain marked veil, a sketch of Confucius on the wall' }, { serial: 'S3025', after: 5, alt: 'A student with a phone on the bus under an exception everyone understands, drummers cutting a cake' }],
  title: 'Fair by which rule', art: 'S3020', cast: [],
  alt: 'A band room with a jar of six hundred dollars on a table and three sections arguing',
  words: [
    'The band had raised six hundred dollars for the spring trip, and the fight over how to divide it started before the money was counted.',
    'Equal shares, said the drummers: sixty students, ten dollars each. By need, said the flutes: some students could not afford the trip at all, and the money should go to them first. By work, said the trumpets, who had run the car wash every Saturday while the drummers slept in. Each side was sure, and each side was right by one rule and wrong by the other two.',
    'That was the sentence the band director wrote on the board. Fair by which rule? Arguments about fairness, she said, are really arguments about which rule fits the case. The way to settle them is not to shout the rule louder but to say why it fits.',
    'The trumpets went first. Work should count, they said, because without the car wash there was no money to divide. The flutes answered that the trip was the point, and a trip that three students could not join was not the band\'s trip. The drummers, to their credit, admitted that sleeping in was not an argument.',
    'The director offered a test older than any of them. Confucius, in China about 2,500 years ago, said not to impose on others what you would not want for yourself. Then she offered a newer one: decide the rule before you know which section you are in. Behind that veil, the band voted for need first, then work, then equal shares of whatever was left, because any of them might have been the student who could not pay.',
    'The rule about no phones on the bus got its exception the same afternoon, for the student whose mother was in the hospital, and everyone could explain why. The cake, when it came to that, was cut by the drummers, who chose last.',
  ],
};
