STORIES['wondering-out-loud'] = {
  about: 'a robot vacuum named Bolt, a wonder question that took a week, and a crossing guard who was kind but not a friend',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3027', after: 1, alt: 'Two questions on a board, a spider and a robot, a boy looking up the spider in a book' }, { serial: 'S3028', after: 2, alt: 'A crossing guard waving kindly at children while a class goes quiet' }, { serial: 'S3029', after: 3, alt: 'A robot vacuum bumping into a sad child and a happy child alike, glitter on the floor' }, { serial: 'S3030', after: 4, alt: 'A teacher telling a class about Socrates in Athens, a scroll marked Plato on the desk' }, { serial: 'S3031', after: 5, alt: 'A class vote on a chart, helper nineteen and friend four, four children holding a because' }],
  title: 'Can a robot be your friend', art: 'S3026', cast: [],
  alt: 'A robot vacuum in a classroom with a name tag reading Bolt, a girl raising her hand',
  words: [
    'The robot vacuum arrived in Room 8 on a Monday, and by Tuesday the class had named it Bolt and Rosa had asked the question that took the rest of the week. Can Bolt be our friend?',
    'Miss Okafor did not answer, and instead she wrote two questions on the board, one above the other. How many legs does a spider have? Can a robot be your friend? The first one, she said, you can look up, and she let Diego look it up: eight. The second one nobody can look up, because it is a wonder question, and a wonder question you have to think through.',
    'So what is a friend, she asked, and the class tried. Someone who is kind to you, said Rosa. Miss Okafor asked whether the crossing guard was kind to them, and everyone said yes. Then she asked whether the crossing guard was their friend, and the room went quiet. Kind, it turned out, was not enough. A not-example had shown them what the meaning still needed.',
    'They tried again. A friend is someone who chooses you, said Diego. A friend is someone who knows when you are sad, said Rosa. Bolt, they agreed, was kind in its way, since it cleaned up the glitter, but it did not choose anyone, and it bumped into sad people exactly as often as happy ones.',
    'On Friday Miss Okafor told them about a man named Socrates, who had lived long ago in a city called Athens. He spent his days asking people what their words meant. Most of them had never checked. His student Plato wrote the questions down, and people still read them two thousand years later. You checked, she said. That is the whole job.',
    'The class voted that Bolt was a helper and not a friend, by nineteen to four. The four who voted friend had a because ready, which Miss Okafor said was the part that mattered.',
  ],
};
STORIES['because'] = {
  about: 'a boy who ran out of becauses, a cafeteria manager who counted trays, and a week that checked',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3033', after: 1, alt: 'A boy offering three becauses, a headcount, an order and a wish, each crossed out on a board' }, { serial: 'S3034', after: 2, alt: 'A cafeteria manager counting trays that came back full while a boy takes notes' }, { serial: 'S3035', after: 3, alt: 'Two reasons on a board pointing in two directions, a girl and a boy each standing by one' }, { serial: 'S3036', after: 4, alt: 'A tally of trays and minutes on a chart for one week of recess first' }, { serial: 'S3037', after: 5, alt: 'A tray count pinned on a wall beside the words how do you know' }],
  title: 'Recess before lunch', art: 'S3032', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy standing at a class meeting asking for recess before lunch, a teacher asking why',
  words: [
    'Sam wanted recess before lunch instead of after, and at the class meeting he said so, and Miss Okafor asked the question she always asked. Why?',
    'Because everybody wants it, said Sam, and Miss Okafor said that was a headcount and not a reason. Because I said so, said Sam, grinning, and she said that was an order. Because I want to, he tried, and she said that was a wish, and Sam ran out of becauses and sat down.',
    'The next day he came back with a real one. Because kids who run around first eat more of their lunch. Miss Okafor asked how he knew, and Sam had asked the cafeteria manager, who had counted the trays that came back full. On the days after gym, fewer trays came back full. That was a reason: it was about the thing, it was true, and somebody had checked.',
    'Ana disagreed, and she had a because too. Because kids come in sweaty and cannot settle down to eat. Miss Okafor wrote both on the board and asked whether either one was about the wrong thing. Neither was. They were two real reasons pointing two ways, which happens more often than anyone likes.',
    'So the class checked. For one week they had recess first, and the cafeteria manager counted trays and Miss Okafor counted minutes to settle. The trays came back emptier, and settling took four minutes instead of two. The class decided the emptier trays were worth four minutes.',
    'Recess moved before lunch in October. Sam kept the tray count on the wall, next to the words Miss Okafor had written when he first stood up: how do you know?',
  ],
};
STORIES['listening-to-disagree'] = {
  about: 'a class pet vote, a rule about saying it back, and a girl who changed her mind for a better because',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3039', after: 1, alt: 'A girl saying so you think to a boy, who shakes his head, then nods on the second try' }, { serial: 'S3040', after: 2, alt: 'Two ideas with two becauses on a board, a rabbit and a fish, and a class ready to pick' }, { serial: 'S3041', after: 3, alt: 'Two hands rising slowly at the question is anyone allergic to rabbits, a girl looking at them' }, { serial: 'S3042', after: 4, alt: 'A girl changing her mind while a teacher speaks about the best because' }, { serial: 'S3043', after: 5, alt: 'A fish in a tank named by a girl and fed by a boy, the say-it-back rule still on the board' }],
  title: 'The rabbit and the fish', art: 'S3038', cast: [],
  alt: 'A class pet vote with a rabbit and a fish drawn on the board and a teacher holding up one finger for a rule',
  words: [
    'The class pet vote was supposed to take five minutes and had already taken twenty when Miss Okafor stopped it with a rule. Nobody could speak until they had said the other person\'s idea back, and the other person had said that is it.',
    'Lena tried first. So you think we should get a fish, she said to Kofi, because fish are boring. No, said Kofi, that is not it. Lena tried again. So you think we should get a fish because nobody is allergic to a fish. Yes, said Kofi, that is it, and it was the first time all morning that anyone had heard him.',
    'Then it was Kofi\'s turn. So you think we should get a rabbit because rabbits are soft and you can hold them. That is it, said Lena. Now they had two ideas with two becauses, and neither one was about the wrong thing, and the class had to pick.',
    'Marcus, who had said nothing, asked the question that settled it. Is anyone allergic to rabbits? Two hands went up, slowly. Lena looked at the hands for a while and said that she had changed her mind, because a class pet that two people could not touch was not a class pet.',
    'Somebody said Lena had lost. Miss Okafor said that changing your mind for a better because was one of the bravest things a thinker could do. The loudest idea was never the best one, she said; the one with the best because was. Lena and Kofi were best friends again by lunch, which had never really been in doubt.',
    'The fish arrived on Friday. Lena named it, which everyone agreed was fair, and Kofi fed it, and the rule about saying it back stayed on the board for the rest of the year.',
  ],
};
STORIES['what-is-fair'] = {
  about: 'three boxes, a fence, a parade, a rule with a reason, and a brownie cut so evenly you could not tell the halves apart',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3045', after: 1, alt: 'Three boxes, one each, the smallest boy still seeing only fence' }, { serial: 'S3046', after: 2, alt: 'A girl giving her box to the smallest boy, who stands on two boxes as drums come around the corner' }, { serial: 'S3047', after: 3, alt: 'Two labels, equal and fair, drawn over the three children at the fence' }, { serial: 'S3048', after: 4, alt: 'A teacher running in a hall during a fire drill, and a boy cutting a brownie while another chooses' }, { serial: 'S3049', after: 5, alt: 'A girl walking home with no box and a big smile after the parade' }],
  title: 'Three boxes and a fence', art: 'S3044', cast: [],
  alt: 'Three children at a fence by a bakery, the smallest unable to see the parade',
  words: [
    'The parade came down Main Street every October, and every October the fence by the bakery was the best place to watch it, if you could see over it.',
    'Priya could see over it. Ben could see over it on tiptoe. Tomas, who was smallest, could see the fence. The bakery had three boxes, and Priya\'s first plan was one box each, which was equal, and Tomas stood on his box and saw a little more fence.',
    'Ben said the plan was fair because everyone got the same. Priya looked at Tomas, who was not complaining, and said that fair had to mean everyone could see the parade, or what was the point. She gave Tomas her box. Now Tomas had two, Ben had one, and Priya had none, and all three of them could see the drums come around the corner.',
    'That was the day Priya learned that fair is not always equal. Equal gives everyone the same thing. Fair gives everyone what they need for the same chance, and sometimes those are different. The way to tell is to look at the smallest person and ask whether it is working for them.',
    'The rest of the day kept teaching. At the fire drill after school, the rule was no running in the hall, and the teacher ran, and everyone understood the exception because everyone could hear the reason. And when there was one brownie left and two people who wanted it, Ben cut and Tomas chose, and Ben cut it so evenly that you could not tell the halves apart.',
    'Fair by need at the fence, a rule with a reason at the drill, and cut-and-choose at the bakery. Priya went home with no box and the best view on Main Street, which was the strangest fair of all.',
  ],
};
// Philosophy, K to 2 (2026-09-29, pass GL): four stories at the kindergarten length with five pictures each.
STORIES['i-wonder'] = {
  about: 'a stick that might be a toy, a brother who said no, and the test that found a hole every time',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3051', after: 1, alt: 'A girl holding up a stick to a boy who has just said a toy is a thing you play with' }, { serial: 'S3052', after: 2, alt: 'A dad with a thought bubble showing a blue sky and a question mark' }, { serial: 'S3053', after: 3, alt: 'Two children sitting in a big cardboard box that nobody bought as a toy' }, { serial: 'S3054', after: 4, alt: 'A drawing of Socrates asking a question with a boy laughing beside it' }, { serial: 'S3055', after: 5, alt: 'A stick held up between two children as a sword, both smiling' }],
  title: 'The stick', art: 'S3050', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding a long smooth stick like a sword while her brother shakes his head',
  words: [
    'Noor found a stick at the park. It was long and smooth and made a fine sword. Her brother Sami said a stick is not a toy. Toys come from a store.',
    'Noor asked him what he meant by toy. Sami thought hard and said a toy is a thing you play with. Noor held up the stick and said she was playing with it right now.',
    'That is a wondering question, said their dad. Why the sky is blue is a finding question. You can look it up. What a toy is, you have to think about.',
    'Sami tried again. A toy is a thing you play with that came from a store, he said. But what about the box, said Noor. They played in a box all summer. Nobody bought it as a toy.',
    'Sami laughed. But what about was a good test. Every time he said what a toy was, Noor found a hole. Long ago, their dad said, a man named Socrates asked questions all day. His friend Plato wrote them down.',
    'Some questions you find out. Some questions you think about. Both are good questions, and a stick can be a sword either way.',
  ],
};
STORIES['because-k'] = {
  about: 'a blue shirt that was not a reason, a wish that was not one either, and a class at the window',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3057', after: 1, alt: 'A class laughing with a boy in a blue shirt who is laughing too' }, { serial: 'S3058', after: 2, alt: 'A boy giving a real reason, the sun out the window, children ready to run' }, { serial: 'S3059', after: 3, alt: 'A girl saying because I want to, a teacher drawing a wish beside the word reason' }, { serial: 'S3060', after: 4, alt: 'A whole class at a window looking at a clear blue sky' }, { serial: 'S3061', after: 5, alt: 'A boy holding a card that says because, pointing out the window to look' }],
  title: 'The blue shirt reason', art: 'S3056', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at circle time pointing at his blue shirt while a teacher asks why',
  words: [
    'Omar wanted to go outside, and he said so at circle time. Why should we go out, asked Miss Ruth. Because my shirt is blue, said Omar.',
    'Everyone laughed, and Omar laughed too, because his shirt really was blue. But that was not a reason, since it was not about going outside at all.',
    'Try again, said Miss Ruth. Omar thought. Because the sun is out, he said, and we can run. That is a reason, she said. It is about going outside, and it is true.',
    'Then Lila said we should go out because she wanted to. Miss Ruth smiled and said that wanting is a wish, not a reason. A reason has to be about the thing.',
    'Omar had one more reason, and he said it would not rain. How do you know, asked Miss Ruth. Omar did not know, so the whole class walked to the window and looked out at the day. The sky was clear and blue, like a shirt.',
    'A reason is what comes after because, and it has to be about the thing. When you do not know, go and look.',
  ],
};
STORIES['my-turn-your-turn'] = {
  about: 'a stick painted with stars, a fish and a rabbit, and a girl who changed her mind',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3063', after: 1, alt: 'A boy holding the stick saying fish while a girl blurts out rabbit before her turn' }, { serial: 'S3064', after: 2, alt: 'A girl saying a boy\'s idea back to him, the boy nodding yes' }, { serial: 'S3065', after: 3, alt: 'A boy saying he does not agree, a shy rabbit in a picture beside him' }, { serial: 'S3066', after: 4, alt: 'A girl changing her mind with a smile, a fish in a bowl on the desk' }, { serial: 'S3067', after: 5, alt: 'Children passing a star-painted stick around a circle, one voice at a time' }],
  title: 'The talking stick', art: 'S3062', cast: [],
  alt: 'A classroom circle with a talking stick painted with stars held up in the middle',
  words: [
    'The class had a talking stick painted with stars. Whoever held the stick could talk, and everyone else had to listen with their ears.',
    'The question that day was what pet to get. Ben held the stick and said a fish, because a fish is quiet. Ava said a rabbit before the stick got to her, and Ben looked sad.',
    'Miss Ruth asked Ava to say Ben\'s idea back first. You think we should get a fish, because a fish is quiet, said Ava. Yes, said Ben, that is it. Then Ava got the stick and said a rabbit is soft to hold.',
    'Ben did not agree, but he did not say Ava was silly. He said he did not agree, because a rabbit can be shy. Ava thought about that. It was a good reason.',
    'When the stick came around again, Ava said she had changed her mind. A fish was better for a class, she said, and nobody said she lost. Miss Ruth said it was brave, and the class got a fish.',
    'One voice at a time. Say it back before you answer. And if a friend has a better reason, you can change your mind.',
  ],
};
STORIES['fair-shares'] = {
  about: 'four cookies, one ice pack, a swing, and a cake cut by the one who picks last',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3069', after: 1, alt: 'A grandma watching a boy give one cookie back so each child has two' }, { serial: 'S3070', after: 2, alt: 'A boy with an ice pack on his knee while a girl nods that he needs it' }, { serial: 'S3071', after: 3, alt: 'Two children taking turns on a swing, ten pushes each' }, { serial: 'S3072', after: 4, alt: 'A girl cutting a cake into two even pieces while a boy waits to pick first' }, { serial: 'S3073', after: 5, alt: 'A plate with two cookies each, a swing, and a cake cut in half' }],
  title: 'Four cookies, two kids', art: 'S3068', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two children at a table with four cookies on one plate, one child holding three',
  words: [
    'Mia and her cousin Leo had four cookies on one plate. Leo took three, and Mia said that was not fair, because two each is fair.',
    'Their grandma agreed. Equal shares, she said, the same for everyone. Leo gave one cookie back. Then he ate his two very slowly, to make them last.',
    'Later Leo fell off the swing and hurt his knee. Grandma had one ice pack, and she gave it to Leo. Mia did not say that was unfair. Leo needed it, and she did not. That is fair too, by need.',
    'On the swing they took turns. Every turn was the same size, ten pushes each. A turn is a fair rule for a swing, Grandma said. Would you like it if it were you, is the test.',
    'At snack time there was one cake left. Grandma gave Mia the knife and said the cutter picks last. Mia cut the cake with great care into two even pieces. Leo picked first, and it did not matter which piece he took.',
    'Equal shares are fair. More for the one who needs it is fair too. And when you cut the cake, the cutter picks last.',
  ],
};
// Psychology, grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-29, pass GM): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['how-psychologists-know'] = {
  about: 'a survey that found what she believed, an experiment that did not, and the three averages that told the rest',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3075', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher reading a one-line conclusion and asking which came first, the music or the grades' }, { serial: 'S3076', after: 2, alt: 'A diagram of two things moving together with a third thing, a quiet house, pushing both' }, { serial: 'S3077', after: 3, alt: 'Twenty volunteers split by a coin flip, one group with headphones and one in silence, taking the same test' }, { serial: 'S3078', after: 4, alt: 'Two score charts with the same mean, one bunched tight and one spread wide' }, { serial: 'S3079', after: 5, alt: 'A report with a proud line and a teacher\'s note in the margin' }],
  title: 'The two classes with the same mean', art: 'S3074', cast: [],
  alt: 'A student with a survey of forty classmates and a claim written in one line on a whiteboard',
  words: [
    'Nadia\'s first psychology assignment was to prove something, and she picked a claim she already believed: students who listen to music while they study get better grades.',
    'She surveyed forty classmates. The music listeners averaged 84 on the last quiz and the silent studiers averaged 79, and she wrote her conclusion in one line. Miss Ortiz read it and asked a question she had not expected. Which came first, the music or the grades?',
    'She could not say. Maybe the students who liked music were the ones who studied longer. Maybe they were the ones with quieter houses. A survey, Miss Ortiz said, finds two things that move together. It cannot find which one moves the other, and it cannot rule out a third thing moving both.',
    'So Nadia ran an experiment. Twenty volunteers signed a consent form that said what would happen and that they could stop at any time. She split them by coin flip, gave both groups the same passage to learn, played music for one group and silence for the other, and tested both. The music group scored a little lower. The claim she had believed was not what the experiment found.',
    'The numbers taught her one more thing. Both groups had a mean near 80, but one group\'s scores were bunched between 75 and 85 and the other\'s ran from 55 to 100. Same mean, different spread. The median told her the middle student; the mode told her the most common score; the range told her how far apart the ends were. One number, she wrote, is never the whole story.',
    'Her final report had a line she was proud of. My survey showed correlation, my experiment tested cause, and I was wrong about my own habit. Miss Ortiz wrote in the margin that this was psychology, and that being wrong in public with data was the whole discipline.',
  ],
};
STORIES['brain-senses-and-growing-up'] = {
  about: 'a tall glass, a barking dog, a cold pool that turned warm, and two people in two of the eight stages',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3081', after: 1, alt: 'A child watching juice poured from the tall glass into the short one, still pointing at the tall glass' }, { serial: 'S3082', after: 2, alt: 'A boy and a girl jumping at a dog barking through a fence, a heart drawn hammering' }, { serial: 'S3083', after: 3, alt: 'A girl in a pool shrieking at the cold, then floating and calling it warm' }, { serial: 'S3084', after: 4, alt: 'A teenager writing in a babysitting log with Erikson\'s stages drawn as a line from infancy to old age' }, { serial: 'S3085', after: 5, alt: 'One sentence written in a family log book' }],
  title: 'The tall glass', art: 'S3080', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenager pouring juice into a tall thin glass and a short wide glass while a five-year-old reaches for the tall one',
  words: [
    'Kofi, sixteen and studying psychology, was babysitting his cousin Ada, who was five, when the textbook walked into the kitchen. He poured the same juice into a tall thin glass and a short wide one, and Ada wanted the tall one, because it had more.',
    'It did not. He poured the tall one into the short one to show her, and she watched the juice come exactly to the line and still wanted the tall one. Piaget, Kofi remembered, had seen this in the 1920s: a child under about seven cannot yet conserve. The amount had not changed, but her thinking said it had.',
    'The dog next door barked and both of them jumped. Kofi felt his heart hammer for a full minute after he knew it was only a dog. That was adrenaline, the fast hormone, and the slower one, cortisol, kept him edgy through the next cartoon. Nerves carried the alarm in a blink; the glands carried it for an hour.',
    'At the pool that afternoon Ada shrieked at the cold and then, five minutes later, said it was warm. The water was the same. Her senses had adapted, the way a smell fades in a room, and Kofi told her the pool had not changed, only her skin\'s report of it. She said he was being weird, which was fair.',
    'That night, filling in the babysitting log, he thought about Erikson\'s stages. Ada was in the one about initiative, trying everything. He was in the one about identity, sixteen and asked at every dinner what he was going to do. Same house, two tasks, thirty years apart. Erikson had drawn the whole line in 1950, from trust in infancy to integrity at the end.',
    'He wrote one sentence in the log that his aunt kept. Ada is exactly where the book says a five-year-old is, and so, unfortunately, am I.',
  ],
};
STORIES['learning-memory-and-thinking'] = {
  about: 'a dog that howled at the bus, a brother who copied a stick, forty Spanish words on a curve, and a jar',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3087', after: 1, alt: 'A diagram of bus brakes leading to a treat leading to a howl, drawn like Pavlov\'s bell' }, { serial: 'S3088', after: 2, alt: 'A mother rewarding a sitting dog while ignoring a pestering boy, a pigeon drawn in the corner' }, { serial: 'S3089', after: 3, alt: 'A boy swinging a stick at a fence post an hour after watching his cousin do it' }, { serial: 'S3090', after: 4, alt: 'A forgetting curve on graph paper with forty Spanish words, review marks bending it flat' }, { serial: 'S3091', after: 5, alt: 'A girl opening a jar with the rubber band from her wrist, laughing' }],
  title: 'The bell and the bus', art: 'S3086', cast: [],
  alt: 'A dog howling at a window as a yellow school bus brakes outside at 3:40',
  words: [
    'Rosa\'s dog began to howl every afternoon at 3:40, and it took her a week of psychology class to see why. The school bus braked outside at 3:40, and the bus had always come just before Rosa, and Rosa had always come with a treat.',
    'The brakes had become Pavlov\'s bell. The dog was not howling at the bus; it was howling at the signal that had always come before the treat. Classical conditioning, the textbook called it, and the textbook was in her house every afternoon.',
    'Operant conditioning was easier to spot once she knew the word. Her mother had taught the dog to sit by rewarding sits, and had taught Rosa\'s little brother to leave the dog alone by ignoring him when he pestered it. Behavior followed by a reward grows; behavior followed by nothing fades. Skinner had shown it with pigeons in the 1930s and her mother had shown it with a dog and a boy.',
    'Then there was the doll. Her brother had watched a cousin swing a stick at a fence post and swung one at the same post an hour later. Bandura, 1961: children who watch, do. Rosa put the stick on a high shelf, which she had learned from nobody.',
    'Her own problem was Spanish vocabulary. She had learned forty words on Sunday and remembered nine by Wednesday, which was not stupidity but a curve Ebbinghaus had drawn in 1885. She tried the fix the curve suggests: ten words a day, each reviewed the next day, then in three days, then in a week. By the test she had all forty, and they stayed.',
    'The last lesson was a jar she could not open. She hunted for the jar opener for five minutes before she used the rubber band on her wrist, and laughed, because functional fixedness has a name and she had just been it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['people-among-people'] = {
  about: 'eleven people who did not move, one sentence that made them, and a cashier who was not rude',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3093', after: 1, alt: 'A teenager pointing at a woman in a blue coat and a man with a backpack, giving each one job' }, { serial: 'S3094', after: 2, alt: 'A boy walking off his stress beside a station, a stress curve drawn above him' }, { serial: 'S3095', after: 3, alt: 'A cashier telling the next customer about her towed car while a boy looks embarrassed' }, { serial: 'S3096', after: 4, alt: 'Eleven still figures on a platform and one figure speaking, breaking the stillness' }, { serial: 'S3097', after: 5, alt: 'A page with a last line read aloud, a teacher holding it up to a class' }],
  title: 'The one in the blue coat', art: 'S3092', cast: [],
  alt: 'A man fallen on a bus station platform with eleven people looking and nobody moving',
  words: [
    'A man fell on the platform at the downtown bus station on a Tuesday. Marcus counted, afterward, that eleven people had looked and nobody had moved, including him, for what felt like a minute.',
    'What broke it was a sentence he had read the week before. Darley and Latané, 1968: the more bystanders, the less likely any one of them acts, because each assumes another will. The cure was to make the responsibility one person\'s. Marcus pointed at a woman in a blue coat and said, you, call 911, and to a man with a backpack, you, help me sit him up. Both did, at once.',
    'The man had fainted from the heat and was fine. Marcus was not; his hands shook for an hour, which his textbook called the stress response doing its job and then failing to switch off. He walked it off, which the same chapter listed among the things that work.',
    'At the café afterward the cashier was short with him, and he decided she was rude. Then he heard her tell the next customer that her car had been towed that morning. He had explained her by her character and himself by his situation, the fundamental attribution error, and he had done it within a second.',
    'He thought about the others on the platform. Asch had shown in 1951 that people will deny what they plainly see to match a group. Milgram had shown in 1961 that most people will follow an authority further than they believe. Eleven people had matched each other\'s stillness, and it had taken one voice, not a hero, to break it.',
    'He wrote it up for class as the assignment asked, and the last line was the one his teacher read aloud. The crowd was not bad. The crowd was a crowd, and the only defense I have found is to decide, before the moment comes, that I am the one who moves.',
  ],
};
// Psychology, grades 6 to 8 (2026-09-29, pass GN): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['the-brain-you-are-growing'] = {
  about: 'a plan to stay up all night, a sister with a map of the brain, and a front part under construction',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3099', after: 1, alt: 'A filing cabinet inside a head, a stack of papers left on the desk with the office locked' }, { serial: 'S3100', after: 2, alt: 'A drawn map of the brain with the front part in dotted lines marked under construction' }, { serial: 'S3101', after: 3, alt: 'A boy at a birthday party on a dare, the alarm and reward parts of a brain lit and the brakes dim' }, { serial: 'S3102', after: 4, alt: 'Two test papers, an 88 after nine hours of sleep and a 74 after a night of studying' }, { serial: 'S3103', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook cover with a brain map and three notes: under construction, decide early, sleep' }],
  title: 'The night before the test', art: 'S3098', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with a plan to stay up all night while his older sister taps the side of her head',
  words: [
    'Amir had a plan for the science test and the plan was to stay up. His sister Leila, who was fourteen and insufferable about brains, told him he had it backwards, and then, worse, explained why.',
    'Your hippocampus files the day while you sleep, she said, tapping the side of her head as if it were a cabinet. Study until one in the morning and you hand it a stack of papers and then lock the office. Amir said that was not how offices worked. Leila said it was exactly how his worked.',
    'She drew him the map from her class. The brainstem, breathing and heartbeat. The cerebellum, balance, the part that lets you ride a bike without thinking. The amygdala, the alarm. The hippocampus, the filing clerk. And the front of the cortex, the part that plans and stops, which she said was still under construction in both of them and would be until their mid-twenties.',
    'That was the part that interested Amir, because it explained his cousin\'s birthday party, where he had done something on a dare that he would never have done alone at a desk. The alarm and the reward parts are done, Leila said. The brakes are being built. So you decide the big things ahead of time, when nobody is cheering.',
    'He tested her claim the only way that seemed fair, by studying until nine, sleeping nine hours, and taking the test. His friend Devin studied until one and came in gray. Amir scored an 88 and Devin a 74. That proved nothing by itself, Leila said, since two people are not an experiment, but the studies with hundreds of people found the same thing.',
    'Amir wrote the map of the brain on the inside of his notebook, with the front part drawn in dotted lines and a note beside it. Under construction. Decide early. Sleep.',
  ],
};
STORIES['attention-memory-and-practice'] = {
  about: 'a chapter reread four times, a blank card, a phone moved to the hall, and an hour cut into four',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3105', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher handing a girl a blank card beside a closed chapter' }, { serial: 'S3106', after: 2, alt: 'A page with a volcano picture and a girl\'s card with holes where the facts should be' }, { serial: 'S3107', after: 3, alt: 'A phone lying face down on a desk with a thin thread of attention still running to it' }, { serial: 'S3108', after: 4, alt: 'A forgetting curve dropping like a cliff and flattening with four small review marks' }, { serial: 'S3109', after: 5, alt: 'A chapter resting on a pillow at bedtime and a quiz marked 94' }],
  title: 'The closed book', art: 'S3104', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl with a chapter reread four times and a quiz marked with a low score',
  words: [
    'Priya had reread the chapter four times and could have told you what the pictures looked like on every page, which is why the quiz was such a surprise.',
    'Her teacher, Miss Okafor, had seen it before. Rereading feels like learning because the page looks familiar, she said, and familiar is not the same as known. She handed her the chapter and a blank card. Read a page, close the book, write what you can, then check. That is called testing yourself. In 2006 two researchers found it beat rereading by a wide margin a week later.',
    'The first page was humbling. She remembered the picture of the volcano and almost nothing beside it. But the second page went better, and by the fourth she could see where the holes were. Rereading had never shown her that, because the page fills its own holes as you look at it.',
    'The phone was the next thing. She kept it face down on the desk while she worked, which she thought was discipline. Miss Okafor said that a phone on the desk, even face down, keeps part of your attention, and attention is the doorway to memory. Priya moved it to the hall. The page got easier, which annoyed her.',
    'The last fix was time. She had been doing an hour on Sunday and calling it done. Miss Okafor drew the forgetting curve, the one Ebbinghaus drew in 1885, dropping like a cliff and flattening a little with each review. She asked for fifteen minutes a day instead. Same hour, four pieces. The curve, she said, does its worst work in the gaps you skip.',
    'Priya put the chapter on her pillow so the cue would be waiting at bedtime, and the next quiz was not a surprise at all. It was a 94, and she had read the chapter once.',
  ],
};
STORIES['feelings-are-signals'] = {
  about: 'one line in a play, a heart doing ninety, a smoke detector with the wrong information, and a solo that came back',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3111', after: 1, alt: 'A boy saying I am nervous with the alarm in a drawn brain dimming as he says it' }, { serial: 'S3112', after: 2, alt: 'A smoke detector drawn over a stage with no fire, a boy counting four in and six out' }, { serial: 'S3113', after: 3, alt: 'A girl saying she is fine with a bottle drawn tight around her, then laughing as she says the true sentence' }, { serial: 'S3114', after: 4, alt: 'A girl standing in a stage light for three long seconds before finding her next line' }, { serial: 'S3115', after: 5, alt: 'A card with three lines: name it, breathe, tell someone who has stood in the light' }],
  title: 'The smoke detector', art: 'S3110', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy backstage with a racing heart while a drama teacher asks what he is feeling',
  words: [
    'Diego had one line in the school play and a heart that was doing ninety. The drama teacher, who had seen a hundred first nights, asked him what he was feeling instead of telling him to relax.',
    'Scared, he said, and then, because she waited, nervous, and something under that, angry at himself for being nervous. Naming it did something. His heart came down a little, and she told him why. In 2007 a researcher named Lieberman found that putting a feeling into words quieted the alarm part of the brain. I am nervous is a sentence that helps, she said. Relax is not.',
    'She called the feeling a smoke detector. Fear is the detector saying something might hurt you, and a stage is not going to hurt you, so the detector is doing its job with the wrong information. You do not smash a smoke detector, she said. You breathe until it sees there is no fire. Four counts in, six counts out.',
    'His friend Nadia, two lines and a solo, had a different method, which was to say she was fine. She was not fine. Bottling it, the teacher said quietly, is the strategy that works right up until it does not, and she asked Nadia to say the true sentence too. Nadia said she was terrified, and then, to her own surprise, laughed.',
    'Diego said his line perfectly. Nadia got halfway through her solo, lost the words, and stood in the light for three seconds that lasted a year. Then she found the next line and finished, and the applause was louder for her than for anyone. Bouncing back is called resilience, the teacher told them afterward, and you build it exactly the way Nadia just did.',
    'Diego kept the rule for tests and tryouts and one terrible phone call. Name it. Breathe. And if the detector will not quiet, tell someone who has stood in the light before.',
  ],
};
STORIES['other-people'] = {
  about: 'a new kid who ate alone, a dropped tray, a name said out loud, and a table that went quiet',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3117', after: 1, alt: 'A girl pointing at a boy in a red jacket and sending him for napkins while she kneels at the tray' }, { serial: 'S3118', after: 2, alt: 'A boy translating in his head before each sentence, a map of Honduras behind him' }, { serial: 'S3119', after: 3, alt: 'A lunch table gone quiet as a girl waves a new student over, a line of matching answers drawn behind them' }, { serial: 'S3120', after: 4, alt: 'A boy admitting he waited to see if anyone else would help, a tray in the background' }, { serial: 'S3121', after: 5, alt: 'A girl saying the shortest thing she knows, somebody is nobody, say a name' }],
  title: 'The kid in the red jacket', art: 'S3116', cast: [],
  alt: 'A dropped lunch tray in a silent cafeteria with a new student standing over it and nobody moving',
  words: [
    'The new kid ate alone for a week and Sofia\'s table had decided, without anyone saying it, that he was stuck up, because he did not talk. On Friday he dropped his tray in the middle of the cafeteria and the whole room went quiet and nobody moved.',
    'Sofia knew the study, because Miss Okafor had made them act it out. Darley and Latané, 1968: the more people who see it, the fewer who help, because each is waiting for another. So she did the thing the study said and pointed at Marcus in the red jacket. Marcus, get napkins, she said, and he went, and she went to the tray.',
    'The new kid\'s name was Tomas, and he did not talk, it turned out, because he had moved from Honduras in August and was still translating in his head before every sentence. Stuck up had been Sofia\'s table explaining him by his character. The truth was his situation. Her teacher had a name for that mistake, and Sofia had made it for a week.',
    'The table pushed back on Monday when she asked Tomas to sit with them. Nobody said no; they went quiet in a way that meant no, and Sofia felt the pull of it. It was the pull Asch had measured in 1951, when a third of his people agreed with a wrong answer to match the room. She had decided on the way to school what she would do, which is the only protection the course offered, and it held.',
    'Tomas sat. By Wednesday he was talking, slowly, and by Friday he was funny. Marcus admitted he had wanted to help with the tray and had waited to see if anyone else would. Everyone had. That was the point, Sofia said, and it was not a criticism, because she had also waited, for about two seconds, before the study kicked in.',
    'Miss Okafor asked the class what they had learned that month, and Sofia said the shortest thing she knew. Somebody is nobody. Say a name.',
  ],
};
// Psychology, grades 3 to 5 (2026-09-29, pass GO): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['your-brain-and-senses'] = {
  about: 'a monster that was a coat, two lines the same length, a path across a field, and six hours of sleep',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3123', after: 1, alt: 'A coat on a hook and a boy\'s brain drawn filling the shape in with a monster' }, { serial: 'S3124', after: 2, alt: 'Two lines of the same length with arrowheads pointing in and out, a ruler laid across them' }, { serial: 'S3125', after: 3, alt: 'A boy at a piano with a path drawn across a field getting wider with every day of practice' }, { serial: 'S3126', after: 4, alt: 'A tired boy at lunch with a lost water bottle, a forgotten folder and a friend he snapped at' }, { serial: 'S3127', after: 5, alt: 'A boy asleep by nine with a coat on a hook that is only a coat' }],
  title: 'The monster that was a coat', art: 'S3122', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy frozen in bed staring at a tall dark shape in the corner of his room at night',
  words: [
    'The monster stood in the corner of Omar\'s room at eleven at night, tall and still with one arm raised, and Omar did not move for a full minute. Then his mother opened the door, and the light showed a coat on a hook.',
    'It had been a coat all along. His eyes had reported a tall dark shape, and his brain, which does not like gaps, had filled the shape in with the scariest thing it knew. His teacher, Miss Adeyemi, said the next day that was the brain doing its job. The senses are reporters, she said. The brain decides what the reports mean, and in the dark it guesses.',
    'She proved it with a drawing. Two lines the same length, one with arrowheads pointing in and one pointing out, and every child in the room was sure the second was longer. She measured them with a ruler. Same. The class laughed, because a brain fooled by two lines could certainly be fooled by a coat.',
    'The second lesson was about Omar\'s own brain, which was eight years old and, Miss Adeyemi said, not finished. It would keep growing and changing until he was about twenty-five. Every time he practiced his piano scales, it built a stronger path for them, the way feet make a path across a field.',
    'The third lesson came from Omar himself. He had been awake until eleven because of the monster and had slept six hours. By lunch he had lost his water bottle, snapped at his friend and forgotten his reading folder. A tired brain forgets, snaps and makes mistakes, Miss Adeyemi said. It is not a bad brain. It is a brain that needs about ten hours, and got six.',
    'That night Omar hung the coat in the closet and shut the door. Then he hung it back on the hook, to see whether his brain would fall for it twice. It did not. He was asleep by nine, and in the morning the coat was only a coat.',
  ],
};
STORIES['how-remembering-works'] = {
  about: 'nine spelling words, an hour that slid away, and a grandfather who knew four hundred addresses',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3129', after: 1, alt: 'A grandfather in a mail carrier\'s cap tapping his head, four hundred addresses drawn as tiny houses' }, { serial: 'S3130', after: 2, alt: 'A line drawn like a slide on paper, words sliding off it by Wednesday' }, { serial: 'S3131', after: 3, alt: 'A girl with the book closed, saying a word out loud and checking it' }, { serial: 'S3132', after: 4, alt: 'A girl saying nine words to a dog on Thursday, a calendar of three words a day behind her' }, { serial: 'S3133', after: 5, alt: 'A phone number written as three chunks on a girl\'s hand' }],
  title: 'Nine words, five days', art: 'S3128', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a couch with a tablet playing and a list of nine spelling words she is not looking at',
  words: [
    'Priya studied her nine spelling words for an hour on Thursday night and got four right on Friday, and she decided, on the walk home, that she had a bad memory.',
    'Her grandfather did not agree. He had been a mail carrier for thirty years and knew four hundred addresses by heart, and he said nobody is born with that. He asked how she had studied. On the couch, she said, with the tablet playing. That was the first problem, he said. Attention is the door to memory, and the door was closed.',
    'The second problem was the hour. He drew a line on paper that fell like a slide: what you learn on Monday is mostly gone by Wednesday unless you meet it again. One long night puts the words in and lets them slide out. Small and often, he said. Three words a day, and each day say yesterday\'s again.',
    'The third problem was the reading. She had read the list over and over, and reading feels like remembering when it is not. Close the book and say them, he said. If you can say a word, you have it. If you cannot, you know exactly which one to look at.',
    'The next week she tried it, three words on Monday at the kitchen table with the tablet off. Three more on Tuesday, plus Monday\'s said out loud, and three more on Wednesday, plus the six. On Thursday she said all nine to the dog. On Friday she got nine right, and it had taken twelve minutes a day instead of an hour of nothing.',
    'Her grandfather gave her one more trick for the phone number she could never keep. Ten digits, he said, are too many for a brain, which holds about seven things at once. Three chunks. She said it in three chunks, and has it still.',
  ],
};
STORIES['big-feelings'] = {
  about: 'a water bottle thrown across a field, a feeling with a name under it, and a smoke detector that shouts about toast',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3135', after: 1, alt: 'A coach on a bench asking a boy what the feeling is called, the boy finding two words' }, { serial: 'S3136', after: 2, alt: 'A boy breathing in for four and out for four with his hands going still' }, { serial: 'S3137', after: 3, alt: 'Two thought bubbles over one bad game: everyone will think I am bad, and that was one bad game' }, { serial: 'S3138', after: 4, alt: 'A boy at a piano recital with steady hands, a little nervous energy drawn as sparks' }, { serial: 'S3139', after: 5, alt: 'A smoke detector on a ceiling with burnt toast below it and a boy deciding it is only toast' }],
  title: 'The smoke detector', art: 'S3134', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy on a field after a lost game, a water bottle flying, teammates walking away',
  words: [
    'Ben lost the last game of the tournament by one point and threw his water bottle across the field, and for a few seconds he could not have told you why.',
    'His coach, who had seen a hundred water bottles thrown, sat him down on the bench and asked what the feeling was called. Ben said he did not know, and the coach waited. Angry, Ben said at last, and when she asked what was under that, he thought for a moment and said sad, because he had wanted it.',
    'Naming it did something. The coach said it always does: a feeling is a signal, and saying the name out loud uses the thinking part of the brain, which turns the volume down. Then she had him breathe in for four and out for four, five times, and his hands stopped shaking.',
    'On the ride home Ben noticed the thought that had come with the anger. Everyone will think I am bad, it said. His father asked him to try a different thought on the same moment: that was one bad game. Same moment, he said, but the second thought felt different, and the second thought made him want to practice instead of quit.',
    'Two weeks later, before his piano recital, the shaky hands came back, and Ben was ready for them. The coach had said some stress is useful: a little before a test gives focus and energy. He named it, nervous, breathed his fours, and played his piece with more care than he ever had in the living room.',
    'The feeling that had thrown a water bottle was still there, his father told him, and it always would be. The job was not to make it go away. The job was to read it, like a smoke detector, and decide whether it was a fire or just toast.',
  ],
};
STORIES['friends-and-other-minds'] = {
  about: 'a loud game that was the best and the worst part of recess, a friend who kept score, and one careful sentence',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3141', after: 1, alt: 'A girl asking her friend how was that for you on the walk back inside' }, { serial: 'S3142', after: 2, alt: 'A girl keeping score of who sat with whom in a notebook, another two eating lunch together' }, { serial: 'S3143', after: 3, alt: 'A girl saying a careful sentence to a friend who says nothing and then okay' }, { serial: 'S3144', after: 4, alt: 'A mother and daughter at a kitchen table talking about a friend who might be afraid' }, { serial: 'S3145', after: 5, alt: 'Three girls playing a quiet game on a Tuesday and a loud one on a Friday' }],
  title: 'The loud game', art: 'S3140', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two girls at recess, one shouting happily in a loud game and one holding her stomach at the edge',
  words: [
    'The loud game was the best part of recess for Zara. In October she found out it was the worst part of recess for Hana, who had been playing it every day for a month without saying so.',
    'Zara only found out because her teacher had given the class a question to try: how was that for you? She asked Hana on the walk back inside, expecting nothing, and Hana said the shouting made her stomach hurt. Same game, two minds, Zara thought, and neither of them was wrong. She had simply never asked.',
    'There was a third girl, Mia, who kept score. Not in the game; in the friendship. Mia counted who sat with whom and who had been invited where. When Zara played with Hana at lunch, Mia said she was not a real friend anymore. That, said the teacher when Zara asked, is the sign: a healthy friendship feels safe, and one that keeps score does not.',
    'The fight with Mia came on Thursday. Zara wanted to say you are mean and nobody likes you, and did not, because the class had practiced the sentence that works. I felt left out when you said I was not a real friend, she said, and I want us to both play. Mia said nothing for a while. Then she said okay.',
    'It did not fix everything. Zara told her mother, who was the trusted adult in the house. Her mother said that some fights take two sentences and some take a month, and that Mia might be keeping score because she was afraid of losing. Zara had not thought of Mia being afraid of anything.',
    'By November the three of them played a quieter game on Tuesdays and the loud one on Fridays, and Hana came to both. Zara still asked how was that for you, and the answers still surprised her, which she had decided was the point.',
  ],
};
// Psychology, K to 2 (2026-09-30, pass GP): four stories at the kindergarten length with five pictures each.
STORIES['my-brain-and-senses'] = {
  about: 'five reporters, a puppy in a box, a shape in the dark, and the boss going to sleep',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3147', after: 1, alt: 'A boy sniffing cookies in an oven as a doorbell rings and an uncle holds a big box' }, { serial: 'S3148', after: 2, alt: 'A puppy in a box, a boy stroking soft fur with one hand and holding a cookie in the other' }, { serial: 'S3149', after: 3, alt: 'A dark room with a shape by the door, then the light on and a puppy bed' }, { serial: 'S3150', after: 4, alt: 'A boy tucked in bed with a puppy nearby, a clock showing ten hours of sleep' }, { serial: 'S3151', after: 5, alt: 'Five senses drawn as five reporters handing news to a brain wearing a crown' }],
  title: 'Five reporters', art: 'S3146', cast: [],
  alt: 'A grandma tapping a boy\'s head while five small reporters with notebooks stand around him',
  words: [
    'Leo\'s grandma said his brain was the boss of him. It had five reporters, she said. They tell the boss the news.',
    'Leo tried them. His nose reported cookies in the oven. His ears reported the doorbell. His eyes reported his uncle at the door with a big box.',
    'Inside the box was a puppy. Leo\'s skin felt its soft fur, and his tongue tasted a cookie at the same time. Five reporters, all at once, said Grandma, and the boss was very happy.',
    'That night the puppy whined in the dark, and Leo saw a shape by the door. A monster, said his brain. Grandma turned on the light, and it was the puppy\'s bed. The eyes reported a shape, she said. The brain guessed wrong.',
    'Leo yawned. A tired brain guesses wrong more, said Grandma, and it gets grumpy too. A boy your age needs about ten to twelve hours of sleep. She tucked him in with the puppy nearby, and the boss went to sleep.',
    'Five senses tell the brain the news. The brain decides, and now and then it guesses wrong. Sleep helps the boss get it right.',
  ],
};
STORIES['remember-it'] = {
  about: 'ten numbers that would not stay, three small pieces, and a little every day',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3153', after: 1, alt: 'A dad turning off the TV so a girl can look and listen' }, { serial: 'S3154', after: 2, alt: 'A phone number cut into three small pieces on paper, a girl saying the first out loud' }, { serial: 'S3155', after: 3, alt: 'A calendar with one piece of a number learned each day, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday' }, { serial: 'S3156', after: 4, alt: 'A girl saying the middle piece three more times on Thursday' }, { serial: 'S3157', after: 5, alt: 'A girl on the phone with her grandma, saying all ten numbers with a smile' }],
  title: 'Three at a time', art: 'S3152', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl frowning at ten numbers on a card while a TV plays behind her',
  words: [
    'Mina could not remember her phone number. Ten numbers is a lot. Her dad said the brain holds only a few things at a time.',
    'First, said Dad, look and listen. He turned off the TV. If your eyes are on the TV, the number does not go in.',
    'Then he broke the number into three small pieces and said the first piece slowly. Mina said it out loud, because saying it puts it in deeper.',
    'On Monday she learned the first piece. On Tuesday she said it again and learned the second. On Wednesday she added the third. A little every day, said Dad.',
    'On Thursday she forgot the middle piece, and Dad said that was normal. Forgetting just means say it again, so she said it three more times.',
    'On Friday Mina said all ten numbers to her grandma on the phone. Look and listen, say it again, a little every day. Her brain had kept it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['my-feelings'] = {
  about: 'a block tower knocked down, a feeling with a name, five slow breaths, and a tower built again',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3159', after: 1, alt: 'A boy finding the word angry and saying it out loud' }, { serial: 'S3160', after: 2, alt: 'A boy breathing in slow and out slow with his hands going soft' }, { serial: 'S3161', after: 3, alt: 'Blocks on the floor, perfectly fine, with a thought bubble saying ruined crossed out' }, { serial: 'S3162', after: 4, alt: 'A boy, his dad and a baby building a tower together' }, { serial: 'S3163', after: 5, alt: 'A feeling with a name tag, a slow breath, and a boy choosing what to do' }],
  title: 'The name of the feeling', art: 'S3158', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with hot red cheeks beside a fallen block tower and a laughing baby',
  words: [
    'Sam built a block tower as tall as his chest. His baby sister knocked it down. Sam felt hot all over and did not know what to do.',
    'His teacher, Miss Bell, had said every feeling has a name. Sam looked for the name and found it. I am angry, he said out loud.',
    'Saying it made the feeling a little smaller. Then he did what Miss Bell had shown the class, breathing in slow and breathing out slow. Five times he did it, and his hands stopped being tight.',
    'The feeling said the tower was ruined for good. But a feeling is not a fact. The blocks were fine. They were just on the floor.',
    'Sam told his dad about the hot feeling and the slow breaths. Dad said that was smart, not weak. Then they built the tower again, and this time the baby helped.',
    'Every feeling has a name. Say the name, breathe slow, and then decide what to do.',
  ],
};
STORIES['making-friends'] = {
  about: 'a new girl by the fence, three steps to a friend, a no, and a fight fixed with words',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3165', after: 1, alt: 'A girl saying hello to a boy with a red ball, the boy smiling' }, { serial: 'S3166', after: 2, alt: 'A girl saying no to a boy who wants to skip the lunch line, kids waiting behind' }, { serial: 'S3167', after: 3, alt: 'Two children with one ball, a girl saying what she feels and what she wants, the boy listening' }, { serial: 'S3168', after: 4, alt: 'A boy waiting patiently for a girl who learns words more slowly, no face pulled' }, { serial: 'S3169', after: 5, alt: 'Three rules on a wall: say hello, be kind, say what you feel' }],
  title: 'Hello, my name is', art: 'S3164', cast: [],
  alt: 'A new girl standing by a fence at recess watching children play',
  words: [
    'Ana was new at school and did not know one name. At recess she stood by the fence and watched. Then she thought of what her mom had said.',
    'Say hello and your name, then ask what they like and ask to play. Ana walked up to a boy with a red ball and said her name. The boy, Kai, liked soccer, and asked if she wanted to play.',
    'They played all week. On Friday, another boy told Ana to skip the lunch line with him. That is mean to the kids waiting, so Ana said no. A good friend helps you be kind.',
    'On Monday Ana and Kai both wanted the ball, and they yelled. Then Ana used the words. I felt sad when you grabbed the ball, and I want a turn. Kai listened. He gave her the ball.',
    'A girl named Rosa learned words more slowly than the rest, and Kai waited for her every time. Everyone is different, said the teacher, and everyone gets respect. Kai, Ana decided, was a good friend.',
    'Say hello and your name. Be kind, tell the truth, and take turns. When you fight, say what you feel and what you want.',
  ],
};
// Speech, grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-30, pass GQ): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['the-communication-process'] = {
  about: 'a joke that died in a text, three registers in one day, a handshake that earned trust, and a phone call that fixed a wrong fact',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3171', after: 1, alt: 'A diagram of a message losing its tone and wink as it passes from a face to a screen' }, { serial: 'S3172', after: 2, alt: 'A girl in three rooms, laughing with a friend, raising a hand in class, and reading a chart at a vet clinic' }, { serial: 'S3173', after: 3, alt: 'A vet greeting a new client with a handshake, direct eye contact and a comfortable distance' }, { serial: 'S3174', after: 4, alt: 'A girl on the phone at a clinic correcting a flea treatment date with a red face' }, { serial: 'S3175', after: 5, alt: 'A phone with a seven-word text, a smiling face and a question mark, and a reply ten seconds later' }],
  title: 'The text that lost its tone', art: 'S3170', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl staring at a phone with a six-word text and no reply, a clock reading 7:42',
  words: [
    'Dani sent the joke at 7:40 in the morning, six words with no punctuation, and by 7:42 her friend Kai had stopped answering. It had been funny in her head. It had been funny out loud at lunch the day before. In a text it read like an insult.',
    'Her speech teacher used it as the day\'s example, with Dani\'s permission. Same sender, same message, different channel. A voice carries tone, a face carries the wink, and a text carries the words alone. The channel had dropped two of the parts, and the receiver had filled the gap with the worst reading. Noise, the teacher said, is not only a loud room. It is anything that keeps the message that arrives from being the message that was sent.',
    'The teacher had them list every register they had used that day. Dani counted three: informal with Kai, standard in class, and technical at the animal clinic where she volunteered after school. There the vet said the dog was febrile, and Dani had learned to say the same. None of them was wrong. Each was wrong in a different room.',
    'At the clinic that afternoon she watched the vet meet a new client: a firm handshake, direct eye contact, a step back to a comfortable distance, and then the words. Dani realized the client had decided to trust her before the diagnosis, and that the handshake had done it.',
    'The last lesson was hers alone. She had told a customer the week before that a flea treatment lasted six months, because she had heard it somewhere. It lasted one. She went back, found the woman\'s number in the file and called to correct it, which took ninety seconds and cost her a red face. Gathering accurate information first, she decided, was cheaper.',
    'That night she texted Kai a second time, seven words with a face and a question mark, and Kai answered in ten seconds. The message had not changed. The channel had been given back its tone.',
  ],
};
STORIES['listening-that-works'] = {
  about: 'an interview with none of the rehearsed questions, the step where listening dies, and a sister who did not want advice',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3177', after: 1, alt: 'Five steps on a classroom wall, receive, attend, understand, remember, respond, with attend circled' }, { serial: 'S3178', after: 2, alt: 'A boy telling a story about a bolt and a part number written on a card while a manager writes' }, { serial: 'S3179', after: 3, alt: 'A boy putting his advice down to listen to his sister on the stairs' }, { serial: 'S3180', after: 4, alt: 'A manager giving four aisles in order on the phone while a boy repeats them back' }, { serial: 'S3181', after: 5, alt: 'Twenty rehearsed answers in a drawer, never opened' }],
  title: 'The question under the question', art: 'S3176', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenager at a hardware store counter being interviewed by a manager with a clipboard',
  words: [
    'Jonah had rehearsed answers to twenty interview questions and the manager of the hardware store asked none of them. She asked what he would do if a customer wanted a part the store did not carry.',
    'He heard the question, which is not the same as listening to it. His speech class had five steps on the wall: receive, attend, understand, remember, respond, and the class had agreed that step two was where most listening died. Jonah attended. The question under the question, he decided, was whether he would send the customer away or help them anyway.',
    'So he answered that one, in under a minute, with one example. His uncle had once needed a bolt nobody stocked, and Jonah had found the supplier online and written the part number on a card. The manager wrote something down. Then she asked whether he took criticism well, and he repeated it back, which had felt silly in class. You want to know what I do when someone tells me I got it wrong, he said, and told her.',
    'At home his sister was upset about a friend, and Jonah caught himself preparing advice while she talked, the surest sign you have stopped listening. He put the advice down. Empathic listening, the third kind, meant hearing how it felt before deciding what to do about it. She talked for ten minutes and did not want advice at all.',
    'The next week the manager called with the job and one instruction: learn the store. Jonah asked for the directions in order, and she gave them with the number of steps first: four aisles, three days each, and a quiz on Friday. He repeated them back, and she laughed and said nobody had ever done that.',
    'He kept the twenty rehearsed answers in a drawer and never needed them. What he needed was the second step and the question under the question, and both of those, it turned out, worked at home as well as in a hardware store.',
  ],
};
STORIES['building-a-talk'] = {
  about: 'eleven pages for the wrong audience, one sentence for a doorway, a harvest log that beat a newspaper, and a chart that nearly changed a vote',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3183', after: 1, alt: 'A parent council at a long table with a budget, a ten-minute slot marked on the agenda' }, { serial: 'S3184', after: 2, alt: 'A harvest log kept since March beside a newspaper story that disagrees with it' }, { serial: 'S3185', after: 3, alt: 'A talk drawn as a road: a hook, one sentence, a preview, three points, a last line' }, { serial: 'S3186', after: 4, alt: 'One bar chart of harvest by month on a screen, a second slide torn up, one note card' }, { serial: 'S3187', after: 5, alt: 'A council vote of nine to two on a board, a man asking for two charts next year' }],
  title: 'Three questions in a doorway', art: 'S3182', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding eleven pages while a teacher asks three questions',
  words: [
    'Amara\'s first draft of her talk about the school garden was eleven pages long. Miss Okafor read the first one and asked three questions: who is the audience, what is the occasion, and what is the purpose?',
    'The audience was the parent council, who knew nothing about the garden and everything about the budget. The occasion was a ten-minute slot before the vote on next year\'s funding. The purpose was to persuade. Amara looked at her eleven pages, written to inform a room of gardeners, and understood why they were the wrong eleven pages.',
    'She narrowed it to one sentence she could say in a doorway. The garden feeds the cafeteria and costs less than the salad it replaces. Then she went for sources. The primary source was the garden\'s own harvest log, kept by the club since March. The secondary source was a newspaper story about school gardens, which said such gardens rarely produce enough to matter. The two disagreed, and the log had the numbers, so the disagreement became her best point.',
    'The shape came from the class. A hook: the salad you ate on Tuesday was grown forty feet from your fork. The sentence. A preview: cost, harvest, and what happens without it. Three points with evidence from the log. A conclusion that repeated the sentence and ended on a line she wrote first and built backward from.',
    'She made one slide, a bar chart of harvest by month. She cut the second slide when she noticed it said everything she was going to say, which meant the council would read it and stop listening. Her notes fit on one card: the sentence, three points, three numbers. The script went in the recycling.',
    'The council voted to keep the garden, nine to two. One of the two came to find her afterward to say the chart had been the thing that nearly changed his mind. Next year, he said, bring two.',
  ],
};
STORIES['delivery-and-nerves'] = {
  about: 'a note card that rattled, four moves against the shaking, thirty-one fillers on a recording, and one honest answer',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3189', after: 1, alt: 'A racing heart drawn twice, once before a race and once before a speech, with the word ready' }, { serial: 'S3190', after: 2, alt: 'A boy practicing a speech aloud standing in a hallway, six tally marks on the wall' }, { serial: 'S3191', after: 3, alt: 'A boy listening to a recording of himself and tallying thirty-one filler words' }, { serial: 'S3192', after: 4, alt: 'A boy pausing before an important line, then repeating a question for the room' }, { serial: 'S3193', after: 5, alt: 'A boy walking off a stage with slightly shaking hands and a small smile' }],
  title: 'Ready, not doomed', art: 'S3188', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy at a lectern with a note card rattling in his shaking hands',
  words: [
    'Theo\'s hands shook so hard during his first practice speech that the note card rattled, and he decided he was simply not a person who could stand in front of a room.',
    'His teacher had a name for it, communication apprehension, and a number: nearly everyone, she said, and the ones who claim otherwise are lying or have practiced. The body reads a room of faces as a threat, and the heart does what hearts do before threats. That was the first thing Theo learned. The second was that the racing heart is the same one he had before a race, and that it meant ready.',
    'The four moves came next. Preparation, until he knew the material well enough to be interrupted. Practice aloud, standing, six times, because the mouth learns what the eyes cannot. One slow breath before the first sentence. And the reframe, said out loud in the hallway like a fool: ready, not doomed.',
    'The recording was the humbling part. He counted thirty-one fillers in a four-minute talk, um and like and so, each one spending the audience\'s attention on nothing. He cut them to twelve on the second recording and to five on the third, not by trying harder but by hearing them. The ear turned out to be a better coach than the will.',
    'On the day, he put a pause before the line he most wanted remembered. The pause felt like a mile to him and, his friend said afterward, like a moment to the room. When a classmate asked a question he could not answer, he repeated it for the room, said he did not know, and offered to find out. The teacher wrote that down.',
    'The hands still shook, a little, and Theo had stopped waiting for them to stop. He had learned what the shaking meant and what to do with it, which was the whole course in one sentence: ready, not doomed.',
  ],
};
// Speech, grades 6 to 8 (2026-09-30, pass GR): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['listening-on-purpose'] = {
  about: 'a poster that should have been a slideshow, three moves that catch a mistake, a voice that was not fine, and four steps to the gym',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3195', after: 1, alt: 'Three moves on a whiteboard, say it back, one question, respond, with a group practicing' }, { serial: 'S3196', after: 2, alt: 'A girl saying the plan is fine in a voice that says it is not, and a boy noticing' }, { serial: 'S3197', after: 3, alt: 'A boy giving fast directions with a wave of the hand while a new student heads for the cafeteria' }, { serial: 'S3198', after: 4, alt: 'A boy counting three steps on his fingers, a new student saying them back' }, { serial: 'S3199', after: 5, alt: 'A six-slide slideshow with a girl\'s name on the title slide and the highest mark' }],
  title: 'Say it back', art: 'S3194', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a finished poster in a classroom where everyone else has a slideshow open',
  words: [
    'Malik built the whole poster on Saturday, and on Monday he found out the poster was supposed to be a slideshow. He had heard the word poster. He had not listened to the sentence around it.',
    'His teacher had a rule for exactly this, three moves, and she made the group practice them until they felt silly. Say it back, so we are making a slideshow with six slides, and ask one clarifying question, whether the six include the title slide. Then respond to what was actually said, not to what you expected. The first two moves catch the mistake, she said, before the third one builds a poster.',
    'The second lesson was about tone. On Tuesday his partner Rosa said the plan was fine, in a voice that was not fine, and Malik nearly took the words and moved on. Words and tone disagreed. He believed the tone and asked, and it turned out Rosa had done the research and had not been credited on the title slide. One minute to fix, a week to discover otherwise.',
    'The third lesson came from directions. A new student asked Malik how to get to the gym. Malik said it fast, down the hall, left, past the thing, through the doors, and the new student ended up in the cafeteria. Malik had given four steps without counting them, without order words, and without asking for them back.',
    'He tried again the next day with a different new student, and this time he said the number first: three steps. First, down this hall to the double doors, then left past the trophy case, and last, the doors on the right. Now say them back to me, he finished. The student said them back, got the second one wrong, and Malik fixed it on the spot.',
    'The slideshow got the highest mark in the class, six slides, Rosa on the title. Malik kept the three moves, and by spring he could not remember what it had been like to hear a word and build a poster around it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['saying-it-clearly'] = {
  about: 'an explanation that started at the bus ride, the same news said three ways, the ends of words, and the farthest person in the gym',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3201', after: 1, alt: 'A coach writing one sentence, one reason, one example, stop on a board' }, { serial: 'S3202', after: 2, alt: 'A girl saying the same news three ways: to a friend, in class, and to a receptionist' }, { serial: 'S3203', after: 3, alt: 'A girl recording herself and hearing the ends of words drop off' }, { serial: 'S3204', after: 4, alt: 'A girl in a gym talking to the farthest person, who nods' }, { serial: 'S3205', after: 5, alt: 'A narrator on a stage heard from the last row, a brother sitting there on purpose' }],
  title: 'The ends of the words', art: 'S3200', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl telling a long story from the bus ride while her brother leaves the room',
  words: [
    'Elena had watched the whole game and could not explain it. Her brother asked who won and she started at the beginning, with the bus ride, and by the time she reached the score he had left the room.',
    'Her speech teacher, Coach Reyes, called it burying the sentence. Find the one sentence first, he said: we won by two on a shot at the buzzer. One reason next, the play that decided it, and one example, what the play looked like. After that, stop. Elena tried it on her brother that night. Three sentences. He stayed for all of them and asked for more.',
    'The second week was about rooms. Coach Reyes had them say the same news, I got the part in the play, three ways. To a friend: I got it, I got it, no way. In class: I was cast as the narrator in the spring play. To the receptionist at the dentist: good afternoon, I was cast in a play, so I may need to move my appointment. Nobody was faking, he said. Each was the right way to talk in that room.',
    'The third week was the ends of words. Elena recorded herself and heard walkin and goin and askin, and a whole sentence that arrived at the back row as a hum. Coach Reyes had her say the last sound of every word for a day. Walking. Going. Asked. It felt like talking underwater until it did not.',
    'Rate came last, and it was the hardest, because slow felt wrong. He timed her: a hundred and sixty words a minute, which a listener cannot follow. He had her aim at the farthest person in the gym and talk to that person at a hundred and twenty, and the farthest person, for the first time, nodded.',
    'By the play, the narrator could be heard from the last row without a microphone, every word with its end on. Her brother, who had left the room in September, sat in that row on purpose.',
  ],
};
STORIES['a-two-minute-talk'] = {
  about: 'a seven-minute talk with no stance, twelve facts in a bin, one chart with one job, and four hands at the opener',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3207', after: 1, alt: 'A stance in one sentence on a board, school should start at nine, with cut facts in a bin' }, { serial: 'S3208', after: 2, alt: 'A talk drawn as five parts: opener, stance, reason, reason, closer' }, { serial: 'S3209', after: 3, alt: 'One chart of hours of sleep by student, a second slide crossed out' }, { serial: 'S3210', after: 4, alt: 'A boy practicing standing in his room, looking at one face at a time drawn on the wall' }, { serial: 'S3211', after: 5, alt: 'Four hands raised in a classroom at the opener, one of them the principal\'s' }],
  title: 'Two hundred and fifty words', art: 'S3206', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with a stopwatch reading seven minutes and twelve facts about sleep on cards',
  words: [
    'Sam\'s two-minute talk was seven minutes long the first time he timed it, and it had no stance, only twelve facts about sleep in the order he had found them.',
    'His teacher asked for the stance in one sentence, and it took him ten minutes to say it: school should start at nine. Everything that did not push that sentence forward went in the bin, which was most of the twelve facts. Two hundred and fifty words, she said, is two minutes. That is a paragraph and a half.',
    'The shape was five parts. An opener: raise your hand if you were tired in first period. The stance. Reason one, with a fact: eleven-year-olds need nine to twelve hours, and the class survey said most got seven. Reason two, with an example: the district across the river moved to nine and saw fewer tardies. A closer that said the stance again in new words.',
    'He made one chart, hours of sleep by student, from the survey. He made a second slide with his whole talk on it and then deleted it, because his teacher pointed out that the class would read the slide and stop listening to him. One picture, one job.',
    'Delivery was practice. Three times aloud, standing, in his room. Eye contact one person at a time, a whole sentence each, then the next face, which his teacher said looks to a room like talking to everyone. Slower than felt right. Loud enough for the back row. Hands out of his pockets, doing what they did when he told a friend a story.',
    'On the day his heart pounded, and it was the ordinary kind of pounding, the kind that means you care. He came in at one minute and fifty-five seconds. Four people raised their hands at the opener, and one of them was the principal.',
  ],
};
STORIES['disagreeing-out-loud'] = {
  about: 'twenty minutes of arguing, a quiet girl with the best idea, notes with three headings, and a disagreement that took forty seconds',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3213', after: 1, alt: 'A boy asking the quiet girl what she thinks, and the room going quiet' }, { serial: 'S3214', after: 2, alt: 'A page with three headings, agree, disagree, who finds out, and the first notes' }, { serial: 'S3215', after: 3, alt: 'Two claims on a board, one with a reason attached and one with a shrug' }, { serial: 'S3216', after: 4, alt: 'One student disagreeing with the idea, I see it differently, and another building on it' }, { serial: 'S3217', after: 5, alt: 'A fundraiser at a game in a far parking lot, candy at one table and hoses at the other' }],
  title: 'The quiet one', art: 'S3212', cast: [],
  alt: 'A student council arguing around a table while one girl draws in the corner of her notebook',
  words: [
    'The student council had argued about the fundraiser for twenty minutes and nobody had asked Naomi, who had been drawing in the corner of her notebook the whole time, what she thought.',
    'Dev asked her, because his speech class had a word for it, elicit, and a reason: the best idea in the room is often sitting in the person who has not spoken. Naomi looked up and said the candy sale and the car wash could be the same day, at the game, where the parents already were. The room went quiet in the other direction.',
    'Dev held the idea for a moment before answering, the second habit, and then wrote it down, the third. Nobody had been taking notes, which was why the argument kept going in circles; the same three ideas had come around four times with different people attached. He put three lines at the top of the page: agree, disagree, who finds out.',
    'Under agree he wrote May, because everyone wanted May. Under disagree he wrote what to sell, which was the actual fight. Naming it out loud shrank it. Then he listened for reasons. Candy made the most last year was a claim with a reason. Candy is obviously the best was a feeling, and when Dev asked what made it think so, the shrug answered.',
    'The vote came after Jules disagreed with Naomi the way the class had practiced. I see it differently, she said, because the car wash needs the parking lot and the game needs it too. Naomi built on it instead of defending it. The far lot, then, she said, and Jules nodded, and it had taken forty seconds.',
    'The fundraiser was May, at the game, in the far lot, candy at one table and hoses at the other, and it raised more than the two previous years together. The notes from the meeting fit on one page, and Naomi kept drawing in the corner of hers.',
  ],
};
// Speech, grades 3 to 5 (2026-09-30, pass GS): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['listening-and-asking'] = {
  about: 'a compass at show and tell, a question about pizza day, the beach rule, and a main idea said back about bees',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3219', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher writing on topic on the board, a beach drawn beside it with a question mark' }, { serial: 'S3220', after: 2, alt: 'A class listening with eyes on the speaker, hands still and bodies turned, one mind wandering and coming back' }, { serial: 'S3221', after: 3, alt: 'A girl answering questions about a compass, a boy adding a comment about his uncle\'s watch' }, { serial: 'S3222', after: 4, alt: 'A girl saying the main idea back to her brother, who holds a report about bees' }, { serial: 'S3223', after: 5, alt: 'A boy holding a volcano rock at show and tell, a girl with her hand up first' }],
  title: 'The question about lunch', art: 'S3218', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding up an old brass compass at show and tell while a boy asks about pizza day',
  words: [
    'Ruby brought her grandfather\'s compass to show and tell and talked for two minutes about the trip where he used it. The first question from the class was whether it was pizza day.',
    'It was not pizza day, and it was not a question about the compass. Miss Tan wrote two words on the board, on topic, and gave the class a rule she called the beach rule. If someone tells you about the beach, where did you swim is a question about the topic. What is for lunch is a question about you. Both are allowed, she said, but only one is listening.',
    'She showed them what listening looked like from the outside: eyes on the speaker, hands still, body turned toward the person. Then she told them the part nobody could see. Your mind on the words, she said, not on what you want to say next. And when it wanders, and it will, bring it back to the last thing you heard.',
    'Ruby got a second turn, and this time the questions were about the compass. Did the needle always point north? What was the trip for? One comment came from Theo, who said his uncle had a compass on his watch, and Miss Tan said that was a comment that added to the talk. I like pizza, she said, would not have been.',
    'That night Ruby practiced the other half of listening on her brother, who was reading a report about bees out loud for school. When he finished, she said the main idea back in her own words: bees make honey from flowers. Yes, he said, surprised. The flowers, the hive and the wax were the details. She had heard the whole thing.',
    'The next show and tell was Theo\'s, a rock from a volcano, and Ruby asked the first question. Was it hot when they found it? It was not, but it was about the rock, and Theo talked for three more minutes because somebody had asked.',
  ],
};
STORIES['telling-it-in-order'] = {
  about: 'directions given in one breath, paper airplanes with no wings, four steps said back, and a field trip told in order',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3225', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher reading four paper airplane steps, half the planes with no wings, then the steps said back' }, { serial: 'S3226', after: 2, alt: 'A wrong fold caught before the first crease, one second on a clock beside a whole ruined airplane' }, { serial: 'S3227', after: 3, alt: 'A boy counting four steps to the library on his fingers, a girl saying them back' }, { serial: 'S3228', after: 4, alt: 'A field trip told in order: a bus, cows, a goat eating a map, lunch on a hill' }, { serial: 'S3229', after: 5, alt: 'A girl who is not new anymore finding her way anywhere in the building, steps counted on fingers' }],
  title: 'Four steps to the library', art: 'S3224', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy giving directions in one breath while a new girl walks toward the nurse\'s office',
  words: [
    'Owen told the new girl how to get to the library in one breath: go down and then left and past the thing with the fish and it is right there. She ended up in the nurse\'s office.',
    'Miss Alvarez had the class practice instructions the next morning with paper airplanes, and she read four steps fast, all at once, and let them fold. Half the planes had no wings. So she read the steps her way, with the number first: four steps. First, fold the paper in half, and next, fold the top corners to the middle. Then, fold the sides down for wings, and last, throw it. Now say them back to me.',
    'The class said them back, and two children had the wings before the corners, and Miss Alvarez fixed it right then, before a single fold. That, she said, is why you say the steps back. A wrong step caught before you start costs one second. A wrong step caught at the end costs the whole airplane.',
    'Owen tried the library again with the same new girl. Four steps, he said. First, walk to the end of this hall. Next, turn left. Then, go past the fish tank. Last, it is the door with the blue sign. Say them back. She said them back and turned right at the second step, and he fixed it, and she found the library.',
    'Telling what happened worked the same way. When Owen told his mother about the field trip, he started with the part where the goat ate his map, and she could not follow it. He tried again in order. First we rode the bus, next we saw the cows, then the goat ate my map, and last we ate lunch on the hill. Now, she said, I can see it.',
    'By spring Owen counted steps on his fingers without thinking about it, and the new girl, who was not new anymore, could get anywhere in the building. She said the steps back every time, which drove Owen a little crazy, and which was exactly the point.',
  ],
};
STORIES['a-one-minute-talk'] = {
  about: 'a talk about everything cut to one rabbit, three facts in complete sentences, the ends of words, and a boy in the back corner who looked up',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3231', after: 1, alt: 'A rabbit named Clover with three facts drawn around her: hay, a box, a thumping foot' }, { serial: 'S3232', after: 2, alt: 'A shopping list that says rabbit, hay, box beside a full sentence with a who and a what' }, { serial: 'S3233', after: 3, alt: 'A girl aiming her volume at a boy in the back corner, the ends of the words drawn as full circles' }, { serial: 'S3234', after: 4, alt: 'A girl practicing out loud in a kitchen, holding up a photo of a rabbit in a box' }, { serial: 'S3235', after: 5, alt: 'A boy in the back corner looking up and asking whether the cat was scared' }],
  title: 'Clover', art: 'S3230', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl with a plan full of a rabbit, a cousin, a beach and a book, and a teacher pointing at one',
  words: [
    'Maya\'s one-minute talk was going to be about everything: her rabbit, her cousin, the beach and a book. Her teacher, Miss Okoro, stopped her at the plan and asked her to pick one.',
    'One topic, Miss Okoro said, three facts about it, and a sentence about why. Maya picked the rabbit, whose name was Clover, and found her three facts at the kitchen table. Clover eats hay every morning, she sleeps in a box in the kitchen, and she thumps her foot when the cat comes in. And why? Because she is quiet and brave. About a hundred words, which is a minute.',
    'The first practice came out as rabbit, hay, box, thump, and Miss Okoro asked for complete sentences, with a who and a what. My rabbit eats hay every morning, has a who and a what. Rabbit, hay, is a list. Maya tried again, and the sentences made the rabbit sound like a rabbit instead of a shopping list.',
    'Then came the four things that carry a sentence across a room. Eye contact, one person at a time, a whole sentence each. Rate, slower than felt normal. Volume, aimed at the boy in the back corner who never looked up. Enunciation, the ends of the words on, so thumping was thumping and not thumpin.',
    'She brought one picture, a photo of Clover in her box, and held it up for the box sentence and put it down after. She practiced out loud three times, standing in her kitchen, with Clover watching from the box. The third time was the first time it felt like talking instead of reciting.',
    'On the day the boy in the back corner looked up at the thumping part, because she had aimed it at him. Afterward he asked whether the cat was scared of the rabbit. It was. That was the best question Maya had ever been asked, and she answered it in a complete sentence.',
  ],
};
STORIES['taking-turns-to-talk'] = {
  about: 'five people talking at once, a talking stick from the reading corner, two sentences that build on an idea, and a principal who stayed ten minutes',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3237', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher holding a talking stick from the reading corner, six ideas on the board, a quiet girl with two' }, { serial: 'S3238', after: 2, alt: 'A circle of children with a stick going around, the loudest boy waiting his turn' }, { serial: 'S3239', after: 3, alt: 'Two sentences on a board: I agree and I want to add, and I think something different, because' }, { serial: 'S3240', after: 4, alt: 'A principal visiting a classroom, a girl with the talking stick answering in a full sentence' }, { serial: 'S3241', after: 5, alt: 'A class garden with beans by a sunny fence, a stick back in the reading corner' }],
  title: 'The talking stick', art: 'S3236', cast: [],
  alt: 'Five children talking at once over a garden plan while a timer runs out',
  words: [
    'The class had five minutes to plan the garden and five people talking at once, and at the end of the five minutes nobody could say what anyone else had said.',
    'Their teacher, Miss Bell, brought out a stick from the reading corner and gave it one rule: whoever holds it talks, everyone else listens, and it goes around the circle. The first time around was slow and strange. The second time around, the class had six ideas on the board, and Priya, who had not said a word all year, had given two of them.',
    'The rules, Miss Bell said, are not there to slow you down. They are there so the quiet person gets heard and the loud person gets listened to. Everyone got a turn before anyone got two, and the boy who usually talked the most found out he had things to say when he waited.',
    'Building on ideas was the next lesson. Instead of starting over each time, they used two sentences. I agree with Priya, and I want to add that the beans could go by the fence. Or, I think something different, because the fence is in the shade. Both took the last speaker seriously. Both kept the plan moving instead of going in circles.',
    'The principal came in during the third planning day, and the class found out that polite talk works in every room. Good morning, Principal Reyes, said the stick holder, using her name. When she asked how the garden was going, Priya said it was going well and that the beans were going by the sunny fence. A full sentence. The principal stayed ten minutes.',
    'The garden went in on a Friday in April with beans by the sunny fence, and the stick went back to the reading corner. It came out again whenever five people started talking at once, which was less and less.',
  ],
};
// Speech, kindergarten to grade 2 (2026-09-30, pass GT): four read-aloud stories at the kindergarten length with five pictures each.
STORIES['my-listening-body'] = {
  about: 'a boy thinking about lunch, a hat instead of a coat, and the words can you say it again',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3243', after: 1, alt: 'A boy asking his teacher to say it again' }, { serial: 'S3244', after: 2, alt: 'A boy with his eyes on his teacher and his hands still, holding his coat' }, { serial: 'S3245', after: 3, alt: 'A boy at the top of a slide, holding up three fingers' }, { serial: 'S3246', after: 4, alt: 'A boy telling the bear story back, a hungry bear first and honey last' }],
  title: 'Get your coat', art: 'S3242', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a hat while the class lines up in coats',
  words: [
    'Milo was thinking about lunch. His teacher said, get your coat and line up. Milo heard lunch, lunch, lunch, and he got his hat.',
    'Miss Ruiz smiled and asked where his coat was. Milo did not know what she had said. So he asked her. Can you say it again, please?',
    'Miss Ruiz said it again, get your coat and line up. This time Milo looked right at her. His hands were still, and his mind was on the words. He got his coat.',
    'At recess Miss Ruiz asked, did you like the slide? Milo answered with more than one word. Yes, I went down it three times, he said. Miss Ruiz laughed.',
    'After the story that afternoon, Milo said it back. First the bear was hungry, and last the bear found honey. He had heard the whole thing.',
  ],
};
STORIES['first-then-do'] = {
  about: 'three snack-time steps said back, a cup that did not spill, and two steps for a friend with a ball',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3248', after: 1, alt: 'A girl saying cup, water, table back to her teacher, who nods' }, { serial: 'S3249', after: 2, alt: 'A girl carrying a full cup to a table with nothing spilled' }, { serial: 'S3250', after: 3, alt: 'A girl telling a boy two steps with a ball' }, { serial: 'S3251', after: 4, alt: 'A ball rolling all the way across the floor to a girl' }],
  title: 'Cup, water, table', art: 'S3247', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at snack time with a cup, a water jug and a table',
  words: [
    'It was snack time, and Miss Ruiz gave Lena three steps. First, get a cup, and then fill it with water. Last, put it on the table where you sit.',
    'Lena said the steps back to her, cup, water, table. Miss Ruiz nodded. The steps were in Lena\'s head now, in order.',
    'Lena got a cup and filled it with water. She carried it to the table and set it down. First things first, and last things last. Nothing spilled.',
    'Then it was Lena\'s turn to give directions. She told Ben two steps. First, get the ball, and then roll it to me. Now you say it, Ben.',
    'Ben said, roll the ball and then get it. Lena fixed it for him: get it first, then roll it. Ben said it right, and the ball rolled all the way to Lena.',
  ],
};
STORIES['my-clear-voice'] = {
  about: 'a drawing of a brown dog, a friend at the back who could not hear, three parts, and whole words',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3253', after: 1, alt: 'A boy looking at a girl in the back row and saying it louder' }, { serial: 'S3254', after: 2, alt: 'A boy telling three parts about his dog, everyone hearing' }, { serial: 'S3255', after: 3, alt: 'A boy saying whole words slowly, a girl in the back smiling' }, { serial: 'S3256', after: 4, alt: 'A boy putting his drawing down and looking at his friends' }],
  title: 'This is my dog', art: 'S3252', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding up a drawing of a brown dog, friends in the back straining to hear',
  words: [
    'It was Theo\'s turn to tell. He held up his drawing of his dog and said, this is my dog. Nobody in the back could hear him.',
    'Miss Ruiz said, talk to the friend at the back. Theo looked at Priya in the back row and said it again, louder. This is my dog.',
    'Theo told the three parts. This is my dog. He is brown and he likes to dig. I like him because he is funny. Everyone could hear.',
    'He said whole words, slow. He said dig, not di, and funny, not fun. Priya in the back smiled.',
    'Theo held his drawing up for the digging part. Then he put it down and looked at his friends. They were the ones he was talking to.',
  ],
};
STORIES['my-turn-and-kind-words'] = {
  about: 'a red truck grabbed and then asked for in words, a hand that waited, and hello, my name is Ava',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3258', after: 1, alt: 'A girl asking for a turn with the truck in words, a boy saying okay' }, { serial: 'S3259', after: 2, alt: 'A girl with her hand up at circle time, waiting while two friends talk' }, { serial: 'S3260', after: 3, alt: 'A girl saying hello, my name is Ava, to a new boy' }, { serial: 'S3261', after: 4, alt: 'A boy bringing a girl the red truck, a thank you between them' }],
  title: 'The hand that waited', art: 'S3257', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl grabbing a red truck from a crying boy',
  words: [
    'Ava wanted the red truck, but Ben had it. Ava grabbed it, and Ben cried. Ava did not get the truck.',
    'Miss Ruiz sat with them and said, say it in words, Ava. Ava tried. I want a turn with the truck, please. Ben said, okay, when I am done.',
    'At circle time, one person talked at a time. Ava had an idea, so she put her hand up and waited. Two friends went first. Then it was Ava\'s turn, and everyone listened.',
    'A new boy came to class after lunch, and Ava walked over to him. Hello, my name is Ava. The new boy said, my name is Sam. Now they knew each other.',
    'At the end of the day, Ben brought Ava the red truck. Thank you, Ava said. Kind words had opened the door.',
  ],
};
// Agriculture, grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-30, pass GU): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['what-agriculture-is'] = {
  about: 'a good crop with a bad price, a bale and a dollar on a board, and a family in agriculture without a tractor among them',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3263', after: 1, alt: 'A bale of cotton and a dollar drawn on a board, an arrow from a strong dollar to fewer bales' }, { serial: 'S3264', after: 2, alt: 'A timeline on a wall: a reaper, a fertilizer plant, a field of short wheat, a Nobel medal' }, { serial: 'S3265', after: 3, alt: 'A cousin testing soil in a lab, an aunt selling insurance, a woman flying a drone over cotton' }, { serial: 'S3266', after: 4, alt: 'A chart with two lines moving like a seesaw, a girl presenting to three farmers in the back row' }, { serial: 'S3267', after: 5, alt: 'A father reading a chart at a kitchen table, a daughter beside him' }],
  title: 'The bale and the dollar', art: 'S3262', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl on a cotton farm at harvest, a tractor behind her and a worried father',
  words: [
    'Nadia\'s family had grown cotton on the same six hundred acres for four generations. In September her father said the crop was good and the price was bad, in the same breath, and did not explain the second part.',
    'Her agriculture teacher, Miss Okonkwo, explained it with a bale and a dollar drawn on the board. Most Texas cotton is sold abroad, to mills that pay in their own money. When the dollar is strong, the same bale costs a foreign mill more of its own money, so it buys fewer bales. A good crop, a strong dollar, a bad price.',
    'The class had started the year with the scope of the thing, bigger than Nadia had thought. Food, fiber, wood products, and the land and water behind them. Ten thousand years old. The reaper in 1834, nitrogen pulled from the air between 1909 and 1913, Borlaug\'s short wheat in the 1960s and his Nobel Peace Prize in 1970. Fewer than two Americans in a hundred farm now, and about one job in ten touches food somewhere between the field and the plate.',
    'That last number changed how Nadia saw her own family. Her cousin Tomás, who had never driven a tractor, tested soil for a lab in Lubbock. Her aunt sold crop insurance. The woman who flew the drone over their fields in July had a degree in computer science. All of them were in agriculture, and none of them had needed a strong back.',
    'For her project Nadia tracked the price of cotton and the value of the dollar every week for a semester on one chart, and the two lines moved like a seesaw. She presented it in the spring, in a room with three farmers in the back, and one of them asked for a copy.',
    'Her father read the chart at the kitchen table. He said he had known it in his hands for thirty years and had never seen it on paper. Then he asked what a soil chemist made, and Nadia understood that the question was about her.',
  ],
};
STORIES['soil-and-plants'] = {
  about: 'a jar of backyard soil settled into layers, a pH of 5.2, seeds started in the dark, and two bean plants crossed',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3269', after: 1, alt: 'A jar almost all sand with a thin band of silt and a skin of clay, a thirsty tomato plant' }, { serial: 'S3270', after: 2, alt: 'A pH meter reading 5.2 in soil, a bag of lime beside it' }, { serial: 'S3271', after: 3, alt: 'A pie of soil: mineral, organic matter, water and air, and a creek sandbar behind it' }, { serial: 'S3272', after: 4, alt: 'Seeds sprouting in a dark cabinet, then on a windowsill, two bean plants crossed' }, { serial: 'S3273', after: 5, alt: 'Tomatoes in August on soil that was a sandbar, a row of tall bean children and a few early ones' }],
  title: 'The jar on the windowsill', art: 'S3268', cast: [],
  alt: 'A jar of soil and water on a classroom windowsill, settled into three layers',
  words: [
    'The soil from Eli\'s backyard garden went into a jar of water on Monday, got shaken hard, and sat on the classroom windowsill for two days. On Wednesday it had told him why nothing grew there.',
    'Sand drops first, silt next, clay last and slowest. Eli\'s jar was almost all sand, a thin gray band of silt, and barely a skin of clay on top. Sandy soil drains fast and holds few nutrients, Miss Okonkwo said, so his tomatoes had been thirsty and hungry at once. Loam, the balance of the three, was what he wanted and did not have.',
    'The second test was pH. His soil read 5.2 on the meter, and most crops want between 6 and 7. A pH of 5 is ten times more acid than 6, she said, and lime raises it. Eli wrote down two fixes before he had planted a thing: compost for the sand, lime for the acid.',
    'Soil, the class learned, is about 45 percent mineral, 5 percent organic matter, 25 percent water and 25 percent air, and it forms at the speed of centuries per inch. Eli\'s backyard had once been a sandbar of the creek, which explained the jar. Nothing was wrong with it that a few truckloads of compost could not fix.',
    'He started his seeds in the dark of a cabinet, because a seed needs water, oxygen and warmth and not light until the shoot is up. Then he moved them to the window. After that he crossed two bean plants, a tall one and an early one, and saved the seeds to see what the children would be.',
    'The tomatoes came in August, in compost and lime, on soil that had been a sandbar. The bean children were mostly tall and a few early, and Eli kept the early ones to cross again, which, Miss Okonkwo said, was how every crop on earth had been made.',
  ],
};
STORIES['animals-and-food'] = {
  about: 'a cow named Sixteen, four stomach parts, a cold chain from the tank to the shelf, and forty gallons of milk a week in a cafeteria bin',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3275', after: 1, alt: 'A Holstein and an Angus side by side, a diagram of four stomach parts between them' }, { serial: 'S3276', after: 2, alt: 'Calves in a barn with the stages birth, weaning, growing, finishing on a chart, a farmer with records' }, { serial: 'S3277', after: 3, alt: 'Milk moving from a cow to a cooled tank to a plant to a cold shelf, a chain drawn under it' }, { serial: 'S3278', after: 4, alt: 'A girl on a bus doing numbers: half again as much food by 2050, a third wasted' }, { serial: 'S3279', after: 5, alt: 'A cafeteria with smaller milk cartons and a count that fell from forty gallons to twelve' }],
  title: 'Four stomachs and a cold chain', art: 'S3274', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a dairy gate face to face with a black and white cow that is chewing',
  words: [
    'Priya had never thought about where milk came from until the class visited a dairy in March and a cow named Sixteen looked at her through a gate and chewed.',
    'Sixteen was a Holstein, black and white, bred over a long life for milk. The farmer said the Angus in the next pasture were the same species and a different tool, bred for muscle by two years old. Both were ruminants. Four stomach parts, rumen, reticulum, omasum and abomasum, turned grass that no person could digest into milk and meat. That was the whole reason cattle existed on land too poor to plow.',
    'The calves were in a separate barn, and the farmer walked the class through the stages: birth, weaning, growing, finishing, each with its own feed. He chose which cows to breed from records, he said, growth and milk and calving ease, because those traits pass down. The pigs he did not keep were monogastric, one stomach, grain eaters, a different business.',
    'The milk itself never got warm. It went from Sixteen to a cooled tank within the hour, then to the plant to be pasteurized, heated gently the way Louis Pasteur had shown in the 1860s. After that it was bottled cold and kept cold to the shelf. Break the cold chain anywhere, the plant manager said, and the milk is lost.',
    'On the bus back Priya did the numbers the teacher had given them. The world would need roughly half again as much food by 2050 as in 2010, on about the same land. A third of the food grown today was lost or wasted between the field and the fork. Growing more and wasting less were the same problem from two ends.',
    'She started with the wasting. The cafeteria, she found, threw out forty gallons of milk a week, mostly opened cartons, and by May the cartons were smaller and the number was twelve. Sixteen, as far as Priya knew, never found out.',
  ],
};
STORIES['running-a-farm'] = {
  about: 'six goats and a page of what could go wrong, a feed line that said more than the plan, a motion the loudest boy lost, and a ledger that paid for itself',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3281', after: 1, alt: 'A one-page plan: six goats, feed and hay costs, a spring sale price, and a list of what could go wrong' }, { serial: 'S3282', after: 2, alt: 'A ledger with a feed line reading five hundred and thirty in February' }, { serial: 'S3283', after: 3, alt: 'A chapter meeting with a motion, a second and a vote, the loudest boy losing' }, { serial: 'S3284', after: 4, alt: 'A tractor on a slope with a roll bar and a fastened belt, a teacher telling the story' }, { serial: 'S3285', after: 5, alt: 'Six goats sold at a spring sale, a ledger showing profit two hundred and fifty' }],
  title: 'Six goats and a ledger', art: 'S3280', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy with six goats in a pen in October, a notebook open to what could go wrong',
  words: [
    'Marco\'s project was six goats, bought in October for six hundred dollars with money he had earned mowing. The first thing his teacher made him do, before he had touched a goat, was write down what could go wrong.',
    'The plan took a page. What he would produce, six market goats by April. What it would cost, feed, hay, a fence repair and a vet visit, four hundred dollars by his guess. What they should sell for, about fourteen hundred at the spring sale. And what could go wrong, which he wrote down as coyotes, cold, and the price falling, and which turned out to be the feed bill.',
    'Records came next, every expense and every sale written down the day it happened, because profit is only income minus expenses and nobody remembers either one in December. By February the feed line said five hundred and thirty, not four hundred, and Marco knew it in February instead of finding out in April.',
    'The chapter meetings ran by rules he had thought were silly until the night they were not. Someone moved that the chapter buy a new scale, and someone seconded. The loudest boy in the room argued against it for ten minutes, and the vote went the other way anyway. The loudest voice, Marco saw, could not win by being loud.',
    'Safety was one story. His teacher had rolled a tractor at nineteen on a slope she knew, and walked away because of a roll bar and a fastened belt. Augers took hands. Chemicals had labels for a reason. She told it once, plainly, and nobody in the class forgot it.',
    'The goats sold in April for thirteen hundred and eighty. Income thirteen eighty, expenses eleven thirty, profit two hundred and fifty, less than the plan said. The records showed exactly where the hundred and fifty had gone: a hundred and thirty in feed and twenty at the sale. Marco bought hay earlier the next year, and cheaper, and that was the whole point of the ledger.',
  ],
};
// Agriculture, grades 6 to 8 (2026-09-30, pass GX): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['where-food-comes-from'] = {
  about: 'a taco with four farms in it, three resources under every road, and six jobs on a dairy before lunch',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3287', after: 1, alt: 'A taco drawn as a map with four roads in from a ranch, a wheat field, a farm and a dairy' }, { serial: 'S3288', after: 2, alt: 'Soil, water and energy drawn as three pipes feeding a Texas field of cattle and cotton' }, { serial: 'S3289', after: 3, alt: 'A boy and his aunt at a dairy with standing cornstalks, drip lines and a satellite picture on a phone' }, { serial: 'S3290', after: 4, alt: 'Six jobs on a dairy: a vet, a soil scientist, a nutritionist, and one tractor' }, { serial: 'S3291', after: 5, alt: 'A taco map pinned to a classroom wall until May' }],
  title: 'The taco with four farms', art: 'S3286', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy holding a taco while a teacher asks where it came from',
  words: [
    'Diego had eaten roughly a thousand tacos in his life, and on the first day of agriculture class Miss Navarro asked him where the last one had come from. The cafeteria, he said, and she waited until he tried again.',
    'The beef had come from a ranch, the tortilla from a wheat field, the tomatoes from a farm, the cheese from a dairy. Each of them had traveled the same chain: grown or raised, harvested, cleaned and packed, moved by truck, sold, cooked. Four farms in one hand, Miss Navarro said, and every one of them ran on the same three things.',
    'Soil, water and energy. Soil that took centuries to build and could wash away in a storm. Water that most Texas farms pumped from underground. Energy for the tractors, the pumps and the trucks. Texas raised more cattle and grew more cotton than any other state, and every animal and every boll had drawn on all three.',
    'Diego\'s aunt ran a dairy outside town, and that weekend he asked her the class\'s three words: conservation, efficiency, technology. She showed him all three without leaving the yard. The cornstalks left standing in the field after harvest, so the soil stayed put through the winter. The drip lines in the vegetable patch, water at the root instead of in the air. And the satellite picture on her phone, showing a dry corner of the far pasture that her boots would have found a week later.',
    'She was not the only person in agriculture on the place. The vet came Tuesdays. A soil scientist from the extension office had tested the fields in spring. The nutritionist who wrote the cows\' diet had never driven a tractor. Diego counted six jobs before lunch, one of them on a tractor.',
    'Back in class he drew the taco as a map with four roads leading into it and three words written under every road. Miss Navarro pinned it to the wall, where it stayed until May, and Diego never again said the cafeteria.',
  ],
};
STORIES['soil-and-seeds'] = {
  about: 'an experiment ruined by two changes, three pots with one difference, a jar test, seeds in the dark, and a graph that told the truth',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3293', after: 1, alt: 'Three pots in one window with sand, clay and loam, a ruler and a three-column table' }, { serial: 'S3294', after: 2, alt: 'Three jars settled into layers, one nearly all sand, one nearly all clay, one balanced' }, { serial: 'S3295', after: 3, alt: 'Seedlings sprouting in a dark cupboard, then moved to a window' }, { serial: 'S3296', after: 4, alt: 'A graph with three lines, the loam line running away in the second week' }, { serial: 'S3297', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook page of a ruined experiment with a red note: two changes, no answer' }],
  title: 'Two pots and one change', art: 'S3292', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl with two pots, one twice as tall, and a question mark over both',
  words: [
    'Layla\'s first experiment was a disaster, and it was a useful one. She gave one pot better soil and more water, it grew twice as tall, and when Miss Navarro asked which change had done it, she could not say.',
    'Change one thing, Miss Navarro said, or you have two guesses and no answer. Layla started over with three pots: the same seeds, the same window, the same water schedule, and only the soil different. Sand in the first, clay in the second, loam in the third. Then a metric ruler every morning and a table with three columns.',
    'The jar test had told her what the soils were. A handful of each shaken in water and left overnight: sand dropped first, silt next, clay last and slowest, and the layers showed the mix. The sandy pot drained so fast the seedling was thirsty by noon. The clay pot held so much water the roots sat in it. The loam pot did neither.',
    'The seeds themselves had surprised her. She started them in a dark cupboard, because a seed needs water, air and warmth to germinate and not light, and they sprouted fine. Light mattered after the shoot was up, when the leaves began making sugar from sunlight, and that was when they moved to the window.',
    'After three weeks the table became a graph, three lines climbing at three slopes. The graph said what the notebook had hidden: the loam line ran away from the others in the second week and never came back. Soil, light, water and temperature were the abiotic things a plant depended on, and she had changed exactly one of them.',
    'She kept the first, ruined experiment in her notebook on purpose, with a note across it in red: two changes, no answer. It was, she decided, the most useful page she had.',
  ],
};
STORIES['animals-on-a-farm'] = {
  about: 'white birds between the cows, four relationships with names, layers on the back of a feed bag, and a dry summer that sorted the herd',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3299', after: 1, alt: 'Four relationships drawn on a ranch: egret and cow, tick and calf, bee and blossom, coyote tracks' }, { serial: 'S3300', after: 2, alt: 'Two calves shouldering each other at one patch of clover inside a fence' }, { serial: 'S3301', after: 3, alt: 'Layers on the back of a feed bag: one cow, the herd, the community, the whole ranch' }, { serial: 'S3302', after: 4, alt: 'A grandfather walking a thin-grass herd at dusk, watching which cows keep their weight' }, { serial: 'S3303', after: 5, alt: 'A cow chewing beside an egret, a boy deciding the fence does not make it less wild' }],
  title: 'The egret and the cow', art: 'S3298', cast: [],
  alt: 'White egrets walking between cows on a Texas ranch, a boy watching from a fence',
  words: [
    'The white birds walked between the cows all day, and on the third morning at his grandparents\' ranch Sam finally asked what they were doing. Eating, his grandfather said. The cows kick up grasshoppers, the birds eat them, and the cows never notice.',
    'Sam had a name for that from class, commensalism, one gains and the other is unaffected, and once he had one name he started finding the others. The ticks on the calves: parasitism, one gains and the other is harmed. The bees in the pecan blossoms taking nectar and leaving pollen: mutualism, both gain. The coyote tracks by the far fence: predation.',
    'His grandmother added the one he had missed. Two calves at the same patch of clover, shouldering each other aside, was competition, and it happened a hundred times a day. A farm, she said, is an ecosystem with a fence around it, and everything in class happens inside the wire.',
    'He sorted the ranch into the class\'s layers on the back of a feed bag. One cow: an organism. All the cows: a population. The cows, the grass, the egrets, the ticks, the bees and the coyotes: a community. Add the soil, the creek and the weather, and that was the ecosystem, forty acres of it.',
    'The weather was the part that mattered that year. It had not rained since April, the grass was thin, and his grandfather walked the herd every evening watching which cows kept their weight. Some handled the heat and the thin grass and some did not, and the ones that did were the ones he would breed. Variation, Sam wrote on the feed bag, is the herd\'s insurance.',
    'On the last morning he watched a cow chew for a full minute and thought about the four-part stomach turning grass no person could digest into milk. He thought about the egret beside her and the tick she was scratching, and decided the fence did not make any of it less wild.',
  ],
};
STORIES['a-small-project'] = {
  about: 'a greenhouse that would have been a hobby, three hens on a one-page plan, a table kept every evening, and a graph with an August dip',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3305', after: 1, alt: 'A one-page plan for three hens: eggs, costs, a gate price, and what could go wrong' }, { serial: 'S3306', after: 2, alt: 'A table of feed and eggs filled in each evening, closed shoes in a pen' }, { serial: 'S3307', after: 3, alt: 'A graph with a flat feed line and an egg line dipping in August' }, { serial: 'S3308', after: 4, alt: 'Twenty-seven dollars of profit written under sales and expenses' }, { serial: 'S3309', after: 5, alt: 'Shade cloth over a hen run moved to fresh ground, the old patch growing back' }],
  title: 'Three hens and a graph', art: 'S3304', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl with a greenhouse catalogue and a plan that says hobby, three hens drawn beside it',
  words: [
    'Amina wanted a greenhouse, and her plan was the first thing that told her no. A two-hundred-dollar greenhouse for forty dollars of tomatoes, Miss Navarro pointed out, was a hobby, which was fine, as long as she knew.',
    'She knew, and chose three laying hens instead, and the plan fit on one page. What she would produce: eggs, from May to September. What it would cost: forty-five dollars for the hens and about sixty in feed. What they should sell for: around a hundred and thirty dollars at the gate. What could go wrong: a fox, the heat, and hens that stopped laying.',
    'Records started the day the hens arrived. Every bag of feed and every dozen sold went into the table that evening, because profit was income minus expenses and nothing else. Nobody remembered either one in September. Safety went in the record too: closed shoes in the pen, a grown-up on the day the wire was cut.',
    'In late August the table became a graph, and the graph said something the notebook had not. The feed line ran flat across the summer. The egg line dipped in the second week of August and stayed down for three weeks, exactly when the heat had been worst, and the hens had gone quiet in the shade.',
    'The season ended with a hundred and thirty-two dollars in sales, a hundred and five in expenses, and twenty-seven dollars of profit. The cost had been weighed against the benefit before a dollar was spent, and the benefit had won by twenty-seven dollars and a graph.',
    'The last line of her plan was next year, and it was already written. Shade cloth over the run before August, and the run itself moved to fresh ground so the old patch could grow back. Take the eggs, Miss Navarro had said, and leave the ground better than you found it.',
  ],
};
// Agriculture, grades 3 to 5 (2026-09-30, pass HB): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['farms-and-food'] = {
  about: 'a sandwich traced back to a wheat field and a peanut patch, six jobs between the farm and the plate, and a flow chart on a refrigerator',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3311', after: 1, alt: 'A path drawn backward from a store shelf to a bakery, a mill and a wheat field' }, { serial: 'S3312', after: 2, alt: 'A farmer, a truck driver, a miller, a baker and a grocer standing in a row' }, { serial: 'S3313', after: 3, alt: 'A board with soil, rain, sunlight and diesel written under natural resources' }, { serial: 'S3314', after: 4, alt: 'Peanut plants with peanuts growing under the soil, dug up and drying' }, { serial: 'S3315', after: 5, alt: 'A flow chart of a sandwich taped to a refrigerator door' }],
  title: 'The path of a loaf of bread', art: 'S3310', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl holding a sandwich in a classroom while her teacher asks where lunch came from',
  words: [
    'Lucia was halfway through a peanut butter sandwich when her teacher asked the class where their lunch had come from. The store, Lucia said, and Miss Ortega smiled and asked where the store had gotten it.',
    'So Lucia traced the bread backward, one step at a time. The store had bought it from a bakery, and the bakery had baked it from flour. The flour had come from a mill, where wheat is ground into powder, and the wheat had grown in a field in the Texas Panhandle.',
    'Miss Ortega asked who had done each step, and the list surprised the class. A farmer had planted the wheat in the fall and harvested it in early summer with a combine. A truck driver had hauled it to the mill. A miller had ground it, a baker had baked it, and a grocer had set the loaf on the shelf.',
    'Then she asked what the farm itself had used. Soil to grow in, Lucia said, and rain, and sunlight, and someone added diesel for the tractor. Miss Ortega wrote every answer on the board under the words natural resources and said a good farmer spends them carefully, because they have to last.',
    'The peanut butter had its own path, which a boy named Omar traced. Peanuts grow underground on low green plants, and Texas grows a great many of them. They are dug, dried, roasted and ground, and the paste goes into a jar.',
    'That night Lucia drew her sandwich as a flow chart, boxes and arrows from two fields to one plate, with a stick figure at every step for each worker. Her mother taped it to the refrigerator, where it stayed until the paper curled.',
  ],
};
STORIES['seeds-and-soil-3'] = {
  about: 'two cups of lima beans, one in a dark closet, pale shoots that turned green, a compost pile that became soil, and a pecan tree resting in January',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3317', after: 1, alt: 'Pale bean shoots pushing up out of a cup inside a dark closet' }, { serial: 'S3318', after: 2, alt: 'A grandmother at a dinner table drawing a seed with food packed inside it' }, { serial: 'S3319', after: 3, alt: 'Tall pale yellow bean stems beside short green ones on a windowsill' }, { serial: 'S3320', after: 4, alt: 'A compost pile of leaves and peels next to a bucket of dark crumbly soil' }, { serial: 'S3321', after: 5, alt: 'A bare pecan tree in winter and the same tree budding in spring' }],
  title: 'The cup in the closet', art: 'S3316', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy placing one cup of beans on a sunny windowsill and another in a dark closet',
  words: [
    'Ethan was sure seeds needed light, so he set out to prove it with two cups of lima beans. One went on the sunny windowsill, and the other went into the dark hall closet, where nobody would bother it.',
    'Both cups got the same soil, the same water and the same warm house. After a week Ethan opened the closet expecting nothing, and found two pale shoots pushing up through the dirt. The beans had sprouted in the dark.',
    'His grandmother, who had gardened for fifty years, explained it at dinner. A seed carries its first food packed inside it, so it needs only water, air and warmth to start. Light matters later, once the shoot is up and the leaves have work to do.',
    'The closet beans proved her right over the next week. Their stems grew long, thin and yellow, stretching for light that was not there, while the windowsill beans stood short, sturdy and green. Ethan moved the pale ones to the window, and in a few days they began to turn green.',
    'The soil was the other surprise. His grandmother\'s compost pile, a heap of old leaves and vegetable peels, had turned in a year into dark crumbly earth. That is decomposition, she said, and the sand mixed into it came from rock worn down by wind and water over a very long time.',
    'In January Ethan pointed at her pecan tree and asked if it had died. It was resting, she said, dormant until spring. In April the buds opened, and Ethan planted his beans beside it, in the dark soil the pile had made.',
  ],
};
STORIES['farm-animals-3'] = {
  about: 'four questions every morning on a farm, teeth made for grass, a duck built for the pond, a hen from egg to egg, and monarchs heading south',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3323', after: 1, alt: 'Hay for cattle, grain for hens, and a girl checking the barn doors after a windy night' }, { serial: 'S3324', after: 2, alt: 'A cow chewing grass, with wide flat back teeth shown in a small drawing beside her' }, { serial: 'S3325', after: 3, alt: 'Ducks waddling across a farmyard and then paddling easily on a pond' }, { serial: 'S3326', after: 4, alt: 'A hen with newly hatched chicks and an egg-to-hen circle drawn beside her' }, { serial: 'S3327', after: 5, alt: 'Cattle with their backs to a cold wind while orange monarch butterflies fly south' }],
  title: 'Four questions every morning', art: 'S3322', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl carrying a bucket toward a water trough at sunrise on a Texas farm',
  words: [
    'Ava spent a long October weekend on her aunt\'s farm outside Waco, and every morning started with the same four questions. Did everyone have food, water, shelter and enough room? Her aunt said those four were the whole job, and everything else was details.',
    'Water came first, because a cow can drink twenty gallons or more on a hot day, and the trough had to be full before breakfast. After that came hay for the cattle, grain for the hens, and a check that the barn doors had held through the night\'s wind.',
    'Ava watched the cows eat and noticed their teeth, wide and flat at the back, grinding grass the way a mill grinds wheat. Her aunt said a cow\'s stomach has four parts to handle all that grass. The chewing Ava saw while the cows rested was cud, food brought back up to chew again.',
    'The ducks were built differently. They crossed the yard clumsily on webbed feet, then slid into the pond and paddled away with no effort at all, which is what the webs are for. The hens had no webs and stayed well away from the water.',
    'On Saturday one of the hens hatched the chicks she had been sitting on for three weeks, and her aunt explained the whole circle. An egg kept warm for about 21 days hatches into a chick. The chick grows into a young hen, and at about five months old she lays eggs of her own.',
    'On the last morning a cold front blew in, and the cattle stood with their backs to the wind. Overhead a loose line of orange butterflies was heading south. Monarchs, her aunt said, on their way to Mexico for the winter. Ava added a fifth question to the list: what is the weather doing?',
  ],
};
STORIES['a-tiny-farm'] = {
  about: 'a squash bed with flowers and no squash, a paintbrush that did the bees\' job, a tally that became a bar graph, and a milk jug in a dry July',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3329', after: 1, alt: 'A neighbor who keeps bees pointing at a squash flower beside a boy' }, { serial: 'S3330', after: 2, alt: 'A boy dabbing pollen between squash flowers with a small paintbrush in early morning' }, { serial: 'S3331', after: 3, alt: 'A tally on a garage wall beside a bar graph that climbs like stairs' }, { serial: 'S3332', after: 4, alt: 'A boy watering squash roots from a cut milk jug beside a cactus' }, { serial: 'S3333', after: 5, alt: 'Bees on squash flowers and a compost bin full of peels and stems' }],
  title: 'The squash that would not come', art: 'S3328', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy looking at squash plants full of yellow flowers and no squash',
  words: [
    'Noah\'s squash plants were covered in yellow flowers by June, and not one of them turned into a squash. The flowers opened in the morning, closed by afternoon, and dropped off.',
    'His neighbor, who kept bees, walked over to look and asked how many bees Noah had seen. He had not seen any. A squash flower has to get pollen from another flower, she said, and bees are the ones who carry it.',
    'Until her bees found his yard, she showed him how to do their job with a small paintbrush, dabbing pollen from one flower into another early in the morning. Three days later the first tiny squash began to swell behind a wilting bloom.',
    'Noah kept a tally on the garage wall, a mark for every squash he picked: four the first week, six the second and nine the third. His teacher helped him turn the marks into a bar graph, and the bars climbed like stairs, which pleased him more than he expected.',
    'Then July brought a dry spell, three weeks without rain. The squash wilted every afternoon while the cactus by the mailbox looked exactly the same. Noah cut the top off an old milk jug to make a watering can, filled it from the rain barrel, and watered at the roots each evening.',
    'By August the neighbor\'s bees had found the yard, the paintbrush went back in the drawer, and the tally reached forty-one. Noah put the peels and old stems in the compost bin for next year\'s soil, which his neighbor said was the whole idea of a farm, tiny or not.',
  ],
};
// Agriculture, kindergarten to grade 2 (2026-09-30, pass HC): four read-aloud stories at the kindergarten length with five pictures each.
STORIES['plants-we-eat-k'] = {
  about: 'a carrot pulled from the dirt, lettuce leaves, broccoli buds, a tomato full of seeds, and a salad with every plant part',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3335', after: 1, alt: 'A girl tearing big green lettuce leaves in a garden' }, { serial: 'S3336', after: 2, alt: 'A grandpa pointing to the green bumps on top of a broccoli head' }, { serial: 'S3337', after: 3, alt: 'A red tomato cut in half with many small seeds inside' }, { serial: 'S3338', after: 4, alt: 'A girl and her grandpa eating a big salad at a garden table' }],
  title: 'Lunch from the garden', art: 'S3334', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl pulling a long orange carrot out of the garden dirt beside her grandpa',
  words: [
    'Rosa helped her grandpa pick lunch from the garden. First she pulled a carrot out of the dirt. It was orange and long, because a carrot is a root that grows under the ground.',
    'Next they picked lettuce. Grandpa said lettuce is leaves. Rosa tore off the big green leaves and left the small ones to grow.',
    'The broccoli looked like a tiny green tree. Grandpa showed her the bumps on top. Each bump was a flower bud that had not opened yet.',
    'Rosa picked a red tomato, and Grandpa cut it in half. Inside were lots of little seeds. A tomato is a fruit, he said, and each seed could grow a new tomato plant.',
    'They made a big salad with a root, leaves, flower buds and a fruit. Rosa ate every plant part, and she asked for more carrots.',
  ],
};
STORIES['seed-to-plant-k'] = {
  about: 'a bean in a clear cup, a root that went down and a shoot that came up, a sunny window, and a pod full of new beans',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3340', after: 1, alt: 'A clear cup showing a white root going down and a green shoot coming up' }, { serial: 'S3341', after: 2, alt: 'A bean seedling in a cup on a sunny window, its leaves turned toward the light' }, { serial: 'S3342', after: 3, alt: 'A tall bean plant in a garden with little white flowers' }, { serial: 'S3343', after: 4, alt: 'A boy holding an open green pod full of beans' }],
  title: 'The bean in the cup', art: 'S3339', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy pushing a bean seed into a clear cup of soil',
  words: [
    'Leo pushed a bean seed into a clear cup of soil and gave it a drink. He put the cup in a warm spot and waited.',
    'After a few days, the seed opened. Leo could see a tiny white root through the cup, going down into the soil. A green shoot came up next.',
    'Now it was a seedling. Leo moved it to a sunny window, because a plant needs sunlight, water and air to grow. Its leaves turned toward the light.',
    'The cup got too small, so Leo moved the bean to the garden, where it had room. It grew tall and made little white flowers.',
    'The flowers turned into green pods. Leo opened one, and inside were new beans just like the one he planted. The bean plant had made more beans!',
  ],
};
STORIES['farm-animals-k'] = {
  about: 'a cow whose ears turned, hens that pecked, a goat that grabbed a leaf, a storm that sent everyone into the barn, and a warm egg',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3345', after: 1, alt: 'Hens pecking at seeds on the ground by a barn' }, { serial: 'S3346', after: 2, alt: 'A goat stretching over a fence to grab a leaf with its lips' }, { serial: 'S3347', after: 3, alt: 'A farmer leading a cow, hens and a goat into a red barn in the rain' }, { serial: 'S3348', after: 4, alt: 'A girl holding a brown egg carefully in both hands' }],
  title: 'The red barn', art: 'S3344', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl at a fence waving to a brown cow in front of a big red barn',
  words: [
    'Maya visited a farm with a big red barn. A brown cow looked at her, and its ears turned toward her voice. Cows can see almost all the way around.',
    'The hens were busy pecking at seeds on the ground. Their beaks picked up the seeds one at a time, quick as a wink.',
    'A goat stretched its neck over the fence. It grabbed a leaf with its lips and chewed it slowly while Maya laughed.',
    'Then dark clouds rolled in, and it began to rain. The farmer led the animals into the barn, where it was dry. A barn is shelter, and every farm animal needs it.',
    'Before Maya left, the farmer gave her a brown egg from a hen. It was still warm. Maya held it with both hands all the way home.',
  ],
};
STORIES['a-day-on-the-farm-k'] = {
  about: 'a farm day that starts before the sun, a sky that tells the day\'s work, a milk truck, and a deer at the edge of the woods',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3350', after: 1, alt: 'A farmer looking up at a cloudy sky over a field of corn' }, { serial: 'S3351', after: 2, alt: 'A milk truck at a farm driving away down a country road' }, { serial: 'S3352', after: 3, alt: 'A deer at the edge of the woods at evening, a boy and his mother watching' }, { serial: 'S3353', after: 4, alt: 'A boy pointing at a milk carton in a store and grinning' }],
  title: 'Up with the sun', art: 'S3349', cast: [],
  alt: 'A mother farmer feeding cows at sunrise while her son watches',
  words: [
    'Sam\'s mom is a farmer, and her day starts before the sun comes up. First she feeds the cows and fills their water.',
    'Then she looks at the sky. Rain today means the corn gets a drink, so she will not need the hose. On hot days, she waters the garden every evening.',
    'At noon, a big truck comes for the milk. The milk goes to a dairy and the store, and soon it is on tables in town.',
    'In the evening, Sam sees a deer at the edge of the woods. Nobody feeds the deer, his mom says. It finds its own food, but our cows count on us.',
    'At the store the next day, Sam finds milk from his mom\'s farm. He points at the carton and grins, because he knows just where it came from.',
  ],
};
// Business, grades 9 to 12 (2026-09-30, pass HD): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['business-basics'] = {
  about: 'a lawn business with one owner, a partner who wanted in, a rock and a window, eleven yards on a Saturday, and a trimmer made in China',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3355', after: 1, alt: 'Two teenage boys shaking hands over a notebook that splits the money in half' }, { serial: 'S3356', after: 2, alt: 'An uncle at a kitchen table drawing a line between a business and a family savings jar' }, { serial: 'S3357', after: 3, alt: 'One boy mowing while the other edges and bags, a row of finished yards behind them' }, { serial: 'S3358', after: 4, alt: 'A landscaping truck with a crew passing the boys on the same street' }, { serial: 'S3359', after: 5, alt: 'A string trimmer on a workbench beside a gas can and a price list' }],
  title: 'Two mowers and a sign', art: 'S3354', cast: [],
  alt: 'A sixteen-year-old boy beside a lawn mower and a hand-painted sign offering yards mowed for thirty dollars',
  words: [
    'Jamal was sixteen when he painted a sign that said Yards Mowed, $30, and by June he had a business. It was a sole proprietorship, though he did not know the name yet: one owner, who kept every dollar and owed every debt.',
    'His friend Eli wanted in. Eli had a trimmer and a bike trailer, and Jamal had the customers, so they wrote a partnership into a notebook. They would split the work, the profit, and anything that went wrong, and they both signed it.',
    'Something went wrong in July. A mower threw a rock through a neighbor\'s window, and the glass cost $240. Jamal\'s uncle, who kept books for small companies, explained what a real business would face. A sole proprietor or a partner can owe business debts personally, so bigger businesses form a corporation or an LLC to keep those debts apart from the family savings. No paper, he added, protects anyone from being careless.',
    'The second lesson was about Saturdays. Working alone, each boy had finished four yards. Working together, with Jamal mowing and Eli edging and bagging, they finished eleven, because nobody had to stop and switch tools. Their uncle called it division of labor, and the eleventh yard was the proof.',
    'They were not the only mowers on the street. A landscaping company with a truck and a crew charged $45 a yard, and nobody could stop the boys from charging less. That is private enterprise, their uncle said. The city still had rules, though, like no blowing clippings into the storm drain, and they followed them.',
    'In August they sat down with the numbers. Their gas came from a station in town, and their new trimmer had been made in China, an import. By the end of summer they had earned $2,310 between them. They split it the way the notebook said, down to the last dollar.',
  ],
};
STORIES['marketing-mix-9'] = {
  about: 'a hundred gray hoodies and twenty-three sales, a survey of eighty students, a new target market, four new choices, and a second order that paid for the first',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3361', after: 1, alt: 'A teacher writing marketing concept at the top of a whiteboard for a robotics club' }, { serial: 'S3362', after: 2, alt: 'A survey form with the school colors, the team name and twenty-five dollars circled' }, { serial: 'S3363', after: 3, alt: 'A pie chart splitting a crowd into club families, tournament fans and everyone else' }, { serial: 'S3364', after: 4, alt: 'A green hoodie with a team name on a table at a robotics tournament, a phone showing a robot video' }, { serial: 'S3365', after: 5, alt: 'A robotics club counting money beside an empty box of hoodies' }],
  title: 'The hoodies nobody bought', art: 'S3360', cast: [],
  alt: 'A table of unsold gray hoodies with a gear logo in a school hallway, students walking past',
  words: [
    'The robotics club ordered a hundred gray hoodies with a gear on the front, paid $18 each, and priced them at $35. After a month, they had sold twenty-three.',
    'Their advisor, Miss Carter, called a meeting and wrote two words on the board: marketing concept. You started with what you liked, she said. Start with the customer. Find out what they want, then make it, price it, place it and tell them about it.',
    'So the club ran a survey. Eighty students answered, and the answers were plain. They wanted the school colors and the team name, not a gear. Twenty-five dollars felt fair, and thirty-five did not. Most said they would buy at a tournament or online, never from a table in a hallway.',
    'The survey changed who they were selling to. Mass marketing, one hoodie for the whole school, had failed. Their real target market was a segment: club members, their families, and the fans who drove to robotics tournaments on Saturday mornings.',
    'The new marketing mix took one meeting. The product was a green hoodie with the team name. The price was $25, which left $10 on each one after the $15 cost. The place was a table at the regional tournament and an online order form. For promotion, a member filmed a thirty-second video of the robot scoring and posted it the night before.',
    'They sold all 120 in two weeks. The first order had cost $1,800 and brought in $805, a loss of $995. The second order cost $1,800 and brought in $3,000. Together, the second order paid for the first with $205 left over, and the gray hoodies became prizes.',
  ],
};
STORIES['business-money'] = {
  about: 'an income statement for one month, a balance sheet for one day, a pie oven that had to earn its keep, a loan with interest, and a first paycheck',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3367', after: 1, alt: 'A balance sheet on a clipboard with ovens, cash and a bank loan drawn as icons' }, { serial: 'S3368', after: 2, alt: 'A new pie oven being wheeled into a bakery kitchen' }, { serial: 'S3369', after: 3, alt: 'A chalkboard doing fifty pies of math: six hundred divided by twelve' }, { serial: 'S3370', after: 4, alt: 'A line chart of bakery sales dipping in a recession and climbing back' }, { serial: 'S3371', after: 5, alt: 'A pay stub showing gross pay, taxes taken out and net pay' }],
  title: 'The bakery\'s two statements', art: 'S3366', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage girl and her aunt at a bakery counter reading a printed income statement',
  words: [
    'Lena worked Saturdays at her aunt\'s bakery, and in April her aunt handed her a sheet of paper called an income statement. It covered March. Revenue was $12,000, and expenses were $10,500: rent of $2,000, wages of $5,500, flour and butter and sugar of $2,400, and electricity of $600. The bottom line said profit, $1,500.',
    'The second sheet was different. It was a balance sheet, a picture of the bakery on March 31 only. The ovens and equipment were worth $31,000, cash was $6,000 and supplies were $3,000, so the assets came to $40,000. The bakery owed $25,000, mostly to the bank. That left $15,000 in owner\'s equity, and assets equaled liabilities plus equity, as they always must.',
    'Her aunt was deciding whether to add pies. A pie oven would cost $600 a month to lease whether she sold one pie or a thousand, a fixed cost. Each pie would sell for $20 and cost $8 in fruit and crust, a variable cost.',
    'Lena did the math on the chalkboard. Each pie left $12 after its own cost, and 600 divided by 12 is 50. Fifty pies a month would break even, and every pie after that would be profit. Her aunt was already selling cakes to forty cafés and families a week, so fifty pies a month looked easy.',
    'The loan was the part her aunt watched most. The bank charged interest, the price of borrowing, and in the recession that began in late 2007, when the whole economy shrank, her sales had fallen hard. The payments had not. She had kept up by cutting her own pay, and she never forgot it.',
    'At the end of the month Lena got her own pay stub. Twenty hours at $12 was $240 in gross pay. After $22 was taken out for taxes, her net pay was $218, and she understood for the first time why those two numbers were different.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ethics-and-careers'] = {
  about: 'a phone repair shop, screens that cost less and cracked sooner, a choice that was legal but not honest, a union contract, and a career plan on one page',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3373', after: 1, alt: 'An uncle and his niece at a workbench with a supplier price sheet between them' }, { serial: 'S3374', after: 2, alt: 'A new price board offering a standard screen and a premium screen with different warranties' }, { serial: 'S3375', after: 3, alt: 'A city business license on a shop wall and a sales tax line on a receipt' }, { serial: 'S3376', after: 4, alt: 'A nurse in scrubs holding a union contract at a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S3377', after: 5, alt: 'A one-page career plan with interests, skills and three careers written on it' }],
  title: 'The cheaper screens', art: 'S3372', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage girl at a phone repair counter holding two replacement screens side by side',
  words: [
    'Priya had worked at her uncle\'s phone repair shop for a year when a new supplier offered replacement screens for $40 instead of $70. They looked the same. They also cracked more easily, the supplier admitted when she asked.',
    'Her uncle thought about it out loud. Nothing in the law said he had to use the better screen, and customers would never know the difference until later. Priya said that was the problem. It was legal, maybe, but it was not honest, and she would not want every customer to know exactly what they had done.',
    'They settled it together. The shop would sell both screens and say so plainly: a standard screen for $90 with a thirty-day warranty, and a premium screen for $120 with a warranty for a year. Most customers chose the premium one, and nobody came back angry.',
    'Priya started noticing the government in the shop, too. The city license hung on the wall, and every receipt added sales tax. When a customer refused to pay for a finished repair, her uncle filed in small claims court, and a judge settled it in twenty minutes.',
    'Her mother was a nurse, and that spring her union reached a new contract with the hospital. The nurses had bargained as a group for a raise and for limits on how many hours a shift could run. That is collective bargaining, her mother said, and no nurse had to argue alone.',
    'At school Priya wrote a career plan on one page. Her interests were fixing things and talking with people. Her skills were patience and fast math. Three careers fit: repair technician, store manager and entrepreneur, and she wrote down the first steps for each.',
  ],
};
// Business, grades 6 to 8 (2026-09-30, pass HF): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['earning-and-taxes'] = {
  about: 'a first job at a snow cone stand, a paycheck smaller than the math, a sales tax at the register, an apprentice cousin, and the ways to pay for school',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3379', after: 1, alt: 'A girl reading a pay stub at a kitchen table, the check beside it' }, { serial: 'S3380', after: 2, alt: 'An aunt with a calculator explaining a pay stub over dinner' }, { serial: 'S3381', after: 3, alt: 'A checkout screen showing earbuds and a total with tax' }, { serial: 'S3382', after: 4, alt: 'A young apprentice electrician in a work shirt with his name stitched on it' }, { serial: 'S3383', after: 5, alt: 'A school counselor and a student looking at a poster of ways to pay for college' }],
  title: 'The snow cone paycheck', art: 'S3378', cast: [],
  alt: 'A fifteen-year-old girl handing a snow cone across the counter of a small stand near a city pool',
  words: [
    'Marisol was fifteen when she got her first job, at a snow cone stand two blocks from the city pool. She worked twelve hours a week at $9 an hour, and before her first payday she did the math in her head: twelve times nine is $108.',
    'The check said $99.74. The stub stapled to it explained the gap: gross pay of $108, then $8.26 for Social Security and Medicare, and nothing for federal income tax, so her net pay was $99.74.',
    'Her aunt, who prepared taxes for a living, explained it over dinner. Social Security and Medicare come out of nearly every paycheck in the country. Texas has no state income tax, she said, and Marisol had earned too little for any federal income tax to be withheld yet.',
    'The second lesson came at the electronics store, where the earbuds she wanted cost $40 and the register asked for $43.30. Austin, like many Texas cities, charges the highest sales tax allowed, 8.25 percent. Marisol checked it on her phone: 8.25 percent of $40 is $3.30.',
    'Her cousin Diego came by that weekend in a work shirt with his name stitched on it. He was an apprentice electrician, paid while he trained, and he expected to earn about $20,000 a year more once his training was done. Marisol asked what that added up to, and he did the math with her: $20,000 a year for forty years is $800,000.',
    'That fall she asked the school counselor how people pay for training like his, or for college. The counselor named savings first, then grants and scholarships, which are never paid back, student loans, which are, and work-study, a part-time job that helps pay for school. Marisol opened a savings account the next day.',
  ],
};
STORIES['banking-and-credit'] = {
  about: 'a debit card that looked like a credit card, three banks and their fees, a register that balanced, an older sister\'s credit history, and a report that remembers',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3385', after: 1, alt: 'A mother drawing a debit card and a credit card side by side on a napkin' }, { serial: 'S3386', after: 2, alt: 'Three bank brochures on a table with fees circled' }, { serial: 'S3387', after: 3, alt: 'A boy writing in a small check register booklet beside a phone app' }, { serial: 'S3388', after: 4, alt: 'A young woman in her twenties holding car keys at a car dealership' }, { serial: 'S3389', after: 5, alt: 'A laptop showing a credit report with on-time payments marked' }],
  title: 'The card that was not money', art: 'S3384', cast: [],
  alt: 'A thirteen-year-old boy holding up a new debit card in a bank lobby, his mother beside him',
  words: [
    'For his thirteenth birthday, Theo\'s parents opened a student checking account with him and handed him a debit card. It looked exactly like a credit card, and Theo said so. His mother said the difference was the most important thing he would learn that year.',
    'A debit card, she explained, spends your own money right away, straight out of the account. A credit card spends the bank\'s money, and you owe it back. Pay the whole bill on time and it costs nothing extra, but leave part of it unpaid and the bank adds interest every month.',
    'They had compared three banks before choosing. One charged $5 a month unless the balance stayed above $100. Another charged $3 every time Theo used a different bank\'s ATM. The credit union they picked charged students no monthly fee and had an ATM inside the grocery store.',
    'His father gave him a check register, a small booklet for tracking the balance by hand. Theo started at $120, added $30 from mowing, subtracted $45 for a movie and snacks, and moved $20 into savings. The register said $85, and so did the bank\'s app, which meant it balanced.',
    'His sister Jada, who was twenty, told him the rest at dinner. She had paid every credit card bill on time for two years, and that record, her credit history, had just helped her get a car loan at a lower interest rate. A friend of hers with late payments had been offered a much higher one.',
    'She showed him her credit report on her laptop: her accounts, what she owed, and whether each payment had been on time. Most late payments stay on a report for seven years, she said, so the first ones matter most. Theo put the debit card in his wallet and the register in his desk drawer.',
  ],
};
STORIES['budgets-and-net-worth'] = {
  about: 'a family budget taped to the refrigerator, savings planned first, fixed and variable expenses, percents that add to one hundred, a net worth on the back, and interest on interest',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3391', after: 1, alt: 'A budget sheet with income, taxes and three savings lines highlighted' }, { serial: 'S3392', after: 2, alt: 'Two columns of expenses, fixed on one side and variable on the other' }, { serial: 'S3393', after: 3, alt: 'A girl with a calculator and a pie chart of a family budget' }, { serial: 'S3394', after: 4, alt: 'The back of the budget page with assets and liabilities listed' }, { serial: 'S3395', after: 5, alt: 'A notebook page with compound interest written at the top' }],
  title: 'The budget on the refrigerator', art: 'S3390', cast: [],
  alt: 'A twelve-year-old girl and her mother reading a budget taped to a refrigerator',
  words: [
    'Every month, Kendra\'s mother taped the family budget to the refrigerator, and the month Kendra turned twelve she asked to help. The rule, her mother said, was that every dollar had a job before the month began.',
    'The budget started with income: $6,000 a month before taxes, and taxes took $1,200. Savings came next, planned before anything was spent: $300 for emergencies, $200 for Kendra\'s college, and $400 for retirement.',
    'Then came the expenses. Rent of $1,500, the car payment of $400 and the phone plan of $100 were fixed, the same every month. Groceries, electricity, gas and everything else were variable, and together they usually came to $1,900.',
    'Kendra worked out the percents with a calculator. Rent was 1,500 divided by 6,000, or 25 percent of the budget. The savings together, $900, came to 15 percent, and taxes came to 20 percent. When she added every line, the total was $6,000 and 100 percent, which her mother said was the whole point.',
    'On the back of the page was a second list, which her mother called a net worth statement. The family owned a car worth $15,000 and had $12,000 in savings, and they owed $8,000 on the car. Assets of $27,000 minus liabilities of $8,000 left a net worth of $19,000.',
    'The savings account paid interest, and her mother showed her why the college money went in early. A thousand dollars at 5 percent earns $150 in three years with simple interest, but $157.63 when the interest earns interest too. Kendra wrote compound interest at the top of her own budget.',
  ],
};
STORIES['starting-a-small-business'] = {
  about: 'a street of barking dogs, a hand-painted sign, costs counted first, a profit split evenly, a free first walk against an app, and a dog bed bought with math',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3397', after: 1, alt: 'Two leashes and a box of waste bags on a kitchen counter beside a notebook' }, { serial: 'S3398', after: 2, alt: 'A brother and sister walking three dogs down a sidewalk' }, { serial: 'S3399', after: 3, alt: 'A phone screen showing a dog-walking app, a sister pointing at the price' }, { serial: 'S3400', after: 4, alt: 'A flyer offering a free first walk taped to a mailbox' }, { serial: 'S3401', after: 5, alt: 'A girl at a pet store comparing a sale tag and a coupon on a dog bed' }],
  title: 'Walks for hire', art: 'S3396', cast: [],
  alt: 'A twelve-year-old girl and her thirteen-year-old brother holding a hand-painted sign offering dog walks for ten dollars',
  words: [
    'Nia and her brother Marcus noticed that half the dogs on their street barked all afternoon while their owners were at work. Nia was twelve and Marcus was thirteen, and by Friday they had a sign on the corner: Dog Walks, $10.',
    'Their mother made them count the costs first. Two leashes and a box of waste bags cost $30, once. Treats and more bags would cost about $5 a week. Whatever was left from their sales after those costs would be their profit.',
    'Three neighbors signed up for walks five days a week, which made fifteen walks and $150 a week. After four weeks they had earned $600. Their costs had come to $50, so their profit was $550, and they split it evenly, $275 each.',
    'Then a dog-walking app arrived in the neighborhood, charging $20 a walk. Marcus wanted to cut their price. Nia pointed out that they already cost half as much, and that the app sent strangers. They kept their price and added an incentive instead: the first walk free for any new neighbor.',
    'Two new customers came from the free walk. Their mother explained the other incentives stores use to win buyers. A sale lowers the price for a while, a coupon takes money off at the register, and a rebate pays some back later, if you remember to mail in the form.',
    'At the pet store, Nia used the math herself. A $60 dog bed was 25 percent off, which saved $15, and she also had a $10 coupon she could use instead. The sale was the better deal by $5, and she bought the bed for the oldest dog on the street, whose owner had been their first customer.',
  ],
};
// Business, grades 3 to 5 (2026-09-30, pass HG): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['work-and-income-3'] = {
  about: 'a box of strawberries that cost more after a freeze, a market full of berries in June, a refrigerator expert, and a loaf of banana bread',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3403', after: 1, alt: 'A farmer pointing to frost-bitten strawberry blossoms in a field' }, { serial: 'S3404', after: 2, alt: 'A market table piled high with strawberries in early summer' }, { serial: 'S3405', after: 3, alt: 'A repair technician fixing a large market refrigerator' }, { serial: 'S3406', after: 4, alt: 'A girl and her grandmother taking banana bread out of an oven' }, { serial: 'S3407', after: 5, alt: 'A girl drawing three jars labeled with pictures on a notebook page' }],
  title: 'The price of strawberries', art: 'S3402', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl and her grandmother at a farmers market stand looking at a sign above boxes of strawberries',
  words: [
    'Every Saturday in April, Ana and her grandmother bought strawberries at the farmers market, and every Saturday a box cost $4. Then one week the sign said $6, and Ana asked the farmer why.',
    'He explained that a late freeze had killed many of the blossoms on his plants. He had only half as many strawberries to sell, but just as many families wanted them. When there is not enough of something for everyone who wants it, he said, the price goes up.',
    'By June the berries on the other farms were ripe, the market was full of strawberries, and the price fell back to $4. Ana wrote it in her notebook: scarce means more, plenty means less.',
    'On the way out, they passed a man fixing the market\'s big refrigerator. Her grandmother said he had trained for four years to learn how refrigerators work. Skills that take years to learn usually earn more money, she said, and people call those skills human capital.',
    'Ana thought about her own skills. She could bake banana bread with her grandmother, and a neighbor had already asked to buy a loaf for $5. That would be income, her grandmother said, money earned for work.',
    'That night they talked about what Ana would do with the five dollars. She could spend it, save it, or share some of it with the food bank. Ana decided to choose before the money came, not after, and she drew three jars in her notebook to remind herself.',
  ],
};
STORIES['spend-save-share'] = {
  about: 'a ten-dollar allowance split three ways, a skateboard that would take nine weeks, a slushie and a toy, and ten dollars for the animal shelter',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3409', after: 1, alt: 'A boy looking at a skateboard in a store window with a price tag' }, { serial: 'S3410', after: 2, alt: 'A calendar with nine weeks circled and a jar filling up' }, { serial: 'S3411', after: 3, alt: 'A boy at a checkout line holding a slushie and a small toy' }, { serial: 'S3412', after: 4, alt: 'A boy marking a tenth week on the calendar' }, { serial: 'S3413', after: 5, alt: 'A boy handing a jar of dollars to a worker at an animal shelter' }],
  title: 'Three jars', art: 'S3408', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy putting dollar bills into three glass jars on a kitchen counter',
  words: [
    'Leo turned nine and started getting an allowance of $10 a week. His mother gave him three jars and a rule: before the money was spent, every dollar needed a job.',
    'Leo wanted a skateboard that cost $36. He decided that each week $5 would go in the spend jar, $4 in the save jar, and $1 in the share jar. His mother showed him how to find the time it would take: 36 divided by 4 is 9, so nine weeks.',
    'He circled the weeks on the kitchen calendar and watched the save jar fill, one four-dollar stack at a time. By the fifth week it held $20, and he could already picture the skateboard rolling down the driveway.',
    'In the sixth week, at the store with his dad, Leo saw a slushie and a small toy by the checkout. He bought both with his save money. It was unplanned spending, and it felt good for about an hour. His dad did not say a word, which Leo later decided was on purpose.',
    'That night he counted again. The save jar was $4 short, so the skateboard would take ten weeks instead of nine. Leo was not happy, but he understood the trade: the slushie had cost him a week.',
    'In the tenth week he bought the skateboard. On the same day the share jar held $10, and Leo chose where it would go. He and his dad took it to the animal shelter, where it bought a large bag of food.',
  ],
};
STORIES['banks-and-borrowing'] = {
  about: 'twenty-five dollars of birthday money, a banker who explained three jobs, a bakery that borrowed for an oven, a five-dollar loan from a brother, and fifty cents of interest',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3415', after: 1, alt: 'A banker drawing three jobs of a bank on a small notepad' }, { serial: 'S3416', after: 2, alt: 'A baker standing next to a big new oven in a bakery kitchen' }, { serial: 'S3417', after: 3, alt: 'A girl handing five dollars back to her older brother' }, { serial: 'S3418', after: 4, alt: 'A piggy bank on a shelf next to a bank savings booklet' }, { serial: 'S3419', after: 5, alt: 'A girl reading a bank statement that shows a little interest added' }],
  title: 'The bank with the big door', art: 'S3414', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl and her father talking with a smiling banker at a desk in a bright bank lobby',
  words: [
    'For her eighth birthday, Maya got $25 from her grandparents, and her dad took her to the bank to open a savings account. The bank had a heavy door, a quiet lobby, and a banker named Mr. Lopez who drew pictures while he talked.',
    'A bank has three big jobs, he said. It keeps money safe, and the government protects the money in savings accounts. It lends money to people and businesses who need to borrow. And it pays interest to people who save, a little extra for letting the bank use their money.',
    'He pointed out the window at the bakery next door. Last year the bakery had borrowed money from this bank to buy a new oven. The baker was paying it back a little each month, plus interest. The money savers like Maya keep in the bank is the money it lends.',
    'Maya told him about the time she borrowed $5 from her brother for a movie ticket. She paid him back on Saturday, all $5, and he did not charge her anything extra. Mr. Lopez laughed and said that a bank would have charged interest, because that is how banks earn money.',
    'At home, Maya kept her piggy bank for small spending money, since it was easy to reach. But it paid nothing, and once a dollar had gone missing behind the dresser. The savings account was harder to reach, which helped her leave the money alone.',
    'A year later, her statement showed something new. Her account paid 2 percent a year, and 2 percent of $25 is 50 cents. Fifty cents was not much, Maya said, but she had not done anything to earn it, and she liked that.',
  ],
};
STORIES['my-first-stand'] = {
  about: 'a five-dollar table, forty cents a cup, thirteen dollars on the first Saturday, a rainy day that made one dollar, and a soccer game that made nineteen',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3421', after: 1, alt: 'Two children counting lemons, sugar and paper cups on a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S3422', after: 2, alt: 'A notebook with sales and expenses written in two columns' }, { serial: 'S3423', after: 3, alt: 'A lemonade stand under gray clouds with only a few customers' }, { serial: 'S3424', after: 4, alt: 'A lemonade stand next to a soccer field with a line of thirsty players' }, { serial: 'S3425', after: 5, alt: 'Two children splitting their profit evenly beside a notebook where they wrote what they learned' }],
  title: 'Lemonade on Saturday', art: 'S3420', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy and a girl behind a lemonade stand at a sunny neighborhood market',
  words: [
    'Owen and Priya were nine when they rented a table at the neighborhood market for $5 a Saturday. They planned to sell lemonade for $1 a cup.',
    'First they worked out the cost of one cup. Lemons, sugar and a paper cup came to 40 cents. The table cost $5 no matter how much they sold, so it was a fixed expense. Every cup they sold needed its own 40 cents of supplies, a variable expense.',
    'The first Saturday was sunny, and they sold 30 cups for $30. Priya did the math in their notebook. The cups cost 30 times 40 cents, which is $12, and the table was $5, so the expenses were $17. Their profit was $30 minus $17, or $13.',
    'The second Saturday it rained. They sold only 10 cups, made $10, and spent $4 on supplies. The table still cost $5, rain or shine, so their profit was just $1. Owen said that was the day he understood what a risk was.',
    'For the third Saturday, Priya had an idea. A soccer tournament was being played at the park, so they set up next to the field. They sold 40 cups, spent $16 on supplies and $5 on the table, and made $19 in profit.',
    'At the end of the month they split their profit evenly and wrote down what they had learned. Know your fixed expenses. Count your variable expenses. And go where the thirsty people are.',
  ],
};
// Business, kindergarten to grade 2 (2026-10-01, pass HH): four read-aloud stories with five pictures each.
STORIES['earning-money-k'] = {
  about: 'a baker who gets up early, the skills her job needs, the income it earns, and a birthday card that held a gift',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3427', after: 1, alt: 'A baker carefully measuring flour into a big bowl' }, { serial: 'S3428', after: 2, alt: 'A customer paying for a loaf of bread at a bakery counter' }, { serial: 'S3429', after: 3, alt: 'A girl opening a birthday card with a five dollar bill inside' }, { serial: 'S3430', after: 4, alt: 'A girl playing bakery with toy bread and a teddy bear customer' }],
  title: 'Mom at the bakery', art: 'S3426', cast: [],
  alt: 'A smiling baker taking a tray of bread from an oven while her young daughter watches',
  words: [
    'Nia\'s mom is a baker at the little shop on the corner of Pine Street. She gets up early.',
    'At the shop, Mom measures flour and salt with great care. Too much salt spoils a loaf. Measuring is a skill her job needs, and so is getting there on time.',
    'People buy the bread, and the shop pays Mom for her work. That pay is called income. Mom uses her income to buy food for the family and to pay for their home.',
    'On her birthday, Nia got a card from Grandpa with five dollars inside. Was that money income? Mom shook her head and smiled. Nia did not do a job for that money, so it was a gift.',
    'That night Nia sold pretend bread to her bear, who paid with buttons. Someday, she said, she would earn real income too.',
  ],
};
STORIES['wants-and-needs-k'] = {
  about: 'a needs list and a wants list, a trip to the moon that did not make it, and one red kite',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3432', after: 1, alt: 'A list with drawings of food, water, a coat and a house' }, { serial: 'S3433', after: 2, alt: 'A boy drawing a kite, a puppy and a rocket on a wants list' }, { serial: 'S3434', after: 3, alt: 'A dad paying for groceries at a store counter' }, { serial: 'S3435', after: 4, alt: 'A boy flying a red kite in a sunny park' }],
  title: 'Two lists on the fridge', art: 'S3431', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy and his dad at a kitchen table writing two lists on paper',
  words: [
    'On Saturday, Leo and his dad sat at the kitchen table with two sheets of paper. One said Needs, and the other said Wants.',
    'On the needs list, Dad wrote food, water, warm coats and the rent for their home. Those are things a family must have to live and be well.',
    'Leo got to fill in the wants list. He wrote a red kite, a game, a puppy and a trip to the moon. Dad laughed at the last one.',
    'Dad\'s pay comes in each week, and it does not stretch forever. So the needs list gets paid first, every single time. If money is left over, the family picks one want.',
    'This week there was enough for one want, and Leo chose the red kite. He flew it in the park on Sunday, and it was the best want of all.',
  ],
};
STORIES['save-spend-share-k'] = {
  about: 'a spend jar, a save jar and a share jar, five weeks of saving, a deposit at the bank, and a shy brown dog',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3437', after: 1, alt: 'A girl feeding a cat and folding towels' }, { serial: 'S3438', after: 2, alt: 'A girl buying a sticker at a small shop' }, { serial: 'S3439', after: 3, alt: 'A girl and her mom handing a jar of money to a bank teller' }, { serial: 'S3440', after: 4, alt: 'A girl giving money at an animal shelter while a brown dog watches' }],
  title: 'Three jars on the shelf', art: 'S3436', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl dropping a dollar into one of three glass jars on a shelf',
  words: [
    'Maya has three jars on her shelf, and each one has a word on it. The words say spend, save and share.',
    'Each week, Maya earns three dollars for feeding the cat and folding towels. She puts one dollar in each jar.',
    'The spend jar buys small things, like a sticker or a pencil. The save jar is for something bigger. One dollar each week adds up, and after five weeks Maya had five dollars.',
    'When the save jar was full, Maya and her mom took it to the bank. Putting money in the bank is called a deposit. Later, taking some out is called a withdrawal.',
    'The share jar was the one Maya loved best. She gave it to the animal shelter, where it helped buy food for a shy brown dog.',
  ],
};
STORIES['borrow-and-make-k'] = {
  about: 'a borrowed marker given back like new, bookmarks that cost three dollars to make, neighbors who bought them, and a friend\'s turn to borrow',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3442', after: 1, alt: 'A boy handing a blue marker back to his friend' }, { serial: 'S3443', after: 2, alt: 'Paper and a ribbon beside a finished bookmark on a table' }, { serial: 'S3444', after: 3, alt: 'Neighbors buying bookmarks from a boy at a small stand' }, { serial: 'S3445', after: 4, alt: 'A boy lending a pair of safety scissors to his friend' }],
  title: 'The bookmark shop', art: 'S3441', cast: [],
  alt: 'A boy making bookmarks with paper and ribbon at a porch table',
  words: [
    'Ben borrowed his friend Sam\'s blue marker to make a sign for his bookmark shop. He used it with care and put the cap back on each time.',
    'The next day, Ben gave the marker back, just as good as new. That is how to borrow well. A lost or broken marker would have been borrowing badly.',
    'Ben was a producer, because he made the bookmarks himself. Each one took paper that cost two dollars and a ribbon that cost one dollar. So each bookmark cost three dollars to make.',
    'His neighbors were the consumers. They bought the bookmarks and used them in their books.',
    'Then Sam asked to borrow Ben\'s new scissors. Ben thought about it first. Sam had given the marker back on time, so Ben said yes.',
  ],
};
// Psychology, college level (2026-10-03, pass JB): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['methods-and-measurement-c'] = {
  about: 'a horse in Berlin that seemed to do arithmetic, thirteen experts who found no trick, a psychologist who changed one thing at a time, 89 percent against 6 percent, and the reason tests are run blind',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3573', after: 1, alt: 'A panel of thirteen experts in hats and coats watching a horse closely' }, { serial: 'S3574', after: 2, alt: 'A young psychologist writing in a notebook beside a horse in a courtyard' }, { serial: 'S3575', after: 3, alt: 'A horse with a screen hiding the questioner from its view' }, { serial: 'S3576', after: 4, alt: 'A man leaning toward a horse and lifting his head slightly as the horse stops tapping' }, { serial: 'S3577', after: 5, alt: 'A modern researcher behind a screen running a test while an assistant records the answers' }],
  title: 'The horse who seemed to count', art: 'S3572', cast: [],
  alt: 'A horse tapping its hoof in a Berlin courtyard while a crowd in early 1900s clothes watches',
  words: [
    'In Berlin in the early 1900s, a retired mathematics teacher named Wilhelm von Osten showed crowds a horse he called Clever Hans. Asked to add 3 and 5, Hans tapped his hoof eight times. He seemed to read a clock and name the value of coins, and von Osten never charged a penny to watch.',
    'A commission of thirteen experts watched Hans closely and found no trick. Von Osten was not secretly signaling, and Hans answered even when strangers asked the questions. Many people took that as proof that a horse could think in numbers.',
    'A young psychologist named Oskar Pfungst was not satisfied. He wrote a hypothesis that could fail: if Hans truly calculated, he should answer just as well when the person asking did not know the answer. Then he changed one thing at a time.',
    'When von Osten knew the answer and Hans could see him, Hans was right 89 percent of the time. When von Osten did not know the answer, Hans was right only 6 percent of the time. When Hans could not see the questioner at all, his arithmetic fell apart too.',
    'Pfungst then found the cue. After asking, the questioner leaned forward slightly, and when the hoof reached the right number, he gave a tiny upward jerk of the head without knowing it. Hans stopped tapping at that jerk, and not one questioner could stop making it.',
    'Hans was not a fraud and not a genius: he was a superb reader of people. Psychologists still call it the Clever Hans effect, and it is why the person who runs a test should not know the expected answer. Von Osten himself was never persuaded.',
  ],
};
STORIES['schools-of-thought-c'] = {
  about: 'a woman in William James\'s seminars, research on memory, every doctoral requirement passed with distinction in 1895, a degree refused, a laboratory at Wellesley, and the presidency of the American Psychological Association',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3579', after: 1, alt: 'A woman taking notes at a table of men in one of William James\'s seminars' }, { serial: 'S3580', after: 2, alt: 'A woman at a desk with memory test cards and a notebook in an 1890s laboratory' }, { serial: 'S3581', after: 3, alt: 'A Harvard letter refusing a degree, lying on a desk beside finished exam papers' }, { serial: 'S3582', after: 4, alt: 'A woman setting up instruments in a new psychology laboratory at Wellesley' }, { serial: 'S3583', after: 5, alt: 'A woman at a podium as president of the American Psychological Association' }],
  title: 'The degree Harvard would not give', art: 'S3578', cast: [],
  alt: 'A woman in 1890s dress walking past a Harvard gate with books under her arm',
  words: [
    'Mary Whiton Calkins wanted to learn the new science of psychology from the best teacher she could find. In the United States, that meant William James at Harvard, whose book The Principles of Psychology appeared in 1890.',
    'Harvard did not admit women. Calkins attended James\'s seminars anyway, as a guest, and he welcomed her. James was a functionalist: he cared less about the parts of the mind than about what the mind is for, and how it helps a person adapt.',
    'Her own research was on memory: how well people recall what they have seen, and what makes some things stick. She measured it with the careful methods that Wundt\'s laboratory in Leipzig, opened in 1879, had made the standard: the same task, the same timing, and many trials.',
    'In 1895 she passed every requirement for a Harvard doctorate, and she passed with distinction. Harvard still refused to grant her the degree, because she was a woman.',
    'The year before, Margaret Floy Washburn had earned a psychology doctorate at Cornell, the first woman in the United States to do so. Calkins went back to Wellesley College, built a psychology laboratory there, taught, and kept publishing her research. Her later work argued that psychology should study the self, the person who remembers and perceives.',
    'In 1905, the American Psychological Association elected Calkins its president, the first woman to hold the office. The degree Harvard refused had not stopped the work. It had only shown which rule needed to change.',
  ],
};
STORIES['perspectives-c'] = {
  about: 'a nursing student frozen outside the library before an anatomy exam, a counselor with seven lenses, a catastrophic thought, skipped study sessions, a family group chat, and a plan that used them all',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3585', after: 1, alt: 'A young woman in a parked car pressing her hand to her pounding heart' }, { serial: 'S3586', after: 2, alt: 'A counselor and a nursing student laughing together in a small office' }, { serial: 'S3587', after: 3, alt: 'A young woman closing a textbook and walking away from a library study room' }, { serial: 'S3588', after: 4, alt: 'A phone lighting up with family messages asking about each grade' }, { serial: 'S3589', after: 5, alt: 'A young woman taking practice quizzes at a sunny desk after a good night of sleep' }],
  title: 'Seven ways to look at one night', art: 'S3584', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young woman sitting in her car outside a library at night, too nervous to go in',
  words: [
    'The night before her anatomy exam, Daniela sat in her car outside the library with her heart racing, unable to go in. She had studied for weeks. At the campus counseling center the next week, a counselor asked her to look at that night through several lenses.',
    'The biological lens came first. Her heart had pounded and her hands had shaken because her body released stress hormones. That alarm once helped people escape real danger, which is the evolutionary lens, and the counselor said it was real but aimed at the wrong target.',
    'Then the cognitive lens. What had she been telling herself in the car? That if she failed this exam, she would fail out of nursing school. The counselor asked how many exams decide a nursing degree, and Daniela laughed for the first time that week.',
    'The behavioral lens explained why it was getting worse. Each time she skipped a study session to calm down, the relief rewarded the skipping, so the skipping grew. The psychodynamic lens asked an older question: whose disappointment was she afraid of?',
    'The humanistic lens asked whether she felt worthy only when she earned an A. The sociocultural lens looked at her setting: a program that cut a fifth of its students each semester, and a family group chat that asked about every grade.',
    'No single lens held the whole truth, so the plan used them all. It had sleep before exams, a sentence to answer the catastrophic thought, practice quizzes instead of skipped sessions, and a talk with her family. Psychologists call that mix the biopsychosocial model. Daniela passed anatomy with a B and kept the plan.',
  ],
};
STORIES['brain-and-behavior-c'] = {
  about: 'a young man with severe seizures, an experimental operation in Hartford in 1953, a psychologist on the night train from Montreal, a star traced in a mirror, and the discovery that memory is not one thing',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3591', after: 1, alt: 'A man in 1950s clothes chatting warmly, then puzzled minutes later about the conversation' }, { serial: 'S3592', after: 2, alt: 'A woman with a briefcase stepping off a night train at a 1950s station' }, { serial: 'S3593', after: 3, alt: 'A man tracing a star while watching his hand in a mirror' }, { serial: 'S3594', after: 4, alt: 'A simple brain illustration with the hippocampus and the cerebellum shaded in two colors' }, { serial: 'S3595', after: 5, alt: 'An elderly patient smiling beside a researcher with a notebook, after a lifetime of memory studies' }],
  title: 'The man who lived in the present', art: 'S3590', cast: [],
  alt: 'A young man in 1950s clothes sitting on a hospital bed in Hartford',
  words: [
    'By 1953, Henry Molaison was twenty-seven and having seizures so severe that daily life had become dangerous, and medicines had not stopped them. A neurosurgeon in Hartford, Connecticut, William Beecher Scoville, offered an experimental operation.',
    'Scoville removed tissue from both sides of the brain, including most of the hippocampus on each side. The seizures eased. But Henry could no longer form new long-term memories: he could hold a pleasant conversation, and minutes later not remember that it had happened.',
    'A psychologist from Montreal, Brenda Milner, began taking the night train to Hartford to test him. His memories from long before the operation were mostly there, and his language was intact. What was gone was the step that turns new experience into lasting memory.',
    'Then Milner gave him a task: trace a star while seeing his hand only in a mirror, where every movement looks reversed. Day after day he got better at it. Each day he said he had never done it before.',
    'That was the discovery. The hippocampus is needed to form new memories of facts and events. A skill like mirror drawing is learned by other parts of the brain, such as the cerebellum and deeper structures that guide movement. Memory is not one thing.',
    'To protect his privacy, the world knew him only as H.M., and he took part in studies for the rest of his life. When he died in 2008, at eighty-two, his name was published at last. He is perhaps the most studied patient in the history of memory science.',
  ],
};
// College Principles of Macroeconomics (2026-10-03, pass JC): four true stories, told for readers, every fact checked.
STORIES['scarcity-and-markets-c'] = {
  about: 'a workshop of ten pin makers, a gap that was probably exaggerated, and the idea that held anyway',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3597', after: 1, alt: 'One worker drawing out wire while another straightens it and a third cuts it' }, { serial: 'S3598', after: 2, alt: 'A lone untrained worker struggling to finish a single pin at a cluttered bench' }, { serial: 'S3599', after: 3, alt: 'A historian comparing an old French report with a copy of The Wealth of Nations' }, { serial: 'S3600', after: 4, alt: 'Two countries trading goods across the sea, each making what it gives up least to make' }, { serial: 'S3601', after: 5, alt: 'Pins pouring from a modern machine beside workers each doing one job' }],
  title: 'Ten workers and forty-eight thousand pins', art: 'S3596', cast: [],
  alt: 'A small eighteenth-century workshop where ten workers each do one step of making pins',
  // Retold 2026-10-03 (pass JF, Mikey: say what The Wealth of Nations is, and give the pins room).
  words: [
    'In 1776, a Scottish philosopher named Adam Smith published An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations, a book now called simply The Wealth of Nations. It asked why some countries grow rich while others stay poor. Smith could have opened with kings or gold. He opened with a workshop that made pins.',
    'A pin seems too small to matter, which was the point. Making one, Smith wrote, took about eighteen steps. One worker drew out the wire, another straightened it, a third cut it and a fourth sharpened the point. In a workshop he said he had seen, ten men shared the steps and made about forty-eight thousand pins a day.',
    'Then he asked what one untrained worker could do alone. Without the practice or the tools, such a worker could not make twenty pins a day, and perhaps not a single pin. That gap was his whole argument. Specialized jobs multiply what people make, because each worker grows quick at one task and no time is lost switching.',
    'Historians have since checked his figures. Smith appears to have taken them from French books about pin making. A French report from 1723 suggests a pin maker working alone could make about two thousand pins a day, far more than Smith allowed. His gap was exaggerated, though dividing the work still more than doubled what each worker made.',
    'About forty years later, the English economist David Ricardo carried the idea across borders. In 1817 he argued that each country should specialize in what it gives up least to make, its comparative advantage, and trade for the rest. Both gain even when one is better at making everything. His example had England trading its cloth across the sea for Portugal\'s wine.',
    'Today pins pour from modern machines, each part doing one job, but Smith\'s question still sits at the heart of economics. Time and skill are scarce, so every hour spent on one task has an opportunity cost. People and countries grow richer by doing what they give up least to do and trading for the rest.',
  ],
};
STORIES['measuring-the-economy-c'] = {
  about: 'a country in the Depression that could not measure its own fall, and the economist who built the first gauge',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3603', after: 1, alt: 'An economist and a small team at desks piled with records from farms, mines and factories' }, { serial: 'S3604', after: 2, alt: 'Members of Congress reading the first estimate of the nation\'s income in 1934' }, { serial: 'S3605', after: 3, alt: 'A mother cooking dinner at home while children play nearby, work no one counted' }, { serial: 'S3606', after: 4, alt: 'A single number on a chalkboard beside a family whose well-being it cannot show' }, { serial: 'S3607', after: 5, alt: 'A modern newsroom screen showing a rising growth line' }],
  title: 'The number that came with a warning', art: 'S3602', cast: [],
  alt: 'A long line of men in 1930s coats outside closed factories during the Great Depression',
  words: [
    'In 1932, the United States was sinking deeper into the Great Depression, and nobody could say how far it had fallen. Presidents and senators were steering blind. There was no measure of the whole economy, only scattered figures on freight cars, factories and stock prices.',
    'The Senate asked for one, and the job went to Simon Kuznets, an economist who had come to America from Russia. He and a small team added up the income earned in every industry, from farms and mines to factories and shops.',
    'In January 1934 they delivered National Income, 1929-1932. It showed that the nation\'s income had roughly halved in three years. For the first time, Congress could see the size of the hole it was trying to fill.',
    'Kuznets made hard choices about what to count. Work sold in a market went in. Work done at home for free, such as cooking and caring for children, stayed out, and so did illegal trade such as bootlegging.',
    'Then he added a warning that is still quoted. The welfare of a nation, he wrote, can scarcely be inferred from a measurement of national income. A number can tell you how much was produced, not how well people live.',
    'His accounts grew into today\'s GDP, the value of final goods and services produced in a year, and in 1971 Kuznets won the Nobel Prize. Every headline about growth or recession still runs on his idea, and still needs his warning.',
  ],
};
STORIES['money-and-banking-c'] = {
  about: 'an island whose money was carved from stone, a fortune nobody had seen, and the belief that makes any money work',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3609', after: 1, alt: 'Villagers agreeing on a new owner for a huge stone wheel that never moves' }, { serial: 'S3610', after: 2, alt: 'A boat in a storm, a great stone wheel slipping into the dark sea' }, { serial: 'S3611', after: 3, alt: 'A stone wheel, a gold bar and a bank computer side by side' }, { serial: 'S3612', after: 4, alt: 'An official painting a black cross on a stone wheel beside a muddy road' }, { serial: 'S3613', after: 5, alt: 'A dollar bill, a stone wheel and a bank card balanced on a scale' }],
  title: 'The stone at the bottom of the sea', art: 'S3608', cast: [],
  alt: 'A giant stone wheel with a hole in its center leaning against a palm-thatched house',
  words: [
    'On the Pacific island of Yap, money was carved from stone. The wheels, called fei, were quarried from limestone on islands hundreds of miles away and carried home across the sea on small boats. The largest stood taller than a man.',
    'The biggest stones were almost never moved. When one changed hands, everyone simply agreed on its new owner, and it stayed where it stood. A stone could change owners many times without anyone lifting it. William Henry Furness, an American who lived on Yap for several months around 1903, found the whole island keeping track.',
    'One family was counted rich because of a stone none of them had ever seen. Generations earlier, it had sunk in a storm on the way home. The island agreed it still counted, Furness wrote, as surely as if it leaned against the owner\'s house.',
    'In 1991, the economist Milton Friedman retold the story to make a point about money everywhere. Money works because people accept it, whether it is stone, gold or a number in a bank\'s computer. Most dollars today exist only as entries in accounts.',
    'Yap also showed money\'s weak spot. When German officials wanted the roads repaired, they painted black crosses on the stones to claim them, and owners suddenly felt poorer. The roads were fixed, the crosses came off, and nothing had moved but belief.',
    'A dollar, a stone wheel and a bank deposit share one secret. Money is a medium of exchange, a unit of account and a store of value only as long as people trust it, which is why central banks guard that trust.',
  ],
};
STORIES['policy-and-the-world-c'] = {
  about: 'a decade of rising prices, a Fed chairman who let interest rates soar, and who paid for the cure',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3615', after: 1, alt: 'A president shaking hands with a very tall new Fed chairman in 1979' }, { serial: 'S3616', after: 2, alt: 'Farmers on tractors parked outside a white stone building in Washington' }, { serial: 'S3617', after: 3, alt: 'Laid-off workers in winter coats lining up at an unemployment office in 1982' }, { serial: 'S3618', after: 4, alt: 'Store prices holding steady on a 1983 grocery shelf' }, { serial: 'S3619', after: 5, alt: 'Two economists weighing jobs against prices on an old balance scale' }],
  title: 'The year rates hit twenty percent', art: 'S3614', cast: [],
  alt: 'A very tall man with a cigar studying rising prices at a long meeting table in 1979',
  words: [
    'By 1979, American prices had been rising fast for most of a decade, and people expected them to keep rising. In 1979 alone, consumer prices rose more than 10 percent. Workers asked for raises to keep up, stores raised prices to pay them, and the expectation fed itself.',
    'That summer, President Jimmy Carter named Paul Volcker to lead the Federal Reserve. In October, Volcker changed course: the Fed would squeeze the growth of money and let interest rates go wherever that took them.',
    'They went very high. The federal funds rate reached about 20 percent, and mortgage rates passed 18 percent. Builders stopped building, car dealers stopped selling, and farmers drove tractors to the Fed\'s building in Washington in protest.',
    'The cost landed on workers. Manufacturing, construction and car making were hit hardest. Two recessions followed, and in late 1982 unemployment reached 10.8 percent, the highest since the Great Depression. In the aggregate demand and supply model, the Fed had shifted demand to the left on purpose.',
    'It worked. Inflation, near 15 percent in early 1980, fell below 4 percent by 1983, and the belief that prices would always race finally broke. Growth returned, and inflation stayed low for most of the next forty years.',
    'Economists still argue over whether the pain could have been smaller. They agree on the lesson. When a central bank fights inflation with higher rates, it trades jobs now for steadier prices later, and someone always pays.',
  ],
};
// College Public Speaking (2026-10-03, pass JD): four true stories, told for readers, every fact checked.
STORIES['communication-and-audience-c'] = {
  about: 'two speakers at a new cemetery, one audience, and the speech that understood the occasion',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3621', after: 1, alt: 'A famous orator speaking for two hours from memory to a weary crowd' }, { serial: 'S3622', after: 2, alt: 'A tall president speaking briefly beside rows of fresh graves' }, { serial: 'S3623', after: 3, alt: 'A crowd outdoors listening closely as a short speech moves from past to future' }, { serial: 'S3624', after: 4, alt: 'An older orator writing a generous letter to the president by lamplight' }, { serial: 'S3625', after: 5, alt: 'Schoolchildren today reciting the same short speech in a classroom' }],
  title: 'Two hours and two minutes', art: 'S3620', cast: [],
  alt: 'A crowd on a November hillside at Gettysburg in 1863, a tall man in a black coat rising to speak',
  words: [
    'On November 19, 1863, a crowd gathered at Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, to dedicate a cemetery for the soldiers killed there that summer. The main speaker was Edward Everett, the most celebrated orator in the country. Abraham Lincoln had been asked only for a few appropriate remarks.',
    'Everett spoke for two hours, from memory, retelling the battle in rich detail. It was a fine speech of its kind, built for an audience that expected long oratory. Then the program went on, with music, and Lincoln rose near the end.',
    'Lincoln spoke 272 words in about two minutes. He had read the occasion, not just the topic. The crowd was standing among fresh graves, so he spoke about why the dead had died and what the living now owed them.',
    'His speech moved through time, from the nation\'s birth to the war in the present to the future the living had to secure. That chronological order let every listener follow without notes. He chose plain words that a crowd could hear outdoors, with no microphone.',
    'The next day, Everett wrote to the president with rare generosity. He would be glad, he wrote, if he could flatter himself that he came as near to the central idea of the occasion in two hours as Lincoln did in two minutes.',
    'Audience analysis means reading the occasion as well as the people. Everett gave the crowd what the program expected; Lincoln gave it what the moment needed, and his short speech is the one schoolchildren still recite.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ethics-and-evidence-c'] = {
  about: 'an invitation to celebrate the Fourth of July, and a speaker who used his audience\'s own laws as his evidence',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3627', after: 1, alt: 'A speaker pausing at a lectern as Fourth of July bunting hangs behind him' }, { serial: 'S3628', after: 2, alt: 'Thick law books stacked on a lectern as evidence beside a speaker\'s notes' }, { serial: 'S3629', after: 3, alt: 'An old Virginia law book open to a long list of crimes' }, { serial: 'S3630', after: 4, alt: 'Listeners leaning forward in their seats as the reasoning lands' }, { serial: 'S3631', after: 5, alt: 'A speaker\'s final question hanging over a silent audience' }],
  title: 'The point that was already conceded', art: 'S3626', cast: [],
  alt: 'A dignified speaker at a lectern before a crowded hall in Rochester in 1852',
  words: [
    'In 1852, the Rochester Ladies\' Anti-Slavery Society in New York invited Frederick Douglass to speak for the Fourth of July. Douglass, who had escaped slavery, chose to speak on July 5 instead, the day after the holiday. The hall was filled mostly with white listeners who opposed slavery.',
    'He began by honoring the founders and their courage. Then he turned. This Fourth of July is yours, not mine, he told them. You may rejoice; I must mourn. The audience, warmed by his praise of the founders, now had to hear the other half of the story.',
    'Then he did something careful with evidence. Must I undertake to prove that the slave is a man, he asked. That point, he said, is conceded already, and he proved it with the audience\'s own laws.',
    'There were seventy-two crimes in Virginia, he said, that carried the death penalty for a black man and only two for a white man. No one writes such laws for cattle or for dogs. The law itself admitted the humanity it denied.',
    'That is ethical and airtight reasoning: evidence the listeners could not dismiss, drawn from their own records, and a refusal to waste time proving what everyone already knew. His anger was real, and every bit of it was backed by fact.',
    'Douglass asked the question that named the speech: what, to the American slave, is your Fourth of July? Strong evidence does not have to be new. Sometimes the most persuasive source is one the audience already trusts.',
  ],
};
STORIES['organizing-and-delivering-c'] = {
  about: 'a commencement speech built on three stories, a preview, signposts and a closing line borrowed with credit',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3633', after: 1, alt: 'Three open books on a podium, one for each of three stories' }, { serial: 'S3634', after: 2, alt: 'A student practicing calligraphy, the lettering flowing across the page' }, { serial: 'S3635', after: 3, alt: 'Three signposts along a path, each pointing to the next part of a life' }, { serial: 'S3636', after: 4, alt: 'The back cover of an old catalog showing a country road at dawn' }, { serial: 'S3637', after: 5, alt: 'An audience of millions watching the same speech on phones and laptops' }],
  title: 'Just three stories', art: 'S3632', cast: [],
  alt: 'A man in a black robe at a Stanford podium before the graduating class on a sunny day',
  words: [
    'On June 12, 2005, Steve Jobs stood before the graduating class at Stanford University. He had never finished college himself, and he did not try to sound like a professor. He opened with a plan the audience could hold in one hand.',
    'Today I want to tell you three stories from my life, he said. That is it, no big deal, just three stories. In one breath he had given his thesis and a preview, and every listener knew how long the speech would take.',
    'The first story was about connecting the dots: dropping out of college, then sitting in on a calligraphy class whose lettering ended up in the first Macintosh. The second was about love and loss: being fired from the company he had started. The third was about death.',
    'Each story began with a signpost, first, second, third, so no one got lost. Each ended with one lesson in a single line. The pattern was topical, three parts of one life, and it was easy to remember.',
    'He closed with a line he did not write. The final issue of The Whole Earth Catalog, he told them, had ended with the words stay hungry, stay foolish. He named the source, said what it meant to him, and wished it for them.',
    'A clear thesis, a preview, three main points with signposts, and a borrowed line credited aloud: the speech runs about fifteen minutes and has been watched by millions. Organization is what lets an audience remember.',
  ],
};
STORIES['informing-and-persuading-c'] = {
  about: 'a persuasive speech with a prepared text, a gospel singer\'s call, and the extemporaneous passage that became its heart',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3639', after: 1, alt: 'A giant bank check returned to a crowd, a symbol of a broken promise' }, { serial: 'S3640', after: 2, alt: 'A gospel singer calling out from the platform behind a speaker at a lectern' }, { serial: 'S3641', after: 3, alt: 'A speaker rehearsing the same theme in a Detroit church months earlier' }, { serial: 'S3642', after: 4, alt: 'A crowd frozen in attention as a speech rises into its most famous passage' }, { serial: 'S3643', after: 5, alt: 'A worn speech draft pushed aside on a lectern, the speaker looking out at his audience' }],
  title: 'Tell them about the dream', art: 'S3638', cast: [],
  alt: 'A vast crowd stretching from the Lincoln Memorial toward the Washington Monument on a summer day in 1963',
  words: [
    'On August 28, 1963, hundreds of thousands of people filled the National Mall for the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom. Martin Luther King Jr. was the last speaker of a long, hot day. He had a prepared text, and he began by reading it.',
    'It was a persuasive speech on a question of policy. The nation had written a promissory note of liberty, he said, and had given Black Americans a bad check. The claim was logos, the image was pathos, and his years of leadership supplied the ethos.',
    'Then, according to his adviser Clarence Jones, the gospel singer Mahalia Jackson called out from the platform: tell them about the dream, Martin. King moved his text to the side of the lectern and began to speak without it.',
    'The dream was not new. He had used the theme in Detroit that June and in earlier speeches. Because he had practiced the ideas without memorizing the words, he could carry them anywhere. That is extemporaneous speaking at its best.',
    'Some who were there remember a hesitation, and it is not certain that King heard Jackson at all. What is certain is that the improvised passage, built on repetition and vivid images, is the part of the speech the world remembers.',
    'Persuasion is preparation that leaves room to listen. King knew his audience, his evidence and his purpose so well that when the moment called for something else, he could give it, and a policy speech became a vision.',
  ],
};
// Business, college level (2026-10-01, pass HK): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['business-and-its-world-c'] = {
  about: 'a salsa maker in San Antonio, a fall at a market booth and an LLC, a bookkeeper who found the missing cash, an order from Monterrey, and the peso',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3447', after: 1, alt: 'A woman signing business papers at a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S3448', after: 2, alt: 'A bookkeeper pointing to unpaid invoices on a laptop screen' }, { serial: 'S3449', after: 3, alt: 'Pallets of salsa jars being loaded onto a truck bound for Mexico' }, { serial: 'S3450', after: 4, alt: 'A chart of the dollar and the peso beside a jar of salsa' }, { serial: 'S3451', after: 5, alt: 'A woman checking her cash and the exchange rate on a laptop on a Monday morning' }],
  title: 'Salsa across the border', art: 'S3446', cast: [],
  alt: 'A woman in a commercial kitchen filling jars of salsa, with crates of chiles behind her',
  words: [
    'Ana Reyes started selling salsa at a San Antonio farmers market with a folding table, a cooler and her grandmother\'s recipe. She was a sole proprietor, which cost nothing to set up and meant every dollar of profit was hers.',
    'It also meant every debt was hers. When a customer slipped on a spill at her booth and threatened to sue, Ana learned what unlimited liability means: her savings and her car could be on the line. That spring she formed a limited liability company, so the business\'s debts would stay with the business.',
    'Sales doubled, yet the bank account kept running low. The bookkeeper she finally hired found the reason in a week: three stores owed her for salsa they had already sold. Strong sales and careless accounting had nearly run her out of cash.',
    'Then a grocery chain in Monterrey asked for two thousand jars. Exporting meant new labels, new paperwork and a customs broker, but under the trade agreement among the United States, Mexico and Canada, her salsa qualified to cross without a tariff. Her chiles came from farms in Chihuahua, where they grow cheaply in huge amounts, so trade ran both ways.',
    'The exchange rate taught her the last lesson. She sold each jar to the distributor for $5. At 17 pesos to the dollar, that jar cost the store 85 pesos. When the dollar strengthened to 20 pesos, the same $5 jar cost 100 pesos, and the store slowed its orders.',
    'Ana now reads three things every Monday morning: her cash, her receivables and the exchange rate. None of them is salsa, and all of them decide whether she can keep making it.',
  ],
};
STORIES['management-c'] = {
  about: 'a warehouse that lost twelve workers a month, a manager who fixed the air before the speeches, recognition that raised effort, and a SWOT of a new scanner',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3453', after: 1, alt: 'A manager pinning a schedule and a map of the floor zones on a board' }, { serial: 'S3454', after: 2, alt: 'A repair crew fixing a large air conditioning unit on a warehouse roof' }, { serial: 'S3455', after: 3, alt: 'Workers gathered around a board covered with notes thanking teammates' }, { serial: 'S3456', after: 4, alt: 'A worker scanning boxes with a handheld scanner beside a laptop' }, { serial: 'S3457', after: 5, alt: 'A chart of rising orders and falling quits taped to an office wall' }],
  title: 'The night shift that kept quitting', art: 'S3452', cast: [],
  alt: 'A night-shift manager talking with workers between tall warehouse shelves',
  words: [
    'Marcus took over the night shift at a distribution center that lost about twelve workers a month. His boss wanted a motivational speech. Marcus wanted a plan first.',
    'He started with the four functions. He planned a schedule posted two weeks ahead instead of two days. He organized the floor into zones, each with its own team. He would lead with short huddles where the team decided how to fix problems, and he would control by tracking orders per hour and errors every night.',
    'The first fixes were not inspiring, and he knew it. The air conditioning had been broken since June, and the schedule changed without warning. Herzberg called these hygiene factors: getting them right does not make anyone love the job, but getting them wrong makes people leave. Marcus fixed the air before he gave a single speech.',
    'Then he added the motivators. A board by the break room listed the best fix of the week and who found it. The strongest worker in each zone became a team lead with real responsibility. Recognition cost almost nothing, and effort rose anyway.',
    'When the company installed a new scanning system, Marcus ran a SWOT with his leads. Its strength was speed, and its weakness was that it stopped when the Wi-Fi dropped. Same-day orders were the opportunity. The threat was a ransomware attack, so they kept a paper backup in every zone.',
    'By spring, quits had fallen to three a month, and orders per hour had climbed from 90 to 110. Marcus still has not given the motivational speech.',
  ],
};
STORIES['money-and-markets-c'] = {
  about: 'a bakery that needed a forty-thousand-dollar oven, a balance sheet that had to balance, a current ratio the bank checked, and a cousin who wanted a quarter of the business',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3459', after: 1, alt: 'A balance sheet with assets, liabilities and equity on a clipboard' }, { serial: 'S3460', after: 2, alt: 'A banker checking a bakery\'s current ratio before approving a loan' }, { serial: 'S3461', after: 3, alt: 'A woman and her cousin talking at a café table over coffee' }, { serial: 'S3462', after: 4, alt: 'A new commercial oven being installed in a bakery kitchen' }, { serial: 'S3463', after: 5, alt: 'A man explaining stocks and bonds at a Thanksgiving table' }],
  title: 'Two ways to pay for a second oven', art: 'S3458', cast: [],
  alt: 'A baker standing beside an old oven with a brochure for a new one in her hand',
  words: [
    'Grace needed a second oven. Her bakery sold out by ten every morning, and the new oven cost $40,000. She had two ways to pay for it: borrow the money, or sell part of the business.',
    'First she read her balance sheet. The bakery owned $120,000 in assets and owed $70,000 in liabilities, so her owners\' equity was $50,000. Assets equal liabilities plus equity, and hers did.',
    'The bank wanted to know whether she could pay her bills over the next year, so it checked her current ratio. Current assets were $36,000 and current liabilities were $24,000, a ratio of 1.5. The bank required at least 1.2, so she qualified for a five-year loan at 8 percent, about $811 a month.',
    'Her cousin offered another way. He would invest the $40,000 for a quarter of the bakery. Grace would never have to repay it, but a quarter of every future profit would be his, and so would a voice in every big decision.',
    'Grace chose the loan, which was debt financing. She kept full control, and the interest was a known cost. When the oven arrived, her balance sheet still balanced: assets rose to $160,000 and liabilities to $110,000, and her equity stayed at $50,000.',
    'Her cousin bought a few shares of a bakery chain\'s stock instead, and some government bonds. He explained it at Thanksgiving: the stock made him a small owner, and the bonds made him a lender. Grace said she would stick to bread.',
  ],
};
STORIES['ethics-and-government-c'] = {
  about: 'a wheel that could come off a toy truck, a quiet fix that would have been a lapse, a recall that cost a week\'s profit, and a dilemma about moving production',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3465', after: 1, alt: 'A woman testing a toy wheel with a small parts cylinder' }, { serial: 'S3466', after: 2, alt: 'A small team meeting around a table covered with toy trucks' }, { serial: 'S3467', after: 3, alt: 'Parents mailing toy trucks back in boxes for refunds' }, { serial: 'S3468', after: 4, alt: 'A woman comparing an overseas factory quote with her workshop payroll' }, { serial: 'S3469', after: 5, alt: 'A toy maker changing the price on a sign while her local team builds trucks' }],
  title: 'The recall that cost a week\'s profit', art: 'S3464', cast: [],
  alt: 'A toy maker examining a small wooden truck with a loose wheel at her workbench',
  words: [
    'Hannah Cole made wooden toy trucks in a workshop outside Austin, with eleven employees and a strong holiday season ahead. In October, a quality test found that one wheel could pop off and fit inside a choking-test cylinder.',
    'Her production manager had a quiet suggestion: change the wheel design for new batches and say nothing about the four thousand trucks already sold. It would cost almost nothing. Hannah recognized it at once as an ethical lapse, knowing something was wrong and choosing it anyway.',
    'She called her team together and laid out the stakeholders. They were the children playing with the trucks, the parents who bought them, the stores that sold them and the employees whose jobs depended on the company\'s name. Federal law also required her to report the hazard to the Consumer Product Safety Commission quickly, and she did.',
    'The recall cost about a week\'s profit. Parents mailed back the trucks, and every one got a refund and a new set of wheels. Several stores said the honest recall made them more willing to stock her toys, not less.',
    'The harder question came in January. A factory overseas offered to build the trucks for a third less. Moving would keep prices low, but it could cost her eleven workers their jobs. That was an ethical dilemma, two defensible claims pulling in opposite directions, and Hannah spent weeks on it.',
    'She kept production local and raised prices by two dollars a truck. Being profitable came first, and obeying the law, but she decided that being ethical and being a good employer were part of what her customers were paying for.',
  ],
};
// Health, grades 6 to 8 (2026-10-01, pass HM): four stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['body-and-hygiene-6'] = {
  about: 'a scout weekend in the Hill Country, an inhaler at midnight, a tick on the back of a knee, repellent at dusk, a website selling garlic pills, and a clean check-up',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3471', after: 1, alt: 'A boy helping his tentmate sit up and use an inhaler in a tent at night' }, { serial: 'S3472', after: 2, alt: 'A scoutmaster removing a tick from a boy\'s knee with fine-tipped tweezers' }, { serial: 'S3473', after: 3, alt: 'Scouts putting on insect repellent and long sleeves by a creek at dusk' }, { serial: 'S3474', after: 4, alt: 'Two boys comparing a website selling garlic pills with a CDC page on a laptop' }, { serial: 'S3475', after: 5, alt: 'A dentist showing a boy a clean dental x-ray at a check-up' }],
  title: 'The tick on the trail', art: 'S3470', cast: [],
  alt: 'A twelve-year-old scout packing sunscreen, a water bottle and insect repellent for a hiking trip',
  words: [
    'Mateo was twelve when his scout troop spent a June weekend hiking in the Texas Hill Country. He packed sunscreen, a water bottle, insect repellent and his toothbrush, which his mother checked twice.',
    'On the first night, his tentmate Owen started wheezing. Mateo knew the steps from health class. He helped Owen sit up, handed him his inhaler and ran for the scoutmaster, ready to call 911 if Owen\'s breathing did not improve. After two puffs and a few slow minutes, it did.',
    'The next afternoon, after a trail through tall grass, the scoutmaster called a tick check. Mateo found one on the back of his knee. With fine-tipped tweezers, she grasped it close to the skin, pulled straight up with steady pressure and cleaned the spot. She told him to watch for a spreading red rash or a fever over the next few weeks, the early signs of Lyme disease.',
    'At dusk the mosquitoes rose over the creek. The scoutmaster explained that mosquitoes can carry West Nile virus. Most people who catch it never notice, but some get very sick, so the whole troop put on repellent and long sleeves before the campfire.',
    'Back home, Owen found a website claiming that garlic pills keep ticks away. Mateo asked the questions from health class: who wrote it, what evidence did it give, and was it selling something? It was selling the garlic pills. The CDC page recommended repellent and tick checks instead.',
    'On Monday, Mateo had his yearly check-up and a dental cleaning. The dentist found no cavities and said the twice-a-day brushing was working. The nurse reminded his mother that a vaccine was due before seventh grade, and Mateo, who now knew how vaccines train the immune system, did not complain.',
  ],
};
STORIES['eating-and-moving-6'] = {
  about: 'a grandfather who needed less sodium, two cans of soup and a little multiplication, milk and beans, evening walks around the block, and a sports drink ad',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3477', after: 1, alt: 'A girl comparing the labels on two soup cans in a grocery aisle' }, { serial: 'S3478', after: 2, alt: 'A girl pouring a glass of milk beside a bowl of beans' }, { serial: 'S3479', after: 3, alt: 'Fresh green beans and a no-salt-added can on a kitchen counter' }, { serial: 'S3480', after: 4, alt: 'A girl and her grandfather walking around the block at sunset' }, { serial: 'S3481', after: 5, alt: 'A girl filling her water bottle at a soccer field' }],
  title: 'The soup aisle', art: 'S3476', cast: [],
  alt: 'An eleven-year-old girl holding a grocery list in a busy store',
  words: [
    'Ana was eleven when her grandfather moved in, and she took charge of the grocery list. His doctor had sent one instruction: less sodium. She had never read a food label in her life.',
    'In the soup aisle, she compared two cans of chicken noodle. The regular can listed 890 milligrams of sodium per serving, and the can held two servings. The lower-sodium can listed 480. If her grandfather ate the whole regular can, that was 1,780 milligrams.',
    'Her grandfather wanted milk with dinner, and Ana learned why it mattered for her, too. Kids ages 9 to 18 need about 1,300 milligrams of calcium a day, because most bone is built by the end of the teens. She also needed about 8 milligrams of iron a day, and the beans on the list had some.',
    'The canned green beans came in a no-salt-added version, and the fresh ones were cheaper that week anyway. Her grandfather said fresh food was what he had grown up on, and he could taste the difference.',
    'After dinner they started walking around the block, twenty minutes the first night and thirty by the end of the week. Ana already got her sixty minutes a day between recess and soccer practice, so the walks were for him, and for the stories he told on the way.',
    'On Saturday a commercial promised that a sports drink would make her play like a champion. Ana checked the label and found 34 grams of added sugar. She filled her water bottle instead and still scored twice.',
  ],
};
STORIES['staying-safe-6'] = {
  about: 'a cruel photo in a seventh-grade group chat, screenshots and a counselor, a bystander who came back, a cut arm and a clean shirt, and two safety plans',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3483', after: 1, alt: 'A girl taking screenshots of mean messages on her brother\'s phone' }, { serial: 'S3484', after: 2, alt: 'A mother and her son meeting with a school counselor' }, { serial: 'S3485', after: 3, alt: 'A boy typing an apology message to a classmate at night' }, { serial: 'S3486', after: 4, alt: 'A boy pressing a clean shirt on his friend\'s cut arm beside a bike' }, { serial: 'S3487', after: 5, alt: 'Two boys writing safety plans at a kitchen table with their phones set aside' }],
  title: 'The group chat', art: 'S3482', cast: [],
  alt: 'A seventh-grade boy looking at a group chat on his phone with a worried face',
  words: [
    'It started with one photo. Someone in Kai\'s seventh-grade group chat posted a picture of Leo tripping in the hallway, with a caption that got meaner with every reply.',
    'Leo stopped answering, and the next morning he would not come out of his room. His older sister saw the messages and did what their health teacher had taught. She told Leo not to answer back, took screenshots of every message, and helped him block and report the accounts.',
    'Then they told their mother, who called the school counselor. The counselor explained that under Texas\'s David\'s Law, the school could act on cyberbullying even though it happened at night and off campus.',
    'Kai had laughed at the first reply and said nothing after that. That night he sent Leo a private message saying he was sorry, he had seen it, and he would not share it. The next day he reported the post too.',
    'A week later, Leo and Kai were riding bikes when Kai skidded on gravel and cut his arm. Leo pressed a clean shirt firmly on the cut and kept pressing while Kai\'s mother drove over. The bleeding slowed in a few minutes.',
    'That weekend, both boys wrote personal safety plans: who to call, where to go and what to do. Leo also changed his passwords to long sentences only he would think of and set his accounts to private. The photo was gone from the group chat by Monday.',
  ],
};
STORIES['substance-free-6'] = {
  about: 'an offer of focus pills at a bus stop, a medicine cabinet that needed a lock, a take-back site, a cold medicine double dose, and vapes shaped like highlighters',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3489', after: 1, alt: 'A boy walking away from an older kid holding out pills at a bus stop' }, { serial: 'S3490', after: 2, alt: 'A woman and her nephew sorting old medicines from a medicine cabinet' }, { serial: 'S3491', after: 3, alt: 'A pharmacist showing a boy two cold medicine labels at a counter' }, { serial: 'S3492', after: 4, alt: 'A boy and a girl reading a science poster about vapes at school' }, { serial: 'S3493', after: 5, alt: 'A boy smiling at a science test marked B-plus' }],
  title: 'The pills at the bus stop', art: 'S3488', cast: [],
  alt: 'A thirteen-year-old boy waiting at a bus stop with his backpack before school',
  words: [
    'Jalen was thirteen and tired before every big test. At the bus stop, an older kid said he had pills that would help him focus, five dollars each, from a friend online.',
    'Jalen remembered what his health teacher had said about pills from friends or online. They can be counterfeit, and some contain fentanyl, which can kill in a tiny amount and cannot be seen or tasted. He said no, said he had a bus to catch, and walked away.',
    'That evening he told his aunt, who thanked him and called the school. Then they looked in her own medicine cabinet together. She found old pain pills from a dental surgery and decided they should not sit unlocked in a house with a teenager.',
    'On Saturday they dropped the old pills at a drug take-back site at the pharmacy. The pharmacist showed Jalen two cold medicines with the same active ingredient and explained how taking both could mean a double dose.',
    'At school, vapes shaped like highlighters had started showing up. Jalen\'s friend Mara said everyone used them. Jalen checked for a science project and found that most teens do not vape, that the aerosol can carry nicotine and other harmful chemicals, and that nicotine hooks a developing brain fast.',
    'He slept nine hours before the science test, ate breakfast and walked in clear-headed. He earned a B-plus, his best science grade of the year, and nobody had to sell it to him.',
  ],
};
// Health, grades 9 to 12 (2026-10-01, pass HN): five stories at the older length with six pictures each.
STORIES['health-literacy-9'] = {
  about: 'a grandfather who called it indigestion, a granddaughter who knew the warning signs, a call to 911 instead of a drive, and a juice cleanse that promised too much',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3495', after: 1, alt: 'A teenage girl kneeling beside her grandfather on porch steps, his hand pressed to his chest' }, { serial: 'S3496', after: 2, alt: 'A girl on the phone with a 911 dispatcher beside her grandfather' }, { serial: 'S3497', after: 3, alt: 'A cardiologist talking with a girl outside a hospital room' }, { serial: 'S3498', after: 4, alt: 'A girl studying a juice cleanse video claim on her phone' }, { serial: 'S3499', after: 5, alt: 'An older man taking his blood pressure at a kitchen table before a morning walk' }],
  title: 'The pressure he almost ignored', art: 'S3494', cast: [],
  alt: 'A teenage girl and her grandfather on a porch beside a lawn mower',
  words: [
    'Lucía was sixteen and mowing her grandfather\'s lawn when he sat down hard on the porch steps. He said it was nothing, just indigestion, but he was pale and sweating on a cool October morning.',
    'She remembered her health class. Pressure in the chest, pain spreading to an arm or the jaw, shortness of breath and a cold sweat can all mean a heart attack. Her grandfather admitted his chest felt tight and his left arm ached.',
    'He wanted her to drive him to the clinic. Lucía called 911 instead, because paramedics can begin treatment on the way and warn the hospital to get ready. The dispatcher asked whether he was allergic to aspirin and, when he said no, told him to chew one.',
    'At the hospital, doctors found a blocked artery and opened it with a stent within an hour. The cardiologist told Lucía that heart muscle starts to die when its blood supply stops, so every minute she saved mattered.',
    'A week later, her aunt shared a video claiming that a juice cleanse could clear arteries in ten days. Lucía checked who made it: a company selling the juice, with no study behind the claim. She sent her aunt the hospital\'s handout on heart health instead.',
    'Her grandfather now takes his blood pressure medicine, walks every morning and keeps his checkups. He tells everyone the same thing: his high blood pressure never hurt, right up until the day it did.',
  ],
};
STORIES['nutrition-and-activity-9'] = {
  about: 'a runner who started each day with an energy drink, a nurse who read the label, six hours of sleep that became nine, and a district meet',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3501', after: 1, alt: 'A school nurse reading the label on an energy drink can' }, { serial: 'S3502', after: 2, alt: 'A tired runner awake in bed with his phone glowing' }, { serial: 'S3503', after: 3, alt: 'A breakfast of oatmeal with peanut butter, a banana, eggs and milk' }, { serial: 'S3504', after: 4, alt: 'A phone charging on a kitchen counter late at night' }, { serial: 'S3505', after: 5, alt: 'A runner crossing the finish line at a district meet' }],
  title: 'The energy drink month', art: 'S3500', cast: [],
  alt: 'A high school runner holding an energy drink can on the walk to school',
  words: [
    'Andre ran cross-country, and by October his breakfast was a 16-ounce energy drink on the walk to school. It woke him up fast. By third period he was jittery, and by practice he felt flat.',
    'The school nurse asked to see the can. It held 160 milligrams of caffeine, well past the 100 milligrams a day pediatricians advise as a limit for teens. It also held 54 grams of added sugars, 108 percent of the Daily Value in one drink.',
    'She asked how he slept. About six hours, he said, because the caffeine kept him up and he scrolled until it wore off. Teens need 8 to 10 hours, and a runner logging forty miles a week needs them more than most.',
    'They worked out a plan together. Oatmeal with peanut butter and a banana, two eggs and milk gave him steady energy, and a sandwich before practice kept him fueled. As a runner, he needed more calories than most of his classmates, not fewer. His coach added a rule for the whole team: water bottles at every practice, and no energy drinks on race days.',
    'He set his phone to charge in the kitchen at ten each night. The first week was hard. By the third week he was sleeping nine hours, and the afternoon crash was gone.',
    'At the district meet in November, Andre ran his best time of the season by forty seconds. He still drinks coffee sometimes, one small cup, and he reads every label before he buys anything in a can.',
  ],
};
STORIES['first-aid-and-safety-9'] = {
  about: 'a classmate who could not breathe, three abdominal thrusts, a stranger online who claimed to be fifteen, and two decisions that had been practiced',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3507', after: 1, alt: 'A teenager giving abdominal thrusts to a choking classmate in a cafeteria' }, { serial: 'S3508', after: 2, alt: 'A grape on a cafeteria table as a boy catches his breath' }, { serial: 'S3509', after: 3, alt: 'A girl showing her brother a message from an unknown account on her phone' }, { serial: 'S3510', after: 4, alt: 'Two siblings showing screenshots on a phone to their mother' }, { serial: 'S3511', after: 5, alt: 'A mother and her two children talking at a kitchen table' }],
  title: 'The grape and the stranger', art: 'S3506', cast: [],
  alt: 'A crowded high school cafeteria at lunch',
  words: [
    'Kenji was eating lunch in the cafeteria when the boy across from him stopped laughing in the middle of a sentence. Devon grabbed his throat with both hands, his face turning red, and no sound came out.',
    'Kenji had practiced this in health class. He asked, "Are you choking?" and Devon nodded. Kenji stood behind him, made a fist just above his navel, covered it with his other hand and gave quick inward and upward thrusts.',
    'On the third thrust, a grape shot onto the table. Devon coughed, gulped air and sat down hard. A teacher had already called the nurse, who checked him over, and the cafeteria slowly went back to its noise. Devon\'s mother called that evening to thank Kenji, and the principal asked the health teacher to run the choking drill in every class.',
    'That night, Kenji\'s younger sister, Aiko, showed him a message from an account she did not know. The sender said he was fifteen, liked the same games and wanted her to send a photo and keep their chats a secret.',
    'Kenji knew the signs from the same class: a stranger who claims to be your age, flatters you, asks for photos and wants secrecy. They took screenshots, blocked and reported the account, and told their mother, who reported it to the police.',
    'Two good decisions in one day, their mother said. Kenji shook his head. He had practiced both, he said, so when the moment came, he already knew what to do.',
  ],
};
STORIES['mind-and-mood-9'] = {
  about: 'a friend who went quiet, a post that was a warning sign, a mother woken at midnight, a call to 988, and a friend who did exactly the right thing',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3513', after: 1, alt: 'A girl reading a friend\'s post on her phone at midnight' }, { serial: 'S3514', after: 2, alt: 'A girl waking her mother to show her a text message' }, { serial: 'S3515', after: 3, alt: 'Parents sitting beside their daughter on a couch while one makes a phone call' }, { serial: 'S3516', after: 4, alt: 'Two friends talking on a park bench about a hard year' }, { serial: 'S3517', after: 5, alt: 'A school counselor talking with a teenage girl in her office' }],
  title: 'The message at midnight', art: 'S3512', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two teenage girls who have been friends since childhood, standing apart in a school hallway',
  words: [
    'Sofia and Grace had been friends since fourth grade. That spring, Grace stopped coming to lunch, quit the soccer team and stopped answering texts. Sofia assumed she was busy, or angry with her.',
    'Then, a little after midnight, Grace posted a single line: nobody would even notice if I was gone. Sofia stared at it. She remembered from health class that talking about being a burden, or about not being here, can be a warning sign.',
    'She texted Grace that she cared about her and asked how she was really doing. Grace answered that she was just tired of everything. Sofia did not try to handle it alone. She woke her mother, who called Grace\'s parents right away.',
    'Grace\'s parents sat with her that night and called the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline together. The counselor on the line helped them make a plan to keep Grace safe until she could see a doctor, and within the week she was meeting with a therapist.',
    'Grace told Sofia later that she had been angry at first. The year had been hard: her parents had divorced, and she had felt like everyone\'s problem. But the next morning, waking up with her mother beside her, was the first time in months she had felt less alone.',
    'Sofia worried that she had been a bad friend during those quiet weeks. Her school counselor told her the opposite: she noticed, she spoke up and she brought in adults who could help, which is exactly what a good friend does.',
  ],
};
STORIES['drugs-and-choices-9'] = {
  about: 'a blue pill a teammate bought online, a no and a phone call, a medicine cabinet sorted with a grandmother, naloxone in the kitchen drawer, and a teammate who came back',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3519', after: 1, alt: 'A teenager turning down a counterfeit pill in a locker room' }, { serial: 'S3520', after: 2, alt: 'A father on the phone with a coach while his son listens' }, { serial: 'S3521', after: 3, alt: 'A teenager and his grandmother sorting a medicine cabinet together' }, { serial: 'S3522', after: 4, alt: 'A box of naloxone nasal spray in a kitchen drawer' }, { serial: 'S3523', after: 5, alt: 'Two teammates talking on a bench after practice' }],
  title: 'The pill in the locker room', art: 'S3518', cast: [],
  alt: 'A high school locker room after practice',
  words: [
    'After a hard practice, a teammate held out his palm in the locker room. On it sat a small blue pill stamped like a real prescription painkiller. He said his cousin bought them online and they helped with sore muscles.',
    'Elijah said no and walked away. He had learned that pills sold online or passed between friends can be counterfeit, pressed to look exactly like the real thing. Many contain fentanyl, an opioid so strong that a tiny amount can kill.',
    'On the drive home, he told his father, who called the coach. The coach called the school nurse and the teammate\'s parents, and the school sent a message to every family about counterfeit pills and where to get naloxone.',
    'That weekend, Elijah helped his grandmother sort her medicine cabinet. They read every label: a pain reliever, an allergy pill, a cough syrup and two old prescriptions. The cough syrup and the allergy pill contained the same active ingredient, so taking both could double the dose.',
    'They carried the old prescriptions to a drug take-back event at the pharmacy, and his grandmother bought a box of naloxone nasal spray from the shelf. It sits in the kitchen drawer now, with the pharmacist\'s instructions taped to the box.',
    'The teammate stopped coming to practice in December. In January he was back, quieter, and told Elijah he had started counseling. He said the hardest part had been the first conversation, and that he was glad someone made it happen.',
  ],
};
// Reproductive and sexual health, grades 6 to 8 (2026-10-01, pass HO): four stories at the older length with six pictures each, about growing up, friendship, boundaries and getting help.
STORIES['puberty-and-the-body-6'] = {
  about: 'the shortest boy in sixth grade, a nurse with a growth chart, the gland that starts it all, a doctor\'s dot on a curve, and a voice that cracked right on time',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3525', after: 1, alt: 'A school nurse pointing to a growth chart on her office wall' }, { serial: 'S3526', after: 2, alt: 'A simple diagram of the brain with the pituitary gland highlighted' }, { serial: 'S3527', after: 3, alt: 'A school nurse\'s drawer of supplies and deodorant' }, { serial: 'S3528', after: 4, alt: 'A doctor measuring a boy\'s height at a checkup' }, { serial: 'S3529', after: 5, alt: 'A boy laughing with classmates during a class presentation' }],
  title: 'The growth chart in the nurse\'s office', art: 'S3524', cast: [],
  alt: 'A shorter sixth-grade boy beside his much taller best friend in a school hallway',
  words: [
    'Malik was twelve and the shortest boy in the sixth grade. When his best friend shot up four inches over the summer, Malik started wearing his hood in the hallway and skipping the class photo.',
    'His mother noticed and took him to see the school nurse, who kept a growth chart on her wall. She explained that puberty usually begins between about 9 and 14 for boys, and that growth spurts can come years apart for two healthy friends.',
    'She showed him how the pituitary gland, a small gland in the brain, sends the signal that starts puberty. Hormones such as testosterone then drive the changes: height, muscle, a deeper voice and more sweat. None of it runs on a schedule set by the calendar.',
    'The nurse also kept a drawer of supplies for girls whose periods began during the school day, and deodorant for anyone who needed it. Puberty, she said, is something every student in the building is going through or will go through.',
    'At Malik\'s next checkup, his doctor measured him and plotted a dot on the same kind of chart. He was growing steadily along his own curve. If anything ever looked unusual, the doctor said, they would check, but nothing did.',
    'By the spring of seventh grade, Malik\'s voice cracked in the middle of a class presentation. The class laughed, and so did he. He was finally on his way, right on his own schedule.',
  ],
};
STORIES['reproduction-and-pregnancy-6'] = {
  about: 'a grainy picture on a refrigerator, one cell that became a cousin, a heartbeat at week 6, folic acid and checkups, and a sticky note of goals',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3531', after: 1, alt: 'An aunt explaining something to her niece at a kitchen table' }, { serial: 'S3532', after: 2, alt: 'A doctor reviewing an ultrasound scan on a monitor' }, { serial: 'S3533', after: 3, alt: 'A bottle of folic acid beside a healthy breakfast' }, { serial: 'S3534', after: 4, alt: 'A woman and her husband sitting at a table with a notebook of plans' }, { serial: 'S3535', after: 5, alt: 'A sticky note of goals beside an ultrasound picture on a refrigerator' }],
  title: 'The ultrasound picture on the fridge', art: 'S3530', cast: [],
  alt: 'A grainy ultrasound picture taped to a refrigerator door',
  words: [
    'When Aunt Rachel taped a gray, grainy picture to her refrigerator, Ivy, who was twelve, could not tell what she was looking at. Her aunt laughed and pointed: a head, a curled body, a tiny hand. It was Ivy\'s cousin, twenty weeks along.',
    'Ivy asked how it all started. Her aunt explained it the way her doctor had. A sperm cell and an egg cell joined in a fallopian tube, and the single cell they made, a zygote, divided again and again on its way to the uterus.',
    'By about week 6, the doctor had seen the heart beating on an ultrasound. By week 12, the major organs had formed. This picture came from the detailed scan around week 20, when the doctor checked the baby\'s heart, brain, spine and limbs.',
    'Prenatal care had started early. Rachel took folic acid every day, kept every appointment, ate well and stayed away from alcohol and smoking. The baby still had about twenty weeks to grow, and the lungs would be among the last organs to be ready.',
    'Ivy asked whether her aunt had always planned it this way. Rachel said she and her husband had waited until they finished school, saved some money and felt ready, because a baby needs years of care, not just nine months.',
    'Ivy wrote her own goals on a sticky note that night: finish high school, go to college, see the ocean. She stuck it next to the ultrasound picture, and her aunt said both belonged on the fridge.',
  ],
};
STORIES['healthy-relationships-6'] = {
  about: 'a new friend who was fun at the start, a password and a seat at lunch, the warning signs a sister named, a boundary said plainly, and room to breathe',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3537', after: 1, alt: 'A student keeping her phone close while another student frowns' }, { serial: 'S3538', after: 2, alt: 'A student looking guilty and confused at her desk' }, { serial: 'S3539', after: 3, alt: 'Two sisters talking on a neighborhood walk' }, { serial: 'S3540', after: 4, alt: 'A student speaking calmly and clearly to a friend' }, { serial: 'S3541', after: 5, alt: 'Friends laughing together at a lunch table' }],
  title: 'The friend who wanted all of Sam\'s time', art: 'S3536', cast: [],
  alt: 'Two seventh-grade students at a school lunch table',
  words: [
    'Sam met Jordan in the opening week of seventh grade, and for a month they did everything together. Jordan was funny and generous and always saved Sam a seat.',
    'Then small things started. Jordan got quiet and angry whenever Sam sat with her old friends at lunch. Jordan wanted Sam\'s phone password, to prove they were close, and grew upset when Sam said no.',
    'By November, Jordan was telling Sam which friends were fake and which club to quit. Sam felt guilty all the time, though she could not say what she had done wrong. She started checking her phone before every lunch to see whether Jordan was upset, and she stopped going to art club, which she loved.',
    'Her older sister, Tess, listened to the whole story on a walk. Tess named what she heard: jealousy, controlling behavior and pulling Sam away from other people. Those are warning signs, she said, in a friendship or in any relationship.',
    'Sam decided on a boundary and said it plainly: I like being your friend, and I am also going to sit with my other friends and keep my phone private. Jordan sulked for a week. Sam did not change her answer.',
    'In the end, Jordan made new friends and Sam kept hers. Sam learned something she would use for years: a healthy relationship feels like respect, trust and room to breathe, and a good friend takes no for an answer. Tess told her she was proud of her.',
  ],
};
STORIES['safety-and-choices-6'] = {
  about: 'a group chat that turned cruel, a classmate who was never at fault, screenshots and a counselor, the laws that protect students, and what courage feels like',
  more: [{ serial: 'S3543', after: 1, alt: 'Two classmates sitting side by side in a science lab' }, { serial: 'S3544', after: 2, alt: 'A girl showing screenshots on her phone to her mother' }, { serial: 'S3545', after: 3, alt: 'A school counselor talking with a girl and her mother' }, { serial: 'S3546', after: 4, alt: 'Two girls talking at a lunch table' }, { serial: 'S3547', after: 5, alt: 'A health teacher talking with a student after class' }],
  title: 'The comments in the group chat', art: 'S3542', cast: [],
  alt: 'A girl looking upset at a busy group chat on her phone',
  words: [
    'The group chat had forty people in it, most of the eighth grade. One evening, a boy started posting comments about a classmate\'s body, and a few others added to them. Lena saw them and felt sick.',
    'The classmate was Maria, who sat beside Lena in science. Lena remembered from health class that comments like these are sexual harassment, and that the harm they do to feelings, trust and schoolwork can last a long time. It is never the target\'s fault.',
    'Lena did not add anything, and she did not delete the evidence either. She took screenshots, showed her mother, and the next morning they went together to the school counselor.',
    'The counselor explained what would happen. Federal law requires schools to stop sexual harassment and protect students who report it, and Texas law requires teachers and counselors to report suspected abuse. The school would handle the posts, and Lena\'s name would be kept private.',
    'The boy who started the comments was disciplined, and the chat was shut down. Maria heard who had spoken up. At lunch she told Lena she had been too embarrassed to tell anyone, and that knowing someone had her back made school bearable again. After that, the two of them ate lunch together most days.',
    'Lena told her health teacher later that telling had been hard. Her teacher said that is what courage usually feels like, and that every person who reports harassment makes the next report easier for someone else.',
  ],
};
